__d(function(e,t,n,a,s,o,i){s.exports=[{id:"cold-trail",packId:"detective",title:"The Cold Trail",subtitle:"Missing three days. Coat on the hook. No note.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"Margaret Holt has been missing for three days. Her coat is still on the hook by the door. She left everything behind \u2014 which means she didn't leave voluntarily. You have a diner receipt, a business card, and a pharmaceutical company that doesn't want questions asked.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Margaret Holt, thirty-four, pharmacist, missing since Thursday. Her sister reported it Saturday when Margaret missed two shifts and stopped answering her phone. The building super let you in. Bed made, coffee cup rinsed and dried on the rack, coat on the hook by the door. People who leave voluntarily take their coat. You stand in the middle of the room and let the stillness tell you something.",choices:[{label:"Search the apartment carefully before touching anything.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Check her phone and laptop first \u2014 battery won't last.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Talk to the neighbors before evidence cools further.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The coat pockets: a receipt from Sal's Diner dated Thursday 7pm, two coffees \u2014 she had company. A business card for someone named Pryce, solicitor, no firm listed, a mobile number handwritten on the back. The ink is barely set. This card is recent.",choices:[{label:"Pryce and the diner are both leads. Check her phone before the battery dies.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Her phone has four percent battery. Last call received Thursday 8:43pm from a number saved as 'E.P.' \u2014 eleven seconds, too short for a real conversation. Last text to her sister at 8:46: 'Something came up. Don't worry.' She never replied to her sister's response. Something stopped her three minutes after that call.",choices:[{label:"E.P. called her and three minutes later she stopped responding. Check the apartment more.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"detective-street",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The woman across the hall heard the door close around eight-thirty Thursday \u2014 later than Margaret usually returned on work nights. No footsteps coming back. The man in 4B saw a dark car idling outside Thursday evening for forty minutes, which he found odd enough to notice but not enough to report.",choices:[{label:"Eight-thirty, a waiting car, no return. The coat confirms she didn't plan to stay away.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You piece together what you have: Margaret met someone for coffee Thursday evening. A man named Pryce called her at 8:43pm. Three minutes later she texted her sister, then went silent. Her coat stayed. Something happened Thursday night and whoever was waiting outside may have been part of it.",choices:[{label:"Go to Sal's Diner. Find who she met for coffee.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Call the number on Pryce's card.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Get the phone records for the past month, not just Thursday.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The waitress at Sal's remembers Margaret because she was a regular. Thursday night she came in with a man \u2014 older, well-dressed, white-haired, paid cash. They sat at the back. The waitress didn't hear the conversation but she noticed Margaret looked tense when they left. Not frightened. Tense. There's a difference.",choices:[{label:"White-haired, well-dressed, paid cash. That might be Pryce.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A man answers. His voice is carefully controlled. You say you're looking for Margaret Holt. Pause. He says he doesn't know anyone by that name. You say his card was in her coat pocket. Another pause. He says he thinks he should speak to his solicitor. You almost laugh \u2014 he is the solicitor. He hangs up.",choices:[{label:"The denial means he knows something. Find out who Pryce is.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A month of call records. E.P. called Margaret six times in the last three weeks. First four she ignored. Fifth she answered \u2014 forty-one minutes. Sixth was Thursday, eleven seconds. Before E.P. started calling, Margaret called the city council's public health line three times. Whatever she saw at work, she tried to report it officially first.",choices:[{label:"She tried official channels first. That's important. Find out what E.P. wanted.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Edmund Pryce, sixty-one, solicitor and legal consultant. No firm listed \u2014 which means he works for one very rich client on retainer. His name appears in a planning dispute connected to Vanta Pharmaceuticals Distribution. Vanta has supply contracts with three city pharmacies. One of them is where Margaret works.",choices:[{label:"Find out what Vanta does and why it might have sent a solicitor after a pharmacist.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Talk to someone at Margaret's pharmacy. Her colleagues will know if something happened.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Find the public health calls \u2014 who did Margaret speak to there?",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Vanta distributes controlled medications to independent pharmacies. Their delivery volumes, cross-referenced against NHS prescription data for the areas they serve, run thirty percent above what should be needed. That gap \u2014 the difference between what gets delivered and what gets prescribed \u2014 is significant. Margaret would have seen the inventory numbers every day.",choices:[{label:"She found the gap. That's what she tried to report. Go to the pharmacy.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The pharmacy manager is visibly uncomfortable the moment you mention Margaret. He says she took a personal leave. You say: when someone leaves involuntarily, it's not personal leave. He says: I think you should speak to the company's legal representative. He says it with the relief of someone who's been waiting to say exactly that sentence.",choices:[{label:"Legal representative is Pryce. Someone got to the manager before you.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The public health inspector Margaret called is a man named Davies. He took her calls, filed a report, and was told by his supervisor a week later that no action was warranted. His supervisor, you discover, has been in three meetings this year with representatives from Vanta. The official channel Margaret tried was monitored by the people she was reporting.",choices:[{label:"The channel was compromised. Davies can still be a witness. Go to the pharmacy now.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You understand the situation. Margaret discovered Vanta was routing controlled medications through the pharmacy beyond what prescriptions warranted. She reported it to public health \u2014 where Vanta had connections. She was ignored. Then Pryce started calling. He represents Vanta. He wanted her silent. Thursday night was the meeting where he found out she wasn't going to be.",choices:[{label:"Find Pryce and confront him with what you know.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Find Margaret first. She might still be reachable.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Get the evidence of the inventory discrepancy into safe hands first.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Pryce's office is a good address in the city. His assistant says he's unavailable. You sit in the waiting room for forty minutes. He walks out eventually and stops when he sees you. You say: I know about Vanta and I know about the inventory numbers. His face doesn't move but something behind it does. He says: Margaret is safe. She made a decision. You say: I'd like to hear that from her.",choices:[{label:"He says she's safe and made a decision. Push for proof.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"detective-street",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You track Margaret's movements through her phone signal \u2014 the last ping before it died puts her in a serviced apartment building two miles from Pryce's office. She checked in Thursday night. She's still there or she was, until recently. The building requires a key fob. You can't get in without help.",choices:[{label:"She's somewhere Pryce arranged for her. Go to Pryce.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You pull Vanta's delivery manifests, which are technically public record. The discrepancy is significant and consistent \u2014 they've been running this for at least eighteen months across three pharmacies. You copy everything and send it to a contact at a regulatory body before doing anything else. Whatever happens next, the evidence is safe.",choices:[{label:"Evidence secured. Now find Margaret.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Pryce tells you Margaret chose to step back from her role, has been given support and accommodation, and will be fine. He says this with the smooth certainty of a man who has scripted it. You say: I want to hear that from her. He says: she doesn't want contact. You say: I'll need to verify that. He says: verify it through proper channels. You say: I am the proper channel.",choices:[{label:"Demand a phone call with her. Right now, from his phone.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Leave. You know where she is. Go there instead.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Tell him you're going to the police with the Vanta records. Watch his reaction.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"He makes the call. He hands you the phone. Margaret's voice \u2014 thin, controlled, careful. She says she's alright. She says she made a decision and would prefer not to have contact for a while. You ask her one thing: the name of her childhood dog. She answers immediately. Jasper. It's her. She's alive and she's speaking freely, but the words sound rehearsed.",choices:[{label:"She's alive. But 'rehearsed' means this isn't over.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"detective-street",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You go to the serviced apartment building. You wait. Two hours later Margaret walks out with a small bag. She sees you and stops. Something in her face breaks open \u2014 not surprise, but relief. She says: I didn't know how to call anyone. I didn't know if he was watching. You say: come with me.",choices:[{label:"She came out voluntarily. Get her somewhere safe.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The reaction is subtle and definitive: a brief stillness, a recalculation. He says: Margaret is cooperating with a process that benefits everyone. He says: the Vanta matter is an internal compliance issue. He says both sentences in the tone of someone who has just decided to give you something. He gives you a different address \u2014 not the first one you found \u2014 and says she's been there since Friday.",choices:[{label:"He gave you the address voluntarily. That means he's changing his approach.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You reach Margaret. She is physically fine. She is not fine in other ways \u2014 a week of isolation and careful management has done something to her. She tells you everything. Vanta. The inventory gap. Her attempts to report it. Pryce's calls. The Thursday meeting where Pryce offered her a settlement: stay quiet, accept a payout, leave the pharmacy. She refused. Then he stopped offering.",choices:[{label:"Ask what happened after she refused Thursday night.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Ask what she wants to happen now \u2014 her call.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Tell her about the evidence you've already secured.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"After she refused Thursday, Pryce told her he couldn't guarantee what would happen next. He didn't threaten her directly. He said: the situation has dynamics beyond my control. She understood it as a threat. She checked into the serviced apartment herself, thinking she was safer somewhere else. She's been waiting to figure out her next move ever since.",choices:[{label:"She moved herself. Pryce didn't imprison her. The situation is different from what you assumed.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Margaret says: I want it reported. I want the Vanta records to go somewhere they can't disappear. I want it on record that I tried official channels and was ignored. She says this with a clarity that tells you the week of isolation sharpened her rather than broke her. She says: what I don't want is to do this alone.",choices:[{label:"She doesn't have to do it alone. Here's what you have so far.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Margaret's face changes when you tell her the inventory records are already in regulatory hands. She says: then it's not just my word. She says: that's the thing I was most afraid of \u2014 that it would come down to just my word against Vanta's resources. With the records, it doesn't.",choices:[{label:"The records change everything. Now plan the next step together.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You and Margaret spend two hours going through what you have and what she has. Her records from the pharmacy \u2014 she kept copies, which Pryce doesn't know. Your inventory analysis. Davies's testimony about his compromised supervisor. The week of isolation, which was her choice but was made under indirect pressure. You build the complete picture. Then you decide how to use it.",choices:[{label:"Go to the police with Margaret. Her safety is documented and the fraud is evidenced.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Go to the regulatory body directly \u2014 the fraud is their jurisdiction, not criminal.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Go to the press. Vanta's lawyers move fast \u2014 public record makes this harder to suppress.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The police take a full statement from Margaret and then from you. The detective who leads it is thorough \u2014 she says the medication routing is a matter for the pharmacy regulator but the pressure applied to Margaret may constitute coercion. She says: we'll look at Pryce. She says it like she means it.",choices:[{label:"The police have it. Now wait.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The pharmaceutical regulator's senior investigator looks at the delivery manifests and says: this is significant. He says: eighteen months, three pharmacies, thirty percent above prescription volumes \u2014 this is systematic. He says he'll open a formal investigation and that Margaret's account is the primary witness statement. He says: she's protected once this is filed.",choices:[{label:"Filed. The regulator is moving. The police will follow.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The journalist you choose has covered pharmaceutical fraud before. She says: this is exactly the kind of story where public record is the best protection. She files within twenty-four hours. Vanta's lawyers send a letter. The letter makes her more confident the story is accurate, not less. By Monday morning, three other publications are following it.",choices:[{label:"The story is out. Now the formal processes catch up.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The formal processes begin to move. Margaret is back in contact with her sister. She's not working at the pharmacy \u2014 that's finished, one way or another \u2014 but she's talking to a lawyer of her own. Pryce has gone quiet. Vanta's communications department is issuing careful statements. The regulator is asking questions that Vanta's lawyers are working hard to answer.",choices:[{label:"Make sure Margaret has everything she needs to navigate what comes next.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Follow up on Pryce \u2014 is he facing any personal consequences?",next:"b8b"},{label:"Check what the regulator is finding as the investigation runs.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Margaret's own lawyer says the situation is clear: she's a protected whistleblower. The coercion element \u2014 Pryce's 'dynamics beyond my control' comment, the isolation, the pressure \u2014 is being reviewed. Her lawyer says: she has a strong position. Margaret says: I just want it on record. Her lawyer says: it will be.",choices:[{label:"She's in good hands. Let the process run.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Pryce is being investigated by the Solicitors Regulation Authority for his conduct in advising Vanta's approach to Margaret. The SRA case is separate from the pharmaceutical investigation. His 'dynamics beyond my control' comment, when reviewed, looks like a barely concealed threat from someone who should have known better.",choices:[{label:"Pryce is accountable. The main story is the pharmaceutical investigation.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The regulator's investigation finds what you found, and more. The thirty-percent discrepancy is confirmed across all three pharmacies. The controlled medications are identified \u2014 the routing isn't random, it targets specific drug categories. The investigation expands beyond Vanta to the prescribing practices that made the routing possible.",choices:[{label:"The investigation is bigger than Margaret's case. That's how it should be.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"detective-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three months pass. Margaret is safe and has moved to a different city, which she chose herself. The regulatory investigation is progressing. Vanta's contracts with the three pharmacies have been suspended pending review. Pryce's SRA case is ongoing. The pharmacy manager who deflected you has given a full statement \u2014 it turned out he was also under pressure.",choices:[{label:"Ask Margaret how she's doing.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Check the status of the criminal investigation into Vanta.",next:"b9b"},{label:"Find out what happened to Davies \u2014 the public health inspector who was ignored.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Margaret says she's better than she expected to be. She says: the thing I kept thinking about was whether I'd done something wrong by noticing. She says: I didn't. She's working part-time at a GP's surgery while she decides what's next. She says: thank you for not stopping when I told you I was fine.",choices:[{label:"She wasn't fine. You knew that.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The criminal investigation into Vanta's directors is running. The controlled medication routing may constitute supply offences. The question the investigators are working through: was it systematic at the company level or was it a rogue element in the distribution chain? The answer matters for who gets charged.",choices:[{label:"That question will take time. Let it run.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Davies is giving evidence to the parliamentary committee reviewing pharmaceutical oversight. He says his supervisor told him the Vanta complaints were below the threshold for action. His supervisor has since resigned. Davies says: I should have pushed harder. He says: I thought the system would catch it. He says: I was wrong about that.",choices:[{label:"He's right about that. Systems catch things only if someone makes them.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You close the file. Margaret is safe. The fraud is being investigated at the level it deserves. Pryce is facing professional consequences. The investigation has expanded beyond what any one person could have predicted when a missing woman's coat was still on the hook. You write up your final report and send your invoice to Margaret's sister, who called you initially and who cried briefly on the phone when you told her everything was going to be alright.",choices:[{label:"Vanta is prosecuted and convicted.",next:"end-prosecuted"},{label:"Vanta cooperates with regulators \u2014 no criminal charges but contracts revoked.",next:"end-regulatory"},{label:"The investigation drags on for years. Margaret moves forward without waiting for it.",next:"end-long"},{label:"The case becomes a landmark for pharmaceutical whistleblower protection.",next:"end-landmark"}]},"end-prosecuted":{id:"end-prosecuted",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Three directors of Vanta's distribution division are prosecuted for controlled substance offences. Two are convicted. The third cooperates and receives a suspended sentence. Pryce is struck off by the SRA. The pharmacy at which Margaret worked is under new management. The pharmaceutical regulator publishes new guidelines based on the case. Margaret is named in the guidelines' foreword \u2014 with her permission \u2014 as the person who first identified the discrepancy. Her name is on something permanent.",choices:[]},"end-regulatory":{id:"end-regulatory",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Vanta's cooperation results in no criminal charges but a complete revocation of its distribution contracts and a substantial civil penalty. The company restructures and some of its directors resign. Pryce is suspended by the SRA for twelve months. Margaret is compensated under the whistleblower settlement framework. The outcome is not the one she might have written \u2014 no prison sentences \u2014 but the contracts are revoked and the routing has stopped. She says: that was the point.",choices:[]},"end-long":{id:"end-long",scene:"ending-neutral",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The investigation takes four years. Margaret doesn't wait for it. She requalifies in a different field, moves twice, and is working as a GP's practice manager when the final regulatory decision comes in \u2014 a quiet press release that most people don't read. She reads it. She sends you a message: it happened. You reply: I know. That exchange is the end of it, and it's enough.",choices:[]},"end-landmark":{id:"end-landmark",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The case is cited in parliamentary debate on pharmaceutical oversight reform. A new whistleblower protection framework \u2014 the Holt Provisions, named informally \u2014 is passed two years after the investigation concludes. Margaret testifies to the select committee that reviewed the framework. She is clear, specific, and calm. A journalist writes that she was the most convincing witness of the session. Margaret reads the article and then goes back to work.",choices:[]}}},{id:"dark-side",packId:"space",title:"Dark Side",subtitle:"The station was built on the far side of a moon. There was a reason.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You're the new station commander for a research outpost on the dark side of Callisto. Your predecessor resigned suddenly. Three researchers have requested transfer. And someone has been transmitting to an unauthorized frequency\u2014every 72 hours, like clockwork.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Station Callisto-7 has a crew of nine and a mission that looks simple on paper: long-baseline astronomy, using Callisto's far side to block Jupiter's interference. You've been commander for six days. Your predecessor, Commander Reyes, submitted his resignation effective immediately three weeks ago with no explanation. Your first anomaly report: an unauthorized transmission detected 72 hours ago, 144 hours ago, and presumably 216 hours ago. Every 72 hours, duration thirty seconds, encoded.",choices:[{label:"Decode the transmission first. Understand what's being sent.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Find out which terminal the transmission originates from.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Read Reyes's final station logs before doing anything else.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"station-command",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The encoding is not complex \u2014 it's a standard compression protocol for data transmission. The decoded content: a numerical sequence, 40 digits, repeating across all three transmissions. The same sequence each time. Not a message. A number. Transmitted to coordinates in Jupiter's outer magnetosphere.",choices:[{label:"The same 40-digit number, transmitted three times to the same coordinates. That's a signal or a key.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"station-command",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The transmission originates from a terminal in Lab 3 \u2014 the deep-field astronomy workstation. Lab 3 is staffed by three researchers: Dr. Okafor, Dr. Wei, and Dr. Solis. Any of them could have initiated the transmission during their shift rotations. The terminal's user log shows the transmission sent during automated maintenance windows when user tracking is reduced.",choices:[{label:"Sent during a window when tracking is reduced. Someone knows the system's blind spots.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"station-command",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Reyes's final logs: professional, thorough, and then a gap. His last thirty days of logs are missing \u2014 not deleted, but moved to a locked archive that requires authorization he apparently took with him when he resigned. His final entry before the gap: 'I cannot in good conscience continue without further guidance. Request immediate command review.' No explanation of what he needed guidance on.",choices:[{label:"He locked his logs when he left. The gap is the information.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"station-command",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Six days in and you have: a regular unauthorized transmission to an unusual coordinate, a predecessor who fled without explanation, and three researchers who requested transfer after Reyes left. The three researchers are Okafor, Wei, and Solis \u2014 the same three who staff Lab 3. You need to understand what happened here before the next transmission.",choices:[{label:"Talk to the three transfer-requesting researchers together.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Talk to them separately. See whose story differs.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Check what the three researchers have been studying that isn't in their official reports.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"All three in the same room produces an interesting dynamic: Okafor does the talking, Wei defers to him, and Solis watches you carefully without speaking. Okafor says: 'We requested transfer because Commander Reyes made a decision we disagreed with. He resigned before the situation could be resolved. We believe it was the wrong decision.' He says this with the careful precision of someone who has rehearsed exactly this much.",choices:[{label:"They disagreed with Reyes's decision. Find out what the decision was.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Okafor: tight, professional, says little. Wei: nervous, says more \u2014 she mentions 'the finding' without specifying it. Solis: looks at the floor and says: 'You're going to find out anyway. The transmission is still running.' Three people with three different relationships to the same secret. Solis is ready to talk.",choices:[{label:"Solis is ready to tell you. Talk to Solis alone.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Their official research reports cover long-baseline stellar observation. Their actual data logs contain something else: a second data stream, running parallel to the stellar observation, tracking a signal source in Jupiter's outer magnetosphere. They have eighteen months of data on a source they haven't reported. The unauthorized transmission is a response \u2014 they've been answering something.",choices:[{label:"Eighteen months of unreported data. They're answering a signal from Jupiter's magnetosphere.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The finding: eighteen months ago, during a routine calibration sweep, the deep-field array picked up a non-natural signal from Jupiter's outer magnetosphere. Not a known satellite, not spacecraft interference, not the standard electromagnetic noise of Jupiter's system. A structured signal. They analyzed it for three months before concluding: it is structured information. They started responding.",choices:[{label:"How confident are they that the signal is non-natural?",next:"b3a"},{label:"What does the signal contain?",next:"b3b"},{label:"Why didn't they report this to Earth command?",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Okafor says: 'We have six independent methods for ruling out natural sources. The signal has three properties that, in combination, cannot arise from known electromagnetic phenomena: it's narrowband, it's phase-coherent, and it changes in response to external signals.' That last property is the one that ended Reyes's career. The signal responds. To them.",choices:[{label:"It responds to them. That's what Reyes couldn't continue without guidance on.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Wei pulls up the decoded signal. It's mathematical \u2014 prime sequences, geometric progressions, and then something that might be a coordinate system native to this solar system. 'We've been sending primes back,' she says. 'And it responded with composites. Then we sent composites and it sent primes back. And then it sent something we haven't decoded yet. Something that isn't pure mathematics.'",choices:[{label:"Mathematical exchange that led to something that isn't mathematics. Understand what that is.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Solis says: 'Because we were afraid of what would happen if we did. Not for us \u2014 for the signal.' She says: 'If we report this and the first response is a military asset, or a quarantine, or an extraction of our data without our involvement \u2014 we're not scientists anymore. We're an incident.' She says: 'Commander Reyes agreed with us. Until he didn't.'",choices:[{label:"They kept it secret to control how it was handled. Reyes eventually couldn't continue that.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You understand the situation. Your three researchers have been in contact with a non-natural signal source for eighteen months. They've built a mathematical dialogue with it. They kept it from Earth because they were afraid of how Earth would respond. Reyes found out, struggled with the decision, and resigned rather than either report it or continue the silence. Now it's yours.",choices:[{label:"Ask to see everything \u2014 eighteen months of data, every transmission, every response.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Understand the current state of the dialogue before making any decision.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Tell them: this is above all our pay grades. Start the report process.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"They give you everything. You spend four hours going through it. The signal is real and its non-natural origin is, to your non-specialist eyes, convincing. The mathematical exchange is real. The thing they haven't decoded \u2014 which arrived eight months ago and repeats every 72 hours if they don't respond \u2014 is a different kind of structure. Not mathematics. Something else.",choices:[{label:"Something that isn't mathematics, repeating every 72 hours. That's the unauthorized transmission they're responding to.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The current state: the signal sent something eight months ago that they don't understand. They've been sending their 40-digit number every 72 hours as a 'we're still here' signal while they work on decoding the unknown element. The source, whatever it is, has been patient. No escalation, no change. Just the original unknown signal repeating, and their response, and silence.",choices:[{label:"It's been patient for eight months. Eight months of a conversation that's stalled at a word neither side understands.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"station-command",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Okafor says: 'And then what? An asset arrives in six weeks and takes our data, and the next transmission from our station is in a military frequency, and whatever is out there decides what humanity looks like is a weapons system.' You say: 'That might not be what happens.' He says: 'It might be exactly what happens.' He's not wrong that it might.",choices:[{label:"He's not wrong. Think about how to report this and to whom and in what form.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"station-command",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The 72-hour clock is running. The next unauthorized transmission \u2014 your three researchers' 40-digit 'we're still here' \u2014 is in nine hours. You need to decide: let it go, stop it, join it, or report it before then.",choices:[{label:"Let the transmission go. Don't disrupt eighteen months of careful work.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Stop the transmission. Report to Earth. Take the consequences of doing it right.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Join the dialogue. Understand what the unknown element means before any other decision.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You let the transmission go. The 40-digit sequence goes out. Okafor thanks you. You say: 'I'm not endorsing this indefinitely. I need to understand what we're doing before the next decision point.' He says: 'That's fair.' Solis says: 'Thank you for not just stopping it.' You say: 'The clock is still running.'",choices:[{label:"The clock is running. Understand what you're dealing with.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"station-command",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You stop the transmission and begin the report. Okafor is tightly controlled. Wei looks like something broke. Solis says: 'This is what Reyes chose not to do. He was afraid of what comes after.' You say: 'I know.' You say: 'But I'm responsible for this station and its decisions, and I can't run it on a secret this size.' The report goes to Earth command.",choices:[{label:"Report filed. Wait for the response and manage the crew through whatever comes.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You sit with the four of them and spend eight of the nine hours on the unknown element. It's not mathematical but it has structure. Wei has been working on it for eight months and she's closest \u2014 she thinks it might be a map, or a dimensional description, or something that doesn't have a category in human information theory yet. 'Maybe it's asking us something,' she says. 'Maybe it's asking where we are.'",choices:[{label:"Maybe it's asking where we are. Think about what responding to that would mean.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Whatever path you chose at the transmission decision, you're now deep in a situation that is going to define this station's place in history \u2014 and your own. The question of what the signal is, what it wants, and what humanity's first coherent response should look like is not one any nine-person outpost was supposed to answer. And yet here you are.",choices:[{label:"The researchers' eighteen months of work is the most valuable thing here. Protect it.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Earth command's response, when it comes, will determine the next steps.",next:"b6b"},{label:"The signal itself is the priority. Understand the unknown element.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You archive the eighteen months of data redundantly \u2014 copies in three locations, one off-station with an academic archive that has a legal protection against military seizure. Whatever comes next, the research record is safe. Okafor says: 'Thank you.' He says it like he's been holding onto something and has just set it down.",choices:[{label:"The work is protected. Now navigate what comes next.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Earth command's response comes in forty-eight hours \u2014 faster than normal transit time, which means they expedited it. The response is careful and the people who wrote it are clearly alarmed and trying not to show it. They say: continue monitoring. They say: do not transmit anything new. They say: a review team is being assembled. They don't say when the review team will arrive.",choices:[{label:"Continue monitoring. Don't transmit anything new. The clock is still running at the source.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Wei has been right that it might be a locational query. You spend forty-eight hours working with all four of them on the unknown element. By the end you have a hypothesis: it's using the mathematics you both understand as a foundation and adding a third layer \u2014 something that uses astronomical position data as its medium. It might be asking: where exactly are you, and are you there continuously?",choices:[{label:"A locational query, asking if you're continuous. That changes what response means.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three weeks in. The review team's arrival date has been given: six weeks. You have a crew of nine people carrying an enormous secret, a signal source that has been patient for eight months, and six weeks to build a policy for how humanity's first contact with something non-human should be managed from a nine-person outpost.",choices:[{label:"Brief the full station crew. Everyone here is affected by this.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Work with the three researchers on a formal research paper for the review team.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Establish a clear protocol for communications with the source until the review team arrives.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You brief all nine crew members. The silence afterward lasts almost two minutes. Then your station medic says: 'So we're it.' You say: 'We're it for now.' Your pilot says: 'Are we safe?' Okafor says: 'Eighteen months of contact and no indication of anything except patience.' The pilot thinks about this. He says: 'Okay.' That's the crew's response: okay.",choices:[{label:"Okay. Nine people holding something extraordinary, quietly.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The research paper takes three weeks and is the most careful document you've ever worked on. Every claim hedged to its evidence, every alternative explanation addressed, every methodological decision justified. When the review team reads it they will have no basis for dismissing the finding \u2014 only for engaging with it. That's the goal.",choices:[{label:"A paper that can't be dismissed. The review team will have to engage.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Protocol for the six weeks: the 40-digit signal continues on schedule \u2014 it's a known quantity and stopping it unilaterally might change what the source does. No new transmissions without consensus of all four people involved. All interactions logged with full documentation. When the review team arrives, they'll have a complete record of every decision and why.",choices:[{label:"Logged, documented, defensible. The review team will understand every decision.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Five weeks in. The review team arrives early \u2014 a contact with Earth command gave you two days' notice. Five specialists, two of whom you recognize as among the world's leading researchers in SETI-adjacent fields. They are quiet when they deplane and immediately ask to see the data. You give it to them. All of it.",choices:[{label:"Watch how the review team responds to the first 24 hours of data.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Introduce them to the three researchers. This is their discovery.",next:"b8b"},{label:"Give them the data and wait. This is now their process.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The review team's lead, Dr. Park, emerges after fourteen hours. She finds you and says: 'The finding is real.' She says it with the flatness of someone who has spent fourteen hours hoping to find a flaw and didn't. She says: 'We need to talk about how this proceeds.' She says: 'The 72-hour clock \u2014 is it still running?' You say: 'Yes.' She says: 'Good. Don't stop it.'",choices:[{label:"Don't stop it. The review team is engaged.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"station-lab",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You introduce the review team to Okafor, Wei, and Solis. The review team's first response is not to challenge the finding but to ask Wei about her work on the unknown element. Wei, for the first time since this began, gets to talk to five people who understand exactly what she's been working on. The conversation runs for six hours. You leave them to it.",choices:[{label:"Wei is finally talking to people who understand. That matters.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You give them the data and step back. The review team works for two days without asking you for anything. On day three, Park comes to your office and says: 'I want to understand the decision tree that got you from discovery to this moment.' You walk her through every decision, every choice, what you were told, what you observed, what you concluded. She takes notes for three hours.",choices:[{label:"She needed the decision record. That was the gap in the data.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The review team reaches a consensus position in four days. They recommend: the signal is genuine, the contact should be maintained by the same team that established it, and the response protocol should be developed collaboratively between the original researchers and specialists from Earth. They recommend against militarization, against a news blackout longer than six months, and in favor of Callisto-7 as the primary contact station.",choices:[{label:"Their recommendation protects the work and the researchers.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Ask what six months of news blackout means practically.",next:"b9b"},{label:"Ask what happens to this station's mission going forward.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The recommendation is not guaranteed to be followed \u2014 it goes to agencies that will make their own decisions. But a unanimous finding from five independent specialists, publicly submitted, is harder to override than a single station commander's report. The researchers' work is on record and credited. That part, at least, is secure.",choices:[{label:"The credit is secured. The larger decisions are above anyone's station.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Six months means: time to establish protocols, time to prepare a framework for public disclosure, time to ensure the contact team understands what they have and can explain it clearly. Park says: 'After six months, this will be public. The question is whether it goes out as a finding or as an incident.' She says: 'We want it to go out as a finding.'",choices:[{label:"A finding, not an incident. Six months to build that framing.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The station's astronomy mission is suspended \u2014 the deep-field array is now fully dedicated to the contact signal. The three transfer requests are withdrawn; Okafor, Wei, and Solis are going nowhere. A fourth researcher arrives from Earth. Then a fifth. In three months, Callisto-7 triples its crew and becomes something no one designed it to be.",choices:[{label:"Something no one designed it to be. That's the story of how discoveries work.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"station-command",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Six months later. The public disclosure goes out as a finding \u2014 a careful, documented, scientist-led announcement that humanity has detected and has been in contact with a non-natural signal source for nineteen months. The world's response is, in aggregate, stranger and less dramatic than anyone expected. Most people continue their lives. Some are frightened. Many are curious. A few are transformed. The signal continues to transmit every 72 hours.",choices:[{label:"The signal continues. Whatever is out there is still patient.",next:"end-contact"},{label:"Reyes finds out. Think about him.",next:"end-reyes"},{label:"The researchers' careers are changed by this. Think about them.",next:"end-researchers"},{label:"What the unknown element meant is finally decoded.",next:"end-decoded"}]},"end-contact":{id:"end-contact",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The signal continues every 72 hours. The contact protocol is now run by a team of twelve from six countries, based at Callisto-7, which has grown to a crew of forty. The mathematical exchange has moved past primes and composites into something neither human language nor mathematics has categories for yet. Wei leads the decoding effort. She says: 'It's like learning a language by being handed the grammar first and having to work backward to the words.' She says it like that's a gift. It is.",choices:[]},"end-reyes":{id:"end-reyes",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"Reyes learns about the disclosure from the news, like everyone else. He contacts you once, briefly. He says: 'I made the wrong call.' You say: 'You made the honest call. You couldn't continue without guidance.' He says: 'I should have found a way to get guidance without leaving.' He says: 'How is it going?' You tell him. He listens for a long time. He says: 'Good.' He says: 'I'm glad it's going well.' You think he means it.",choices:[]},"end-researchers":{id:"end-researchers",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Okafor, Wei, and Solis are the most famous scientists alive. This is not, they report, as comfortable as it sounds. Wei says: 'People expect us to know things we don't know yet.' She says: 'The only honest thing I can tell anyone is: we're still learning the language.' Solis says he's glad they didn't report it immediately \u2014 he says the eighteen months of secret dialogue built a foundation that a rushed announcement would have destroyed. Okafor says nothing about any of it publicly. He's too busy with the work.",choices:[]},"end-decoded":{id:"end-decoded",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The unknown element is decoded two years after your arrival at Callisto-7. It is, as Wei suspected, locational \u2014 but not a question. An answer. Coordinates. Coordinates within the solar system, in a region of Jupiter's outer magnetosphere where no natural body should exist. When a survey probe is sent to those coordinates, it finds something at the edge of detection: a structure, small, artificial, at the exact location the signal has been transmitting from. The source is not in Jupiter's magnetosphere. The source is at the coordinates it sent you. It's been telling you where to look.",choices:[]}}},{id:"distress-signal",packId:"space",title:"Distress Signal",subtitle:"It's been broadcasting for eleven years. No one answered. Until now.",length:"Short",free:!0,brief:"Your survey ship has picked up a distress signal from an abandoned research station. Protocol says log it and move on. But the signal contains something that shouldn't be possible\u2014and someone on your crew isn't telling you everything they know.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The signal is faint and eleven years old \u2014 the station went dark in the early days of the outer survey and was listed as a decommissioning failure. Standard procedure is to log the position and transmit a notification to the registry. But your comms officer, Chen, has been staring at her panel for four minutes without speaking, which is not standard procedure. Then she says: 'Commander, the signal isn't a distress broadcast. It's a message. It's addressed to a specific ship. It's addressed to us.'",choices:[{label:"Ask Chen to play the full message.",next:"s2"},{label:"Ask Chen how our ship's designation could be in an eleven-year-old signal.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Check the station's registry file before doing anything else.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Chen says she doesn't know. Our ship was commissioned eight years ago. The message is eleven years old. Three years before our ship existed, someone transmitted a message addressed to us by name. Either the timestamp is corrupted, or someone knew our ship would be built, or something else is happening that doesn't have a good explanation.",choices:[{label:"No good explanation is still information. Hear the message.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The station's registry: Relay Station Koval, established 2041, research and communications relay, crew of six, emergency decommission logged 2043. Cause: systems cascade failure. All crew listed as evacuated. But there's a discrepancy \u2014 the evacuation manifests list five crew. Station registered with six.",choices:[{label:"One person unaccounted for. Hear the message.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The message plays. A woman's voice, tired but clear. She says: 'If you're receiving this, the timing worked. I know you don't know what that means yet. The station is not abandoned \u2014 I'm here, and I've been here because I needed to be. I can't explain in a broadcast. Come to the station. Come alone if you can. Don't bring anyone who wasn't on the original survey mission.' The message ends. Chen is very still. Then she says: 'I was on the original survey mission.'",choices:[{label:"Ask Chen what she knows about this.",next:"s3"},{label:"Ask who else on your crew was on the original mission.",next:"b2a"},{label:"The message said timing worked. Ask Chen what that means.",next:"b2b"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You check the crew manifest against the original survey mission records. Three people on your current crew were also on the survey eleven years ago: Chen, your chief engineer Vasquez, and your medic, Dr. Osei. The woman in the message said don't bring anyone who wasn't on the original mission. She meant these three specifically.",choices:[{label:"Three crew members knew this station. Get them together.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Chen says: 'Timing worked' refers to something Dr. Maren \u2014 the voice in the message \u2014 was working on before the evacuation. She says it carefully, like she's choosing what to reveal. She says: 'Maren was trying to solve a specific navigation problem. The kind of problem where the solution depends on knowing what ship will arrive at a particular time and place.' She says this with the manner of someone who has known for some time that this moment would come.",choices:[{label:"Chen has been expecting this. Get the truth from her before you go anywhere.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Chen tells you \u2014 with visible relief at finally saying it \u2014 that Dr. Maren was the station's lead researcher. She was working on a navigational problem involving a region of space the survey had flagged as anomalous. When the station was ordered to decommission, Maren refused to leave. She had a reason. Chen knew she had stayed. She didn't report it.",choices:[{label:"Why didn't Chen report it?",next:"b3a"},{label:"What was the anomalous region?",next:"b3b"},{label:"Is Maren definitely still alive?",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Chen says: 'Because Maren asked me not to. She said if the wrong people knew she was there, the research would be taken from her before it was complete. She said: give me time.' Chen says: 'I gave her time. Eleven years.' She says it with the weight of a decision that settled somewhere inside her a long time ago.",choices:[{label:"Eleven years of keeping someone's secret. Now go find out what it bought.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The anomalous region is in the station's survey data \u2014 a gravitational signature that doesn't match any charted body. Maren's working hypothesis, in the files she shared with Chen before the evacuation, was that the signature indicated a stable navigable pathway through a region previously considered impassable. If she was right, it changes the geometry of the outer survey.",choices:[{label:"A stable pathway. That's what she stayed for.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Chen says: 'The message was pre-recorded for automated transmission. She set it to broadcast when a ship matching certain parameters entered range. The fact that we received it means the system was still running when we arrived. She built it to last.' She pauses. 'I believe she's alive. I've believed it for eleven years. But I don't know.'",choices:[{label:"Go find out.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You make the decision: approach the station. You bring Chen, Vasquez, and Osei. You leave the rest of the crew on the ship with orders to wait. The station is three hours away at slow approach. You spend the time reviewing Maren's research files \u2014 the ones Chen saved, illegally, for eleven years.",choices:[{label:"Study the research. Understand what Maren found before you meet her.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Talk to Vasquez and Osei. Find out what they knew.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Monitor the station's systems during approach. Is it active?",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"ship-corridor",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The research files describe an approach vector through the anomalous region that reduces transit time to the far outer stations by sixty percent. It's not theoretical \u2014 the files include eleven years of refinement. Maren has been running calculations continuously. The pathway is real and it's navigable and the data is complete.",choices:[{label:"Sixty percent reduction in transit time. This changes everything about outer survey economics.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"ship-corridor",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Vasquez says he suspected Maren was still there but didn't ask. Osei says he knew \u2014 Maren had told him during the evacuation that she wasn't leaving and asked him for medical supply codes, which he gave her. Three of your crew knew or suspected a person was living in an abandoned station for eleven years. They said nothing because they trusted her reason.",choices:[{label:"Three people kept this secret because they believed in her. Find out if they were right.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The station's systems are active \u2014 power signature, life support, data processing. All running. And there's something else: a navigational beacon operating on a frequency not in the standard registry. She built her own beacon. She's been transmitting navigational data \u2014 refined, updated, continuous \u2014 on a channel no one was tuned to.",choices:[{label:"She's been broadcasting the data for eleven years, waiting for someone to receive it.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"station-dock",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The station dock is clean \u2014 maintained. Someone has been keeping it functional. The airlock opens when Chen enters the access code from eleven years ago, which shouldn't work and does. Inside: the smell of recycled air and something that might be food. The lights are on. The station has been someone's home.",choices:[{label:"Call out. Let her know you're here.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Check the station systems first. Verify everything is stable.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Follow the signs of habitation. Find where she lives.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"station-interior",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your voice carries through the station. Thirty seconds of silence. Then footsteps, unhurried. A woman comes around a corridor junction \u2014 older than her file photograph, hair longer, calm in the way that comes from years of solitude rather than years of peace. She looks at Chen first. She says: 'You waited.' Chen says: 'You asked me to.'",choices:[{label:"Give them their moment, then ask Maren to explain everything.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"station-interior",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The systems are better than they should be for an eleven-year-old decommissioned station. She has replaced components with improvised equivalents, cross-connected systems to create redundancy, and documented every modification in meticulous logs. The station isn't just habitable \u2014 it's an engineering achievement.",choices:[{label:"She built a life here with what she had. Find her.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"station-interior",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The habitation signs lead to the research module \u2014 of course. She converted the secondary lab into living quarters but the primary lab is exactly what it was meant to be, except more: every surface covered in calculations, charts, and refined navigational data. It is the work of a decade spent on one problem.",choices:[{label:"She is the work. Find her.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"station-research",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Dr. Maren is real, alive, and unchanged in the ways that matter. She explains: the pathway through the anomalous region is navigable but narrow. She needed eleven years to map it precisely enough to be safe. She needed to be here, on the station, with the sensors, continuously. She couldn't do it remotely. She couldn't share it prematurely. And she needed it to be received by someone she trusted, who would understand what it was.",choices:[{label:"Ask why she couldn't share it through official channels when she had enough.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Ask if the data is complete and what she needs from you now.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Ask what she wants to happen next \u2014 she's spent eleven years earning an answer to that.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"station-research",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maren says: 'Because when the station was ordered decommissioned, I found out why. There's a commercial consortium with exclusive survey rights for the region we were studying. A stable pathway through that region is worth more than the consortium paid for their rights. If they knew it existed, they would bury it or buy it before it reached the public record.' She says: 'I needed it to reach the public record first.'",choices:[{label:"She stayed to prevent suppression. The data needs to go somewhere the consortium can't reach it.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"station-research",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The data is complete. She needs you to carry it back, submit it to the navigation authority, and ensure the station's records \u2014 eleven years of logs, all of it \u2014 accompany the submission. She says: 'I need witnesses. I need people who can say they were here and saw this.' She looks at Chen, Vasquez, Osei. 'I need people who will tell the truth.'",choices:[{label:"You're all witnesses. Now decide how to get this into the right hands.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"station-research",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She says: 'I want to come home.' She says it simply, without drama. She says: 'I want the data published. I want my name on it. And then I want to sleep somewhere that isn't recycled air.' She says the last part with the only humor she's shown since you arrived, and it's enough to make Chen laugh, which is enough to change the room.",choices:[{label:"Get her home. Bring the data with you.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"station-research",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You spend six hours downloading and verifying eleven years of data. Maren walks Chen through the highlights, showing her what the pathway looks like, how it behaves, what makes it navigable. The data is, by every metric, complete and extraordinary. The question is what you do next \u2014 the consortium is real and the commercial interests are significant.",choices:[{label:"Submit the data directly to the navigation authority before leaving the station.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Encrypt and distribute the data across multiple networks before leaving.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Contact the press. Public knowledge before official channels.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"station-research",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You uplink to the navigation authority directly from the station \u2014 the station's comms array is better than your ship's. Maren watches the submission complete. She says: 'It's done.' She says it like she's been holding something heavy for a very long time and has just put it down.",choices:[{label:"Submitted. Now leave before anything changes.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"station-research",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You encrypt the full dataset and distribute it across seven independent servers \u2014 academic archives, research networks, navigation databases. The data is in too many places to suppress. Maren watches the distribution complete and counts the confirmations. When the seventh arrives she says: 'Now it can't be bought.'",choices:[{label:"Distributed and safe. Leave.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"station-research",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maren knows a science journalist she trusts from before her eleven years of solitude. You transmit the story \u2014 the pathway, the stay, the consortium, the eleven years \u2014 with the data attached. The journalist publishes within hours. By the time you undock, the story is being read by enough people that suppression is no longer viable.",choices:[{label:"Public. Leave.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"ship-corridor",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maren boards your ship with a bag she packed in ten minutes \u2014 she has been ready to leave for a while. She takes one look around the ship and says: 'It's very clean.' You say: 'We maintain it.' She says: 'I know. I've been reading your registry.' You realize she's known your ship's entire history, every transit log, every maintenance record. She found you before you found her.",choices:[{label:"Ask her when she knew you'd be the ones to answer.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Give her the quarters and let her rest.",next:"b8b"},{label:"Tell the rest of the crew what's happening \u2014 they deserve to know.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"ship-corridor",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She says: 'Three years ago. Your ship's survey pattern put you in range of the signal window I calculated. I've been updating the message annually \u2014 adjusting the parameters as I refined the pathway.' She says: 'I was building a key to fit a lock I designed the lock for.' She says: 'You were the lock.'",choices:[{label:"Eleven years of planning for a specific arrival. That's extraordinary patience.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"ship-corridor",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She accepts the quarters without ceremony. She sleeps for fourteen hours \u2014 the longest she's slept without one ear on the station's systems in eleven years, she'll tell you later. When she wakes she comes to the bridge and watches the stars and says nothing for an hour. Then she says: 'Thank you for coming.'",choices:[{label:"Eleven years of waiting. One hour of looking at open space.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You brief the full crew. There's silence when you finish. Then your pilot says: 'She stayed eleven years to finish a map?' Maren is in the back of the briefing room. She says: 'It's a very important map.' The pilot thinks about this. He says: 'Fair enough.' That's good enough.",choices:[{label:"'Fair enough' is adequate acceptance for an extraordinary circumstance.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The journey back takes twelve days. During that time: the navigation authority acknowledges receipt of the data. The consortium's lawyers send a notification to the authority claiming prior rights \u2014 which the authority's legal team reviews and rejects, because the data is now public. The pathway is in the navigation record and cannot be removed.",choices:[{label:"Ask Maren what she thinks about the consortium's failed challenge.",next:"b9a"},{label:"The crew's response to Maren over twelve days matters. Watch it.",next:"b9b"},{label:"Think about what you'll report when you get back.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maren says: 'They'll try other angles. They always do.' She says: 'But the data is in too many places now. They can fight the credit, they can slow the adoption, they can make it expensive. They can't make it not exist.' She says: 'That's why I waited until it was complete. An incomplete finding can be disputed. A complete one just is.'",choices:[{label:"A complete finding just is. She waited for completeness.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"By day four, Maren is eating with the crew. By day seven, she's running equations with Vasquez on a problem that has nothing to do with her eleven-year project \u2014 just because the problem is interesting. By day ten, your pilot has asked her to check his navigational calculations for a transit that's coming up. She does it and finds one error. He thanks her. It's ordinary, which is what she wanted.",choices:[{label:"Ordinary. After eleven years of extraordinary, ordinary is the gift.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your report will include: discovery of a personnel discrepancy in the station's decommission records, discovery of Dr. Maren alive and well after eleven years, submission of navigational research of significant public value, and recommendation that three crew members' prior knowledge of the situation be reviewed by command. You'll be honest about what Chen and the others knew. Maren said to be honest.",choices:[{label:"Honest report, complete record. That's the job.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Twelve days of transit. The world Maren returns to is different from the one she left \u2014 eleven years of different. She asks you, on the last day, what you think will happen to the pathway. You say: ships will use it. She says: how many? You say: all of them, eventually. She thinks about this. She says: good.",choices:[{label:"All of them. Her eleven years reduces transit time for everything that follows.",next:"end-pathway"},{label:"She goes home. What happens after matters less than that.",next:"end-home"},{label:"The story changes what's possible. That's the right measure.",next:"end-changed"},{label:"Chen and the others kept her secret. That trust was the foundation of everything.",next:"end-trust"}]},"end-pathway":{id:"end-pathway",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The Maren Pathway enters the navigation record six months after your return. The first commercial ship to use it reduces its transit time by fifty-eight percent. The second by sixty-one. Maren is credited as lead researcher. The station is designated a heritage site. She doesn't go back to it \u2014 she has seen enough of it \u2014 but she's consulted when the heritage team do their survey. She tells them: the secondary lab had better light. She tells them to restore it.",choices:[]},"end-home":{id:"end-home",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Maren's first day back on a planet with a real sky and real weather. She stands outside for an hour. It's raining. She doesn't come in. When Chen asks if she's alright she says: 'It's raining.' Chen says: 'Yes.' Maren says: 'I haven't felt rain in eleven years.' She stays out for another twenty minutes. Then she comes in and makes tea and sits by the window and watches it rain, and that is enough for one day.",choices:[]},"end-changed":{id:"end-changed",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The pathway changes the economics of outer survey. Three stations that were marginal become viable. Two that were planned but unfunded get built. The research that happens at those stations, in the years that follow, is downstream of a woman who spent eleven years alone because she believed the problem was worth solving. You think about this sometimes. You don't know how to measure it. You don't think measurement is the right tool for it.",choices:[]},"end-trust":{id:"end-trust",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"Chen, Vasquez, and Osei are reviewed by command. The finding: they withheld information of potential safety relevance, but the information concerned a person's voluntary choice and the withholding did not create risk to others. No disciplinary action. Command notes, dryly, that the withheld information ultimately produced a navigational finding of significant value to the outer survey program. The note closes: 'Trust placed well is still trust.' Chen keeps a copy of it.",choices:[]}}},{id:"reentry",packId:"space",title:"Reentry",subtitle:"You've been gone four years. Earth won't recognize you. Or you it.",length:"Short",free:!1,brief:"You're returning from a deep survey mission after four years. Your contracts, your apartment, your relationships are legally dissolved. Earth has changed. You've changed more. The shuttle lands in three hours and you need to decide who you're going to be now.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three hours from reentry. You've been in the outer system for four years, two months, and eleven days. When you signed the mission contract, you understood the terms: extended absence triggers automatic legal dormancy for all personal obligations and holdings. Your apartment was released after two years. Your bank accounts are in escrow. Your research contract requires renegotiation. Whatever relationships you had when you left are legally undefined. You're returning to a city that has moved four years without you.",choices:[{label:"Review your professional options before landing.",next:"s2"},{label:"Try to contact someone who was waiting for you.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Think about where you'll sleep tonight before anything else.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You send a message to Dr. Yuen, your former research partner. Two hours pass. She responds: 'I'm at the university. Come find me.' Three words and a location. Four years and that's enough.",choices:[{label:"Three words is something. Think about what you're returning to.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your agency provides transitional housing for the first thirty days \u2014 standard policy for long-duration crew. The housing is functional and anonymous and you will be enormously grateful for it. You make the booking before you do anything else. The first night is settled. Everything else can be solved in order.",choices:[{label:"First night settled. Think about what comes after.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You have three hours. Your professional situation is clear on paper: your skills are current, your mission data is valuable, and your reputation in the survey field is enhanced by four years in the outer system. The personal situation is less clear. Four years of correspondence that was delayed six to twenty minutes each way \u2014 real conversations conducted at the speed of light lag.",choices:[{label:"Think about your research. The data from the mission is what you built.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Think about the people. What changed on their end, you can only guess.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Think about yourself. Four years of outer system changes a person.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The mission data: four years of deep-field stellar observation from a position no previous survey has occupied. It's eighteen months of analysis work at minimum, and it will produce at least two significant papers. Your research position at the university went to someone else \u2014 positions don't hold for four years \u2014 but your data means you can negotiate from strength.",choices:[{label:"Your data is your leverage. Think about who to approach first.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The people. Your correspondence showed you what they were willing to share across a time-delayed channel. What it couldn't show: the things people don't write. The decisions made in a quiet room that they never found the words for. You know the versions of the people you left. You're about to meet who they became.",choices:[{label:"You know their four-years-ago versions. Prepare for the current ones.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"How you've changed: you're quieter. You went four years with a crew of six and a universe of data and very little small talk. You're more patient \u2014 four years of light-speed correspondence requires patience. You're more certain about what matters and less certain about most things. The person you were before the mission would recognize you but would find you strange.",choices:[{label:"You'd find yourself strange. Think about how to explain that to people who knew you.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two hours to landing. The shuttle crosses from night to day as it descends, and through the small window you see the city from above \u2014 the same geography, different lights. Things were built while you were gone. Other things are gone. You can't tell which is which from here.",choices:[{label:"The mission data needs a home institution. Think about who to approach for that.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Your former apartment building is visible from this angle. Think about what's under it.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Think about Dr. Yuen's three-word message and what it means.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three institutions would want your data and your analysis work: your old university, a research consortium that expanded while you were gone, and a private firm that funds independent research in exchange for publication rights. Each offers something different. Your old university offers familiarity. The consortium offers resources. The firm offers independence.",choices:[{label:"Familiarity, resources, or independence. Think about what you need most right now.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your old apartment is on the eleventh floor of a building you can see from the shuttle window \u2014 or the place the building is. Someone else is there now, has been there for two years. The legal dormancy clause meant you gave up a place you had lived in for eight years because you were a hundred million kilometers away. That's the deal. You knew the deal.",choices:[{label:"You knew the deal. The transitional housing is fine for thirty days.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three words: 'I'm at the university. Come find me.' Yuen could have written more. She chose not to. That choice is information. It might mean she's busy. It might mean she's uncertain. It might mean that after four years of light-speed lag, three words is exactly as much as the moment calls for. You won't know until you're standing in front of her.",choices:[{label:"You won't know until you're there. Stop trying to calculate it.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"One hour to landing. The cabin is quiet \u2014 your crew of six has been together for four years and there's not much left to say before the end. Or there's too much. You've navigated the outer system together. Now you're going to disperse into your separate lives in a city that's been moving without all of you.",choices:[{label:"Say something to the crew before you land. Not a speech. Something true.",next:"b4a"},{label:"The silence is fine. You've said everything that needed saying.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Think about what you're going to do in the first hour after landing.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"shuttle-cabin",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'I know we're about to get very busy with our own things. I wanted to say \u2014 this crew worked.' You don't elaborate. They know what you mean. Three of them nod. One says: 'The data was good.' That's the highest compliment in your field. It's enough.",choices:[{label:"The data was good. That's the job. Now land.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The silence is the right thing. Four years of small-cabin proximity and professional trust and the particular kind of friendship that forms when there's nowhere else to go \u2014 it doesn't need a speech. You land as you've been operating: quietly, together, doing the work.",choices:[{label:"Land.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"shuttle-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"First hour after landing: debrief protocol, mandatory medical check, then you're free. You'll go to the transitional housing or to the university to find Yuen, or both. You'll eat something that isn't mission rations. You'll feel the gravity that you've been in for four years but will notice again now that you're back where it's normal.",choices:[{label:"The gravity will feel right. Think about landing.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"landing-bay",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Landing. The shuttle touches down and your body registers the difference between this gravity and what you've been living in. Not bad. Just a reminder. The bay is efficient and the debrief is standard and the medical check shows everything you expect: fine, with the expected adjustments for long-duration low-gravity exposure. Then a door opens and the city is there.",choices:[{label:"Go straight to the university and find Yuen.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Go to transitional housing first. Get settled before anything else.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Walk. Spend an hour in the city before you do anything purposeful.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"city-street",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The university is forty minutes by transit. You take it. The transit is exactly as you remember and completely different \u2014 the same routes, new vehicles, faces that are strangers where strangers were before. You arrive. Yuen is in her office. She looks up when you knock. She says: 'You're here.' You say: 'I'm here.' That's the whole reunion, right there.",choices:[{label:"That's enough. Talk to her.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"transitional-housing",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The transitional housing is exactly what was promised: functional, anonymous, yours for thirty days. You put your bag down and sit on the bed and look at the ceiling for about ten minutes. Then you get up and go to the university to find Yuen, because sitting on the bed is not how you want to spend your first afternoon back.",choices:[{label:"Ten minutes is enough to sit with it. Now go find her.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"city-street",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You walk for an hour. The city does what cities do: it's mostly the same and partly different and it doesn't know you came back and doesn't care. You find a coffee shop you used to go to. It's still there, different name. You get coffee. It's good. Four years of mission rations and coffee is genuinely good. You stay for half an hour and then go find Yuen.",choices:[{label:"Good coffee. Four years earns you this. Now go.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"university",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You and Yuen talk for two hours. There are things she says that you didn't expect and things you say that surprise you as you say them. There is a conversation about the mission data that turns naturally into a conversation about working together again, which turns into a practical discussion about where that could happen. She says: 'I have a position I can offer you. It's not what you had before, but it fits what you have now.'",choices:[{label:"Ask about the position before deciding.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Say yes. Trust that she knows what fits what you have now.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Ask what she means by 'what you have now.'",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"university",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The position: lead researcher on a long-baseline observation project using the network she's been building. Your data from the outer system is a perfect anchor dataset. The contract is two years with renewal options. It pays well and it's in the same building where she works. She says: 'It's yours if you want it.' She says it like she's been holding it for a while.",choices:[{label:"She's been holding it. Think about that.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"university",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She looks at you when you say yes \u2014 not surprised, but something. She says: 'Good.' She says: 'I'll have the contract ready tomorrow.' She says: 'Do you have a place to stay?' You say: 'Transitional housing.' She says: 'That's fine for now.' She says it like 'now' is a specific word with a specific duration.",choices:[{label:"'For now.' Think about what she means.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"university",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She says: 'You're different.' She says it accurately and not unkindly. She says: 'You're quieter and more certain and you don't fill space with words anymore. That's useful in a research lead.' She says: 'The position works with what you are now, not what you were four years ago.' She says: 'That matters.'",choices:[{label:"She noticed the specific ways you changed. That means she's been paying attention.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"university",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The conversation moves past the professional into other things \u2014 the four years of light-delayed correspondence and what was in it and what wasn't. You're careful because you're not sure what you're doing yet. She's careful because she's been careful for four years. You're both being careful around the same thing.",choices:[{label:"Say something that isn't careful. Test what you actually feel.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Keep it professional for now. The rest can come in its own time.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Ask her directly what she wants. You've had enough of uncertain signals.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"university",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'I thought about you more than I expected to.' You say it without hedging. She's quiet for a moment. Then she says: 'I thought about you the right amount.' You think about what the right amount is and decide it's more than the expected amount. That's enough for one day.",choices:[{label:"Enough for one day. The rest is time.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"university",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You keep it professional and it works fine. The conversation ends with a handshake on the position and a plan to meet tomorrow for the contract. You leave the university and walk to the transitional housing and it's a good walk and a good evening and the things you didn't say are still there for when you need them.",choices:[{label:"Tomorrow. One day at a time.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"university",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She laughs \u2014 not mockingly, the real kind. She says: 'I want to see if we work now as well as we worked before.' She says: 'I don't know if we do. Four years is a lot of changing.' She says: 'I'm willing to find out.' You say: 'Me too.' She says: 'Good.' That word again, carrying a lot.",choices:[{label:"'Me too.' That's an answer to something you didn't quite ask.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"city-evening",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your first evening back. Transitional housing, a view of the city, mission rations officially ended. You eat actual food and feel the difference in your body. The city is loud in a way you forgot space isn't. You're tired in a way that isn't mission-tired. You sit at the window and watch the lights.",choices:[{label:"Think about the data. You spent four years building it. It's yours.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Think about tomorrow. The contract, the position, Yuen.",next:"b8b"},{label:"Think about nothing in particular. You've earned a quiet evening.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"city-evening",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The data. Four years of outer system observation that no other survey has. It's eighteen months of analysis minimum and it will change what's known about stellar density in your target region. You built it one observation cycle at a time, in a ship with six people, in the dark. It's yours. It's also the best thing you've ever made.",choices:[{label:"The best thing you've ever made. Take care of it.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"city-evening",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Tomorrow: sign the contract, get the office key, start setting up the analysis environment. And Yuen \u2014 whatever that turns into, it turns into in its own time. You don't need to solve it tonight. Four years of patience is good training for this kind of not-knowing.",choices:[{label:"Good training for not-knowing. That's what the mission gave you.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"city-evening",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Nothing in particular. The lights, the sounds, the food that isn't rations. Gravity. Four years is enough for one quiet evening. Tomorrow you'll start on the rest.",choices:[{label:"Tomorrow.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"city-morning",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Day two. The contract is signed. You have an office, an access card, and a desk at which to begin the analysis of four years of data. It's exactly what you need. Yuen comes by at midday with coffee and a question about a specific observation from month thirty-one of your mission that she's been thinking about. You spend an hour on it. It's a good hour.",choices:[{label:"The hour is the beginning of the work. That's the most important thing.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Think about the question she asked. She's been thinking about month thirty-one.",next:"b9b"},{label:"The coffee at midday is a ritual that means something.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"city-morning",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The work is the most important thing because it always has been and you've been away from it \u2014 from real, new, significant work \u2014 for the four years of the mission. The mission was work too but this is analysis, synthesis, the part where the data becomes knowledge. You've been building toward this for a long time.",choices:[{label:"Building toward this for a long time.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"city-morning",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Month thirty-one. You remember it: an anomalous reading you logged and flagged for review. Yuen, six years away with a twenty-minute communication delay, received your observation log and noticed it in month thirty-three. She sent a question. Your answer took twenty minutes to reach her. Two months after you made the observation, you had a conversation about it. She's been thinking about it since. For two years.",choices:[{label:"Two years of thinking about one observation. That's the kind of person she is.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"city-morning",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Coffee at midday with a question about your data. It's not declared as anything. It's just the shape that working together takes. You know this shape from before. It's a little different now \u2014 she's a little different, you're a little different \u2014 but the shape is recognizable. That's something.",choices:[{label:"Recognizable. That's something.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"university-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Week three of thirty. The analysis is running. The data is good \u2014 the first results suggest the anomalous region you logged in month thirty-one is more significant than either of you thought. You have a place to sleep that's yours until the thirty days are up and then will need to be solved. You have a position and a colleague and a research question that's going to take years. You're back.",choices:[{label:"You're back and it's different and that's okay.",next:"end-different"},{label:"The research question will take years. Good.",next:"end-work"},{label:"The thirty days will resolve into something. It already is.",next:"end-home"},{label:"Month thirty-one was the beginning of something. Now finish it.",next:"end-discovery"}]},"end-different":{id:"end-different",scene:"ending-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"It's different from before. The city is different and you're different and the work is different and whatever is developing with Yuen is different from what was there before, which was good, and this might also be good but it's its own thing. You settle into the difference the way you settled into everything in the outer system: slowly, with attention, without fighting what is. That's the skill you didn't know you were developing for four years. It turns out it's useful everywhere.",choices:[]},"end-work":{id:"end-work",scene:"ending-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The research question takes three years and produces four papers. The anomaly from month thirty-one is, in the end, a previously uncharacterized stellar nursery at the edge of the survey region. You and Yuen are co-authors on all four papers. The third paper is cited heavily for the next decade. You're in the middle of the fourth when someone offers you another long-duration mission. You think about it for longer than you expected to. Then you say no. The work here isn't done yet.",choices:[]},"end-home":{id:"end-home",scene:"ending-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The thirty days become a lease. The lease becomes a home, eventually \u2014 same city, different building, not the eleventh floor but similar light at the same time of day. Yuen knows which window is yours before you tell her. That tells you something. The work is good. The city is familiar in new ways. You went to the outer system for four years and came back to something that is better than what you left, which is not what you expected. It is what it is.",choices:[]},"end-discovery":{id:"end-discovery",scene:"ending-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Month thirty-one's anomaly leads to a classification paper eighteen months after your return. The stellar nursery it identifies becomes a reference object for surveys in that region for the next twenty years. Your name and Yuen's name are on the initial classification. The observation that prompted it \u2014 a flagged anomaly in a ship's log, transmitted with a twenty-minute delay, questioned across a hundred million kilometers \u2014 is the origin point. You went to the outer system and came back with the beginning of something. That's what you went for.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-colony",packId:"space",title:"The Colony",subtitle:"Two hundred people. One supply ship. And someone sabotaged the water.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You're the security officer on a first-generation colony. The water filtration system was deliberately damaged\u2014not failed, damaged. Two hundred colonists have three days of reserves. Someone on this colony wants to force an evacuation. You have to find them before they succeed.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The filtration failure alarm went off at 0340. By 0400 your chief engineer, Mira Solano, had confirmed it: deliberate damage to three independent filter modules, each requiring a specific tool to access, each removed the same way. Not a cascade failure. Sabotage. Two hundred colonists have seventy-two hours of reserve water. The next supply ship is ninety hours away. You have a colony to protect and a saboteur to find.",choices:[{label:"Start with access logs. Who could reach all three modules?",next:"b1a"},{label:"Secure the water reserves immediately before doing anything else.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Assess whether partial repair is possible to buy more time.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"colony-engineering",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Access to all three filter modules requires engineering authorization \u2014 Level 3 or above. Your access log shows seven people with Level 3 clearance. One of them accessed all three module zones between 0100 and 0300. The access log entry: Engineer First Class Darel Pham. But Pham was on the night watch roster and can verify his location for that window. Someone used his credentials.",choices:[{label:"Credentials used falsely \u2014 or Pham is lying about his alibi. Investigate both.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"colony-engineering",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You put guards on the reserve tanks and secondary systems. Your deputy, Officer Chen, handles the security deployment while you assess the damage. Solano confirms: the damage is irreparable with what the colony has. But she thinks she can rig a partial bypass that recovers forty percent of filtration capacity. Forty percent doesn't solve the problem but it extends the window to ninety-six hours \u2014 just enough to meet the supply ship.",choices:[{label:"Ninety-six hours if the bypass works. The bypass buys you time to find the saboteur.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"colony-engineering",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Solano's assessment: full repair is impossible without parts on the supply ship. Partial bypass is possible \u2014 she can recover forty percent of capacity in approximately eight hours of work, extending reserves to ninety-six hours. She says: 'I can fix enough to buy time. You have to find who did this.' You say: 'I know.'",choices:[{label:"Time bought if the bypass succeeds. Find the saboteur now.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two tracks running: Solano on the bypass, you on the investigation. The sabotage required Level 3 access, specific knowledge of the filtration system, and approximately two hours of careful work without triggering the primary alarm. This was not impulsive. Someone planned this.",choices:[{label:"Find out who on the colony wants or needs an evacuation.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Interview Pham. His credentials were used \u2014 he knows something.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Check who has filtration engineering knowledge beyond their official role.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You pull the colony's grievance records and transfer requests. Three people have filed formal requests for Earth transport in the past six months, all denied \u2014 the colony is understaffed and transfers require a replacement. One is medical: a colonist named Vasquez whose child needs specialized care not available here. One is personal: a couple who want to separate, one of whom needs to leave. One is professional: an agronomist named Kellis who says the colony's soil program is fundamentally flawed and he refuses to continue.",choices:[{label:"Three people who need or want out, denied. One of them may have decided to force the issue.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Pham says he was on watch at the north perimeter from midnight to 0400. The perimeter log confirms his location \u2014 he checked in every thirty minutes. His credentials were used remotely, which requires either his physical access card or his biometric data. He doesn't have the card with him. He looks in his pocket. He says: 'I had it at 2300 when I started watch.' Someone took it during the watch.",choices:[{label:"His card was taken while he was on watch. Find who had access to him.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Filtration engineering is Solano's specialty, but she's been training junior engineers. Her three trainees: Pham (whose credentials were used), a woman named Adisa, and an external specialist who joined the colony four months ago named Kellis. Kellis is an agronomist who volunteered for engineering training during his first month here. An agronomist who learned filtration engineering and then filed a transfer request that was denied.",choices:[{label:"Kellis learned filtration engineering and then asked to leave. He's worth talking to.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your primary persons of interest: Vasquez (child's medical needs), Kellis (denied transfer, knows filtration engineering, unusual background), and whoever took Pham's card on the north perimeter watch. These might be the same person or they might be separate.",choices:[{label:"Interview Kellis first. He has the most specific combination of motive and means.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Check the perimeter video for anyone near Pham during his watch.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Talk to the colonist with the sick child. Desperation changes people.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"colony-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Kellis is thirty-seven, competent, and controlled. He says the soil program is failing and the colony will experience crop failures within three years regardless of what he does. He says Earth command knows and is choosing to ignore it because admitting failure costs money. He says: 'I am not the person who damaged the filtration. But I understand why someone did.' He says the last part carefully, like it means something precise.",choices:[{label:"'I understand why someone did' \u2014 he knows who did it or he's deflecting.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"colony-security",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The perimeter video from Pham's watch: at 0147, a figure approaches Pham from inside the compound, ostensibly to deliver a message. They exchange something. The figure's face is obscured by their hood. But their gait, their height, their build \u2014 you know this person. You've seen them walk through the common area every day. You just can't place who it is yet.",choices:[{label:"You know this person by sight. Run the physical parameters against your roster.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"colony-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Vasquez is open about his situation: his daughter has a treatable condition that requires equipment the colony doesn't have. He has been documenting the denial of his transfer for six months. He says: 'I would do anything to get my daughter the treatment she needs.' He says it without shame. He also says: 'I would not kill two hundred people to do it. Dying of thirst is not an evacuation. It's a massacre.'",choices:[{label:"He distinguishes evacuation from massacre. That's significant. Keep looking.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The picture is clarifying. Kellis knows who did it. The perimeter figure's physical profile eliminates some suspects. Vasquez's logic suggests the saboteur expected the colony to be evacuated, not harmed \u2014 they may not have accounted for the supply ship's timing. The sabotage was planned for a 72-hour window. The supply ship's ninety-hour schedule makes that window fatal, not evacuating.",choices:[{label:"Confront Kellis with what you know. He's protecting someone.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Run the perimeter figure's physical profile against your colony roster.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Check who has reason to believe the supply ship would arrive within 72 hours.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"colony-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Kellis, confronted with the specificity of what you know, is quiet for a long time. Then he says: 'You need to understand that the person who did this thought an evacuation order would come before anyone was hurt.' He says: 'They were wrong about the ship schedule. They made a mistake about the timeline.' He says: 'It's my sister. Her name is Adisa. She's in Engineering.'",choices:[{label:"Adisa. His sister. The third filtration trainee.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"colony-security",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Physical profile \u2014 height, build, gait \u2014 narrowed to four candidates from your roster of two hundred. Cross-referenced against who had a known relationship with Pham and access to him during the watch: one match. Engineer trainee Adisa Kellis. She knew Pham, she knew the filtration system, and she had reason to approach him on the perimeter without attracting attention.",choices:[{label:"Adisa Kellis. The profile matches, the opportunity was there.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The supply ship schedule is publicly known. But six days ago, there was a schedule update \u2014 the ship moved forward four hours due to a cargo redistribution, making it ninety hours away instead of seventy-one. The update went to colony leadership. The saboteur may not have received or noticed the update. They planned for a 72-hour window that was going to close in time. It doesn't close in time.",choices:[{label:"They planned for 71 hours, not 90. The missed schedule update is the critical error.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"colony-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Adisa Kellis, twenty-nine, engineer trainee, Darel Kellis's sister. You find her in Engineering, helping Solano with the bypass. She's working. When you approach she looks at you and stops working and says: 'I didn't know the ship moved.' She says it like a confession and an explanation in the same breath.",choices:[{label:"Ask her to come with you and tell you everything.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Ask her why first. Understand what she was trying to do.",next:"b5b"},{label:"She's still helping with the bypass. Let her finish it first.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"colony-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She comes with you. She tells you everything clearly and without drama. She damaged the filtration because she believed an evacuation would be ordered within 72 hours \u2014 she had researched the protocol, which specifies that a water system failure triggers mandatory evacuation assessment. She thought the colony would be ordered home. She wanted to go home. Her brother is sick \u2014 not her, her brother Darel Kellis, the agronomist. She wanted him to go home too.",choices:[{label:"She wanted to save her brother. And she miscalculated the ship's schedule by nineteen hours.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"colony-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She says: 'Darel is sick. It's the soil. The soil in sector 4 has an environmental contaminate he's been exposed to for eight months. Earth knows. They refused the transfer because he's the only one who can manage the crop program.' She says: 'I thought if the colony had to evacuate, they would have to let him go.' She says: 'I thought the ship would be here in time. I didn't know it had moved.'",choices:[{label:"Her brother is being kept here by institutional necessity and he's getting sicker.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"colony-engineering",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You let her finish the bypass work. It takes three hours and when she's done the bypass is functional and the filtration capacity is at forty-one percent \u2014 better than Solano expected. Then you take her aside. She says: 'I know.' She says: 'Can I tell you why before anything else happens?' You say: 'Yes.'",choices:[{label:"Hear the reason before the consequence.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"colony-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Adisa's full account: her brother Darel has been diagnosed with a contamination-related condition caused by the sector 4 soil. The condition is progressive but treatable on Earth. Earth command has refused his transfer three times because he's the colony's only qualified agronomist. She believed sabotaging the filtration would trigger a mandatory evacuation assessment and the colony would be recalled. She miscalculated the ship's arrival by nineteen hours. She helped build the bypass because it was still the right thing to do even after she was caught.",choices:[{label:"Her brother's situation needs to be escalated to Earth command independently.",next:"b6a"},{label:"She committed sabotage that nearly killed two hundred people. The consequence is real.",next:"b6b"},{label:"The contamination in sector 4 is the original problem. Investigate it.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You file an urgent medical escalation for Darel Kellis, documenting the contamination exposure, the denial of transfer, and the progressive nature of his condition. You file it independently of any security report, as a medical emergency, which routes it differently than a standard request. The response comes in six hours: transfer approved. The same people who refused three times approve the fourth request within hours of a medical emergency filing.",choices:[{label:"The right route through the right channel. Darel gets his transfer.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Adisa knows. She says: 'I know what I did. I'm not asking for less than what's fair. I'm asking you to understand why before you decide what fair is.' You say you do understand. You also say: two hundred people are on reduced water rations because of a nineteen-hour miscalculation. She says: 'I know.' She says it with the weight of someone who has been calculating that specific nineteen hours since she was found out.",choices:[{label:"She understands the consequence. The question is what's proportionate.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"colony-engineering",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Sector 4 soil contamination: you pull the environmental monitoring records. The contamination is real and documented \u2014 flagged six months ago by routine monitoring, escalated to Earth command, who responded: continue monitoring, reassess in one year. The contamination is known. The decision to leave Darel Kellis in daily exposure to it was a bureaucratic choice, not ignorance.",choices:[{label:"A documented contamination, a known exposure, and a refused transfer. That's the context.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The supply ship arrives ninety-two hours after the failure alarm \u2014 two hours past the original reserve window, but Solano's bypass bought four hours of additional margin. The colony is intact. The filtration parts are on the ship. Repairs take eighteen hours. The water crisis is over. What remains: Adisa in provisional custody, Darel's medical situation, the sector 4 contamination, and your report to Earth command.",choices:[{label:"Write the security report honestly and completely.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Consult with the colony director before filing anything.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Talk to Adisa about what she wants in the report.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The report: deliberate sabotage, identified responsible party, motivation documented, the sector 4 contamination and denied transfer as context, and a recommendation that Earth command review its transfer denial protocols. You write it with the precision of someone who knows every word will be read by people who may want to simplify it. You make it resistant to simplification.",choices:[{label:"Complete and resistant to simplification. That's the right report.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Colony Director Inouye reads the investigation summary and is quiet for a while. Then she says: 'Adisa could have killed everyone.' You say: 'She miscalculated by nineteen hours.' Inouye says: 'And her brother?' You lay out the sector 4 contamination, the denials, the documented exposure. Inouye says: 'File everything. All of it. The system made this happen and the system needs to know.'",choices:[{label:"'The system made this happen.' File everything.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"colony-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Adisa says: 'Tell the truth. About Darel, about the soil, about the denied transfers. And tell the truth about what I did and why.' She says: 'I'm not asking for sympathy. I'm asking for accuracy.' She says: 'If the report is accurate, people will draw their own conclusions. That's all I want.' You say: 'That's what I was going to do.' She says: 'I know. I just needed to say it.'",choices:[{label:"Accuracy. That's the standard.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Earth command's response to the full report takes four days. Darel Kellis's transfer is on the supply ship's return manifest \u2014 approved. Adisa Kellis is to be transported to Earth for a formal review. The sector 4 contamination is escalated to an environmental health priority. And, buried in the response: a review of transfer denial protocols for documented medical cases.",choices:[{label:"The institutional response is better than expected. Think about why.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Make sure Adisa's transport is dignified, not punitive, until the review.",next:"b8b"},{label:"The bypass held the colony together. Thank Solano formally.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The institutional response is better because the report gave them no alternative. Every claim documented, every context provided, every denial on record. Earth command's lawyers, looking at the complete file, saw clearly that the denial of transfer in the face of documented medical contamination was going to be hard to defend in a formal review. The accurate report produced the accurate response.",choices:[{label:"The report did the work.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Adisa's transport is handled with the minimum necessary restraint \u2014 she's not a threat to the ship and she's not going to run. You say so in writing and the transport team accepts it. She spends the transit in standard quarters. Her brother is on the same ship. They don't speak much on the transit, according to the ship's log \u2014 they sit in the same room and that's enough.",choices:[{label:"In the same room and that's enough. That was what she wanted for him.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"colony-engineering",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Solano's bypass held the colony together for ninety-six hours under stress. You write a formal commendation that goes into her service record. She reads it and says: 'It's engineering. I just fixed what I could fix.' You say: 'That's what the commendation says.' She says: 'Good. Keep it factual.'",choices:[{label:"Factual. That's the standard she and Adisa both wanted.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The colony stabilizes. The filtration system is repaired and upgraded with the new parts. A second agronomist is scheduled to arrive in two months, which reduces the single-point-of-failure problem that kept Darel Kellis here. The sector 4 contamination is being addressed. Adisa's review is proceeding on Earth.",choices:[{label:"Think about what the colony looks like after this.",next:"b9a"},{label:"The review of transfer denial protocols \u2014 will it change anything?",next:"b9b"},{label:"Two hundred people came close to something terrible. Think about that.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The colony is more careful now. The filtration system has additional monitoring. The access protocols have been tightened. The colonists know what almost happened \u2014 Inouye made the decision to tell them, fully, which was the right call. They are more attentive to their own systems. They are also more likely to raise concerns through official channels, having seen what unofficial action costs.",choices:[{label:"The cost and the lesson.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The protocol review produces a new framework: documented medical conditions causing progressive harm qualify for automatic transfer assessment, with a 30-day response requirement. The Kellis case is the first reference in the new framework. It took a near-disaster to produce a policy that should have existed before. That's how institutions learn. Slowly and expensively.",choices:[{label:"Slowly and expensively. But learned.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two hundred people. Seventy-two hours of reserves in a sealed environment with no option to leave. The margin was Solano's bypass and a nineteen-hour miscalculation. You think about the colonists who rationed their water during those ninety-two hours and didn't know why. You think about whether to tell them how close it was. You decide to let Inouye make that call. She knows them better.",choices:[{label:"Inouye's call. That's right.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"colony-base",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three months later. Darel Kellis is on Earth, receiving treatment, improving. Adisa Kellis's review concludes: guilty of sabotage, mitigating factors accepted, sentence reduced to supervised service on Earth for two years before return eligibility. Sector 4 is remediated. The second agronomist has arrived. The colony continues.",choices:[{label:"The colony continues. That's the point of all of it.",next:"end-continues"},{label:"Adisa's sentence reflects the mitigating factors. That's the system working.",next:"end-justice"},{label:"Darel is better. That was what she wanted.",next:"end-brother"},{label:"The new protocol protects the next Darel Kellis before the next Adisa acts.",next:"end-reform"}]},"end-continues":{id:"end-continues",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The colony continues. In five years it reaches self-sufficiency in water \u2014 the upgraded filtration, the remediated sector, and a third filtration unit funded partly because of the security review following the sabotage. The colonists who rationed water during those ninety-two hours are still here, most of them. They don't talk about it often. When they do, they say: we got through it. That's accurate.",choices:[]},"end-justice":{id:"end-justice",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"Adisa's sentence is two years of supervised service, reduced from the maximum because the review found the institutional failures that motivated her \u2014 the contamination, the denials, the bureaucratic indifference \u2014 as genuine mitigating factors. She completes the service and applies to return. The colony's director writes a letter of support. You do too. You don't know if she'll come back. You think she might.",choices:[]},"end-brother":{id:"end-brother",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Darel's treatment takes eight months. He sends one message during it, to the colony, to the people who knew him. It says: I'm going to be alright. He says: I heard what Adisa did. He says: I wish she hadn't. He says: I'm glad I'm here. He says it without contradiction, which is the only honest way to hold what happened. He's back on a colony eighteen months later \u2014 a different one, with better soil.",choices:[]},"end-reform":{id:"end-reform",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The new medical transfer protocol is named informally in the literature after the case that produced it. The policy prevents three separate denials in its first year \u2014 three people who would have been held in place against documented medical advice are transferred. None of them have to damage anything. None of them have to calculate ship arrival times. The system, updated by the cost of one near-disaster, does what it should have done before. That's the best outcome you could have asked for. Not the fastest, not the cleanest. But the one that actually helps the next person.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-derelict",packId:"space",title:"The Derelict",subtitle:"The ship is intact. The crew manifest says fourteen. There's only one survivor.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"Your salvage crew has reached a drifting colony ship two years overdue. The vessel is intact, life support functional, and one crew member is still alive\u2014in an emergency sleep pod, with no memory of what happened. Thirteen people are missing. You have their ship, one survivor, and six hours of oxygen.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"ship-exterior",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Kovalev sits in drift, all running lights active, navigation beacon transmitting normally. It has been two years and four months since its last contact. From outside it looks fine. Inside, according to your suit's sensor sweep: life support functional, one bio-sign in the aft section, and no movement anywhere else on a ship rated for fourteen. You dock and go in.",choices:[{label:"Go straight to the bio-sign. The survivor is the priority.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Check the bridge first. The logs will tell you what happened.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Survey the ship section by section. Don't assume anything.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The bio-sign is in emergency sleep pod seven. The pod's external monitor shows its occupant has been under for twenty-six months \u2014 near the system's safe limit. You crack the pod. Inside: a woman, late thirties, who comes around slowly and says, before anything else: 'How many of them made it?'",choices:[{label:"Tell her you found only her. Ask what she remembers.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The bridge logs run until month six of the journey, then stop. Not corrupted \u2014 stopped. Someone shut down the logging system manually. The last entry before shutdown: 'Situation nominal. Crew healthy. Proceeding on schedule.' Six months of silence. Then a bio-sign in a sleep pod for twenty more months.",choices:[{label:"Logs shut down manually after six months. Find the survivor.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"ship-corridor",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Survey findings: the ship is in perfect mechanical condition. The galley shows signs of extended single-person use \u2014 one set of utensils worn, one place at the table. Thirteen sleep pods are empty and sealed from the outside, which requires a second person. Someone sealed them. The fourteenth pod is occupied.",choices:[{label:"Sealed from outside. Someone locked them in. Find the survivor.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The survivor's name is Risa Tanaka, the ship's engineer. She has no memory after month six of the voyage. She remembers a normal day, a meal, going to her bunk. Then the pod. Twenty-six months of sleep with nothing in between. The thirteen other crew: Captain Vos, two pilots, ten colonists. She doesn't know where they are.",choices:[{label:"Search the ship for the thirteen missing crew.",next:"b2a"},{label:"The gaps in Risa's memory might not be genuine. Approach carefully.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Check the sealed sleep pods systematically.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"ship-corridor",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You search the ship. No bodies. No evidence of violence, illness, or accident. The ship is clean and maintained and empty of thirteen people who should be on it. The only sign of extended human occupation beyond Risa's pod area: a single maintenance log in the engineering section, handwritten, spanning twenty months. Risa's handwriting.",choices:[{label:"Risa maintained the ship for twenty months \u2014 but she says she has no memory of it.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Risa's physiological readings from the pod show normal sleep-pod metrics for twenty-six months. She wasn't faking unconsciousness. But: the pod's entry log shows it was opened twice before you arrived \u2014 once at month seven, once at month twenty-two. She was awake at least twice. The gaps in her memory may be the gaps in what she's willing to remember.",choices:[{label:"The pod was opened twice. She was awake. She doesn't remember or won't say.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"ship-corridor",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The thirteen sealed pods: all operational, all showing normal internal environment, all empty. The seals are external \u2014 pod locked from outside by the same person. The locking mechanism requires a crew member's biometric key. Only three crew had engineering access: Captain Vos, the chief pilot, and Risa.",choices:[{label:"Risa had access to lock the pods. But what happened to the people in them?",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You sit with Risa in the aft section and go through what you know: she has no conscious memory of twenty months, but the ship was maintained during that time, the pods were opened and closed by someone, and the thirteen crew are absent from a sealed, intact vessel. Somewhere between month six and now, something happened. She is the only person who knows what.",choices:[{label:"Show her the maintenance log in her handwriting. Ask her to look at it.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Tell her the pod was opened twice. Ask her what she's not telling you.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Ask her about month six specifically. What was unusual about that day?",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Risa looks at the maintenance log for a long time. She says: 'That's my writing.' She turns pages \u2014 twenty months of careful technical notes, system checks, repair records. She says: 'I don't remember writing any of this.' She looks up. She says: 'But whoever wrote this kept the ship alive. Whoever wrote this was trying to get somewhere.'",choices:[{label:"She maintained the ship without conscious memory. Why? Get her to try to remember.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Risa is quiet for a while. Then she says: 'There was an illness.' She says it slowly, like recovering something from very far down. She says: 'Something in the water recycler. Captain Vos identified it. It wasn't fatal but it \u2014 it affected judgment. It spread fast.' She says: 'I don't remember what happened after that. I think I made a decision I can't remember making.'",choices:[{label:"She made a decision she can't remember. Find out what it was.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Month six. Risa works through it. A normal day, then \u2014 something with the water recycler, which she mentions almost incidentally, as a technical detail. You stop her. Ask about the recycler. She says: there was a contamination flag. She cleared it. She says: 'Or I thought I did.' She says: 'I don't know if I did.'",choices:[{label:"A contamination she may not have cleared properly. That's the beginning.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"ship-engineering",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find the water recycler's incident log, which runs independently of the main bridge log. Month six, day nineteen: a microbial contamination flag. It wasn't cleared. The contamination spread through the water system for three weeks before \u2014 according to Risa's maintenance notes, which she apparently wrote with no conscious memory \u2014 she isolated and purged it. By then the crew had been exposed for three weeks.",choices:[{label:"Find the medical records for the crew's exposure.",next:"b4a"},{label:"The contamination explains behavior changes but not where thirteen people went.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Check the ship's airlock logs. If people left the ship, it's recorded.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"ship-medical",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The medical records for month six through seven show escalating behavioral anomalies: insomnia, paranoia, impaired decision-making. The colony ship's AI flagged eleven of fourteen crew as cognitively impaired by month seven. Captain Vos's record stops at month seven. All records stop at month eight. After that: Risa's maintenance notes, and silence.",choices:[{label:"The crew were impaired by month seven. Something happened at month eight.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"ship-engineering",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Risa's maintenance notes mention, in month nine, 'resolving the departure situation' \u2014 a phrase that appears once and is never explained. Departure. If thirteen people departed a ship in deep space without another vessel, there is only one way that ends. But you haven't found thirteen bodies. You haven't found any evidence of what departure means in this context.",choices:[{label:"'Departure situation.' Find the airlock logs.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"ship-engineering",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The airlock log: multiple uses in months seven and eight. The outer airlock opened thirteen times. Each use logged with a biometric key \u2014 not Risa's. Captain Vos's key for the first three. Other crew keys for the subsequent ten. Each opening was brief. Nothing came in. Something went out. Thirteen times, the outer airlock opened, and whatever went out didn't come back.",choices:[{label:"Thirteen airlock openings. They went out. Risa was already in her pod or she locked herself in after.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The picture is assembling. The contamination impaired the crew's judgment. The crew \u2014 led by Captain Vos \u2014 made decisions that are not recoverable from the evidence available. Thirteen airlock openings. Thirteen missing people. Risa, either already in her pod or who put herself there after, maintained the ship for twenty months in a state she cannot fully remember and is now the last person who knows any part of what happened.",choices:[{label:"Tell Risa what the airlock logs show. She needs to know.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Ask Risa what she knows about the pod seal \u2014 who locked it and when.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Find the ship's remaining audio logs. Risa might have recorded something.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Risa listens to the airlock data without expression. Then she says: 'I knew.' She says it quietly. 'I knew and I couldn't stop it. The contamination \u2014 it made them certain. They were certain they needed to go. Captain Vos was certain. I tried to argue and they locked me in the pod.' She says: 'They believed they were doing the right thing. I believe that.'",choices:[{label:"They believed they were doing the right thing. That's the hardest kind of loss.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Risa says: 'Captain Vos locked me in. I think he was trying to protect me.' She says: 'He knew something was wrong with his thinking. He just couldn't stop it. He locked me in so I'd survive whatever he was about to do. I woke up twice \u2014 the pod has a maintenance cycle \u2014 and both times I sealed myself back in because I couldn't face what was outside.'",choices:[{label:"She woke twice and sealed herself back in. She knew.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Audio logs in the maintenance section \u2014 personal recordings Risa made in her months of awareness. You find thirty-one recordings. The early ones are technical \u2014 ship status, maintenance notes. The later ones are something else. Month sixteen: 'I have made my peace with what happened. I do not understand it but I have made my peace with it. I am going to bring this ship home. That's what I can still do.'",choices:[{label:"She knew and she decided to bring the ship home. That's what the twenty months were for.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Risa knows what happened \u2014 partially, fully, or in the way trauma knows things that language can't access. She was put in the pod by her captain who was trying to protect her. The other twelve followed him out the airlock believing, in their contamination-impaired certainty, that this was the right thing to do. She maintained the ship afterward. She brought it home, or tried to, in twenty months of work she can barely remember.",choices:[{label:"Ask her what she wants to happen to the ship and its record.",next:"b6a"},{label:"The contamination source is still potentially active. Check it.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Tell her: we need to get back to your ship. Six hours of oxygen was always a deadline.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She says: 'I want the full record kept. I want what happened to them to be understood, not judged. The contamination made them certain. Certainty is not the same as right.' She says: 'I want their names on the official record as crew, not as lost. They were crew. They were people. The thing that happened to them was not their fault.'",choices:[{label:"Names on the record as crew. That's what she asked for.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"ship-engineering",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The water recycler is now clean \u2014 Risa purged it in month nine, according to her notes. But the contaminant's origin is in the engineering logs: a faulty seal in a filtration module that was scheduled for replacement at the next maintenance stop. A seal that, in Risa's maintenance notes, she says she 'noticed was already flagged before departure.' It was a known issue that wasn't addressed before launch.",choices:[{label:"A known issue before departure. That's a different kind of accountability.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Risa says: 'I know.' She stands \u2014 slowly, twenty-six months of pod atrophy in her legs. She says: 'I've been trying to get home for two years. I'm not going to miss the lift.' She takes one last look around the aft section, which was her whole world for a very long time. Then she walks toward the dock.",choices:[{label:"She's ready. Move.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You spend the last available hour on the ship's bridge, pulling every remaining log, every data fragment, every recording. Risa helps \u2014 she knows the system better than anyone. The picture of what happened is as complete as it can be without the people who would know the rest. You have enough for a report. Whether the report will be believed, acted on, or used to blame the surviving person is not yet determined.",choices:[{label:"Ask your crew's medic to assess Risa before you file anything.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Brief your crew on what you've found before leaving the Kovalev.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Take the ship's drive data with you \u2014 it may be needed as evidence.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"ship-aft",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your medic, Okafor, examines Risa carefully. He says: extended sleep pod use, muscle atrophy, vitamin deficiencies, all expected. He also says, quietly, to you: 'She's carrying significant psychological weight. Whatever happened on this ship, she's been living with it alone for two years.' He says: 'She needs support before she needs questions.'",choices:[{label:"Questions can wait. Get her home first.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You brief your crew in five minutes. The facts, nothing more. Thirteen people are not on the ship. One person is. The contamination explains the behavior but not, in any legal sense, the outcome. There is significant ambiguity in the events between month seven and month nine that the available evidence cannot resolve. Your crew listens without comment. Then your pilot says: 'Do we tow the Kovalev?' You say: 'Yes.'",choices:[{label:"Tow the Kovalev. Its records matter.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"ship-bridge",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You extract the ship's main data drive \u2014 its entire operational record from launch to now. It's evidence in the clearest sense, and you're the first salvage crew on scene. Whatever investigation follows, the drive needs to be in chain of custody from this moment. You label it, seal it, and store it separately from all other salvage.",choices:[{label:"Evidence secured. Leave the Kovalev.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"your-ship",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You leave the Kovalev under tow and begin the journey back. Risa sleeps for fourteen hours \u2014 real sleep, not the pod's suspension. When she wakes she eats something and then asks: 'What happens to me now?' You say: 'An inquiry. Probably a long one.' She says: 'And the ship?' You say: 'Evidence.' She says: 'And the crew?' You say: 'You decide how you tell their story.'",choices:[{label:"Ask her what story she wants told.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Tell her about the support services available when they return.",next:"b8b"},{label:"The inquiry will make decisions about blame. Think about how to frame the evidence.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"your-ship",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Risa says: 'I want people to understand that they were not themselves. I want the contamination to be understood as a cause. I want nobody to say they chose badly when they were not capable of choice.' She says: 'I want them to be people who died in an accident. Not people who did something wrong.'",choices:[{label:"That's the story she wants. Help her tell it clearly.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"your-ship",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You tell her about the survivor support services, the psychological care, the legal representation she'll need. She listens carefully. She says: 'I've been running a ship alone for twenty months. I can navigate a debrief.' She says it with a quiet confidence that tells you she has already thought about everything that's coming. She's been thinking about it for two years.",choices:[{label:"She's ready. Two years of solitude will do that.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"your-ship",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"How to frame the evidence: the contamination is documented, the behavioral changes are logged, the airlock record is factual. Risa's role \u2014 what she did in those months, what she chose, what she knew \u2014 is the ambiguous part. The inquiry will decide what 'maintained the ship' means in context. You can present the evidence clearly without prejudging what it proves.",choices:[{label:"Clear evidence, no prejudging. That's the right approach.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"your-ship",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The return journey takes eight days. By day three, Risa is walking the length of your ship twice daily for exercise, which your medic prescribed and which she follows with the discipline of someone who has managed their own health for two years. By day six she's helping with minor maintenance checks \u2014 not because she was asked but because she needs to be useful. By day eight she knows your ship as well as she knew the Kovalev.",choices:[{label:"She belongs on a ship. Think about what comes after the inquiry.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Ask her about the contamination's known-issue history \u2014 she mentioned it once.",next:"b9b"},{label:"The families of the thirteen will need to be notified. Think about that obligation.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"your-ship",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Risa says she hasn't thought past the inquiry. She says: 'I've been thinking about getting home for two years. I don't have a plan for after that.' She says: 'I think I might need to not be on a ship for a while.' She says it like a diagnosis. You say: that makes sense. She says: 'It won't last. I'm an engineer. Ships are what I do.'",choices:[{label:"Ships are what she does. She'll come back to them.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"your-ship",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The known issue: the filtration seal that caused the contamination was on a pre-departure inspection list as 'scheduled for replacement at next stop.' Risa says she flagged it. She says: 'I was told the replacement could wait. We were behind schedule.' She says it with the flatness of someone who has been thinking about this specific fact for two years.",choices:[{label:"She flagged it and was overruled. That's part of the record.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"your-ship",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Thirteen families. You think about the notifications. The families who have been waiting two years with no contact, no status update, no information. The inquiry will make official notification \u2014 you're not in that chain. But Risa is. She is the person who was with them for the last conscious months they were themselves. The families may want to hear from her, eventually. Or they may not.",choices:[{label:"That's Risa's decision to make. Not yours.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"station-dock",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You dock at the nearest station. The inquiry team is waiting. Risa walks off your ship into a situation she has been preparing for since month sixteen of twenty-six months alone. She is composed and she is ready and she is, for the first time in more than two years, not alone.",choices:[{label:"The inquiry determines the contamination was the primary cause.",next:"end-cause"},{label:"The inquiry is long and painful but Risa's account is believed.",next:"end-believed"},{label:"The inquiry finds the known-issue seal was the systemic failure.",next:"end-systemic"},{label:"Risa's maintenance record saves the ship's legacy.",next:"end-record"}]},"end-cause":{id:"end-cause",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The inquiry's finding: the contamination was the primary cause. The behavioral changes were documented, the impaired decision-making was established, and the inquiry recommends that the crew be recorded as having died in an accident caused by a systems failure, not as responsible for their own deaths. Risa's maintenance record during those twenty months is entered into the official log as evidence of an engineer maintaining professional function under extraordinary circumstances. Captain Vos is named as a crew member who attempted to protect a colleague even while impaired. That is on the record.",choices:[]},"end-believed":{id:"end-believed",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The inquiry is fourteen months long. Risa is questioned thirty-seven times. Every time, her account is consistent: the contamination, the impairment, the airlock, the pod, the twenty months. The inquiry concludes there is no basis for any finding against her. The finding says: 'Ms. Tanaka's actions in the period following the incident reflect an engineer's commitment to her vessel and to the possibility of return.' She reads the finding in a room by herself and then calls her sister.",choices:[]},"end-systemic":{id:"end-systemic",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The inquiry finds the known-issue seal and the decision to delay its replacement as the systemic failure that initiated the chain of events. The company that operated the Kovalev faces significant civil liability. New filtration maintenance protocols are mandated for all colony vessels. Risa's pre-departure flag \u2014 which she submitted formally and which was overruled in writing \u2014 is in the record. She did the right thing before departure. The system didn't listen. The inquiry says so.",choices:[]},"end-record":{id:"end-record",scene:"ending-space",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Risa's maintenance log \u2014 thirty-one audio recordings and twenty months of handwritten engineering notes \u2014 is eventually published as part of the inquiry's public record. A researcher who studies human performance in extreme isolation writes about it. She describes Risa's log as 'the clearest documented case of professional identity sustaining function when conscious identity could not.' Risa reads the paper and says: 'I just fixed the ship. That's what you do.' She's back on a ship by then, which is exactly where she should be.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-expedition",packId:"explorer",title:"The Expedition",subtitle:"Six days into the jungle. The map ends here.",length:"Medium",free:!0,brief:"You're leading a small expedition to verify a remote site reported by a dying cartographer. The map is incomplete, the porters have heard something, and the ruins\u2014if they exist\u2014have been found before. By someone who didn't come back.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"jungle-camp",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Day six. The river bends east here, which your map says it shouldn't. Dr. Parkes, your botanist, is making notes about a vine she hasn't seen before. Torres, your lead porter, hasn't spoken in two hours. Last night someone \u2014 or something \u2014 moved through the camp perimeter. Nothing was taken. The site Harlow described before he died is either two days north or it doesn't exist.",choices:[{label:"Check the map against the terrain. The river bend might tell you where you are.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Ask Torres what's bothering him. He knows this forest better than any map.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Examine the vine Parkes found. If it's undescribed, you might already be somewhere significant.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"jungle-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The river doesn't match Harlow's map \u2014 but it does match a map you photographed in the archive before leaving. An older map, hand-drawn, that uses different reference points. You're three kilometers east of where you thought. The site, if Harlow's coordinates are correct, is one day north, not two.",choices:[{label:"Share this with the group and adjust the route.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"jungle-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Torres says the forest has gone quiet in the wrong way \u2014 not the quiet of animals avoiding people, but the quiet of animals that have left. He's seen this before, he says, near places where the ground isn't stable. He also says his grandfather told him about a cleared stone place two days north that the local villages don't speak about by name.",choices:[{label:"Torres knows something. Get the full story and adjust your expectations.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"jungle-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Parkes identifies the vine as a cultivar \u2014 not wild, but something that was once cultivated and has since gone feral. Cultivated plants don't exist without cultivation. Someone planted this vine, probably centuries ago, probably in organized plantings. You're in the right zone. The site is near.",choices:[{label:"Tell the group. The vine is confirmation. Push north.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"jungle-trail",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You push north. By midday the terrain changes \u2014 the canopy thins, and under your boots the ground has an artificial regularity, too flat to be natural. Torres stops at a stone outcrop that isn't an outcrop. It's a wall. Partially buried, moss-covered, but cut stone. You've found the edge of something.",choices:[{label:"Follow the wall and map its extent before going further.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Look for an entrance. Walls have doors.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Document what you can see right now before it gets complicated.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"ruins-perimeter",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The wall runs forty meters east-west before disappearing into the hillside. On the east end, a second wall intersects at right angles. You're standing at the corner of something large \u2014 a building or compound. The scale is much bigger than Harlow's description implied.",choices:[{label:"The scale changes the nature of the find. Look for a way in.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"ruins-perimeter",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find an entrance \u2014 a gap in the wall where dressed stones once formed a threshold. The lintel has fallen but the void remains. Beyond it, filtered green light and the sense of an enclosed space. Torres won't cross the threshold without knowing what's inside. You don't blame him.",choices:[{label:"Scout the interior carefully, then report back.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"ruins-perimeter",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You photograph and sketch everything visible from the perimeter: stone quality, construction method, the angle of collapse. Parkes notes the vegetation patterns \u2014 certain plants follow the buried wall lines, making the building's footprint legible from above if you knew what to look for. This site has been here for centuries. The documentation matters.",choices:[{label:"Documentation in hand. Now go inside.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"ruins-interior",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Inside the compound: a central courtyard, overgrown but recognizable. Four smaller chambers off the main axis. Carvings on the east wall \u2014 not decorative, systematic. Harlow mentioned carvings in his dying notes and described them as 'a record, not an art.' He also said the previous expedition found something in the central chamber. That expedition was led by a man named Velt. Velt disappeared.",choices:[{label:"Go to the central chamber first. Whatever Velt found is there.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Study the carvings on the east wall. Harlow called them a record.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Search the four smaller chambers systematically before the central one.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"ruins-central",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The central chamber has been disturbed \u2014 recently, relative to the centuries of stillness around it. Soil displaced, stones moved back into position imperfectly. Someone came here within the last decade and then tried to hide the evidence. The floor beneath the displaced soil contains a recessed space. Empty now, but shaped for something specific.",choices:[{label:"Something was removed from here. Find out what Velt found.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"ruins-interior",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The carvings are a sequence \u2014 dates, or counts, rendered in a symbol system you don't recognize but Parkes begins to decode using the cultivar vine as a reference point. The plants depicted in the carvings match the plants growing above the buried walls. This is an agricultural record, a catalog. The site was a center of knowledge, not just a settlement.",choices:[{label:"An agricultural knowledge center. The central chamber might hold the core of that knowledge.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"ruins-chambers",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The smaller chambers yield: two are empty, one contains corroded metal implements whose function you can't determine, and one contains a stone shelf with a grid of small recesses \u2014 seed storage, Parkes says immediately. Each recess would have held a sealed container. Most are empty. Three still have the lids intact.",choices:[{label:"Intact sealed storage after centuries. The central chamber must hold the key to this.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"ruins-central",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The central chamber's disturbed floor tells a story. The recessed space was opened within the past ten to fifteen years \u2014 the soil displacement, the moss regrowth patterns. Velt disappeared twelve years ago. His expedition was funded by a botanical consortium. Whatever he found was important enough to remove and important enough to disappear over.",choices:[{label:"Find what Velt took. It didn't disappear \u2014 it went somewhere.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Document the disturbance carefully. Velt's removal is part of the site's history.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Search the rest of the site. Velt might have missed something.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"ruins-central",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find Velt's camp site under the root system of a strangler fig at the compound's eastern edge \u2014 collapsed tent poles, a rusted tin, and a journal. The journal is partially legible. Velt found an intact set of scrolled bark documents in the central recess. He took them. He describes them as 'the catalog' \u2014 a complete record of the cultivar system. He writes his last entry three days before his scheduled extraction. It ends mid-sentence.",choices:[{label:"He had the documents and then he was gone. Find out who was tracking him.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"ruins-central",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The disturbance documentation reveals something systematic: Velt didn't just take what was in the recess, he made impressions of the carvings on the surrounding walls before he left \u2014 there are traces of the material he used. He was thorough. Whatever he found, he documented it as well as removed it. That means copies exist, even if the originals disappeared with him.",choices:[{label:"If he made impressions, those might have reached his sponsors. Investigate the consortium.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"ruins-chambers",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Velt missed the seed chamber \u2014 or he knew about it and chose not to take from it. The three intact sealed recesses contain desiccated but possibly viable seed material. If these seeds are the cultivars in the carvings \u2014 the agricultural varieties this center maintained \u2014 they're not historical artifacts. They're potentially living genetic material for plant varieties that no longer exist elsewhere.",choices:[{label:"Living seeds from an extinct cultivar tradition. This is the find. Now protect it.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"ruins-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You set up a proper camp at the site perimeter. Torres relaxes once he understands the silence \u2014 the animals avoid the site not from instinct but because the canopy structure here is different, affecting sound. Parkes is working methodically through the seed chamber. You have a partial picture of what this place was, what Velt found, and what happened when he found it. The question is what you do next.",choices:[{label:"Document everything completely before making any decisions about extraction.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Send Torres back for additional supplies \u2014 this needs more time than planned.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Try to trace what happened to Velt's journal and documentation through radio contact.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"ruins-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three days of documentation: photogrammetry of every carved surface, comprehensive sampling, GPS mapping of the full site. Parkes completes a preliminary analysis of the seed material \u2014 she identifies seven distinct cultivar varieties, none in any current botanical database. You build a record thorough enough to reconstruct the site's significance without needing to be here.",choices:[{label:"The documentation is the expedition's most important output. Now decide on the seeds.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"jungle-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Torres makes the round trip in four days. While he's gone, you and Parkes work the site. When he returns he brings news: there was another party asking about the trail head. Four people, well-equipped, no obvious affiliation. They'd been there two days before Torres arrived. They knew you were ahead of them.",choices:[{label:"Someone is following you. The consortium connected to Velt's disappearance is still active.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"ruins-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Radio contact with your logistics contact. She runs the name Velt through her network: disappeared twelve years ago, his expedition funded by Meridian Botanical Consortium, which dissolved six months after his disappearance and reformed under a different name \u2014 Helix Agricultural Research. Helix is still active and has significant commercial interests in plant genetics. Your expedition was grant-funded. Helix is one of the secondary grant bodies.",choices:[{label:"The organization that funded Velt also partly funds you. This is not a coincidence.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"ruins-camp",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The picture is clearer now. Velt found this site, removed the catalog documents, and disappeared before he could report back. His funders \u2014 who became Helix \u2014 may have known what he found. Your expedition was partly funded by Helix. You have found the site, the seed material, and Velt's journal. You're in a position that Velt was in twelve years ago.",choices:[{label:"The parallel is alarming. Get everything out fast, through channels Helix doesn't control.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Confront the situation directly \u2014 contact Helix and see how they respond.",next:"b6b"},{label:"The seeds and documentation are the priority. Secure those before anything else.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"ruins-camp",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You divide the documentation across three separate encrypted drives, give one to Torres to carry by a different route, keep one yourself, and send one with a trusted colleague via a third path. You report to the university directly, bypassing the grant body. You do not mention the seeds in the initial communication. The site report goes to the university's independent archive first.",choices:[{label:"The redundancy is protection. Now move.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"ruins-camp",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You radio Helix's listed contact. The response comes within hours \u2014 far too quickly for a routine inquiry. They say they're glad you've found the site and ask you to remain in position while they send a representative. You say: how long? They say: four days. Four days is exactly how long it would take a prepared party to reach you from the trail head.",choices:[{label:"They were already coming. Get out with what you have.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"ruins-camp",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Parkes carefully removes the three intact seed containers using conservation protocols, packing them in the temperature-stable containers you brought for botanical samples. She catalogs everything with photographs. You encrypt and transmit the seed analysis to the university's botanical department independently of any grant reporting. If something happens to the physical samples, the analysis exists.",choices:[{label:"The analysis is transmitted. The physical samples are secured. Move.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"jungle-trail",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You break camp and move. On the second day back, Torres indicates a parallel movement in the forest \u2014 someone pacing you at a distance. Not closing. Watching. You maintain your pace. The following party doesn't approach, which might mean they want to see where you go, or it might mean they're waiting for the right moment.",choices:[{label:"Change routes. Make it harder to follow without making direct contact.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Stop and wait. If they want to talk, make them come to you on your terms.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Increase pace and get to the river crossing before they can cut you off.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"jungle-trail",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You take the eastern tributary route \u2014 longer, less direct, but it comes out at a different extraction point than the one Helix would know from your filed itinerary. The parallel movement falls behind after the first river crossing. They either don't know the eastern route or didn't bring the equipment for it.",choices:[{label:"You've opened distance. Keep moving.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"jungle-trail",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You stop at a defensible clearing and wait. After an hour, two people emerge from the tree line \u2014 not four. A man and a woman, well-equipped, no weapons visible. They say they're freelance researchers, interested in the site. They know its location. They don't know what you found inside. They want to negotiate.",choices:[{label:"Negotiate from a position of what you have. Hear them out.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"jungle-trail",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You push hard and make the river crossing by midday. The following party reaches the bank as you're halfway across. They don't enter the water. They watch. On the far bank, Torres says: they'll have a crossing point downstream. Two hours. You have a two-hour head start and need to use it.",choices:[{label:"Two hours. Move fast.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"jungle-outpost",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You reach the trail head. Your logistics contact is there with the vehicle. She says Helix has called her twice asking about your status. She told them the truth \u2014 she didn't know \u2014 because she didn't know. The following party hasn't appeared yet. You have the seeds, the documentation, and Velt's journal. You need to decide who gets what and in what order.",choices:[{label:"Get to the city, submit to the university, file the official find report publicly.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Get the seeds to a seed bank first. They're the most time-sensitive element.",next:"b8b"},{label:"Submit to the university and simultaneously release preliminary findings publicly \u2014 public record protects.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"jungle-outpost",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The drive to the city takes eight hours. You submit the full documentation package to the university's archive on arrival. The find report is filed with the relevant national heritage authority. Helix calls your expedition phone four times. You don't answer until the submissions are confirmed received. Then you answer and say: it's been filed.",choices:[{label:"Public record now. Helix can't make it disappear.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"jungle-outpost",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Parkes knows the nearest certified seed bank \u2014 three hours off the main route. You go there first. The seeds are checked in under the expedition's name and immediately transferred to a secondary facility as backup. The bank's director, understanding the situation quickly, flags the deposit as priority. The seeds are now in an institution whose entire purpose is protecting them.",choices:[{label:"Seeds secured independently. Now the documentation.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"jungle-outpost",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You submit to the university and simultaneously send a preliminary findings summary to three academic contacts who will post it publicly. Within twelve hours, the site is in the public record. A botanist in Germany posts about the seed findings. A historian in Brazil recognizes the carving style. The site has an audience now \u2014 one that Helix cannot control.",choices:[{label:"Public record is the best protection. Now the formal submission.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"detective-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The find is public. The university issues a press statement. Parkes is in contact with three botanical institutions about the seed analysis. Velt's journal is submitted to the heritage authority as evidence of the previous expedition and its undocumented findings. Helix issues a statement saying they are proud to have supported the expedition. The brass neck of it is almost admirable.",choices:[{label:"Respond to Helix's statement publicly \u2014 their connection to Velt needs to be acknowledged.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Let the academic community respond. They're better equipped to handle Helix's claims.",next:"b9b"},{label:"Focus on the investigation into Velt's disappearance. That's the thread that needs pulling.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"detective-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You publish a short statement noting that Helix's predecessor funded Velt's expedition, that Velt disappeared without reporting his find, and that the site's current discovery was made independent of Helix's influence. You don't accuse. You document. The statement goes into the public record and journalists begin asking questions you didn't ask.",choices:[{label:"Let the journalists follow it. Your job is the academic record.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"detective-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The botanical and archaeological community responds with considerable interest and some skepticism \u2014 the standard mix. Three senior researchers independently request access to the site. The seed analysis generates a paper within weeks. Helix's claims to involvement are noted and noted with raised eyebrows. The academic record is self-correcting, slowly but thoroughly.",choices:[{label:"The community is engaged. That's protection of a different kind.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"detective-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You pass Velt's journal to a journalist who covers unexplained disappearances in the research community. She has three other cases she suspects are connected to commercial interests in biological research. Velt becomes part of a larger story. His disappearance is reconsidered. An investigation is opened. It's slow, but it's moving.",choices:[{label:"Velt's case is in motion. The site's record is secure.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"detective-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Six months after the expedition. The site is formally registered as a heritage site under national protection. The seed analysis has produced two papers. One cultivar variety shows promise in drought-resistant agriculture. Parkes is leading a follow-up expedition with proper institutional backing. Helix has withdrawn from academic grant-giving, citing 'strategic refocus.' Velt's case is still open.",choices:[{label:"The site's legacy is secured. The scientific value is already being realized.",next:"end-legacy"},{label:"The seeds matter most \u2014 one variety changes what's possible in agriculture.",next:"end-seeds"},{label:"Velt's case resolves. Helix's role in his disappearance is established.",next:"end-justice"},{label:"The site changes what was known. A chapter of history is rewritten.",next:"end-history"}]},"end-legacy":{id:"end-legacy",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The site enters the academic record as one of the most significant agricultural knowledge centers of its era. The carvings are translated over five years by a team that includes a researcher Parkes trained. The cultivar program produces results that are published and replicated. Torres is credited in the official site documentation \u2014 his grandfather's knowledge was the earliest surviving reference to the place. You receive a letter from the heritage authority thanking you for the expedition report. It is thorough and correct and means less than the work itself.",choices:[]},"end-seeds":{id:"end-seeds",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The drought-resistant cultivar identified from the seed chamber is the finding that changes things practically. It takes four years of greenhouse cultivation to produce viable crop-scale quantities and eight years before it reaches farmers in three countries. The paper crediting the discovery names the site, the expedition, and the seed chamber. It notes that the variety was preserved in sealed stone containers for an estimated four hundred years. The people who sealed those containers knew what they were doing.",choices:[]},"end-justice":{id:"end-justice",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The investigation into Velt's disappearance concludes two years later. It establishes that he was met at his extraction point by representatives of Meridian Botanical Consortium, that the catalog documents were taken from him, and that he was paid significantly to remain silent. He isn't dead. He's living in a different country under a different name. When the journalist finds him, he says: I took their money and I've regretted it since. The catalog documents are recovered through legal proceedings. They go to the heritage archive where they should have been twelve years ago.",choices:[]},"end-history":{id:"end-history",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The site rewrites the understanding of agricultural knowledge transmission in the region. The systematic nature of the cultivar catalog \u2014 the completeness of it, the care \u2014 shows that what was thought lost was never lost, just unfound. Three other sites are identified using the carving style as a template. Each one adds to the record. The map that Harlow drew before he died, incomplete and faded, turns out to have been accurate within two kilometers. Not bad for a dying man.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-forger",packId:"detective",title:"The Forger",subtitle:"The signature is perfect. That's what's wrong with it.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"A painting worth half a million has been authenticated as genuine. The insurance company hired you to prove it isn't. The forger is someone who knows exactly how authentication works \u2014 and how to beat it.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Hartley Mosley: oil on linen, mid-period, authenticated by the Foundation's own expert and valued at five hundred and forty thousand pounds. The insurance company hired you because Carew \u2014 the expert \u2014 authenticated a painting last year that turned out to be a fake, and then denied he'd ever seen it. They want to know if this is happening again.",choices:[{label:"See the painting first. Before you talk to anyone.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Talk to Carew before he knows you're investigating.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Research the previous fake case before approaching anyone.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Hartley shows you the painting with barely concealed anxiety. It's beautiful \u2014 Mosley's palette and composition are right. Under UV light, the lower-left corner fluoresces differently from the rest. Not wrong. Just different. You photograph everything and say nothing to Hartley about what you've found.",choices:[{label:"Now go talk to Carew about his authentication process.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Carew says the authentication was thorough and the painting is genuine. He also says something unexpected: the previous case \u2014 someone used his name on a report he never wrote. He says: I have never seen that painting. Someone had access to his letterhead and signature style.",choices:[{label:"If his signature was forged before, this case is more complex. See the painting.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The previous fake: a Mosley sold to an Edinburgh gallery, authenticated by 'Carew'. The report uses his letterhead perfectly. Carew claims he never saw it. A student exposed the fake eighteen months later \u2014 the linen was wrong. The forger knew authentication formats but not materials science.",choices:[{label:"This forger learns from mistakes. Go see the Hartley painting.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You sit with everything you've gathered so far. The UV anomaly in the lower-left corner. Carew's claim about the previous forgery of his signature. The pattern of a forger who understands authentication process. You need to trace the painting's history before it reached Hartley.",choices:[{label:"Ask Hartley directly where the painting came from.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Research the dealer who sold it \u2014 Strand.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Commission a materials analysis of the UV anomaly before going further.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Hartley says it came through a dealer named Strand, who acquired it from a French estate sale. Documentation exists and looks credible. Hartley says: Strand is reputable. He says it with the firmness of someone who needs it to be true.",choices:[{label:"Find Strand and verify the French estate provenance.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Strand has an unblemished record publicly but was involved in a disputed sale eight years ago \u2014 nothing proven. He acquired this painting from a private estate sale in France handled by an executor named Rourke. Rourke is a solicitor who managed the Beaumont estate.",choices:[{label:"The estate executor is a key figure. Get the materials analysis first.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A university conservation lab analyses a microscopic sample. The report comes back in two days: the titanium white pigment in the lower-left corner contains a chemical marker not commercially available before 1987. Mosley died in 1981. Part of this painting was applied or altered after his death.",choices:[{label:"This is the core finding. Now trace where the alteration was done.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The materials finding is now confirmed: the lower-left corner contains post-1981 pigment. The painting's restoration history shows it was worked on three years before the sale by Vance Conservation \u2014 a studio run by a former apprentice of the Mosley Foundation's own restoration department. The lower-left was the area they worked on.",choices:[{label:"Find who commissioned the Vance Conservation work.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Contact Vance Conservation directly and ask what they found.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Take the materials finding to Carew. He authenticated it \u2014 does he have an answer?",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The commission came from the Beaumont estate through Rourke. Beaumont was ill when it was commissioned \u2014 the instructions were given by Rourke as executor. Rourke organised the restoration, then sold the painting to Strand. He controlled what happened before and after the Vance Conservation work.",choices:[{label:"Rourke is central to this. But talk to Vance first.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Vance Conservation's director says the lower-left quarter was water-damaged \u2014 'extensively non-recoverable' were her words in the internal assessment. She says the written report was sent to the executor, Rourke. She assumed he disclosed the extent of the damage in the sale. She's surprised to learn the sale documentation says only 'minor restoration work.'",choices:[{label:"Rourke received the full damage assessment and wrote 'minor.' That's the fraud.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Carew is not defensive \u2014 he's angry at himself. He says: I tested the paint surface but not at that depth. The brushwork and composition are genuine Mosley. The lower corner is not original. He says: someone showed me the painting after restoration and presented it as minor conservation. I should have asked to see before-and-after photographs.",choices:[{label:"Carew was deliberately shown only what someone wanted him to see.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You now understand the mechanism. The painting was a genuine Mosley with extensive water damage to the lower-left quarter. Rourke had it restored \u2014 replacing the damaged area with new paint \u2014 without disclosing the extent of the damage in the sale. The authentication by Carew was then done on the post-restoration painting only. The fraud is one of concealment, not fabrication.",choices:[{label:"Find Rourke and confront him with the Vance Conservation records.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Verify whether Beaumont instructed the concealment before approaching Rourke.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Take everything to the insurance company now and let their lawyers confront Rourke.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Rourke is careful. He says the restoration was standard conservation work, disclosed in the sale documentation. You point out that 'minor restoration' doesn't describe replacing an entire quarter of the paint surface. He says: that is a question of interpretation. He says it with the precision of a man who has already run this through a lawyer.",choices:[{label:"His interpretation won't survive the Vance Conservation internal assessment.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Beaumont is dead. His correspondence isn't. Letters between Beaumont and Rourke from the period of the restoration show Beaumont was explicit: disclose the minimum necessary. He writes: the painting's value depends on its completeness story. Rourke's response acknowledges the instruction. The concealment was coordinated between them.",choices:[{label:"Beaumont's instructions don't excuse Rourke's choice to follow them.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The insurance company's lawyers review the file. They say it's sufficient to decline the claim. They also say they'll be referring Rourke's documentation to the Solicitors Regulation Authority. They say: this is a well-constructed file. Now let's see what Rourke says when we approach him formally.",choices:[{label:"Wait to see how Rourke responds to the formal approach.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Vance Conservation internal assessment is the critical document. It explicitly describes the damage as 'lower left quarter entirely non-recoverable' and was sent to Rourke's office. Rourke wrote 'minor restoration' in the sale notes. The gap between those two descriptions is the fraud.",choices:[{label:"Present both documents to Rourke simultaneously.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Have the documents authenticated and take them straight to the police.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Show the documents to Strand first \u2014 did he know?",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You put both documents side by side in front of Rourke. He looks at them for forty seconds. Then he says: Beaumont told me not to disclose the full extent. He says: I followed my client's instructions. He says: I don't believe I committed fraud. He says it with the careful phrasing of a man who has reviewed exactly where the legal lines are.",choices:[{label:"He's constructing a 'following instructions' defence. Get it on record.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The detective who takes the file says it's a clean case of misrepresentation. She says Rourke's choice of language in the sale notes was deliberate and demonstrably inconsistent with the information he held. She says: we'll bring him in for interview. She says it with the efficiency of someone who's handled art fraud before.",choices:[{label:"The police have it. Now watch what Rourke does.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Strand says he had no idea the damage was that extensive. He says Rourke's documentation described minor conservation and he accepted that. He's angry \u2014 the kind of anger that comes from realising you were used. He says he'll provide a statement confirming what Rourke told him. Another brick in the wall.",choices:[{label:"Strand's statement strengthens the case. Now go to Rourke.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Rourke's defence crystallises: he followed Beaumont's instructions, disclosure was made in the sale documentation (however vague), and the authentication by Carew stands independently of the restoration. His lawyer is expensive and thorough. You have a strong civil case. The criminal case depends on whether 'following instructions' creates sufficient ambiguity for a jury.",choices:[{label:"Find the Beaumont-Rourke correspondence that makes the instruction explicit.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Focus on whether Rourke arranged Carew's viewing deliberately \u2014 that's more direct.",next:"b6b"},{label:"The civil case is already solid. Recommend the insurance company proceed there.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Beaumont-Rourke letters are in the estate archive. They're explicit: Beaumont instructs minimum disclosure, cites the painting's value depending on its complete provenance, and Rourke acknowledges the instruction. A defence lawyer will argue Rourke was following his client. A prosecutor will argue that following instructions to commit fraud is still fraud.",choices:[{label:"Take the letters and the full file to the police.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You piece together how Carew's viewing was arranged: Rourke contacted the Foundation and requested an authentication. He provided the painting to Carew for viewing \u2014 post-restoration only. He did not provide pre-restoration photographs, which would have shown the extent of the damage. This is deliberate staging of what the expert saw.",choices:[{label:"Deliberate staging of an authentication is the clearest fraud charge. Go to police.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The insurance company's lawyers say the civil case is solid \u2014 Hartley will recover his losses. They also say the criminal referral is their call to make, and they're making it. You've done your job. The outcome of the criminal process isn't yours to control.",choices:[{label:"The civil and criminal processes are now both in motion.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"detective-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The fraud investigation is formally open. Rourke is interviewed under caution. He exercises his right to silence for the first interview, then provides a prepared statement in the second. The statement acknowledges he 'may have understated' the extent of the restoration in the sale documentation, while maintaining he followed estate instructions. His lawyer is good. The case is live.",choices:[{label:"Ask whether the insurance company will push for criminal prosecution.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Check what's happening with Carew's professional review.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Find out what Hartley plans to do now.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The insurance company says: yes. They say the Solicitors Regulation Authority case will proceed in parallel. They say: we've dealt with Rourke's type before. They say the combination of the SRA case and the criminal investigation creates enough pressure that Rourke may decide cooperation is his best outcome.",choices:[{label:"That's a reasonable reading. Wait to see if Rourke cooperates.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Mosley Foundation's professional review of Carew concludes he followed proper procedures for the information he was given. No censure. But the Foundation announces new protocols: future authentications require pre- and post-restoration photographs from any painting with documented conservation history. Carew helped write the new protocol.",choices:[{label:"The right outcome for Carew. The system learned from this.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Hartley is pursuing both the insurance claim and a direct civil action against the estate. He's furious \u2014 at Rourke, at Strand (unfairly), at the Foundation (somewhat unfairly), and at himself for not commissioning independent authentication. He says: never again. You don't tell him that's not a guarantee.",choices:[{label:"Let Hartley's lawyers handle the civil case.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three months in. The civil case is moving \u2014 Hartley's lawyers are confident of recovering his losses. The criminal case is slower. Rourke's 'following instructions' defence creates genuine uncertainty. His lawyer has filed applications that are standard procedure but effective at slowing things. The SRA case is running independently. You are called as an expert witness in the civil proceedings.",choices:[{label:"Give evidence. Describe the investigation clearly.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Prepare the evidence carefully \u2014 Rourke's lawyers will challenge your methodology.",next:"b8b"},{label:"Ask the prosecution whether additional evidence would help the criminal case.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You give evidence for three hours. Rourke's lawyer challenges your qualifications and methodology at every turn, which is what they're paid to do. The judge is patient. Your file is comprehensive and your conclusions are supported at every point. The civil tribunal is satisfied with the evidence.",choices:[{label:"The civil case is proceeding well. Wait for the judgment.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You spend a week preparing your evidence package. Every conclusion documented, every source verified, every step in the reasoning laid out so that Rourke's lawyers can't find a gap. When they challenge you in the hearing, you have an answer for every question. The expert witness in a case like this sets the evidentiary floor \u2014 the more solid your package, the higher that floor.",choices:[{label:"The preparation paid off. The hearing goes well.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The prosecution says: the Beaumont-Rourke correspondence is the most useful addition. They also say: anything showing Rourke knew the 1987 pigment was there before he wrote 'minor restoration' would be decisive. You go back to the Vance Conservation records. Their internal assessment was sent to Rourke's office nine weeks before he wrote the sale documentation. He knew the details when he chose the language.",choices:[{label:"The nine-week gap is decisive. File it with the prosecution.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"detective-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The civil judgment comes in: Hartley is awarded his full purchase price plus costs, to be paid by the Beaumont estate. Rourke's personal liability in the civil case is contested by his own legal team, who argue he acted as agent rather than principal. That argument fails on the facts \u2014 the documentation choices were his. The criminal case reaches trial six months after you filed the original report.",choices:[{label:"The criminal trial is the decisive moment. What's the outcome?",next:"s10"},{label:"Ask how Rourke is presenting his defence at trial.",next:"b9a"},{label:"The painting itself \u2014 what's its status during trial?",next:"b9b"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Rourke's defence at trial is exactly what you expected: he followed his client's instructions, the disclosure was made (however characterised), and he cannot be held responsible for Carew's authentication methodology. His lawyer is skilled at making duty of disclosure sound ambiguous. The prosecution's strongest point is the nine-week gap between receiving the damage assessment and writing 'minor restoration.'",choices:[{label:"The nine-week gap is the heart of the case. Wait for the verdict.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The painting is in a secure facility pending the trial's outcome. A gallery in London has offered to display it during proceedings \u2014 the art world attention is significant. The Mosley Foundation has confirmed the painting's core is genuinely Mosley's work; only the lower-left restoration is not his. A rare Mosley with documented damage history, honestly disclosed, is still worth significant money \u2014 just not five hundred and forty thousand.",choices:[{label:"Honest disclosure would have been better for everyone. Wait for the verdict.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The trial runs for twelve days. The jury deliberates for three. You follow the verdict through the news. The question is not whether the fraud occurred \u2014 the facts are clear \u2014 but whether Rourke's 'following instructions' argument is enough to create reasonable doubt on criminal intent.",choices:[{label:"The jury convicts. The verdict is clear.",next:"end-conviction"},{label:"The jury acquits on the criminal charge but the civil judgment stands.",next:"end-acquittal"},{label:"Rourke pleads guilty mid-trial to avoid the full verdict.",next:"end-plea"},{label:"The case settles before verdict \u2014 Rourke cooperates fully in exchange.",next:"end-settlement"}]},"end-conviction":{id:"end-conviction",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Guilty on the fraud charge. The sentence: eighteen months suspended, a significant fine, and his practising certificate revoked. The Beaumont estate pays Hartley's civil award. The painting is reauthenticated with full disclosure \u2014 genuine Mosley, lower-left section restored 1987, valued at considerably less than Hartley paid but sold to a collector who finds the history interesting. Carew attends the reauthentication and signs the new certificate. He says: this is the correct document for this painting. You think he's right.",choices:[]},"end-acquittal":{id:"end-acquittal",scene:"ending-neutral",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The jury acquits on the criminal charge \u2014 'following instructions' creates enough ambiguity. But the civil judgment is unaffected: Hartley recovers his full purchase price from the estate. Rourke's SRA case proceeds separately and results in a twelve-month suspension of his practising certificate. The fraud is established in every civil and professional forum. Only the criminal conviction is missing. That gap between what happened and what's provable beyond reasonable doubt is a gap you'll think about.",choices:[]},"end-plea":{id:"end-plea",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Rourke pleads guilty on day seven to a reduced charge \u2014 misrepresentation rather than fraud. The distinction matters legally; in practice the outcome is similar. He pays the civil award, loses his practising certificate, and receives a suspended sentence. The plea saves months of further proceedings and gives Hartley a faster resolution. Rourke says, in his allocution, that he was following instructions. The judge says: that is not a defence. Rourke says: I understand that now.",choices:[]},"end-settlement":{id:"end-settlement",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Three days before the verdict, Rourke's lawyer approaches the prosecution with a cooperation offer. Rourke will provide a full written account \u2014 Beaumont's explicit instructions, the restoration details, the staging of Carew's viewing \u2014 in exchange for the criminal charges being reduced to misrepresentation. The prosecution accepts. The written account is thorough, public record, and more damning in its detail than a guilty verdict would have been. Rourke loses his certificate. Hartley is paid. The painting is honestly documented. The record is complete.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-healer",packId:"medieval",title:"The Healer",subtitle:"She cures what the physicians cannot. Now they want her burned.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You are the steward of a northern manor. Your lord's wife has brought in a village woman who cured three people the physicians gave up on. The Church has sent an inquisitor. The physicians are furious. The woman herself seems unconcerned. You have three days to resolve this before it becomes something no one can stop.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maret of the lower village has been staying in the manor for two weeks. She cured the miller's fever when the physicians could not, saved a child from the coughing sickness, and has now been asked to attend Lord Aldric himself, who has been bedridden with a joint ailment for four months. The physicians call her a fraud. Brother Cassius of the Church's inquiry office calls her dangerous. Lady Aldric calls her a blessing. You are Edmund, steward of this house, and it is your job to keep it from burning.",choices:[{label:"Speak with Maret herself before anything else.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Talk to the physicians. Understand their complaint precisely.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Find Brother Cassius and determine what he actually intends.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"manor-garden",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maret is in the herb garden, which she has apparently taken to tending. She's perhaps forty, practical in manner, unbothered by the attention around her. You ask how she cures what the physicians cannot. She says: 'I watch carefully and I use what works. The physicians use what their books say.' She says it without malice. She's not trying to be dangerous. She might be anyway.",choices:[{label:"She watches and uses what works. That's her method. Understand what threatens about it.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The senior physician, Dr. Harwick, is furious in the controlled way of a man who knows fury is beneath his dignity. He says: 'She has no training, no license, and no authorization to practice medicine.' He says: 'What she does cannot be explained, which means it is either fraud or something worse.' He says the word 'worse' with the emphasis of a man who wants you to draw the implication yourself.",choices:[{label:"'Something worse' means the Church. Find out how far the physicians have taken this.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Brother Cassius is precise and reasonable in manner, which is more alarming than if he were fanatical. He says he has received a complaint from the physicians' guild and is conducting a standard inquiry. He says: 'I am not here to cause harm. I am here to determine whether harm is being caused.' He asks if he may speak with the woman. You say you will arrange it. You buy yourself time without agreeing to anything.",choices:[{label:"You bought time. Use it to understand the situation fully.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The situation in three parts: Maret has cured three people using methods the physicians consider irregular. The physicians have complained to the Church. Brother Cassius is conducting an inquiry that could go several ways depending on what he finds. Lord Aldric wants Maret to treat his ailment. Lady Aldric wants her protected. You need to understand each of these forces clearly.",choices:[{label:"Find out what Maret actually does. Observe a treatment if possible.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Research what the Church's inquiry process can and cannot do.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Speak with the three people she healed. Their testimony is the most direct evidence.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"village",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maret treats a laborer with an infected wound \u2014 the kind that often turns to putrefaction. She cleans it with a solution she prepares from plants you can't identify, wraps it with treated cloth, and instructs the man in how to change the dressing. She makes no invocations, speaks no words of power. It's methodical and careful. If this is sorcery, it's the most mundane sorcery you've ever seen.",choices:[{label:"Methodical and mundane. The practice is medical, not mystical. Document this.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Church's inquiry process for suspected irregular healing: it can recommend censure, prohibition from practice, or \u2014 in extreme cases \u2014 formal charges. It cannot proceed to extreme measures without evidence of deliberate heresy. What it can do, even without such evidence, is create enough uncertainty that a lord might dismiss a healer to avoid the trouble. That is probably the actual goal.",choices:[{label:"The goal may be dismissal through inconvenience, not conviction. That changes the approach.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"village",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The miller, the child's mother, and a former patient all speak with you. The miller says: 'She knew which herb would reduce the fever when the physicians had tried three treatments.' The mother says: 'She sat with the child for two nights and kept the airway clear.' The former patient says: 'She told me to stop eating the preserved meat, which was what was causing the sickness.' Observation, attention, and practical knowledge. Not mystery.",choices:[{label:"Observation, attention, practical knowledge. These are the tools. Build the case.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You have a clearer picture. Maret uses herbal knowledge and careful observation \u2014 not mysticism. The physicians' complaint is about guild authority, not genuine concern about harm. The Church inquiry is a tool being used for a professional dispute. Lord Aldric is ill and wants her help. Lady Aldric is determined to protect her.",choices:[{label:"Meet with Brother Cassius. Present what you've observed.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Meet with Harwick. Find what it would take to satisfy the physicians.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Arrange for Lord Aldric to receive Maret's treatment. His recovery is the best argument.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Brother Cassius listens to your account with the patience of someone who has heard many accounts. You describe what you observed: the methodology, the plant materials, the lack of any invocation. He says: 'Unauthorized practice of medicine is a separate matter from heresy.' He says: 'My inquiry is specifically whether she uses forbidden means.' He pauses. 'Based on what you describe, she does not appear to.' He says: 'But I must ask her myself.'",choices:[{label:"He's distinguishing medical practice from heresy. Arrange the meeting carefully.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Harwick says: 'We do not object to folk remedies that fall within the guild's list of permitted treatments.' He produces the list. Several of Maret's treatments are on it. Some are not. He says: 'If she confines herself to permitted treatments, we have no complaint.' You say: 'What about the treatments that saved lives and aren't on your list?' He says: 'That is precisely the problem.'",choices:[{label:"The problem is the list, not the outcomes. Find a way around the list.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"manor-chamber",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lord Aldric receives Maret. She examines his joints carefully, asks questions about his diet and sleep, prepares a poultice and a tea from the herb garden. He is skeptical but willing \u2014 four months of physician's treatment has done nothing. Three days later he reports reduced pain. A week later he walks the great hall for the first time in months. His recovery is witnessed by everyone in the manor.",choices:[{label:"The lord walking is witnessed by everyone. Use this.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The situation has shifted. Cassius has a clearer picture of Maret's practice. Harwick has been shown that the list is inadequate. Lord Aldric is recovering. But none of these things individually resolves the formal complaint, which is still pending with the inquiry office.",choices:[{label:"Arrange the formal interview between Cassius and Maret. Control the setting.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Find a physician willing to examine Maret's methods and issue a formal opinion.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Approach the guild's senior authority above Harwick. The complaint came from below \u2014 go above.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The interview in the manor hall, before witnesses. Cassius asks Maret about her methods. She answers simply and directly \u2014 the plants she uses, why she uses them, what she observes in patients that guides her choices. She says: 'I know what the Church considers forbidden. I use none of it.' She says it with the confidence of a woman who has thought about this carefully. Cassius's questions become less pointed over the course of an hour.",choices:[{label:"She answered clearly and Cassius is satisfied on the heresy question. Now the guild.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find Dr. Wren, an older physician with a different temperament than Harwick. He examines Maret's methods with genuine interest. He says: 'Several of these are consistent with treatises I have read from the eastern schools.' He says: 'The use of willow bark for joint inflammation is documented but not yet widely practiced here.' He says: 'I will provide an opinion that her methods have scholarly basis.'",choices:[{label:"Scholarly basis from a respected physician changes the guild's complaint.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"city",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The guild's senior warden receives you formally. He hears the case without emotion. Then he says: 'The complaint as filed does not meet the threshold for formal action \u2014 it is a guild dispute, not a criminal matter.' He says: 'Harwick exceeded his authority in involving the inquiry office.' He says: 'The complaint will be withdrawn.' He says it with the finality of a man who dislikes seeing his institution used for professional petty disputes.",choices:[{label:"The complaint is withdrawn. The Church inquiry loses its foundation.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The formal complaint is weakening. Cassius's inquiry is becoming harder to sustain without the guild's active complaint. Lord Aldric is improving. The testimony of three healed patients is on record. But Harwick has not accepted the outcome gracefully, and ungraceful men with guild authority can still cause trouble.",choices:[{label:"Confront Harwick directly. This needs to end formally.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Secure Lord Aldric's formal protection for Maret. A lord's word outweighs guild politics.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Find a formal status for Maret that places her within recognized structures.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Harwick, confronted in private, says what he wouldn't say publicly: 'She makes us look inadequate.' You say: 'No. She treats what you haven't treated. That is not the same as inadequacy \u2014 it is specialization.' You offer him a way to save face: a joint consultation, where Harwick and Maret together treat difficult cases. His methods and hers. He considers this longer than you expected. He says: 'That might be acceptable.'",choices:[{label:"'Might be acceptable.' He's looking for a way out. Give it to him.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"manor-chamber",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lord Aldric, walking again for the first time in months, grants Maret formal protection as manor healer \u2014 a recognized role that places her under the lord's authority and beyond the guild's jurisdiction for treatments within the manor's lands. He does it with the satisfaction of a man who has found a solution that is both just and satisfying.",choices:[{label:"Manor healer. A recognized role. The formal complaint has nowhere to go.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"city",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find a precedent: some monastic communities have recognized lay healers operating under ecclesiastical sanction. Brother Cassius, presented with this precedent, becomes thoughtful. He says: 'If she were to operate under the sanction of the local priory, my inquiry would close.' He says: 'This is not unheard of.' You take this to Maret. She says: 'Would I have to become a nun?' You say: 'No.' She says: 'Then I'll consider it.'",choices:[{label:"Ecclesiastical sanction as a lay healer. A recognized structure.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The path forward is visible. Maret's practice can be formalized \u2014 under the lord's protection, under ecclesiastical sanction, or through a working arrangement with the physicians that acknowledges her methods. The formal complaint from Harwick is losing force. Cassius is nearly satisfied on the heresy question. The crisis is passing.",choices:[{label:"Formalize the arrangement before Harwick can change his mind.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Get Cassius's formal closure of the inquiry first \u2014 that's the greatest threat.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Speak with Maret about what she actually wants. She hasn't been asked.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The formalization happens quickly: Lord Aldric's grant of manor healer status, witnessed and recorded, with a letter to the guild acknowledging Maret's role within the manor's authority. Harwick receives the letter with the expression of a man swallowing something unpleasant. He doesn't contest it. The formal arrangement is done before he can gather himself.",choices:[{label:"Done before he can contest it. The arrangement holds.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Cassius closes the inquiry formally with a written finding: 'No evidence of forbidden practice. Methods consistent with natural herbal knowledge.' He writes it carefully and it is, once recorded, irreversible. Harwick's complaint \u2014 which rested partly on the inquiry's legitimacy \u2014 loses its foundation without the Church's active involvement.",choices:[{label:"Inquiry closed, finding recorded. The Church is done with this.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"manor-garden",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maret, in her garden, listens to the options you've assembled: lord's protection, ecclesiastical sanction, joint working arrangement with the physicians. She says: 'Which keeps me most free to do the work?' You say honestly: 'The lord's protection.' She says: 'Then that one.' She goes back to her planting. The crisis resolves itself around her while she tends her herbs.",choices:[{label:"The crisis resolves around her while she tends her herbs. Perfect.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The formal threat is resolved. Maret has protection. Cassius is satisfied or nearly so. Harwick is defeated or accommodated. Lord Aldric is improving. Lady Aldric is pleased. You have one remaining concern: Harwick's wounded professional pride can still generate future trouble, even if the current crisis is closed.",choices:[{label:"Arrange the joint consultation Harwick mentioned. Give him a role.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Leave Harwick to his wounded pride. The formal arrangement is sufficient.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Find out if Maret would be willing to share her knowledge with a willing physician.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Harwick agrees to the joint consultation arrangement with less resistance than you expected. You think he's been embarrassed by Lord Aldric's recovery and is looking for any way to participate in the success rather than be defined by his opposition to it. The joint consultation begins the following month. Harwick watches Maret's methods with the focus of a man studying something he knows he doesn't understand.",choices:[{label:"Studying something he doesn't understand. That's better than opposing it.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Harwick's wounded pride is a future concern but a manageable one. He will complain to his colleagues and write letters to the guild and be a professional nuisance for years. That is preferable to the alternative. The formal arrangement is sufficient and the crisis is closed. Let him be wounded.",choices:[{label:"Let him be wounded. The arrangement is secure.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"manor-garden",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maret says: 'I'll teach anyone who genuinely wants to learn.' She pauses. 'Is Harwick someone who genuinely wants to learn?' You say honestly: 'No. But Dr. Wren might be.' She considers this. She says: 'Then bring Wren.' Wren comes the following week and the session between them lasts four hours. Wren takes extensive notes and calls it the most useful afternoon of his recent career.",choices:[{label:"Wren is a genuine student. The knowledge spreads through the right channel.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A month after the crisis. The manor is calm. Lord Aldric is walking the grounds. Maret has treated six more patients from the surrounding villages. Cassius has returned to his office with a closed file. Harwick is maintaining a dignified silence or an irritated one, depending on who you ask. You have done your job.",choices:[{label:"Think about what the crisis revealed about the systems involved.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Maret's long-term situation still needs watching. She's vulnerable.",next:"b8b"},{label:"Lord Aldric's recovery is the story everyone will remember.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"What the crisis revealed: the physicians' guild protects its authority more than its patients. The Church's inquiry process can be deployed as a tool in professional disputes. A lord's protection is real and substantial. And practical effectiveness \u2014 what actually works \u2014 eventually defeats institutional resistance when someone is willing to do the slow patient work of making the case.",choices:[{label:"Slow patient work. That's what stewardship is.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maret's long-term vulnerability: she depends on the lord's protection, which is only as good as Lord Aldric's health and goodwill. She has no license, no guild standing, no institutional backing beyond his word. If he dies or the manor changes hands, the protection evaporates. You note this and begin, quietly, to think about how to build her something more durable.",choices:[{label:"Something more durable. A longer-term project.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lord Aldric walking the grounds is the story everyone will remember. The servants, the tenants, the visiting merchants \u2014 they all saw the lord who couldn't stand, standing. They know Maret did it. Whatever Harwick says in the future will run against that memory. Memory is a form of protection too.",choices:[{label:"Memory as protection. She's safer than any document could make her.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three months after the crisis began. The manor is stable. You're working on a longer-term arrangement \u2014 something that doesn't depend solely on Lord Aldric's continued health. Lady Aldric has taken an interest; she corresponds with two other noble households about their own healers. Maret herself seems entirely unconcerned with the larger picture. She's in the garden.",choices:[{label:"The larger picture needs someone to manage it. That's you.",next:"b9a"},{label:"The correspondence Lady Aldric is building could become a network of protected healers.",next:"b9b"},{label:"Ask Maret what she actually wants her life to look like in five years.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Managing the larger picture: you draft a formal charter for the role of manor healer, with succession provisions \u2014 so that if Lord Aldric dies, the charter binds his heir. You have the lord sign it while he's well and walking and in a good mood. Lady Aldric witnesses it. It's not a perfect protection but it's more durable than a verbal arrangement.",choices:[{label:"Durable charter. The protection survives the lord if not forever.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lady Aldric's correspondence is building something useful. Three households with similar situations \u2014 healers working outside guild structures, protected by noble authority \u2014 are now in communication. Information is being shared about which plants work for which conditions. Knowledge is spreading through a network of practical people who have found what works. This is not a reform movement. It's just people solving problems.",choices:[{label:"People solving problems. The best kind of network.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"manor-garden",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maret thinks about it for a moment. She says: 'I want to keep doing this. I want to teach someone who will do it after me. I want not to be burned.' She says the last part with the slight humor of someone who has considered it seriously. She says: 'Beyond that, I don't have particular ambitions.' She says: 'I'm already doing what I want to do.' You think this might be the most useful information you've received in three months.",choices:[{label:"She knows what she wants. Secure it for her.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"manor-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Six months after you first had to deal with this crisis. Maret is established, protected, and treating patients across the manor's lands. Harwick has accepted a grudging equilibrium. Cassius's inquiry is formally closed. Lord Aldric is healthy enough to manage his own affairs again. The charter is signed. The knowledge is spreading. You have done your job well.",choices:[{label:"The job well done. Think about what it cost and what it bought.",next:"end-cost"},{label:"Maret teaching an apprentice. That's the long game.",next:"end-teaching"},{label:"The network Lady Aldric built is the most valuable thing that came from this.",next:"end-network"},{label:"Lord Aldric healthy. That's the immediate and lasting win.",next:"end-lord"}]},"end-cost":{id:"end-cost",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"What it cost: three weeks of careful negotiation, several political debts called in, one confrontation that needed to happen, and the slow work of building a case that was always stronger than its opponents' arguments. What it bought: a woman who cures people continues to cure people; a man who couldn't walk walks; and the gap between what the guild's list allows and what actually helps human beings has been acknowledged, at least in one manor, as a gap worth crossing. Not a revolution. A specific victory in a specific place. Sometimes that's the best that good work can do.",choices:[]},"end-teaching":{id:"end-teaching",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Maret's apprentice is a girl from the lower village, twelve years old, who has been following her around since the beginning. Maret teaches her the plants, the methods, the observations. In fifteen years, the girl will be the one the manor turns to when the physicians fail. In forty years, she may teach someone else. The knowledge moves forward because one woman was allowed to practice it and had the sense to share it. The system didn't plan this. It was a side effect of not burning someone who was good at what she did.",choices:[]},"end-network":{id:"end-network",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Lady Aldric's correspondence grows into something neither she nor you expected: a loose but real network of noble households protecting unconventional healers. Within ten years it is large enough that the physicians' guild cannot ignore it. Within twenty years, several of the treatments Maret used are on the guild's permitted list. The origin of that change is not a law or a decree but a series of letters written by a noblewoman who didn't want her friend burned.",choices:[]},"end-lord":{id:"end-lord",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Lord Aldric lives twelve more years after Maret begins his treatment. He never loses the joint mobility she restored. He takes a walk every morning, which the servants come to expect and plan around. He pays Maret well and speaks of her without qualification as the best healer he has encountered, which, given his experience of physicians over four decades, means something. When he dies he leaves a provision in his will that the manor healer role is protected for the charter's term. He thought of it himself. Maret, told of the provision, says: 'That's very sensible.' She goes back to her garden.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-informant",packId:"detective",title:"The Informant",subtitle:"He walked in with everything. That's the problem.",length:"Short",free:!0,brief:"A stranger named Finch arrives with a complete murder case against a property developer. Someone assembled it deliberately. Your job is to find what's real and who's hiding behind it.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The man sits across from you without being invited. His name is Finch. He says a property developer named Voss killed a man named Renwick six months ago and the police closed the case wrong. He has a folder: meeting logs, financial records, a witness statement, a photograph of Voss's car outside Renwick's building. It is the most complete unsolicited tip you have ever received. That is exactly what worries you.",choices:[{label:"Take the folder and read it carefully before you respond.",next:"s2"},{label:"Push the folder back. Ask who sent him before you touch anything.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Accept it and ask how an accountant assembled a case file this thorough.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Finch says nobody sent him. He says he has been sitting on this for six months because every official door stayed closed. You study him. His hands are still. His story is practiced but not false. The grief for Renwick, when it surfaces, has the texture of something real. You take the folder.",choices:[{label:"Open the folder and go through it systematically.",next:"s2"},{label:"Ask him to wait outside while you read.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Finch says Renwick was his friend. He has been gathering this for six months because he had nothing else to do with the grief. He knows about the financial dispute \u2014 Renwick was going to expose Voss's falsified planning applications. He says this too smoothly. You take the folder and tell him to wait.",choices:[{label:"Read the folder.",next:"s2"},{label:"Read the folder, but note the smoothness in your head.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The folder is meticulous \u2014 meeting logs, shell company filings, a witness statement. And one thing that doesn't fit: a name circled in pencil in one document's margin. Aldrich. No explanation. Finch didn't mention Aldrich.",choices:[{label:"Ask Finch who Aldrich is while he's still here.",next:"s3"},{label:"Say nothing to Finch. Check the name yourself.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Verify the witness first \u2014 that's the easiest claim to test.",next:"b2b"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Gerald Aldrich. Property developer, northern corridor, primary competitor of Voss. Finch worked at a firm acquired by Aldrich's holding company three years ago. Finch is Aldrich's employee. He didn't mention this.",choices:[{label:"Confront Finch with the Aldrich connection now.",next:"s3"},{label:"Say nothing yet. Let him think you don't know.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"detective-street",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The witness, Clare Dowd, is home when you arrive. She's nervous before you've said anything. She gives you the statement from the folder nearly word for word. Someone coached her. Someone who knew what was in that folder.",choices:[{label:"Ask who coached her.",next:"s3"},{label:"Note it and go back to Finch with this.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You put it to Finch: Aldrich is his employer. He doesn't flinch. He says Aldrich funded the investigation, yes, but Voss did kill Renwick. He knows because Renwick called him two nights before his death. Renwick said he was going to confront Voss. He asked Finch if he was doing the right thing. Finch said yes. Then Renwick died.",choices:[{label:"Ask what Renwick said in that call \u2014 every detail.",next:"s4"},{label:"There's a phone record for that call. Verify it exists.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Finch is still being used by Aldrich. Find out what Aldrich actually wants.",next:"b3b"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The call is real \u2014 Renwick called Finch at 9:47pm two nights before his death. Eleven minutes. You also find Renwick called Voss's number at 10:08pm the same evening. Four minutes. And three days earlier, a call to Gerald Aldrich's mobile. Renwick was talking to both men in his final days.",choices:[{label:"Ask Finch what Renwick said in their call.",next:"s4"},{label:"Focus on why Renwick called Aldrich three days before he died.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You dig into Aldrich. He and Voss have been competing for the same planning approvals for four years. Renwick worked at the planning office that approved both men's applications. If Renwick was going to expose Voss, it would help Aldrich enormously. It would also expose Aldrich's own identical financial structure, if anyone looked.",choices:[{label:"Find what Renwick was actually working on at the planning office.",next:"s4"},{label:"Go back to Finch. He needs to know about Aldrich's exposure too.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Through a contact you get Renwick's planning office files. He had documented corruption in two applications \u2014 Voss's and Aldrich's. He hadn't reported either yet, waiting until both were complete. The document naming both men is dated four days before his death. Finch's folder contained no mention of the Aldrich application. The folder was curated to show you only one direction.",choices:[{label:"Find someone at the planning office who knew Renwick was working on both.",next:"s5"},{label:"Take this to the detective who closed the case \u2014 Morris.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Confront Finch: he either knew about the Aldrich file or he was used.",next:"b4b"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"DC Morris was transferred two weeks after closing the Renwick case. He looks like a man who has made peace with something. He says quietly: someone above me wanted it closed. Not pressure. A directive. He says he closed it clean and that whatever's in the file is accurate. He says: find the drive. Renwick had an encrypted drive. It went into evidence and never came back.",choices:[{label:"Ask who gave the directive.",next:"s5"},{label:"The drive is the priority. Where does evidence go when it disappears?",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Finch listens as you lay out the Aldrich planning file. His face changes \u2014 something that looks like genuine surprise and then something darker. He says: Aldrich told me the case was purely about Voss. He says: if Renwick was going to expose Aldrich too, then Aldrich had full motive. He says it with the dawning expression of someone realising they've been used.",choices:[{label:"Whether Finch knew or not, Renwick had two enemies. Find out which one acted.",next:"s5"},{label:"Ask Finch to tell you everything Aldrich said when he agreed to fund this.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A woman named Holt worked with Renwick eight years. She tells you he was meticulous and never shared work until finished. The week he died he was using an encrypted drive she hadn't seen before. She reported this after his death. Her supervisor said it wasn't relevant. That supervisor is on the approval list for one of Aldrich's planning applications.",choices:[{label:"The supervisor-Aldrich link is the thread. Pull it.",next:"s6"},{label:"Find the encrypted drive Renwick was using.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Ask Holt if Renwick said anything about who he was worried about.",next:"b5b"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Morris took the drive. You find him in his flat holding it like he's been deciding what to do with it for months. He says: I've been waiting for someone who wasn't sent by Aldrich. He hands it over. On the drive: Renwick's full report \u2014 both names \u2014 and a voice memo recorded an hour before he died.",choices:[{label:"Listen to the memo.",next:"s6"},{label:"Take the drive and Morris's story directly to the confrontation.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Holt says Renwick told her two weeks before he died that he was scared, but not of Voss. He said: the one I'm worried about thinks he's untouchable. She says he never named the person. But he said untouchable, and Voss was a man who knew exactly where the limits were.",choices:[{label:"Aldrich is the one who thinks he's untouchable. Find the drive and confront him.",next:"s6"},{label:"Go to Voss first. He spoke to Renwick last.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renwick's voice, on the drive, recorded an hour before he died. He says: I have finished the report. Both names. I am going to submit it Monday. He says: if something happens to me, assume Aldrich is responsible, not Voss. Voss's file he can survive. Aldrich's he cannot. He says it with the calm of someone stating a fact they hope won't be needed. It was needed.",choices:[{label:"Take the recording to Voss \u2014 let him hear it.",next:"s7"},{label:"Take the recording to Aldrich. Watch what he does.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Find Voss first and get his version of events before using the recording.",next:"b6b"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Aldrich receives you in a glass building. You play the recording. The room goes quiet. He says: where did you get that. He says it without asking who Renwick was or denying anything about the file. You say it doesn't matter. He says: what do you want. He says it the way someone says it when they're calculating what they can offer.",choices:[{label:"You want a name. Tell him to give you who went to Renwick's flat.",next:"s7"},{label:"You want him on record. Tell him you want a formal statement.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Voss is smaller than you expected and when you play the recording he looks tired. He says: I know. He says Renwick called him that evening and told him the same thing \u2014 that both files were going in Monday, and this was Voss's chance to get ahead of it. He says: I spent the weekend with lawyers. He says: Aldrich couldn't say the same.",choices:[{label:"Ask Voss to go on record with everything he just told you.",next:"s7"},{label:"You have enough. Go directly to confront Aldrich.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Aldrich's lawyer arrives seven minutes after you sit down. In those seven minutes: you play the recording, you put Renwick's report on the table with both names, you mention the coached witness and the phone number tracing to Regent Financial, you mention Fenn's Portuguese property. Aldrich's expression doesn't break but something leaves it. He says, very quietly, one sentence before the lawyer walks in. A name. Not his own.",choices:[{label:"Get out and find the person whose name he gave you.",next:"s8"},{label:"Stay. Wait for the lawyer to arrive and make the most of the formal setting.",next:"b7a"},{label:"You have what you need. Go \u2014 the name and everything else.",next:"b7b"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Aldrich's lawyer, Pryce, is expensive and gives nothing away. But in the formal meeting that follows, Aldrich makes one mistake: he confirms, through a careful non-denial, that he knew about both planning files before Renwick died. That is not the statement of a man who found out after. Pryce shuts it down. But you have it noted.",choices:[{label:"Take the non-denial and everything else. Now assemble the full case.",next:"s8"},{label:"Get the name Aldrich gave you and find that person first.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You write up everything in the car outside Aldrich's building. Renwick's report, the recording, Morris's statement, the coached witness, the financial connections, the call records, Voss's account, the name Aldrich just gave you. It is a complete case. The question is what to do with it.",choices:[{label:"Take it to a detective inspector outside the compromised chain.",next:"s8"},{label:"Organise it into three packages for police, oversight, and press simultaneously.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You spend a day getting it right. Renwick's full report. The voice recording. Morris's statement about the cover-up. The coached witness and her retraction. The Aldrich-Finch connection. Fenn's pension. The building access records if you have them. You make three copies. You write a two-page summary a detective, an oversight officer, or a journalist could follow without missing anything. You seal the third copy and address it to a journalist whose name Renwick's daughter gave you \u2014 in case the first two disappear.",choices:[{label:"Take the first copy to a senior detective outside the chain.",next:"s9"},{label:"Take the first copy to the planning oversight board.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Give the first copy to the journalist. Public pressure first.",next:"b8b"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The oversight board's senior investigator reads the two-page summary in front of you and says: this is sufficient to open a formal audit. She says it with the precision of someone who has been waiting for a case this clean. She also says: I'll notify the police. She means a different part of the police than Fenn's chain. She means the part that investigates the other part.",choices:[{label:"Leave the full package and let the process run.",next:"s9"},{label:"Ask what happens to the murder case specifically.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The journalist reads it carefully and then says: give me forty-eight hours and don't talk to anyone else. She knows what she has. You give her forty-eight hours. The story runs on a Thursday and by afternoon Aldrich's lawyers are sending letters to publications across the city. Which means the story is accurate.",choices:[{label:"The press route is running. Now take the package to the police too.",next:"s9"},{label:"Let the press do its work. The investigation will follow.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"detective-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The investigation reopens formally. A detective inspector from a different division takes the lead. Aldrich's lawyers file injunctions that slow things down for three weeks, then stop when the voice recording is ruled admissible. You are asked to give evidence at a preliminary hearing. You give it. Afterwards, the detective inspector shakes your hand and says: this is a good file. That is significant praise from a detective inspector.",choices:[{label:"Ask what happens to Finch \u2014 he was used, but he was also part of the deception.",next:"s10"},{label:"Ask about the murder charge specifically. Will it stick?",next:"b9a"},{label:"Ask if Morris will be reinstated.",next:"b9b"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The detective inspector says: the voice recording establishes motive and Renwick's own assessment of the threat. The name Aldrich gave you, if it leads where it looks like it leads, may establish more. She says: the planning fraud conviction is certain. The murder charge depends on what we find at the end of that name. She says: don't go looking for it yourself.",choices:[{label:"You agree. Let it run.",next:"s10"},{label:"You do not agree, but you tell her you do.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Morris is reinstated three months later with a note in his file that his judgment was sound. He sends you a card that says only: good work. From Morris, that is a considerable statement. Fenn's Portuguese property is flagged for investigation. His pension is under review.",choices:[{label:"Good. Now wait for the outcome.",next:"s10"},{label:"Ask what's happening with Finch.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The case runs for eight months. Finch calls you once, two months in. He says he didn't know about Aldrich's planning exposure. He says he thinks he's been a fool. You say: Renwick's case was real even if the way you brought it to me wasn't. He asks how it ends. You tell him you don't know yet. The planning fraud conviction is certain. The murder charge depends on the name Aldrich gave you and where it leads.",choices:[{label:"Tell Finch the truth: the outcome isn't clean but it's real.",next:"end-win"},{label:"Wait for the verdict before you say anything.",next:"end-slow"},{label:"The case ends before the murder charge resolves. Accept the partial outcome.",next:"end-neutral"},{label:"The person Aldrich named is found. The murder charge proceeds.",next:"end-full"}]},"end-win":{id:"end-win",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The planning fraud convictions come through solid. Aldrich loses his development approvals. The murder investigation reaches the person whose name he gave you \u2014 a man who has since returned from abroad and who, presented with the evidence, chooses cooperation over trial. Aldrich is charged. The trial takes six months. The verdict isn't what you'd call satisfying \u2014 manslaughter, not murder, on the grounds of what his lawyer calls 'direction at a distance' \u2014 but it is a verdict. Renwick's daughter sends you a card. It says: thank you for not stopping. You keep it on your desk.",choices:[]},"end-slow":{id:"end-slow",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The case takes longer than anyone said. The planning fraud conviction comes through in month four. The murder investigation is still running at month eight when you stop tracking it daily. At month eleven, you see the verdict in the news: Aldrich, manslaughter, four years. The journalism about the case is good. Finch is described fairly. Morris is mentioned in a paragraph about the original cover-up. You read it twice and then you go back to work.",choices:[]},"end-neutral":{id:"end-neutral",scene:"ending-neutral",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The planning fraud case closes cleanly. Both Voss and Aldrich lose their approvals and face fines. The murder investigation runs for two years on the name Aldrich gave you and ends without a conviction \u2014 the person Aldrich named cannot be placed at the scene beyond reasonable doubt. The truth is established. The legal outcome is incomplete. Renwick's daughter says, when you call her: the planning case is what he cared about. The rest was for us. You think about that for a long time.",choices:[]},"end-full":{id:"end-full",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The name leads to a man who was in Renwick's building that night and whose connection to Aldrich, once established, completes the chain. The murder trial runs six months. Aldrich is convicted. His legal team appeals. The appeal fails. Fenn's pension is clawed back. Morris is reinstated. The planning oversight board uses Renwick's original report as the basis for new regulations affecting both the Voss and Aldrich cases. The work Renwick was doing when he died is the work that outlasts everyone who tried to stop it.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-last-knight",packId:"medieval",title:"The Last Knight",subtitle:"The war is over. Your sword is still drawn.",length:"Short",free:!0,brief:"The siege ended three days ago. Your lord is dead, your companions scattered, and the victorious army is deciding what to do with survivors. You are a knight without a cause, in a city that isn't yours, with a sword you're not sure what to do with anymore.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"ruined-courtyard",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three days since the gate fell. The city is occupied now, the lord's banner down, the victors' colors on every tower. You are Sir Aldric \u2014 fourth son, twenty-three years in service, one campaign too many. Your lord died at the wall. Half your companions surrendered immediately. The others are in the cells below the keep. You hid in the chapel for two days and now you are in the courtyard with your sword at your hip and no particular plan.",choices:[{label:"Surrender formally. Find the commander and offer your sword.",next:"s2"},{label:"Find out what happened to your companions in the cells before deciding anything.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Leave the city. The war is over. Find somewhere else.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"ruined-courtyard",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find a guard who will talk. Your companions \u2014 four of them \u2014 are in the lower cells, alive, awaiting the commander's decision on their fate. Common soldiers are being released. Knights are a different matter. Knights have names and families and ransom value. And sometimes, knights are made examples of.",choices:[{label:"Your companions are alive and waiting. Do something that helps them.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"city-gate",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The city gate is guarded. Two soldiers who don't know your face. You could probably talk your way out \u2014 a merchant, a priest, a servant. You've done harder things in the field. But leaving without knowing what happens to your companions sits wrong. You turn back.",choices:[{label:"You can leave but not yet. Find out what's happening first.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The hall where your lord once held court now belongs to Commander Varis of the eastern force \u2014 a man you've heard of, competent and pragmatic, not known for cruelty but not known for leniency either. He's dealing with the immediate problems of occupation: food supply, preventing looting, deciding what to do with two hundred prisoners. He doesn't know you're free. You could approach him openly or use the anonymity.",choices:[{label:"Approach openly. Surrender formally and negotiate from honesty.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Use the anonymity to learn more before presenting yourself.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Find a way to speak with your companions in the cells first.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You present yourself to Varis's adjutant as Sir Aldric, knight of the late lord's household, surrendering formally. The adjutant looks at you with the mild surprise of someone encountering unexpected honesty. He says: 'The commander is occupied. You'll wait.' He doesn't put you in a cell. He gives you a chair in the anteroom.",choices:[{label:"A chair in the anteroom is better than a cell. Wait and learn what you can.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Anonymity in a chaotic occupation is surprisingly easy. You're a man with a sword and a plain face in a hall full of armed men. You listen. Varis is pragmatic \u2014 he's releasing common soldiers, holding knights for ransom decisions, and trying to manage the city's food supply before winter. He's also short of competent administrators. The occupation is messy.",choices:[{label:"He's short of competent administrators. File that away.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"prison-cells",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The cells are in the lower keep. You bribe a guard with the last of your coin and get five minutes with your companions. Sir Deren says: 'They'll ransom the names they know. My family will pay.' Sir Mira says: 'I have no family.' She says it without self-pity, like a tactical assessment. Her situation is the one to solve.",choices:[{label:"Mira has no family. She'll need a different solution. Find it.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You're beginning to understand the shape of the occupation. Varis needs practical help. Your companions need either ransom or a different fate. The city needs to survive the winter. Somewhere in that tangle there is a path that gets your companions out and possibly keeps you alive in a way that means something.",choices:[{label:"Offer Varis your service. You know this city. He doesn't.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Find someone with authority over the prisoner decisions. Not Varis \u2014 someone more accessible.",next:"b3b"},{label:"The food supply problem is the city's crisis. Solving it buys goodwill.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Varis receives you eventually. He's not unkind. He says: 'You surrendered freely. That earns some consideration.' You offer what you know: the city's storage cellars, the maintenance schedule for the water system, the personalities of the merchant leaders whose cooperation he'll need for winter. He listens. He says: 'And what do you want?' You tell him about Mira.",choices:[{label:"He heard the offer and the ask. Now wait for his response.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Varis has a quartermaster \u2014 a capable, tired woman named Hess who is managing the actual logistics of the occupation. She's the one making practical decisions while Varis handles the political ones. You find her and offer your knowledge of the city's stores, the water system, the seasonal supply chains. She looks at you with the expression of someone who has found an unexpected resource. She says: 'Tell me about the north granary.'",choices:[{label:"Tell her about the north granary. Make yourself useful.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"market-district",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The city's food supply relies on three guild merchants who controlled the distribution network. You know all three \u2014 you've dealt with them on your lord's behalf for years. You go to the wealthiest one and make a simple argument: the occupation is going to be here until spring. Either cooperate with Varis now or lose your business by March. He listens. He's a merchant. He calculates.",choices:[{label:"The merchant calculates. So do you. Bring this to Varis as a gift.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"However you've approached it, you've made yourself useful to the occupation and established some standing with Varis or his people. Your companions are still in the cells. The decisions about their fate are coming \u2014 Varis is clearing the prisoner question this week.",choices:[{label:"Make a formal case for Mira's freedom directly to Varis.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Find out if Mira would accept entering Varis's service as a condition of release.",next:"b4b"},{label:"The ransom solution works for Deren. Focus your efforts on Mira and one other.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Varis hears your case for Mira \u2014 a knight without family, without ransom value, who fought well and was captured honorably. He says: 'What do you want for her?' You say: 'Her freedom and her sword.' He looks at you steadily. He says: 'That's a significant ask.' He says: 'What are you offering that you haven't already given me?'",choices:[{label:"He wants more. Offer what you have left.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"prison-cells",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mira, in the cell, listens to the offer: freedom in exchange for a period of service to the occupying force. She says: 'How long?' You say: 'Unknown \u2014 I'm still negotiating.' She says: 'Is this your idea or theirs?' You say: 'Mine.' She thinks about it for the length of a breath. She says: 'If the alternative is execution, yes. If the alternative is ransom I can't pay, yes.'",choices:[{label:"She'll consider it. Take it to Varis.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You triage: Deren's family will pay ransom. The two younger knights have family connections that can be leveraged. Mira is the problem. You focus everything on Mira and ask Hess \u2014 who you've been helping with logistics \u2014 if she would advocate for one prisoner release to Varis. Hess says: 'What would this knight do if free?' You say: 'Whatever needs doing. She's competent and she keeps her word.'",choices:[{label:"Hess is considering it. Give her what she needs to advocate.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The negotiation for your companions' fates has reached its critical point. You've spent political capital, knowledge, and whatever goodwill you built in the last few days. Now it comes down to what Varis decides.",choices:[{label:"Make your final case directly and clearly, without games.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Use Hess as your advocate. She has Varis's practical trust.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Offer something personal \u2014 your own service, your sword, your future.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say to Varis: 'I have been useful to you and I ask one thing. Sir Mira has no family to ransom her and no cause to fight for anymore. Release her and I will ensure she troubles no one. She is a good soldier looking for honest work, not a war to re-fight.' Varis looks at you for a long moment. He says: 'And you?' You say: 'I go with her, or I stay and serve, whichever you prefer.' He thinks.",choices:[{label:"He's thinking. Wait.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Hess takes your case to Varis and presents it in the language he responds to: practical terms. A knight with no ransom value sitting in a cell costs guards. Releasing her into productive work costs nothing and gains goodwill. She also mentions \u2014 you didn't ask her to \u2014 that your knowledge of the city has already saved the occupation significant difficulty with the supply chain. Varis makes his decision.",choices:[{label:"Hess made the case in the right language. Wait for the outcome.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You offer your own service \u2014 your sword, your knowledge, your loyalty to the occupation for a defined period \u2014 in exchange for Mira's freedom. Varis says: 'You're already offering useful service. What's different about this?' You say: 'This is formal. I'm asking you to hold me to it.' He understands the difference. A formal oath from a knight means something specific in his world.",choices:[{label:"He understands what a formal oath means. Wait.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Varis makes his decision. Deren and the others with family will be ransomed in the normal way \u2014 that was never in question. Mira's situation, he has considered. He says: 'I will release this knight to your custody, Sir Aldric. She will leave the city within two days and not return while my force occupies it. You will be held responsible for any breach.' He pauses. 'And you?'",choices:[{label:"Ask to leave with Mira. Your work here is done.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Offer to stay in service. You're useful here and you know it.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Tell him you need a day to decide.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Varis says: 'You have been useful. That earns you the same terms as your companion.' He pauses. 'Leave the city. If the situation changes and I need your knowledge again, I will send for you. Do not make me regret this courtesy.' You say: 'I won't.' You mean it.",choices:[{label:"Two days to leave. Go get Mira.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Varis looks at you with the measured assessment of a commander evaluating an offer. He says: 'For how long?' You say: 'Through winter. Until the occupation is stable and the city doesn't need me.' He says: 'And your companion?' You say: 'She leaves. I stay. That was always the trade.' He nods once. 'Done.'",choices:[{label:"Done. You're staying. Mira goes free.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"occupied-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Varis gives you until morning. You spend the evening with your companions \u2014 Deren cheerful about his ransom, the others relieved, Mira quiet. She says: 'Whatever you're deciding, I'll make it work.' She says it without knowing what the decision is. That's Mira: she makes things work with what's given to her.",choices:[{label:"She makes things work. Decide before morning.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"city-gate",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Whatever you decided, you find yourself at the city gate with Mira. She carries her sword again \u2014 Varis returned it. She says: 'Where are we going?' If you're leaving together, she asks with the interest of someone who genuinely doesn't know. If you're sending her alone, she asks it differently \u2014 like a soldier receiving orders.",choices:[{label:"Going east. You've heard there's work for capable people in the eastern towns.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Mira goes east. You're staying.",next:"b7b"},{label:"You don't know yet. Walk out the gate and see what the road looks like.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"road-outside",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The road east is clear and the weather is holding. Mira walks beside you and after a mile says: 'You didn't have to do that.' You say: 'I know.' She says: 'Why did you?' You think about it. You say: 'Because you're the kind of person who makes things work, and the world should have more of that.' She considers this. She says: 'That's a reasonable argument.'",choices:[{label:"A reasonable argument, she says. Good enough.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"city-gate",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mira looks at you. She says: 'You're staying.' Not a question. You say: 'Through winter.' She says: 'Good. The occupation needs someone honest.' She says it like it's obvious. She shakes your hand and walks east. You watch her until the road bends. Then you go back inside.",choices:[{label:"She walks east. You go back inside.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"road-outside",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The road outside the city goes east and west and south. You've walked dozens of roads like this, always with a destination and a cause. This is the first time in twenty-three years that you're standing at a junction without knowing which way is right. It's uncomfortable and it might also be the first truly free decision you've made in your adult life.",choices:[{label:"Free decision, genuinely free. Choose.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"road-outside",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Whatever path you've taken, you're moving. The city behind you is occupied but intact. Deren will be ransomed by spring. Mira is free. You did what could be done. Now there's the question of what comes after a war, for a knight who has done his service and doesn't know what he's for anymore.",choices:[{label:"A knight without a cause can become something else.",next:"b8a"},{label:"A winter's service is its own kind of purpose.",next:"b8b"},{label:"The road is enough for now. Purpose can come later.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"road-outside",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Something else. You've been good at following orders for twenty-three years. The skills that made you a knight \u2014 observation, judgment, the ability to make decisions under pressure \u2014 those don't go away. They can be used differently. There are roads that need someone to keep them honest. There are disputes that need someone to mediate. There is more work than there are knights, if you're willing to let 'knight' mean something broader.",choices:[{label:"Broader meanings. New work. Let it develop.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"city-service",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Winter service to Varis turns out to be genuinely useful work. The occupation is less brutal than it could be, partly because you know where the friction points are and address them before they become incidents. Varis notices. He asks you, in January, if you would stay past winter. You say you need to think about it. He says: 'Think quickly. Spring brings decisions.'",choices:[{label:"Think quickly. Spring brings decisions.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"road-outside",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The road is enough. You've been purposeful for twenty-three years and you are tired of purpose. You walk east and let the days happen. Mira is somewhere ahead of you on the same road, probably. The eastern towns have work and the work doesn't require you to be anyone in particular. That's restful, for a while.",choices:[{label:"Restful, for a while.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"road-east",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A month after the city. Wherever you've been, you're in a small market town with an inn that serves hot food and a board listing the local troubles: a dispute over water rights, a merchant claiming theft, a family that wants help recovering something taken by soldiers passing through. Small work. The kind of work that doesn't make songs.",choices:[{label:"Take the water rights dispute. It's practical and visible.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Find the family first. Soldiers taking things from families is the oldest wrong.",next:"b9b"},{label:"The merchant's theft claim is the simplest. Start small.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"market-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The water rights dispute involves two farms and a mill. The resolution is mostly about listening to both sides long enough to understand that they've been talking past each other for two seasons. You spend a day, propose a sharing arrangement, and both parties agree before sunset. The innkeeper says: 'That's the fastest that's resolved in three years.' You say: 'They both wanted it resolved. I just made it easy.'",choices:[{label:"They wanted it resolved. You made it easy. That's the work.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"market-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The family's case: a passing soldier took their eldest daughter's wedding gift \u2014 a silver clasp from her grandmother. The soldier is gone. But soldiers have supply routes, and supply routes have quartermaster records, and quartermaster records list what comes through. You track the clasp to a market two towns over, buy it back for less than it's worth to anyone but the family, and return it. The daughter looks at you like you've done something impossible. You haven't. You've just followed a trail.",choices:[{label:"Following a trail. That's a skill that travels.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"market-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The merchant's theft turns out to be an accounting error on his own part, which you discover by asking his apprentice when the merchant isn't present. You tell the merchant in terms that allow him to save face. He's grateful and embarrassed. He pays you in food and says you can stay at his warehouse if you're in town again. You accept. Small accommodations add up.",choices:[{label:"Small accommodations add up. The work continues.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"market-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A week in the market town. Small problems, solved with patience and knowledge. It doesn't make songs. But the water dispute stayed resolved and the family has the clasp back and the merchant's apprentice can add correctly now. You think about the twenty-three years of campaign and siege and command and what it all amounts to. You think it might amount to this.",choices:[{label:"The small work is enough. This is what a knight can be.",next:"end-small"},{label:"Find Mira. This kind of work is better with someone you trust.",next:"end-mira"},{label:"Return to Varis's service. The city needs what you know.",next:"end-return"},{label:"The road continues east. There is more work than there are days.",next:"end-road"}]},"end-small":{id:"end-small",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The small work. You stay in the eastern towns through winter, finding disputes to resolve and wrongs to correct that don't require battles. By spring you're known in four towns as the man with the sword who knows how to listen. It doesn't make songs. The songs are about the siege and the fall of the gate and the commander's strategy. But the water is running cleanly and the roads are safer and the family's clasp is on a bride's dress at her wedding, which her grandmother attended. The grandmother tells you, afterward, that it is the best she can say about any piece of silver: it did what it was made for.",choices:[]},"end-mira":{id:"end-mira",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You find Mira two towns east. She's already working \u2014 she found a merchant who needed escort and extended it into a longer arrangement. She has bread and a stable to sleep in. She says: 'I thought you might come this way.' You say: 'The eastern towns have work.' She says: 'They do.' She moves over on the bench and you sit down and you discuss the water dispute in the next town, which she has already heard about, and from which she has already formed an opinion. It's a good opinion. You argue about it for the better part of an hour. That's the beginning.",choices:[]},"end-return":{id:"end-return",scene:"ending-neutral",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You return to the city in spring, as Varis asked. The occupation is stable but complicated \u2014 spring brings political decisions from the distant lords who sent Varis, decisions that don't always match what the city needs. You spend another year mediating between the occupation's requirements and the city's reality. It's the most exhausting work you've ever done because it's all persuasion and no force. By the following spring you've earned the respect of both sides and the trust of neither. You think that might be the right outcome for someone doing what you're doing.",choices:[]},"end-road":{id:"end-road",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The road continues east and east and east, through towns and disputes and small wrongs and the occasional problem that requires the sword, which you use once in two years and only to stop something that would have been worse without it. The sword is still there. You're less sure it's what defines you. By the time you reach the eastern sea and can go no further, you've been called a mediator, a troubleshooter, a useful stranger, and once, by an old woman in a border town, 'the kind of man the road sends when the road knows you need him.' You think about that for a long time. You decide she might be right.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-last-witness",packId:"detective",title:"The Last Witness",subtitle:"She saw what happened. Now everyone wants her to forget.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"A woman named Nadia Voss saw a senior politician leave the scene of a hit-and-run. She came to you because the police told her she was mistaken. She isn't mistaken. And the politician's people are now watching her apartment.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Nadia Voss sits across from you and describes what she saw: a black car, a cyclist, an impact, and a man in a dark coat who got out, looked at the cyclist, made a call, and got back in the car, which drove away. The cyclist died. The man she saw was Marcus Hale, a minister in the current government, whose face is on billboards around the city. The police took her statement and then a detective called her back to say she must be mistaken about the identification. She is not mistaken. She knows his face.",choices:[{label:"Ask her to walk you through exactly what she saw, in detail.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Ask who the detective was who told her she was mistaken.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Find out how she knows Hale's face well enough to be certain.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She was walking home, twenty meters from the junction, good light \u2014 early evening, not yet dark. The car hit the cyclist at the crossing, not fast but hard enough. The man who got out was there for forty seconds. She stood still because she was frightened. When the car left she went to the cyclist. He died before the ambulance arrived. She was the only witness.",choices:[{label:"Forty seconds, good light, twenty meters. That's a solid identification. Now find out who suppressed it.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The detective who called her back was a DS named Price. Price is in the serious crimes unit \u2014 not traffic, which is where a hit-and-run would normally go. A serious crimes detective calling a witness to walk back a traffic incident identification is not routine. Someone moved the case.",choices:[{label:"DS Price and the moved case. Find out who moved it and why.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She knows Hale's face because she works two days a week in the constituency office of his party \u2014 not his office, a different member, but the same building. She has seen Hale in person three times. She is entirely certain. She also says: when she told the detective this, his tone changed.",choices:[{label:"She works in the building and knows him personally. The identification is solid.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The cyclist who died was named Tom Rees, thirty-one, a cycle courier. His family reported no police follow-up after the first week. The case is technically open but inactive. Nadia's statement is in the file but marked 'identification uncertain.' DS Price added that note. Hale's office has not been contacted.",choices:[{label:"Find DS Price. He knows who told him to mark the identification uncertain.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Get the traffic camera footage from the junction that night.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Talk to Tom Rees's family. They may know more than the police told them.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Price won't meet you. He sends a message through an intermediary: he's not available and the case is active. Active means someone told him to say active. You dig into Price's record and find a lateral promotion six weeks after the Rees incident \u2014 a better desk, a commendation. Price was rewarded for something around this time.",choices:[{label:"A promotion six weeks after the suppression. Follow the chain above Price.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The traffic cameras at the junction malfunctioned that evening \u2014 logged as a network failure affecting three cameras in the area, fixed two days later. Camera malfunctions happen. Three in the same area on the same evening is less common. You check the maintenance log. The failure was reported at 6:52pm. The incident occurred at 7:04pm. The malfunction preceded the incident.",choices:[{label:"Malfunction reported before the incident. Someone knew where and when.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Tom Rees's mother says the police told her it was a hit-and-run with no witnesses and no useful camera footage. She was not told about Nadia's statement. She was not told the case had an identification. She was told: we're doing everything we can. She says: I knew they weren't. She says it without bitterness, which is somehow worse.",choices:[{label:"The family wasn't told a witness existed. That's a specific suppression.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The suppression has three components: DS Price marking the identification uncertain, the family not being told a witness existed, and the camera malfunction that preceded the incident. These require coordination at a level above Price. Someone with authority over the investigation shaped what it found.",choices:[{label:"Find who Price reports to and who had oversight of the Rees case.",next:"b3a"},{label:"The camera malfunction is technical \u2014 find who could have arranged it.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Protect Nadia first. If someone is watching her apartment, she's at risk.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Price reports to a DCI named Stavros. Stavros is on the coordination committee for police liaison with the Home Office \u2014 which Hale's department sits adjacent to. The oversight relationship between Hale's ministry and this police unit is indirect but real. Stavros has the authority to direct how cases are prioritized.",choices:[{label:"Stavros connects to Hale through institutional proximity. Find what that proximity means in practice.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Camera maintenance in the city is contracted to a company called Velux Systems. You check their engineer dispatch log \u2014 the reported malfunction at those three cameras was logged by a Velux operative at 6:47pm. Five minutes before the malfunction timestamp. Someone called it in before it happened. The Velux operative's number traces to a burner phone.",choices:[{label:"The cameras were deliberately disabled before the incident. This was planned.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"detective-street",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You go to Nadia's building and watch the street for an hour. A dark car is parked across from her building \u2014 no plates visible, tinted windows. You photograph it. You find Nadia and tell her: don't go home tonight. She says: I knew something was wrong when they started parking there. She says it calmly, which tells you she's been living with this for a while.",choices:[{label:"She's being watched. Get her somewhere safe and keep going.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The surveillance of Nadia, the camera malfunction, the institutional connection between Stavros and Hale's department \u2014 the suppression isn't improvised. It's coordinated. Someone with resources arranged the cameras, the police deflection, and the witness monitoring. That's not one person acting alone.",choices:[{label:"Find Hale's protection detail. They would have been with him that evening.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Look at Hale's movements that evening through official records.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Approach Stavros directly. He knows you're coming anyway.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Ministerial protection officers file movement logs. Hale's log for that evening: his protection detail recorded him as 'in transit' between 6:45 and 7:30pm, with no specific location noted. 'In transit' is unusual \u2014 normally locations are logged even in motion. This entry covers the exact window of the incident. Someone redacted or generalized the location data.",choices:[{label:"The protection log covers the exact window. Someone modified it.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Hale's public schedule for that evening: a private dinner, location not publicized. The dinner was at a club in the city's south side \u2014 which means his route from his department would pass the junction where Rees died. The timing fits. And the dinner was attended by twelve people, one of whom is a close associate of DCI Stavros.",choices:[{label:"The route, the timing, and a dinner guest who is Stavros's associate. The network is clear.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Stavros sees you without making you wait, which means he's been expecting contact. He is careful and calibrated. He says the Rees case is being handled appropriately. He says the witness identification was uncertain because the witness was distressed at the scene. He says all of this without a single false statement that you can directly challenge. He's describing the cover story, perfectly.",choices:[{label:"He's confirming the cover story exists. Use that.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The network: Hale in the car. His protection detail's log modified to cover the route. Stavros directing Price to suppress the identification. The cameras disabled before the incident \u2014 planned ahead, which means someone knew the route. The surveillance on Nadia. This is organized enough that going to the police without bypassing Stavros is futile.",choices:[{label:"Go above Stavros \u2014 to an independent body outside his chain.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Find the protection officer who modified or omitted the location log.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Get the evidence to a journalist. Public knowledge changes the calculus.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Independent Office for Police Conduct takes your file seriously \u2014 the combination of a marked-uncertain witness statement, a promoted detective, and a protection log anomaly is sufficient to open an investigation. They operate independently of Stavros's chain. They also, quietly, notify the parliamentary standards authority about the ministerial protection log anomaly.",choices:[{label:"Two independent investigations running. The case is now outside the network's control.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The protection officer who filed the 'in transit' entry is a man named Gould. He's former military, professional, and has been Hale's protection lead for four years. He isn't reachable through official channels. But his wife teaches at a school near Nadia's former address \u2014 a small connection that tells you where he lives. You knock on his door. He opens it and says: I wondered when someone would come.",choices:[{label:"He was expecting someone. He's been carrying this.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The journalist you choose has political credentials and a serious record. She understands the risk \u2014 a story about a minister requires absolute evidentiary foundation. You give her the camera malfunction documentation, the protection log anomaly, and Nadia's original statement. She says: I can't publish without more. She says: find me the connection between Stavros and Hale's dinner that evening.",choices:[{label:"She needs the Stavros-Hale connection documented. Find it.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The investigation is now in motion \u2014 through the IOPC, through Gould's potential testimony, through a journalist with the documentation, or through some combination. The question is whether the case can be assembled quickly enough, and publicly enough, to prevent further suppression. Nadia is safe but that safety is contingent on the case moving faster than the network's response.",choices:[{label:"Get Nadia's statement formally recorded through a legal channel before anything else.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Find the Velux Systems operative who called in the camera malfunction.",next:"b6b"},{label:"The dinner guest who connects Stavros to Hale \u2014 find documentation of their relationship.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A solicitor records Nadia's full statement in the form of a statutory declaration \u2014 a formal legal document whose falsification is criminal. It's dated, witnessed, notarized, and filed with three separate parties. Nadia's statement is now in the legal record. If anything happens to her, the statement survives. She says: I should have done this weeks ago. You say: you did it now.",choices:[{label:"Statement secured. The case has an anchor.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Velux operative who called in the malfunction: you trace the burner phone's purchase through a retail camera. The buyer is visible \u2014 a man who appears in the background of two photographs from Hale's public schedule events. Security staff. Someone in Hale's extended security circle disabled the cameras before the minister's car used that route.",choices:[{label:"Security staff who worked Hale's events disabled the cameras in advance. That's premeditation.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Stavros and the dinner guest: you find three joint appearances in police liaison events with Hale's department over two years. Expense records, publicly filed, show Stavros attended a private dinner hosted by Hale's department three months before the Rees incident. The relationship is documented in public records \u2014 you just had to look.",choices:[{label:"The relationship is in the public record. The journalist has her connection.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"detective-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The surveillance on Nadia's apartment ends suddenly. The car that was there disappears. This might mean Hale's people have backed off \u2014 or it means they know the case is now too public to manage through direct pressure. Either way, Nadia is safer than she was. The IOPC is running. Gould has made contact with a lawyer. The journalist is building her story.",choices:[{label:"The disappearance of the surveillance might mean Hale's people are changing strategy.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Contact Gould's lawyer and find out what Gould is prepared to say.",next:"b7b"},{label:"The case is advanced enough to go back to the Rees family. They deserve to know.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The strategy change is confirmed when Hale's office issues a brief statement saying the minister is cooperating with inquiries related to a road incident. Cooperating means someone told him the case can't be buried. A minister who is cooperating is a minister whose lawyers have told him the evidence is real. That's a better signal than a denial.",choices:[{label:"'Cooperating with inquiries' is not innocence. It's calculation.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Gould's lawyer says: Gould will provide a full account of that evening's movements, the log entry he was instructed to generalize, and the name of the person in Hale's office who gave the instruction. He says: Gould is not party to a cover-up willingly. He says: the instruction came from above his grade and he followed it and he's regretted it since. He says: Gould will cooperate with the IOPC.",choices:[{label:"Gould cooperating with the IOPC is decisive. The case is solid.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Tom Rees's mother. You tell her: a witness came forward and was suppressed. You tell her the investigation is now independent and moving. You tell her: your son's case was not abandoned \u2014 it was interfered with, and that interference is now being investigated. She says: will someone be held accountable? You say: I believe so. She says: is the witness safe? You say: yes.",choices:[{label:"The family knows. They've been waiting for this.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The case assembles its final pieces. Gould's testimony on the log. The camera operative's identity. Stavros's documented relationship with Hale's network. Nadia's formal statement. The journalist's story. The IOPC investigation. It is comprehensive and it is moving at a pace the cover-up can't outrun. The question is what happens at the political level.",choices:[{label:"A minister facing a criminal investigation doesn't automatically resign.",next:"b8a"},{label:"The IOPC investigation of Stavros is as important as the Hale case.",next:"b8b"},{label:"Nadia's safety is still the immediate concern. Confirm she's protected.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Hale's position becomes untenable when the journalist publishes. The story runs with the camera malfunction documentation, Nadia's statement, and the protection log anomaly. The Prime Minister's office issues a statement that Hale will stand aside pending investigation. Stand aside is not the same as resigned, but it's the right direction.",choices:[{label:"Stood aside. The political process and the criminal process are now both running.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The IOPC investigation of Stavros and Price runs parallel to the criminal investigation of Hale. Stavros's involvement \u2014 directing Price, his relationship with Hale's network \u2014 is separately actionable as misconduct in public office. The two investigations will eventually converge at trial.",choices:[{label:"Two converging investigations. Both needed.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Nadia is staying with a friend in a different area of the city. She's been quiet \u2014 not frightened quiet but considering-what-comes-next quiet. She says: I'm going to have to give evidence at trial, aren't I. You say: yes. She says: that's fine. She says: I saw what I saw. She says: it doesn't get less true because it's inconvenient for him.",choices:[{label:"She's ready. The trial will be her moment to say what she saw.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"detective-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Hale is charged with causing death by dangerous driving and perverting the course of justice. Stavros faces misconduct charges. Price faces a lesser misconduct finding. The trial is set for eight months hence. The journalist's story runs and runs \u2014 a minister, a cover-up, a cyclist who died while the witness was being told she was mistaken.",choices:[{label:"The charge of perverting the course of justice may be harder to prove than the driving charge.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Nadia is now a protected witness for the trial. That matters.",next:"b9b"},{label:"The Rees family is pursuing a civil claim simultaneously.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The prosecution's view: the perverting charge is the stronger of the two in terms of documentation. The driving charge rests primarily on Nadia's identification. The cover-up charge is documented through three converging evidence streams: Gould's testimony, the camera malfunction, and the Stavros-Price chain. One witness; three streams of evidence for the cover-up.",choices:[{label:"The cover-up may be more provable than the original act. That's an irony.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Nadia is formally designated a protected witness. She gets a new contact number, a single point of contact in the prosecution service, and practical security measures that she describes as 'strange but fine.' She says: I just want to give evidence. She says: that's what I've wanted since the night it happened.",choices:[{label:"Protected and ready. Eight months to trial.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Rees family's civil claim against Hale proceeds in parallel. Civil proceedings have a lower evidentiary standard than criminal. Their solicitor says the civil claim is likely to succeed regardless of the criminal verdict. Mrs. Rees says: I'm not doing this for the money. She says: I want his name on the record next to Tom's name. She says: that's what civil claims are for when criminal ones fail.",choices:[{label:"She understands the system. The civil claim is its own form of accountability.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Eight months later. The trial. Nadia gives evidence for two hours. She describes exactly what she saw, in the same detail she described it to you in your office, without embellishment or retraction. She says: I was twenty meters from the junction. I saw his face for forty seconds in good light. I know who I saw. Hale's barrister cannot shake her.",choices:[{label:"The verdict: guilty on both charges.",next:"end-guilty"},{label:"Guilty on the cover-up, not proven on the driving.",next:"end-partial"},{label:"The jury finds him not guilty. But the Rees civil claim succeeds.",next:"end-civil"},{label:"Guilty. The political consequences are as significant as the sentence.",next:"end-political"}]},"end-guilty":{id:"end-guilty",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Guilty on causing death by dangerous driving and perverting the course of justice. Hale receives a custodial sentence. He resigns from government with a statement that doesn't contain the word sorry. Stavros is found guilty of misconduct and loses his position. Price receives a formal reprimand and early retirement. Nadia calls you after the verdict and says: I said what I saw. You say: yes. She says: that's all I wanted to do. The Rees family is at the court when the sentence is read. Mrs. Rees keeps a photograph of Tom in her coat pocket. She doesn't take it out, but she knows it's there.",choices:[]},"end-partial":{id:"end-partial",scene:"ending-neutral",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"Guilty of perverting the course of justice. Not proven on causing death by dangerous driving \u2014 the jury accepted that Nadia's identification, however confident, was a single witness for a fast and traumatic event. Hale is sentenced for the cover-up. It is a significant sentence and a career-ending conviction. Tom Rees's death is never legally attributed to him. His name is not on that record. The Rees civil claim proceeds and succeeds, which puts his name on the civil record. Mrs. Rees says: it's not everything but it's not nothing.",choices:[]},"end-civil":{id:"end-civil",scene:"ending-neutral",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The jury acquits on both charges \u2014 the deliberate camera malfunction is attributed to a security aide acting without ministerial knowledge, and the protection log generalization is attributed to poor practice rather than direction. Hale walks out of court and is met by party colleagues. Three weeks later, the civil claim judgment arrives: liable for Tom Rees's death on the balance of probabilities. He pays. He remains in public life, diminished. Nadia says: I told the truth. She says: what the jury decided isn't my responsibility.",choices:[]},"end-political":{id:"end-political",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Guilty. The sentence is custodial. But the political consequences run further than the courtroom: the cover-up, fully documented, raises questions about who else knew. A parliamentary inquiry is opened. Three more officials are identified as having knowledge of the suppression. The inquiry runs for two years and produces a report that changes how ministerial protection details are governed. Nadia's statement, recorded formally before any of this began, is quoted in the final report as 'a model of witness resilience in the face of institutional suppression.' She reads the report on the train home and doesn't call anyone.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-lost-observatory",packId:"explorer",title:"The Lost Observatory",subtitle:"They mapped the sky from here. Then they vanished.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"High in the mountains, an eighteenth-century astronomical observatory was abandoned overnight\u2014instruments intact, calculations unfinished. You've found the building. Now find out what they saw that made them leave.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"mountain-pass",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The observatory sits at 3,400 meters on a south-facing ridge, exactly where Brother Clement's letters said it would be. Built in 1743, abandoned in 1751. The Church declared it a matter of administrative inconvenience. The astronomers never published their final calculations. The building has been here, locked, for two hundred and seventy years. Your guide refuses to come past the stone boundary marker. He won't say why.",choices:[{label:"Approach the building and assess its condition before entering.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Ask your guide what he knows about the boundary marker.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Find the entrance. You've come too far to stop at a boundary.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"observatory-exterior",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The building is stone construction, remarkably intact for its age and altitude. The roof has partially collapsed over the east wing but the main dome structure stands. The dome's shutter mechanism \u2014 a complex wooden-and-iron system \u2014 is still in position. Through a narrow window, you can make out the silhouette of a large instrument.",choices:[{label:"The instrument survived. Get inside.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"mountain-pass",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your guide says the marker was placed by the village below after the observatory was abandoned. He says: the men who left here came down the mountain in winter, without their instruments, without their notes, and refused to speak about what they had seen. The village decided that what they had seen was not meant to be looked at. The marker is a reminder.",choices:[{label:"A reminder, not a prohibition. Go to the observatory.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"observatory-entrance",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The entrance is a heavy oak door, swollen with moisture and time but not locked \u2014 the latch mechanism simply corroded into place. With careful pressure it opens. The smell that emerges is old paper and mountain air, cold and dry. Two hundred and seventy years of stillness.",choices:[{label:"Go in.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"observatory-main",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The main chamber. A refractor telescope, large for its era, pointed through the still-open dome slit at the afternoon sky. A writing table with papers. An astronomical clock, stopped. Three chairs. It has the quality of a room where people stood up suddenly and left \u2014 not panicked, but final. The papers on the table are the last calculations. They were in the middle of them.",choices:[{label:"Read the calculations on the table first.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Examine the telescope \u2014 what were they looking at?",next:"b2b"},{label:"Check the astronomical clock. When did it stop?",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"observatory-main",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The calculations are celestial mechanics \u2014 tracking an object across several nights. The numbers are precise and methodical, and the final column is incomplete, mid-calculation, as if a pen was simply set down. Whatever they were tracking, it was moving. And its path was predictable enough to calculate forward.",choices:[{label:"A moving predictable object in 1751. That's a comet, or a body with a regular orbit.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"observatory-main",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The telescope is pointed at a fixed position \u2014 the altitude and azimuth adjusters are still set. You sight through it at the current sky. Empty. But the position corresponds to a region of sky near the ecliptic, the path the sun and known planets follow. Something on or near the ecliptic held their attention long enough to fix the telescope.",choices:[{label:"The ecliptic is where new planets and comets appear. Find the calculation notebooks.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"observatory-main",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The clock stopped at 3:14 \u2014 either AM or PM, no way to know which from the mechanism. The date on the calendar-ring corresponds to February 1751. The final observation log entry is dated February 12. They left in winter, in February, after eight years of work. Something happened on a February night.",choices:[{label:"February 1751. Find the observation logs to match the date.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Behind the main chamber, a smaller room lined with shelves. Observation logs, bound in oilskin, labeled by year. All eight years are here. The final volume is 1750-1751. The last entry is February 12, 1751, and it is different from every other entry \u2014 instead of systematic notation, it's a paragraph, written in Latin, in a different hand from the main record.",choices:[{label:"Translate the Latin paragraph carefully.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Compare the hand with the letters you have from Brother Clement.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Read through the preceding entries to understand what they were tracking.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your translation: 'We have completed the calculation. The body's return is certain and its path through the inner system is not what our predecessors believed. We have written to the Bishop. We will say nothing further until we have received guidance. God grant we are wrong.' Not a discovery report. A request for permission to be wrong.",choices:[{label:"They found something disturbing enough to seek Church guidance before publishing.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The hand in the final entry matches Brother Clement's personal correspondence \u2014 it's his writing, not the primary observer's. Clement was the senior among them, the one who had final authority. He wrote this entry himself. The primary observer's signature appears below his, then two others. All four men signed the final entry. Whatever they found, they agreed on it.",choices:[{label:"Four signatures on a Latin paragraph before they disappeared. Read the preceding calculations.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Six months of entries leading to February. They were tracking what they called 'the wandering companion' \u2014 an object that appeared in late 1750 and whose orbital parameters they refined over sixteen weeks of observation. By February, they had enough data to calculate its path forward. The final entry follows immediately after the calculation series.",choices:[{label:"The wandering companion, its path calculated, and then they left. What was its path?",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find the key document: a sealed envelope inside the back cover of the 1751 log, addressed to the Bishop but never sent. Inside, a fair copy of their calculation \u2014 the orbital path of the wandering companion, plotted forward across two centuries. The calculation ends with a specific date and a location in the inner solar system.",choices:[{label:"Read the full calculation and understand what they predicted.",next:"b4a"},{label:"The date they predicted \u2014 when is it?",next:"b4b"},{label:"Their prediction was never published. Find out if the Bishop received their letter.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The calculation is sophisticated for its era. They tracked a periodic body \u2014 something that returns on a known cycle. Their orbital math is elegant and, you realize with a cold clarity, correct. They predicted a specific approach geometry for this body. Their concern, written in the margin: the path they calculated passes through the zone where an encounter with Earth was possible.",choices:[{label:"They thought they'd found an impact risk. That's why they wrote to the Bishop.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The date in their calculation: 2031. Two hundred and eighty years after they made this prediction. They weren't concerned about their own time \u2014 they were concerned about something their calculations told them would happen long after they were gone. And they wrote to a Bishop who suppressed the whole matter.",choices:[{label:"2031. Their prediction is about our future. Verify their calculations.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Among the papers, a draft of the letter to the Bishop \u2014 and a response. The Bishop's letter, dated March 1751, instructs the astronomers to cease their observations, burn their conclusions, and 'make no further record of the wandering companion, as such knowledge is not beneficial to the faithful.' They kept the records anyway. They simply locked the building and left.",choices:[{label:"They refused to burn it. They locked it instead, and left it for someone else to find.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"observatory-main",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You sit in the cold main chamber with the full picture. Four astronomers in 1751 tracked a periodic body, calculated its orbital path, and determined it would make a close approach to Earth in 2031. They were so alarmed they wrote to the Bishop instead of publishing. The Bishop told them to destroy the records. They didn't. They left everything here, locked, for someone to find.",choices:[{label:"Verify their orbital calculation using modern methods. Are they right?",next:"b5a"},{label:"Photograph and preserve everything first, then verify.",next:"b5b"},{label:"The date 2031 is eight years away. If they're right, this needs to reach astronomers immediately.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You photograph their calculation and transmit it to a colleague in computational astronomy. His response comes in six hours: their math is correct for their era, but their observational data was limited. The uncertainty range is wide. They might be right. They might be off by enough that the approach is insignificant. The only way to know is modern observation.",choices:[{label:"Uncertainty isn't comfort. Get the full records to the right people.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You spend two days documenting everything \u2014 the calculation, the logs, the Bishop's letter, the sealed envelope. Every page photographed, the originals left in place. You build a complete digital archive of everything before you consider what to do with it. Whatever happens next, the record exists.",choices:[{label:"Documentation complete. Now decide what this means and who needs to know.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You phone a contact at a space agency's planetary defense division \u2014 a person whose job is specifically to take calls like this. She asks you to transmit everything. She doesn't dismiss it. She says: historical orbital records are useful for extending baseline data. She says: we'll look at it. She says it with the professional calm of someone who is now paying very close attention.",choices:[{label:"Professional attention from the right people. Document everything while you wait.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"observatory-main",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You've transmitted the records. The response from the planetary science community is swift and serious \u2014 not alarmed, but engaged. The body the astronomers called 'the wandering companion' corresponds to a known periodic object in current catalogs. It was last observed in 1891. Its orbit was considered fully characterized. The 1751 observations add a data point that slightly shifts the orbital calculation.",choices:[{label:"Ask your contact what the shifted calculation means for the 2031 approach.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Focus on the historical story \u2014 the suppression and the deliberate preservation.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Find out what happened to the four astronomers after they left here.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"observatory-main",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your contact says: the 1751 data narrows the uncertainty in the orbital solution. The 2031 approach will happen \u2014 it's in the catalog. The question the 1751 data helps answer is how close. With their observations included, the miss distance calculation tightens. Currently: comfortable miss distance. Not an impact scenario. The astronomers were alarmed by an uncertainty that their data itself helps resolve.",choices:[{label:"They found a problem and their data is part of the solution. There's something right about that.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The historical story is significant: a Church-suppressed astronomical finding, preserved against explicit instruction, discovered two hundred and seventy years later to be scientifically valid. A historian of science you contact says: this is not unprecedented but it is rare. She says: they did the right thing by locking it instead of burning it.",choices:[{label:"They made the right choice. Tell the full story.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"observatory-archive",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Archive research through your university contact: Brother Clement died in 1753 in a monastery, having taken a vow of silence. The primary observer, a layman named Faretti, moved to Portugal and worked in cartography. He died in 1779. He never published anything astronomical. The other two joined religious orders. All four kept their silence. They kept the records but not the story.",choices:[{label:"They kept their agreement with the Bishop but not their scientific duty. Or maybe both.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"observatory-exterior",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The planetary defense team confirms: the 2031 approach is a known event, well-characterized, no impact risk. The 1751 data is now formally incorporated into the orbital solution and will be published. The observatory's find is international news \u2014 a suppressed astronomical discovery, perfectly preserved, with a discovery story that couldn't be written better if you'd tried.",choices:[{label:"Make sure the heritage protection of the observatory is established before anything else.",next:"b7a"},{label:"The discovery story needs to be told accurately \u2014 write it before others do.",next:"b7b"},{label:"The astronomers kept their silence. Decide how much of their personal story to share.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"observatory-exterior",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You file for heritage protection through the appropriate national authority. The observatory, its contents, and the surrounding site are registered within months. A conservation team will assess the structure. The dome mechanism \u2014 still functional after two centuries \u2014 is considered particularly significant. Your guide's village is consulted about site management. He looks less troubled when the protection order is confirmed.",choices:[{label:"Protected. The building that preserved the discovery is now preserved itself.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"observatory-exterior",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You write the discovery account carefully \u2014 the approach up the mountain, the guide's boundary marker, the quality of stillness inside, the sealed envelope in the back cover of the log. A science journal publishes it alongside the technical paper. The combination of scientific validity and narrative precision means it reaches a broader audience than either would alone.",choices:[{label:"The story and the science together. That's the right way to tell it.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"observatory-exterior",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You decide: publish the science, publish the suppression story, but leave Clement and Faretti and the others as 'the four astronomers.' No named individuals. They chose silence and a kind of anonymous preservation. The discovery can be credited to their work without overriding their choice. The paper notes: 'the astronomers' identities are known and will be held in the site archive.'",choices:[{label:"Their anonymity is respected. Their work is acknowledged.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"mountain-pass",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You descend the mountain with the documentation and a full photographic record. Your guide walks you to the trailhead and at the boundary marker he stops. You thank him. He says: my grandfather said the men who came down were not frightened. He says: he said they looked like men who had done what needed doing. You think about that on the descent.",choices:[{label:"They did what needed doing. Lock the room and leave the evidence for the next generation.",next:"b8a"},{label:"You understand them better now than when you started.",next:"b8b"},{label:"The story of the boundary marker should be part of the record.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"mountain-pass",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The logic of what they did is clear: they couldn't publish against the Bishop's explicit order, they couldn't destroy what they'd found, and they couldn't carry it with them into silence indefinitely. They locked it and left it. They were right that someone would eventually come. They left it for you, though they didn't know you.",choices:[{label:"A gift left two hundred and seventy years ago, finally received.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"mountain-pass",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"What you understand: they were scientists in a time when science required negotiating with power. They couldn't beat the Bishop. They could outlast him \u2014 or rather, they could arrange for their evidence to outlast him. Which it did. Which everything does, eventually, if it's preserved well enough.",choices:[{label:"Evidence outlasts the power that tries to suppress it. That's the lesson.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"mountain-pass",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You record the guide's account of the boundary marker and the village's oral tradition about the astronomers. It becomes a section in the published discovery paper: 'Local Memory and Suppressed Science \u2014 how village tradition preserved the frame of a discovery that the official record attempted to erase.' The guide is named in the acknowledgments. He says, when you tell him: that is correct.",choices:[{label:"He was part of the discovery too. The acknowledgment is deserved.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"detective-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The papers are published. The technical finding \u2014 the incorporation of the 1751 data into the orbital solution \u2014 is covered in specialist press. The broader story \u2014 the suppression, the locked observatory, the sealed envelope \u2014 reaches general audiences. The observatory is now on an international list of significant scientific heritage sites.",choices:[{label:"Follow the 2031 approach story as it develops.",next:"b9a"},{label:"The historical story matters most \u2014 what does it say about suppressed science?",next:"b9b"},{label:"Think about what comes next for the site itself.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"detective-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The 2031 approach is observed and confirmed: a miss distance of several million kilometers, well outside any risk threshold. The 1751 data contributed a small but real improvement to the orbital solution's accuracy. A group of astronomers proposes naming the object's approach geometry for the observatory. The formal designation is approved. The wandering companion now has a name attached to the mountain.",choices:[{label:"Named. The finding is permanent.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"detective-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A historian of science writes the definitive account: suppression of astronomical findings by religious authority was not rare in the eighteenth century, but the deliberate preservation of suppressed material \u2014 the locked room, the sealed envelope \u2014 is unusual. She calls it 'methodical defiance.' She says: they didn't fight the Bishop. They planned beyond him.",choices:[{label:"Methodical defiance. That's exactly what it was.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"detective-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The conservation assessment of the observatory recommends full structural stabilization and the creation of a small interpretive site. The dome mechanism, repaired, is operational. The refractor telescope, serviced, can be used. A local astronomy club takes on stewardship. The first night of public observing at the site draws forty people from the village below. Your guide comes. He looks through the telescope and says nothing for a long time.",choices:[{label:"He looked through the same instrument as the men his village remembered.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"detective-city",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A year after the discovery. The observatory is open to researchers. Parkes \u2014 no, wrong expedition; your colleague here is an astronomer named Vidal \u2014 is leading a study of the site's observational records, looking for other undescribed objects in the historical data. Your guide has been appointed to the site's advisory council. The boundary marker is still there. He said: it can stay. It's history now too.",choices:[{label:"The boundary marker is part of the story. Everything here is.",next:"end-preserved"},{label:"The science vindicated them. That's the most important thing.",next:"end-vindicated"},{label:"2031 comes and goes. The approach is observed. The prediction was right.",next:"end-predicted"},{label:"The site becomes a model for how to handle suppressed historical science.",next:"end-model"}]},"end-preserved":{id:"end-preserved",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The observatory is fully conserved and operational. The original instruments are displayed in situ. The sealed envelope \u2014 now famous \u2014 is held in a climate-controlled case next to its reproduction. Visitors come to see the place where four men did the most difficult thing: they refused to destroy what they'd found, accepted that they couldn't publish it, and left it, precisely, for someone else. You visit once more, five years later. The guide is there. He says: it used to be quiet. You say: that's all right. He thinks about this. Then he nods.",choices:[]},"end-vindicated":{id:"end-vindicated",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The technical paper incorporating the 1751 data is cited forty times in its first year. The orbital solution for the wandering companion is now among the most thoroughly documented of any periodic body. The 1751 contribution is noted in every citation. Faretti and Clement \u2014 identified eventually, quietly, with their names held in the site archive as they requested \u2014 are acknowledged in a footnote that reads: 'Historical observation data courtesy of the astronomers of the unnamed observatory, 1750-1751.' Anonymous and precise. As they would have wanted.",choices:[]},"end-predicted":{id:"end-predicted",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"2031. The wandering companion's approach is observed by thousands of amateur astronomers and tracked by every planetary defense system on Earth. It passes at a comfortable distance, exactly as the modern calculations said it would. A live feed from the observatory's refractor \u2014 the same instrument that began the story two centuries ago \u2014 is watched by forty thousand people. In the final frames, you can see the dome slit, and through it, the body passing, and briefly, the sense of something completed.",choices:[]},"end-model":{id:"end-model",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The observatory case becomes a reference point for discussions of suppressed science: how it happens, what it costs, what it takes to recover it. A protocol for handling similar finds \u2014 the 'locked room problem,' as a philosopher of science names it \u2014 is developed from the case. It addresses the question of what a researcher owes to suppressed material when they find it, and what they owe to the people who did the suppressing, and how those obligations differ. It is a difficult document. It is the right document.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-messenger",packId:"medieval",title:"The Messenger",subtitle:"You know what's in the letter. You were told not to read it. You read it.",length:"Short",free:!1,brief:"You carry a sealed letter from one lord to another. You broke the seal. What you read could prevent a war\u2014or start one, depending on what you do next. You are three days from delivery and entirely alone with this decision.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You are Kael, a courier. The sealed letter from Lord Aren to Lord Veth has been in your saddlebag for two days. You were told: do not read it. You read it. The letter contains an accusation \u2014 Lord Aren accuses Lord Veth's steward of embezzling the trade revenue, and names a specific sum. If the letter reaches Veth directly, his steward will be tried and probably executed. But the accusation is wrong. You know the steward. He didn't do it. The embezzler is Lord Aren's own brother, and the letter is a deflection.",choices:[{label:"Think through what you actually know and how you know it.",next:"s2"},{label:"Consider whether this is truly your problem to solve.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Think about what the consequences are for each possible action.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Is it your problem? You're a courier. Your job is to carry letters, not evaluate them. Except you read this one. And knowing what you know and delivering it anyway makes you complicit in whatever follows. You didn't choose to know. But you do know. That changes the calculation.",choices:[{label:"Knowing changes the calculation. Think through what you know.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The consequences: deliver the letter, Veth's steward is accused and probably dies for something he didn't do. Don't deliver, you've failed your commission and Lord Aren will pursue you. Deliver late with explanation, you've admitted to reading a sealed letter, which is its own crime. There is no clean option. Think through what you actually know.",choices:[{label:"No clean option. Think through what you know.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"What you actually know: the letter accuses the steward Davan of embezzling two thousand marks from the trade revenue. You know Davan \u2014 he was kind to you and has a reputation for scrupulous accounting. You know Lord Aren's brother Collin has expensive habits and debts you've heard merchants discuss for years. What you don't know: whether your read of this is correct, or whether you're wrong in a way that kills a man.",choices:[{label:"Your knowledge of Davan is personal, not documented. How certain are you?",next:"b2a"},{label:"The letter is sealed and your commission is clear. Deliver it.",next:"b2b"},{label:"You need evidence before you can act on what you believe. Find it.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"How certain: you've dealt with Davan twice in three years. Both times he was precise about coin, scrupulous about records, and visibly uncomfortable when the numbers didn't balance. Men like that don't embezzle two thousand marks. You could be wrong. But a man's life is at stake and certainty isn't available \u2014 what you have is a strong belief based on real observation.",choices:[{label:"Strong belief based on real observation. Decide what to do with it.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The commission is clear. You were hired to carry this letter. You broke the seal and that's on you. But delivering the letter means letting a false accusation kill a man you believe is innocent. You cannot separate the action from the knowledge you have. You cannot un-know this.",choices:[{label:"Cannot un-know it. Make the decision with what you have.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Evidence: you're three days from Lord Veth's seat, two days back to Lord Aren's territory. Evidence of Collin's debts exists in the merchant quarter of Aren's city. Evidence of Davan's accounting is in Veth's own records. You can't get to either in the next three days without completely failing your commission and alerting everyone that something is wrong.",choices:[{label:"Evidence isn't reachable in time. Decide without it.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"village-inn",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You stop at a village inn for the night. You sit with the letter and three days left to delivery. Your options have not changed but they're becoming more real as the road shortens.",choices:[{label:"Deliver the letter as instructed. Your job is not to be judge.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Refuse to deliver. Accept the consequences for yourself.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Deliver the letter, but tell Lord Veth what you know.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"village-inn",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your job is not to be judge. But your job was also not to read the letter, and you did. You've already stepped outside the courier's role. The question isn't whether you stay in role \u2014 you left that when you broke the seal. The question is what you do with the knowledge you have.",choices:[{label:"What do you do with the knowledge you have.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"village-inn",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Refusing to deliver means Lord Aren will send another messenger, or come himself, and the letter will reach Veth one way or another. Your refusal doesn't save Davan \u2014 it only removes you from the chain. Davan still dies. You just don't have to watch.",choices:[{label:"He still dies either way. Your refusal helps only you. Think further.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"village-inn",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Deliver and tell Veth what you know. This means admitting you read a sealed letter. It means accusing Lord Aren of false accusation, based on your personal knowledge of two people, with no documentary evidence. It means Lord Veth must decide between a lord's sealed accusation and a courier's word. That's a high bar for Veth, and considerable exposure for you.",choices:[{label:"Considerable exposure. But Davan's life is the weight on the other side.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two days out. The options are sharpening. You're leaning toward delivering and telling Veth what you know \u2014 but you're aware that this plan has significant risks and requires Veth to believe you over Lord Aren. Think through how to present this if you do it.",choices:[{label:"If you tell Veth, what evidence can you point to, even indirectly?",next:"b4a"},{label:"Veth will ask why you read the letter. Prepare an honest answer.",next:"b4b"},{label:"There is a third party who might help \u2014 think about who has standing here.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Evidence you can point to: the merchants' open knowledge of Collin's debts \u2014 verifiable, independent, specific amounts. Davan's record of scrupulous accounting \u2014 verifiable through Veth's own records. The timing of the accusation, which follows closely after Aren's new trade agreement that should have produced significant revenue. Not a proof. A direction for Veth to investigate before acting.",choices:[{label:"A direction for investigation, not a proof. That's what you can offer.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Why you read the letter: because a long road gives a person too much time to think, and curiosity is a failing, and you regret it, and you would rather be honest about that failing than lie about it or hide what you found. That's the honest answer. It's not a good answer for a courier. It is the true one.",choices:[{label:"The true answer, not the comfortable one.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A third party: the Church has a presence in Veth's city and sometimes mediates between lords. A merchant council maintains trade records. Davan himself, if warned, might be able to produce records in his own defense. You can't warn Davan ahead of the letter \u2014 Aren's accusation would be that Davan was tipped off. The merchant council is the most neutral party with access to relevant information.",choices:[{label:"The merchant council as an independent voice. A possible path.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"city-gate",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You are at Lord Veth's city. The letter is in your saddlebag. You have made a decision, or you're about to make one. The gate guard asks your business. You say: messenger from Lord Aren. He sends you to the hall. You ride through the gate with everything decided or still deciding.",choices:[{label:"Deliver the letter to Lord Veth and say nothing beyond your commission.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Deliver the letter and ask to speak privately with Veth before he reads it.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Find the merchant council before delivering the letter.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You deliver the letter and say nothing. Veth breaks the seal and reads. His face changes. He calls for his steward master. You are thanked and dismissed. You ride out knowing what comes next. You carry that knowing for the rest of your life.",choices:[{label:"Carry it for the rest of your life. Or do something now.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Veth looks at you with surprise when you ask to speak privately. He is a careful man \u2014 he sends the attendants out and waits. You tell him: you read the letter. You tell him what you know about Davan and Collin. You tell him what evidence could be found if he looks in the right places. He listens without expression. When you finish, he says: 'Why are you telling me this?' You say: 'Because I believe you will find out the truth if you look, and without this warning, you won't know to look.'",choices:[{label:"He'll look if he knows to look.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"merchant-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The merchant council's senior factor receives you with the wariness of a person who knows couriers don't usually come to them first. You tell him you're carrying a letter you haven't yet delivered, and that you believe the letter contains a false accusation based on information he may be able to verify independently. He asks: what information? You tell him about Collin's debts. He says, after a moment: 'That's common knowledge in trade circles.' He says: 'Why does this matter to an accusation?'",choices:[{label:"Tell him enough that he understands what he might be asked to testify to.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"However you've acted, you're now in Lord Veth's hall or on your way to it. The letter has been delivered or is about to be. What happens next depends on what you said and to whom.",choices:[{label:"Veth opens the letter in your presence and reads it.",next:"b6a"},{label:"You are asked to wait while Veth considers the situation.",next:"b6b"},{label:"The merchant factor has arrived ahead of you and is already speaking to Veth's people.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Veth reads the letter and then looks at you. He says: 'You told me Aren's brother has debts.' You say: 'Yes.' He says: 'You told me Davan has a reputation for precision.' You say: 'Yes.' He says: 'This is a serious accusation from a lord. And you are asking me to investigate before I act.' You say: 'I am asking you to look at the accounts before you convict a man.'",choices:[{label:"Look at the accounts first.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Veth is careful. He reads the letter three times before speaking. He asks you two questions: what did you see of Davan's character, and what do you know of Collin's debts? Your answers are clear and specific. He says: 'I will not act on this letter immediately. I will investigate first.' He says: 'If your information proves false, that falls on you.' You say: 'I know.'",choices:[{label:"He'll investigate. That's what you asked for.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The merchant factor is a faster rider than you. He's already given Veth's chancellor a brief account of Collin's known debts and the trade patterns in the Aren region. The chancellor looks at you as you arrive. He says: 'You're the courier?' You say: 'Yes.' He says: 'The letter?' You give it to him. He takes it to Veth. He comes back. He says: 'The lord will investigate before acting.'",choices:[{label:"Investigate first. The right outcome for what you put in motion.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Veth is investigating. His account masters are reviewing the trade revenue records. A rider has been sent to the merchant quarter of Aren's city to verify the information about Collin's debts. Davan has been informed that an accusation exists but not yet acted upon \u2014 he's cooperating with the account review. You are still in the city, asked to remain available.",choices:[{label:"The investigation will clear Davan or it won't. Wait.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Ask Veth's chancellor what happens to you regardless of the outcome.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Lord Aren will be informed that his letter is under review. Think about what comes next.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The investigation takes four days. You wait in a guest room that isn't quite comfortable, eating food that isn't quite warm, knowing that somewhere in this building someone is looking at numbers that will either confirm or deny everything you believed when you made your choice.",choices:[{label:"Wait. The numbers will tell.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The chancellor says: 'You broke a sealed letter.' You say: 'Yes.' He says: 'That's a crime in most circumstances.' He says: 'If the investigation vindicates your account, Lord Veth may choose to treat it as a matter between yourself and Lord Aren.' He says: 'If it doesn't, you've falsely implicated a lord and delayed justice for embezzlement.' He says: 'You know what you're risking.' You say: 'Yes.'",choices:[{label:"You know the risk. Wait for the numbers.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lord Aren will be informed, eventually, that his letter was reviewed before action was taken. He will also be informed \u2014 if the investigation finds what you believe it will find \u2014 that his accusation was false. Whatever comes after that, between the two lords, is above your station to predict. But it will come. You set it in motion when you broke the seal.",choices:[{label:"You set it in motion. Live with that.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The investigation finds discrepancies. Not in Davan's accounts \u2014 his accounts are, as you believed, precise and correct. The discrepancies are in the records that reach Davan's desk from Aren's territory. Amounts recorded as received that don't match the originating records. Collin's name appears in three related transactions over two years. The account masters present this to Lord Veth.",choices:[{label:"The discrepancies are in Aren's records, not Davan's. The investigation found the truth.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Ask to be informed of the outcome before it's acted upon.",next:"b8b"},{label:"Think about what Veth does next and what it means for Lord Aren.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Davan is summoned and shown the findings. He says he was aware the incoming records were inconsistent and had flagged it to Lord Aren three months ago. He received no response. He says: 'I did not report it further because Lord Aren is my lord's ally and I lacked the standing to accuse a lord's household.' He says: 'I am glad someone else found it.'",choices:[{label:"He flagged it and was ignored. That's part of the record now.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The chancellor tells you: the account review vindicates your account. Davan is cleared. The discrepancies implicate Collin, Lord Aren's brother. Lord Veth is preparing a formal response to Lord Aren. He says: 'You'll want to be gone before that letter is sent.' You ask why. He says: 'Because Lord Aren will know you're the reason it was investigated. Give yourself a day's head start.'",choices:[{label:"A day's head start. He's warning you.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Veth's response to Lord Aren will be a formal counter-accusation \u2014 or a demand for explanation \u2014 that shifts the political situation between two lords entirely. Aren sent a letter to deflect blame from his brother. He's going to receive a letter that places the blame exactly where it belongs. The political fallout from that exchange is for lords to navigate. You are a courier who broke a seal and told the truth.",choices:[{label:"Told the truth. That's what you did.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"city-gate",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You leave Veth's city with a letter of safe conduct in your saddlebag \u2014 a formal statement from Lord Veth that you acted as a witness to a false accusation and that your testimony was truthful. It's not a pardon for breaking the seal; it's something more useful: documentation that what you did, you did for reasons that proved sound.",choices:[{label:"The letter of safe conduct. Think about what it is and isn't.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Lord Aren is going to know what you did. Think about the road ahead.",next:"b9b"},{label:"Davan is alive. That's the weight on the other side of all of this.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"What the letter is: evidence that you told the truth. What it isn't: protection from Lord Aren, who can still pursue you for breaking the seal and for the damage done to his position. Lords have long memories and long reaches. The letter gives you some protection from other lords. It gives you none from him.",choices:[{label:"None from him. The road ahead is the road ahead.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lord Aren is going to know. Veth's letter will reach him in four days. In five days, Aren will know a courier broke his seal, read his letter, and told Veth what was in it \u2014 and that the investigation vindicated the courier. He is going to be furious. He is also going to be in a complicated position, politically. Furious men in complicated positions are unpredictable.",choices:[{label:"Unpredictable. Ride accordingly.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Davan is alive. He's been cleared. He'll continue to be a careful steward of accounts that are scrupulous and precise. He doesn't know it was a courier who read a sealed letter and told a lord to look before acting. He may never know. That's fine. The point wasn't credit. The point was Davan alive.",choices:[{label:"Davan alive.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"forest-road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The road is long and you are a courier who has broken his commission, told a truth that prevented an injustice, and made an enemy of a lord. You have Veth's letter of safe conduct and the knowledge that you did the thing you could do with the knowledge you had. Whether that was right depends on who you ask.",choices:[{label:"Whether it was right depends on who you ask. You believe it was.",next:"end-right"},{label:"The safe conduct is the practical outcome. Build a new commission from it.",next:"end-practical"},{label:"Davan's life is the only outcome that matters.",next:"end-life"},{label:"The road ahead is uncertain. So is every other road.",next:"end-road"}]},"end-right":{id:"end-right",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You believe it was right. You carry that belief the way you carry the letter of safe conduct: as something with practical weight, not just moral comfort. You broke the seal. You told the truth. The truth was verified. A man who would have died is alive. You've made an enemy. The balance is not comfortable. You think it's correct. That's the best you can say about a decision made alone on a road with no good options. You ride on.",choices:[]},"end-practical":{id:"end-practical",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"Veth's letter of safe conduct becomes the foundation of a new commission. He needs couriers he can trust with sensitive correspondence \u2014 couriers who know the cost of what they carry. You become one of those. The commission pays better and the work is harder and you carry sealed letters that you do not read, and the discipline of that restraint is harder than you expected. The one time you read one, it was the right call. You know that doesn't mean it would be the right call the next time.",choices:[]},"end-life":{id:"end-life",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Davan's life. You meet him once, two years later, at a trade fair in a neutral city. He doesn't know who you are. He's buying supplies, precisely and carefully, and he's arguing with a merchant about an invoice that doesn't add up. You watch him for a moment and then continue past. He looks like a man who is very good at his work. He looks alive. That's sufficient.",choices:[]},"end-road":{id:"end-road",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The road ahead is uncertain. Lord Aren's pursuit, if it comes, comes at its own pace. Veth's commission, if it comes, comes at its own pace. Your own reputation as a courier who breaks seals, or as a courier who tells lords the truth when the truth is urgent \u2014 that depends on who tells the story. You ride, which is what you do. You carry what you're given, and sometimes what you're given is a choice you didn't ask for and have to make anyway.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-naturalist",packId:"explorer",title:"The Naturalist",subtitle:"The specimen doesn't match anything in the catalog. Anything.",length:"Short",free:!1,brief:"Collecting specimens in a remote highland valley, you find an organism that shouldn't exist here\u2014or anywhere. It could be the discovery of a century, a misidentification, or a fraud. You have two days of field time left to find out.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Day four of six in the highland valley. You're cataloging insect specimens for a regional biodiversity survey when you catch something wrong in the net \u2014 wrong size, wrong coloring, wrong everything. You set it under the field loupe and your first thought is: I've made an error. Your second thought is: I haven't made an error. Whatever this is, it's not in the catalog.",choices:[{label:"Preserve the specimen immediately before examining it further.",next:"s2"},{label:"Examine it carefully first \u2014 you may still be wrong.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Check your reference materials before concluding anything.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The more you examine it, the less like a known species it looks. The wing venation alone puts it outside any family you can place. The coloring is not a regional variation pattern you recognize. Your hand trembles slightly as you move it to the preservation vial. You're not wrong.",choices:[{label:"Preserve it and start documenting.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your field guides cover the regional fauna comprehensively. The specimen doesn't appear in any of them. You photograph it against the guide pages to show scale and comparison. The mismatch is clear in the photographs. The camera doesn't make identification errors.",choices:[{label:"Documented against reference. Preserve and continue.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You have the specimen preserved, photographed from six angles, and documented with GPS coordinates and habitat notes. You also have two more specimens from the same microhabitat \u2014 a damp rock face in a north-facing hollow. Three individuals. Not a single anomalous find. A population.",choices:[{label:"Search the microhabitat systematically for more specimens.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Secure what you have and radio your supervisor.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Look for signs of previous collection at this site.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Another forty minutes of careful searching yields five more individuals, two egg cases, and what appears to be a molt from a fourth instar larva. The species has a full life cycle represented here. This is an established population in a microhabitat that has probably been here for generations.",choices:[{label:"Established population. This isn't a vagrant or accidental find.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your supervisor, Dr. Huang, answers immediately. You describe the specimen. There's a pause. She says: photograph the wing venation close-up and transmit it. You do. Another pause, longer. She says: stay there. She says: don't move anything else. She says: I'm going to make some calls.",choices:[{label:"She recognized something. Wait and collect more data while she calls.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Near the rock face you find something that shouldn't be here: an old aluminum label tag, corroded, partially legible. A field collection label from a different expedition \u2014 the date, barely readable, is forty years ago. Someone was here before, in this hollow, with a collection kit. Whatever they found, they didn't publish it.",choices:[{label:"Forty years ago and no publication. The discovery was made and then disappeared.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"highland-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Evening camp. You have nine preserved specimens, extensive photographic documentation, and a growing suspicion that this find is significant enough to require more care than a standard survey discovery. You also have one more full day of field time before the extraction window. How you use tomorrow matters.",choices:[{label:"Return to the hollow. Characterize the full extent of the population.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Search the broader valley for additional populations.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Investigate the old collection label. Who was here forty years ago?",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"highland-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Full characterization of the hollow: the population occupies approximately twenty square meters of specific microhabitat \u2014 north-facing, damp, with a particular moss species as the primary substrate. Outside this zone, no individuals. The distribution is narrow and specific, which makes it either very new or very adapted.",choices:[{label:"Narrow distribution suggests high specialization. This species evolved here or arrived very recently.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find two more populations in similar microhabitats three hundred meters north and eight hundred meters east. The species isn't a single remnant colony \u2014 it's distributed across the valley in specific habitat pockets. The population structure suggests it's been here long enough to colonize multiple sites.",choices:[{label:"Multiple populations across the valley. This is an established species with range.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"highland-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The label's collection number, partially legible, corresponds to a series from a regional museum that catalogued this valley in the 1980s. You radio the museum. A curator checks. She says: that collection number is in our archive but the specimen is listed as 'anomalous, unidentified, held pending review.' She says it hasn't been reviewed in thirty-eight years. There's a jar in the archive with your species in it.",choices:[{label:"Someone found it before, labeled it unknown, and it sat. Time to change that.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"By midday of your final field day you have a clear picture: a previously undescribed species occupying specific microhabitat across this highland valley, with a possible museum specimen from 1986 sitting unidentified in an archive. The find is real. The question is how you handle the publication process, the museum specimen, and the priority question.",choices:[{label:"Priority: contact the museum to formally establish the archive specimen's status.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Priority: full population survey before extraction \u2014 you need the biological picture.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Priority: start the description process now while you're still in the field.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"highland-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The museum curator pulls the 1986 specimen while you're on the radio. She confirms: same species. The 1986 collector was a survey team from a different institution. None of them published an undescribed species finding \u2014 the specimen was simply filed. Under the rules of taxonomic priority, whoever formally describes and names the species first in publication gets credit. The 1986 collection doesn't count unless it was described.",choices:[{label:"The 1986 find wasn't described. Your find and description will be first.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Full population survey: four distinct colonies in the valley, total estimated population of two to three hundred individuals. Specific microhabitat requirements, restricted range. The conservation status will be significant \u2014 a newly described species with such a narrow distribution is immediately of concern.",choices:[{label:"Population data complete. This species needs both description and protection.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"highland-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You begin the preliminary morphological description in your field notebook \u2014 the character states that define this species from its nearest known relatives. Wing venation, body proportions, coloring patterns, the specific features that make this distinct and describable. The description process starts here, in the field, with the living specimens available for reference.",choices:[{label:"Description started in the field where it belongs. Continue on extraction.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Final hours. You pack with care \u2014 the preserved specimens wrapped individually, the photographic documentation backed up across three devices, your field notes comprehensive. Dr. Huang calls back: she's reached two taxonomic specialists who want to see the photographs. One says it may represent a new genus, not just a species. That is a different order of significance.",choices:[{label:"New genus possibility changes the stakes. Ask what evidence would confirm it.",next:"b5a"},{label:"New genus or species, the find is significant. Focus on getting specimens safely out.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Ask whether the 1986 museum specimen changes the taxonomy question.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The specialist says: molecular analysis will confirm genus status. She says: the morphological evidence is compelling but not conclusive. She says: get the specimens to a lab with DNA extraction capability. She says: this is significant enough that we should be careful and right, not fast and wrong.",choices:[{label:"Careful and right. That's the standard. Head for extraction.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You prioritize the specimens. Nine preserved individuals, the egg cases, the molt. Everything documented and secured. Whatever the taxonomic resolution \u2014 species or genus \u2014 the physical evidence needs to reach the lab intact. The discovery is only as good as the specimens that prove it.",choices:[{label:"Specimens secured. Extraction.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"highland-valley",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Huang says: the 1986 specimen was never described, so it doesn't affect taxonomic priority. But it does mean the species has been in that valley for at least forty years without being formally recognized. She says: the description process should acknowledge it. She says: scientific history is better when it's complete.",choices:[{label:"Acknowledge the 1986 find in the publication. Completeness matters.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"mountain-road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Extraction day. The helicopter comes at ten. You're packed and ready at nine-fifteen, which gives you forty-five minutes to sit with what you've found. A new species \u2014 possibly a new genus \u2014 in a highland valley. Nine specimens, documentation, coordinates, habitat data, population survey. Everything needed to make a real find into a real contribution.",choices:[{label:"Think about the naming. Every described species needs a name.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Think about the conservation implications. Narrow range, undescribed, unprotected.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Think about what comes next \u2014 the lab work, the description, the paper.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"mountain-road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The naming is a small ceremony that feels larger than it is. You think about the hollow, the north-facing rock face, the specific moss. You think about the 1986 collector who found it and filed it as unknown. You think about the forty years of sitting in a jar, waiting. You have an idea for the name.",choices:[{label:"The name is decided. Save it for the paper.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"mountain-road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"An undescribed species with a narrow range and specific microhabitat requirements is immediately vulnerable \u2014 to climate change, to habitat alteration, to the simple bad luck of a small population. Formal description means it can be given legal protection. The description paper and the conservation assessment will need to be written simultaneously.",choices:[{label:"Description and conservation together. That's the responsible approach.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"mountain-road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The lab work: molecular analysis to confirm genus status, detailed morphological description, comparison with type specimens of related species. The paper: introduction, materials and methods, description, diagnosis, etymology, discussion. Six months of careful work to make a four-day find permanent.",choices:[{label:"Six months to do it right. Worth it.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lab. The molecular analysis comes back three weeks after extraction. New genus confirmed \u2014 not just an undescribed species but an entirely new genus, the first addition to this insect family in twenty-two years. The paper will be significant. Your supervisor calls it the best field find of her career. She's been doing fieldwork for thirty-one years.",choices:[{label:"Write the paper carefully. A finding this significant deserves precision.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Contact the 1986 survey team. The original collector deserves to know.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Talk to conservation authorities about the habitat protection simultaneously.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The paper takes four months. Every character state described with precision, every measurement reported with variation data, the etymology explained, the habitat documented, the population data included. When the reviewers respond, they have few comments. The paper is accepted with minor revisions at the best specialist journal in the field.",choices:[{label:"Accepted. The species is now formally described.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find the 1986 collector \u2014 a retired entomologist living in another country. She's eighty-one. When you explain what she found and filed as unknown, she's quiet for a long time. She says: I knew it was different. She says: we ran out of time on that survey and I thought someone would get back to it. She says: I'm glad you did. You say: it's in the acknowledgments.",choices:[{label:"She's in the acknowledgments. The story is complete.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The conservation authority moves faster than you expected \u2014 a new genus with narrow range and restricted microhabitat triggers immediate review for protective status. The highland valley is proposed for inclusion in a protected area network. The timeline from discovery to protection proposal is seven months, which is fast for a bureaucratic process.",choices:[{label:"Seven months from find to protection proposal. The system worked.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The paper is in press. The new genus is formally established. The highland valley, or the specific microhabitat sites within it, are flagged for protection review. Your name is on the description \u2014 the person who described the genus and the type species within it. That credit is permanent.",choices:[{label:"Permanent credit, properly earned. Think about the valley.",next:"b8a"},{label:"The 1986 collection is formally acknowledged. The story is complete.",next:"b8b"},{label:"A new genus changes what's known about the family's distribution.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The valley is protected now in the specific zones where the genus was found. A follow-up survey is planned for next season. You applied to lead it. Dr. Huang says you should. You think about the net, the wrong thing in it, the moment before you understood what you were looking at.",choices:[{label:"The beginning was a mistake that wasn't a mistake.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The 1986 collector receives a copy of the paper when it's published. She reads it and sends you a note \u2014 handwritten, which feels right. She says: you named the species well. She says: I would have chosen the same. She says: good work. From a retired entomologist who has found two species of her own, that is significant praise.",choices:[{label:"Good work. That's the standard.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two other researchers read the paper and go back to specimens they'd filed as 'anomalous' in their own archives. Both find your genus. The distribution turns out to extend beyond the highland valley \u2014 it's present at altitude across a broader range than your survey covered. Your find is the anchor of a range map that will take five years to complete.",choices:[{label:"The anchor find. Other discoveries build from yours.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A year after the field work. The paper has been cited eight times in its first month of publication. Three institutions have expressed interest in the follow-up survey. The conservation review is proceeding. The genus has a name. The species has a name. Both names will persist in the literature as long as the literature persists.",choices:[{label:"The names persist. That's the permanent thing.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Eight citations in a month is significant. The field recognized the find.",next:"b9b"},{label:"The follow-up survey will answer the questions this one raised.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The name you chose is on the type specimen in the museum \u2014 the best-preserved of your nine collected individuals. It will be there when the museum is different and the field has moved on and the highland valley has changed in ways neither you nor anyone alive now can predict. The specimen and its name persist beyond the person who named it. That's the point of it.",choices:[{label:"Beyond the person. That's the point.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Eight citations: two responding to the taxonomy, three noting the conservation implications, two building on the habitat characterization for related species research, one a phylogenetic analysis that confirms the genus placement. The paper is already useful to people working on different questions. That's a successful paper.",choices:[{label:"Useful to others. The best kind of contribution.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The follow-up survey will address: the full range extent, the population genetics, the microhabitat requirements in detail, the larval biology. Questions your four-day survey generated but couldn't answer. The original find is the beginning of a research program, not its conclusion.",choices:[{label:"Beginning of a program. There's more work to do.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"research-station",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"In the field notes, there's a line from day four: 'I've made an error. I haven't made an error.' You read it back now and think about that moment \u2014 the net, the wrong thing in it, the careful move to the loupe, the growing certainty. That's what it feels like when a discovery happens. Not dramatic. Just the accumulating weight of something being true.",choices:[{label:"The find was worth the care you gave it.",next:"end-careful"},{label:"The 1986 collector is in the acknowledgments. That feels right.",next:"end-acknowledged"},{label:"New genus. That will matter for as long as this family is studied.",next:"end-permanent"},{label:"The valley is protected now. The species can persist.",next:"end-protected"}]},"end-careful":{id:"end-careful",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The care you gave it: preservation before excitement, documentation before announcement, description before claim. Each step done right, in order, without shortcuts. The result is a paper that will stand. The type specimen is in the museum. The name is in the literature. The genus will be studied, revised, debated, built upon. You did the thing correctly, which is the best thing a field researcher can say about their work.",choices:[]},"end-acknowledged":{id:"end-acknowledged",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The 1986 collector \u2014 her name is Dr. Ingrid Sorel \u2014 is in the acknowledgments as 'the first collector of the type taxon, whose archived specimen contributed to the characterization of the genus.' She sends you a note. She says she spent forty years wondering whether that jar in the museum held something real. She says: it did. You say: yes. That exchange is also part of the record now, in the way that personal letters become history.",choices:[]},"end-permanent":{id:"end-permanent",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"New genus. In a family with dozens of described genera, a new one is added rarely. Yours is the fourth in this family in thirty years. The paper establishing it will be cited every time someone works on the family, which means it will be cited indefinitely. The morphological characters you described, the molecular data you generated, the population data you gathered \u2014 all of it is now the foundation that subsequent work is built on. You made a foundation. That's the permanent thing.",choices:[]},"end-protected":{id:"end-protected",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The highland valley's specific microhabitat sites receive protected status eighteen months after your survey. The follow-up survey confirms the population is stable. The specific moss species that serves as the primary substrate is itself flagged for monitoring. The protection extends not just to the genus you described but to the microhabitat that makes it possible. The discovery changed what the valley is \u2014 legally, ecologically, permanently. The net caught the right thing.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-plague-doctor",packId:"medieval",title:"The Plague Doctor",subtitle:"The sickness is real. The cure being sold is not.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You are a traveling physician arriving in a town where plague has already taken thirty people. A man named Colm is selling a cure\u2014and it's working, or seems to be. But something is wrong with his story, and you have patients who are dying while you try to understand what.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"town-gate",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The town of Verath has been sick for three weeks. Thirty dead, sixty ill, and a man named Colm who arrived a week ago with a remedy he sells for a silver coin. People who take it seem to recover. You've come from the south with your own methods and your own medicine chest, and nothing you've seen yet explains why Colm's cure should work. Which means either it does work and you don't understand why, or it doesn't work and something else is happening.",choices:[{label:"Observe Colm's treatment before forming any conclusions.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Examine your patients first. Understand the disease before questioning the cure.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Speak with those who have recovered after taking Colm's remedy.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"town-square",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Colm is theatrical and confident. He speaks of the remedy's origins with vague authority \u2014 eastern knowledge, he says, from a physician in Alexandria. The remedy itself is a dark liquid in small glass vials. He administers it with ceremony, making the recipient drink it all. You watch three recipients carefully. All three are ambulatory and apparently improving.",choices:[{label:"Ambulatory and improving. That's the fact. Find out what's in the vial.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your patients: fever, rash concentrated on the trunk, joint pain, and in severe cases confusion. The pattern is consistent across cases and you recognize it \u2014 this is not the plague that has swept other regions. It resembles a disease of contaminated water you treated once in the east. The water supply is the question.",choices:[{label:"The water supply. If the disease is waterborne, the cure isn't a cure \u2014 it's a coincidence.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"town-square",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three recovered patients. All describe similar trajectories: sick for four or five days, took Colm's remedy on day four or five, improved over the next two days. One woman says she was improving before she took the remedy \u2014 she took it because her husband insisted. She says: 'I was already getting better.' She says this without drawing any conclusion from it.",choices:[{label:"Already getting better before the remedy. That's significant.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"town-square",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The disease pattern suggests contaminated water. The recovery pattern suggests the disease runs its course in five to seven days regardless of treatment. Colm's remedy is administered on day four or five, after which patients improve \u2014 which they might have done anyway. This is not necessarily fraud; it might be coincidence that is being misread as cause.",choices:[{label:"Test the water supply. That's the immediate priority.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Examine the contents of Colm's remedy. What is it actually?",next:"b2b"},{label:"Find patients in different stages and see if the recovery timeline holds.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"town-well",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The town has three wells. The sick cases cluster geographically \u2014 you map them carefully. All but two of the sick live within two hundred meters of the eastern well. The two outliers both buy water from a cart that sources from the eastern well. The eastern well is the source. You can confirm this by testing who is and isn't sick relative to their water source.",choices:[{label:"The eastern well is the source. Close it and see if new cases stop.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"town-square",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You acquire a vial through a patient who will share it. The contents: a dark herbal decoction with strong flavors. You identify willow bark, which reduces fever. Honey, which soothes. Something astringent you can't place. Nothing harmful, but nothing specifically curative for this disease either. It's a good general remedy for fever and discomfort. Not a cure. A comfort.",choices:[{label:"A comfort, not a cure. The patients were recovering naturally. Find the actual source.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find six patients in different stages. Three early-stage, three late-stage, one who recovered without taking Colm's remedy. The recovery timelines: five to eight days across all cases, remedy or not. The patient who recovered without the remedy recovered in six days \u2014 within normal range. The disease has a natural course. Colm's remedy is administered at the curve's peak.",choices:[{label:"The natural course. Colm is selling at the curve's peak and claiming the credit.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"town-square",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The evidence is building: a waterborne disease with a natural course of five to eight days, an eastern well as the likely source, and Colm's remedy administered at the point where patients are already on the recovery curve. The remedy may help with symptoms but is not curing the disease. Colm is either a fraud who knows this or a charlatan who believes his own story.",choices:[{label:"Speak with Colm directly. Judge whether he believes what he's selling.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Close the eastern well first. Stop new cases before addressing Colm.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Take your findings to the town's mayor or alderman. This needs official action.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"town-square",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Colm is intelligent and not, you judge, entirely cynical. He believes the remedy works. He describes its effects in genuine terms. He has seen people improve after taking it and drawn the natural human conclusion. He also says something that catches you: 'I've been telling people to drink only from the north well. I don't know why, but the sick cases are all from the east side of town.' He noticed the pattern. He doesn't know what it means.",choices:[{label:"He noticed the pattern and is accidentally doing something right. Build on this.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"town-well",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You go to the alderman and make the case for closing the eastern well: the geographic clustering of cases, the water cart connection, the need to stop new infections regardless of what else is true. The alderman listens. He's cautious about contradicting Colm publicly, because Colm has given the town hope. You say: 'Close the well quietly. You don't have to say why yet.'",choices:[{label:"Close the well quietly. That's the immediate harm reduction.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"town-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Alderman Fetch receives you with the wariness of a man who knows something is wrong but has been hoping it isn't. You lay out the evidence: the water source, the recovery timeline, the remedy's contents. He says: 'If I tell people the remedy doesn't work, there will be panic.' You say: 'Tell them to stop drinking from the eastern well. That's the truth and it stops the spread.'",choices:[{label:"The truth that stops the spread. That's the right framing.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"town-square",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The eastern well is being addressed. Colm's situation is becoming clearer. But there are still sixty ill patients who need care, new cases that may already be in the pipeline, and a community that has placed its hope in a remedy that helps with symptoms but won't cure the disease.",choices:[{label:"Coordinate with Colm on patient care. His remedy has real symptomatic value.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Set up your own treatment practice and let people choose.",next:"b4b"},{label:"The community's trust in Colm is itself valuable. Don't destroy it needlessly.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You approach Colm with the honest information: the water source, the disease's natural course, and the value his remedy has for fever and comfort. He listens carefully. He says: 'The remedy doesn't cure it?' You say: 'Not by itself. But it helps them bear the course.' He thinks about this for a long time. He says: 'I thought I was saving them.' You say: 'You're helping them through it. That's not nothing.'",choices:[{label:"'That's not nothing.' He needed to hear that.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You set up in the building the alderman provides. Your treatment: herbal fever reducers, careful hydration with clean water \u2014 specifically, not from the eastern well \u2014 and detailed attention to the disease's progression. Patients who come to you improve at the same rate as those using Colm's remedy. The rate is the disease's natural course. But clean water stops new cases immediately.",choices:[{label:"Clean water stops new cases. That's the intervention that matters.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"town-square",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The community's trust in Colm has kept people calm and seeking treatment rather than fleeing, which would have spread the disease. That trust has value even if its object is partially mistaken. You decide not to undermine Colm publicly. You work alongside him, quietly redirecting the water question, ensuring patients get adequate care, and letting the disease run its course.",choices:[{label:"Alongside, not against. The outcome is the patients recovering.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The eastern well has been closed. New cases have stopped appearing. The sixty ill patients are progressing through the disease's natural course with adequate care. Colm is either working alongside you or has adjusted his approach based on what he's learned. The crisis is stabilizing.",choices:[{label:"Track the recovery of the sixty patients to confirm the timeline holds.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Investigate the eastern well to confirm the contamination source.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Address the families of the thirty who died. They deserve an account.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Day seven after the well closure: fifty-one of the sixty have recovered or are clearly improving. Nine remain ill, all of whom fell sick in the last days before the well was closed. The timeline matches: five to eight days, natural course. Three of the nine are more severely ill \u2014 older, or with conditions that make the disease harder. They need more careful attention.",choices:[{label:"Three severe cases need focused attention. Treat them directly.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"town-well",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The eastern well: you examine it carefully. Upstream of the well, the water flows through land that recently saw a sheep die of illness \u2014 the carcass was buried improperly, too close to the water table. The contamination source is clear. You record it, recommend proper burial of the carcass and treatment of the well before any future use.",choices:[{label:"Source identified. Document it for future prevention.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"town-square",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Thirty families. You spend two days with them, group and individual. You explain what the disease was, how it spread, and why those thirty died. You say: 'They fell ill before we understood the source. The eastern well was the cause. It has been closed.' You say: 'What took them was not something they or anyone could have prevented with better knowledge than was available.'",choices:[{label:"Not something that could have been prevented with the knowledge available. That's true.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The three severe cases require careful management. Two elderly patients, one younger man with an existing condition. These are the people the disease does not release as easily as it releases the young and healthy. This is where your skill matters \u2014 not in explaining mechanisms but in being present and careful with people who are genuinely fragile.",choices:[{label:"Stay with the severe cases through the critical nights.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Focus on the specific interventions that reduce severity in vulnerable patients.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Be honest with the families about the risk. They need to know.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You stay. Three nights, rotating through the three severe patients, keeping their fevers managed, ensuring hydration, watching for the specific signs of deterioration you know to be serious. One patient turns a corner on the second night. Another is slower but steady. The third \u2014 the older woman \u2014 is the hardest. You stay with her through the worst of it.",choices:[{label:"Through the worst of it. You stay.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"In severe cases: aggressive fever management, careful hydration to replace what fever takes, specific attention to the breathing which the disease affects in elderly patients. You know the progression. You intervene at each point where intervention is possible. Two of the three respond. One older patient you cannot hold \u2014 the disease has advanced too far. You do not pretend otherwise.",choices:[{label:"You do not pretend otherwise. That's the honest practice.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The families of the three severe patients. You tell each: the disease is serious and your patient is in the severe category. You describe what you're doing and what you're watching for. You say: 'I cannot promise outcomes. I can tell you what I'm seeing and what I'm doing.' One family asks if their mother will survive. You say: 'I am doing everything that can be done.' That is both honest and true.",choices:[{label:"Everything that can be done. Both honest and true.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three days of focused care on the severe cases. Two recover \u2014 slowly, incompletely yet, but the crisis passes. One patient dies \u2014 the older woman, who was too far into the disease when you found her. The families who lost someone in this town now number thirty-one. You mark this and continue.",choices:[{label:"Mark the dead and continue. That's what a physician does.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Speak with Colm about the one who died. He was caring for her too.",next:"b7b"},{label:"The town's alderman needs a full account of what happened here.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You have a record you keep \u2014 every patient, their course, the outcome. The older woman's name is in it with the date and what you did. You will read it again someday and remember what you could and couldn't do. The record is not for her \u2014 she's beyond it. The record is for the next patient who presents similarly, so that you are better equipped.",choices:[{label:"For the next patient. That's the purpose of the record.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"sick-house",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Colm is outside the house when you come out. He knew before you told him. He says: 'I gave her the remedy on day four.' He says: 'I thought she was past the worst.' You say: 'She was too far along by the time either of us saw her.' He says: 'I should have known.' You say: 'You saw what we see. You couldn't have known more than that.' He accepts this, or begins to.",choices:[{label:"He's learning what you learned earlier. Let him.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"town-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The alderman receives a full account: the disease, its source, the timeline of closure, the patient outcomes, the thirty-one deaths. He listens without interrupting. Then he says: 'What should we do with the eastern well?' You say: 'Clean it properly, have it tested, and wait a full season before use. What killed the sheep upstream needs to be fully removed.' He writes it down.",choices:[{label:"It's written down. That's what survives individual memory.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"town-square",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two weeks after your arrival. The sixty who were sick have almost all recovered. No new cases in ten days. The eastern well is being managed. The thirty-one dead have been buried and mourned. Colm is still in the town, quieter now, thinking about what he learned. You are preparing to leave.",choices:[{label:"Talk to Colm before you go. What he does after matters.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Leave your full medical notes with the alderman. The town needs a record.",next:"b8b"},{label:"The town needs a real physician. Think about how to address that gap.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"town-square",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Colm says: 'The remedy still helps with symptoms.' You say: 'Yes.' He says: 'I should tell people that's what it does.' You say: 'That would be the honest practice.' He says: 'I'm not a physician.' You say: 'No. But you're observant and you care about the outcome. That counts for something.' He considers this. He says: 'I might stop charging a silver coin.' You say: 'That would also help.'",choices:[{label:"'A silver coin.' The honesty is working its way through him.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"town-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your medical notes: the disease presentation, the water source, the recovery timeline, the interventions that helped and those that didn't. You write it for someone who may not be a physician \u2014 for the alderman, or whoever the town trusts next time. You make it as clear as you can. The knowledge should stay in the town even when you don't.",choices:[{label:"Knowledge that stays. That's the long-term help.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"town-square",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The town is a week's travel from the nearest trained physician. You can't change that. What you can do: leave the alderman with the name and location of a physician you know who might be persuaded to visit periodically. And leave your own willingness to return if they send word. That's not a solution but it's less of a gap than before.",choices:[{label:"Less of a gap. That's what's possible.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You leave Verath on a clear morning. Behind you: thirty-one graves, sixty recoveries, one man with a simpler and more honest practice, a well that will be clean by spring, and your notes in the alderman's records. The balance is not comfortable but it's real.",choices:[{label:"Real is enough. Think about the thirty-one.",next:"b9a"},{label:"The sixty who recovered might not have without the well closure. Think about that.",next:"b9b"},{label:"Colm's practice, adjusted, might do real good. Think about that.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Thirty-one names. You don't know all of them \u2014 you came after twenty had already died. But the eleven who died during your time you know. You carry them in the way physicians carry their losses: not as failure, necessarily, but as weight that doesn't leave. The weight is useful. It keeps you careful.",choices:[{label:"Useful weight.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Without the well closure, the sixty would have recovered but new cases would have continued to appear. More than sixty sick, more than thirty-one dead, before the disease ran out of new people to infect through the eastern well. The closure stopped the spread at sixty. That number could have been two hundred.",choices:[{label:"The counterfactual matters.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Colm, with an honest practice: a comfort remedy with real symptomatic value, sold at a reasonable price, without claims of cure. There are towns worse served than that. He's observant, he cares, and he's willing to adjust what he thinks when shown evidence. Those are the traits that matter. He might help more people than he's harmed, going forward.",choices:[{label:"More help than harm, going forward.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The road ahead is long and there are other towns. You know that somewhere down this road there is another situation like Verath \u2014 a disease being misunderstood, a remedy being oversold, and people who need someone to look carefully at what's actually happening and tell them the truth of it.",choices:[{label:"Another town, another disease, another careful look. That's the work.",next:"end-work"},{label:"The truth of it, carefully told. That's what a physician owes.",next:"end-truth"},{label:"Colm and the healer and the remedy \u2014 knowledge of what works spreads.",next:"end-knowledge"},{label:"The eastern well will be clean by spring. Small permanent improvements matter.",next:"end-well"}]},"end-work":{id:"end-work",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Three towns in the next year. Each one different, each one a version of the same problem: a disease being misunderstood, a cause being missed, a community looking for certainty you can't honestly offer. You offer what you can: careful observation, honest assessment, and whatever intervention the situation warrants. Sometimes it's enough. The weight of the ones it isn't enough for doesn't go away. You carry it and you keep moving. That's the work.",choices:[]},"end-truth":{id:"end-truth",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The truth of it, carefully told: the disease was waterborne, the remedy was symptomatic, the recovery was natural. You told this truth in a way that didn't destroy the community's hope, didn't undermine the one person trying to help, and didn't pretend certainty you didn't have. The truth, in medicine, is usually complicated and incomplete and the best you can do is say clearly what you know and what you don't. That is, you have come to believe, the whole of the physician's obligation.",choices:[]},"end-knowledge":{id:"end-knowledge",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Colm writes to you, eight months later, from a different town. He says his remedy has helped with fever and discomfort in cases where the cause was different \u2014 not water, he thinks, but something in the grain. He says: 'I found the source this time before you had to come find it.' He says: 'I watched the pattern the way you showed me.' He says it as simply as a man reporting a fact. The knowledge moved through him and kept going.",choices:[]},"end-well":{id:"end-well",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The eastern well is clean by spring, as the alderman's records recommend. They test it in March \u2014 the contamination is gone. They reopen it in April with a marker placed nearby that reads, in the alderman's own hand: 'This well was closed in the autumn of the sickness year. It was the cause. It has been cleaned. Do not bury animal carcasses upstream.' The marker is not eloquent. It does not need to be. It is what someone needs to know, written in the place where they will need to know it.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-rivals",packId:"explorer",title:"The Rivals",subtitle:"You both found it. Only one of you gets to name it.",length:"Short",free:!1,brief:"You've reached a remote canyon system first\u2014by hours. But Dr. Renn's team is two miles behind you, with more resources, better connections, and a very good lawyer. The discovery is real. The question is whether it stays yours.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"canyon-rim",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The canyon is everything the photographs suggested and more \u2014 a stratified red-rock system with intact petroglyphs on the north face and, at the base, the remains of a structured settlement. You've been here three hours. Your satellite phone shows Renn's team making camp two miles south. They'll be here by morning. You need to establish your claim before they arrive.",choices:[{label:"Document everything you can before dark. Timestamps are evidence.",next:"s2"},{label:"Get your claim filed immediately \u2014 documentation second.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Send a message to Renn. Discuss this before it becomes a conflict.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"canyon-rim",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You file a preliminary discovery notification with your university and the national heritage authority using satellite uplink. Timestamp: 4:47 PM. Whatever happens next, your arrival time is on record. Renn can dispute the quality of your documentation, but not the time.",choices:[{label:"Filed. Now document everything before dark.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"canyon-rim",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renn answers quickly. His voice is calm, which means he's already thinking about this. He says: we both knew this was a race. He says: let's be professional about it. He says: I'll see you tomorrow morning. He says all of this before you've said more than hello. He's been rehearsing this conversation.",choices:[{label:"He's been preparing. So should you. Document everything now.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"canyon-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three hours of documentation before dark. The petroglyphs are extensive \u2014 a narrative sequence running forty meters along the north face. The settlement at the base has at least twelve distinct structures. You find one intact ceramic vessel under a collapsed wall. You photograph everything and your GPS logs your position every thirty seconds.",choices:[{label:"Examine the ceramic vessel closely \u2014 it may date the site.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Focus on the petroglyph sequence \u2014 it's the most visually significant element.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Map the settlement structure \u2014 its scale will matter for the formal claim.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"canyon-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The ceramic vessel has painted decoration in a style consistent with a culture that inhabited this region between 900 and 1200 CE. The preservation is exceptional \u2014 sealed under the collapsed wall, protected from moisture. This vessel alone could anchor the site's significance in any academic or legal context.",choices:[{label:"The vessel is the key artifact. Document it meticulously and prepare for Renn's arrival.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"canyon-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The petroglyph sequence depicts what appears to be a calendar system \u2014 astronomical markers with associated symbols. If the interpretation holds, this is not just habitation evidence but a site of cultural and astronomical significance. Renn specialized in this exact symbol system in his doctoral work. He knows exactly what this is worth.",choices:[{label:"Renn will recognize the significance immediately. Your documentation needs to be airtight.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"canyon-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The settlement is larger than it initially appeared \u2014 eighteen structures, a central plaza, water management features. The scale indicates permanent habitation, not seasonal use. A site of this size and permanence is significant regardless of what artifacts it contains.",choices:[{label:"Scale and permanence make this a major find. Document everything for the morning confrontation.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Morning. Renn's team arrives at eight \u2014 five people to your two, better equipment, a drone for aerial mapping. Renn himself is polite and precise. He says: you were here first. He says it as a fact, not a concession. He also says: your team is two people. This site needs more than two people to document properly. He's offering something. The question is what it costs.",choices:[{label:"Hear his proposal. You can always say no.",next:"b3a"},{label:"You have the timestamps and the preliminary filing. You don't need his team.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Ask what he actually wants before discussing anything else.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renn proposes joint documentation \u2014 his resources, shared credit, co-authored publication. He says: the site benefits from proper documentation. He says: I'm not here to take this from you. He says: I'm here to ensure it's done right. His lawyer, he mentions casually, has thoughts on the Heritage Act's discovery provisions.",choices:[{label:"The lawyer mention is a threat wrapped in a proposal. Think carefully.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You tell Renn the site is under your claim and your documentation is filed. He nods. He says: I know. He says: I'm not disputing your arrival time. He says: I'm noting that a two-person team documenting an eighteen-structure site with forty meters of petroglyphs will take six weeks and the seasonal window closes in three. He has a point.",choices:[{label:"He's right about the timeline. Listen to what he's proposing.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renn says he wants co-credit on the primary publication and naming rights for the petroglyph sequence \u2014 which he'll call after his doctoral mentor. He wants your name on the site itself. He says: you found it. That should be yours. He says it with the sincerity of a man who has rehearsed it enough times that it sounds sincere.",choices:[{label:"He's offering you the site name and taking the sequence. Decide if that's acceptable.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The negotiation is uncomfortable in the way that all negotiations involving discovery credit are uncomfortable \u2014 because what's really being divided is not just credit but the thing that will be remembered. Your name on a site means something different from your name on a paper. You need to decide what matters most to you.",choices:[{label:"Site naming is most important \u2014 the place should bear your name.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Publication primacy is most important \u2014 the discovery paper should be yours.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Joint everything, equal credit, no division of ownership between you.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renn agrees to site naming rights going to you in exchange for co-first authorship on the primary publication. His lawyer drafts a one-page agreement. You read it carefully \u2014 it's clean. He's getting what he came for: academic credit. You're keeping what you came for: the discovery.",choices:[{label:"Agreement signed. Work begins.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renn is less comfortable with this arrangement. He says the primary publication is the academic record that matters for his career. You say: the timestamps make me the discoverer. His lawyer says something quietly. Renn says: sole first author for you, I'm corresponding author with equal billing on all subsequent papers. You counter: and the site name is mine. He agrees.",choices:[{label:"Negotiated. You're first. He's not absent.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renn is surprised by the joint proposal. He was prepared for a fight and you've offered a handshake. He says: that's \u2014 he stops. He says: yes. The joint credit agreement is simpler to draft than the divided version. His lawyer has it done in an hour. The site gets a hyphenated name that both your research programs can use.",choices:[{label:"Joint from the start. Work begins on equal footing.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"canyon-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Seven people working a site designed for two. The documentation goes faster than you expected \u2014 Renn's drone captures the full petroglyph sequence in a morning. His ceramic specialist provides an on-site preliminary analysis of the vessel. Your preliminary interpretation of the settlement plan is confirmed and expanded. The site is bigger and more significant than either of you initially assessed.",choices:[{label:"The collaboration is working despite everything. Note what's emerging.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Watch Renn's team carefully. Partnership doesn't mean the same goals.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Focus on the calendar interpretation \u2014 it's the most important finding.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"canyon-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"What's emerging: the site has three distinct phases of occupation spanning four centuries. The settlement at the base is the most recent. The petroglyphs predate it. And under the plaza, ground-penetrating radar shows a structure that predates both \u2014 something buried, intact, and large.",choices:[{label:"Three phases and something buried beneath. The site is more than a discovery.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"canyon-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renn's team is professional and thorough, which is slightly annoying. They're also clearly documenting things in ways that will serve their specific research interests \u2014 which is what you're doing too. The tension of competing interest and shared work is productive in the way uncomfortable things sometimes are.",choices:[{label:"Competing interests, shared work. The site is the beneficiary.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"canyon-base",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The petroglyph calendar, now fully photographed and digitized, turns out to be a lunisolar system \u2014 tracking both solar seasons and lunar cycles in a single integrated notation. Renn's specialty, but your identification. The paper that comes from this will be genuinely collaborative in the way that happens when two people know different things about the same thing.",choices:[{label:"The calendar is the headline finding. Build the publication around it.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Day four. The seasonal window is tighter than expected \u2014 weather moving in from the west. You have enough for a strong preliminary report and a second-season plan. Renn suggests accelerating the buried structure investigation. You agree. What you both know, though neither says it: whoever leads the second season controls the site's next chapter.",choices:[{label:"Propose a formal joint second-season agreement now, before the weather hits.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Say nothing about second season. Get the preliminary report right.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Offer Renn the buried structure lead in exchange for something specific.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renn agrees to the joint second-season proposal faster than you expected. He says: I was going to suggest the same thing. He might be telling the truth. The agreement is drafted that evening by lamplight. The site's future is now formally shared, which removes the uncertainty and limits your upside in equal measure.",choices:[{label:"Shared future, shared constraints. Head out before the weather closes in.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say nothing about second season and Renn says nothing about second season and you both know you're both thinking about second season. The preliminary report is excellent. The silence between you on the walk out has the quality of a competitive game at halftime.",choices:[{label:"First season complete. Second season is unresolved.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"canyon-camp",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You offer Renn the buried structure lead for second season in exchange for sole first authorship on the calendar paper. He thinks about it \u2014 three seconds, which for Renn is a long time. He says: the calendar paper is significant. You say: the buried structure is significant. He says: yes. Deal.",choices:[{label:"You keep the headline. He gets the excavation. Fair.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"canyon-rim",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You walk out of the canyon as the first clouds reach the rim. Seven people, two months of work compressed into four days, and a preliminary report that will make the rounds at the next major conference. Renn walks beside you. He says: you were here first. You say: yes. He says: it's a good site. You say: yes.",choices:[{label:"The professional respect is real, even if the competition was also real.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Ask Renn why he really came \u2014 whether he knew you'd be here.",next:"b7b"},{label:"The site's story is just beginning. Think about what comes next.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"canyon-rim",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renn is good at what he does, which is the most honest thing you can say about a competitor. The four days were difficult and productive in equal measure. The preliminary report is better for having two research programs contributing to it.",choices:[{label:"Better for the competition. That's something.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"canyon-rim",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renn says: I knew you'd be ahead of me. He says: I've been following your grant applications for two years. He says: I thought if you found it first, I could offer you resources in exchange for credit. He says: that's exactly what happened. He says it without apology, which is somehow more respectable than if he'd apologized.",choices:[{label:"He had a plan. So did you. The site benefited.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"canyon-rim",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Second season. The buried structure. The calendar's full interpretation. The three-phase occupation story. There are years of work in that canyon. Whether you and Renn navigate those years as partners or competitors or both is unresolved. The site doesn't care. It's been waiting longer than either of you has been alive.",choices:[{label:"The site will outlast the competition. Start planning.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"desert-road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The drive back to the nearest town takes three hours. You call your university's research office to report the outcome. They are pleased. They ask about second season funding. You give them the preliminary assessment. They say they'll have a figure within two weeks. You say: good. You look out the window at the desert. The canyon is already invisible behind the ridge.",choices:[{label:"The preliminary report is strong. File it and prepare the conference presentation.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Think about what you know about the buried structure and what it might mean.",next:"b8b"},{label:"The credit arrangement is settled, but think carefully about whether it holds.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"desert-road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The report is filed and the conference presentation scheduled. The abstract goes out and generates significant response \u2014 the combination of petroglyphs, settlement scale, and the buried structure anomaly is genuinely compelling. Three institutions express interest in supporting second season funding.",choices:[{label:"Interest from institutions changes the second-season calculus.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"desert-road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Ground-penetrating radar showed the buried structure at depth below the plaza \u2014 large, regular, and apparently intact. Intact means undisturbed. Undisturbed at that depth means the upper occupation layers sealed it, probably deliberately. Whatever is beneath the plaza was put there and then covered. That is a different kind of burial than simple abandonment.",choices:[{label:"Deliberately sealed. Second season just became more urgent.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"desert-road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The credit arrangement is documented but documentation doesn't guarantee behavior. Renn is capable and ambitious. So are you. The second season, if it proceeds jointly, will require the arrangement to hold under the pressure of significant findings. You've worked with people before where the arrangement didn't hold. You know what that looks like.",choices:[{label:"Document everything more carefully than you think you need to.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"university-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Six months later. The preliminary report is published and well received. Second season funding is confirmed \u2014 yours, Renn's, and a third grant from an institution that saw the abstract. The site is now formally protected. The buried structure investigation is scheduled. The petroglyph calendar paper is in review at a top journal. Your name is on it first.",choices:[{label:"The site's credit is established. Think about legacy.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Second season is the real test. Prepare properly.",next:"b9b"},{label:"Check in with Renn. The partnership needs maintenance.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"university-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The site bears your name. The discovery paper has your name first. The calendar interpretation \u2014 the most significant academic contribution \u2014 is your intellectual work, however the collaboration enhanced it. The legacy is what you arrived hoping for. It's also incomplete, because the buried structure hasn't been opened yet.",choices:[{label:"Legacy established but not finished. Second season will complete it.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"university-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Second season preparation: expanded team, specialist equipment for the buried structure, a conservation specialist for whatever they find. You've planned this carefully. So has Renn. The overlap in your plans is either a sign of like minds or competing interests in cooperation's clothing. You won't know which until you're back in the canyon.",choices:[{label:"Back in the canyon. That's where it resolves.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"university-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Renn is working on his buried structure methodology \u2014 he's consulted three engineers and a geophysicist. He's thorough. You talk for an hour about the second season plan and the conversation is good, which doesn't mean anything is guaranteed, but it doesn't hurt. He says: I'm looking forward to what's under the plaza. You say: me too. Both true.",choices:[{label:"Both true. That's a reasonable place to be.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"university-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Second season. The buried structure is excavated over three weeks. What you find changes the significance of everything above it \u2014 the settlement, the petroglyphs, the calendar. The site is now considered one of the most important finds of the decade. The paper that results has both your names. Renn's name is second.",choices:[{label:"First, and the site is yours. The competition ended the way it should.",next:"end-first"},{label:"The partnership held. That matters more than the order of names.",next:"end-partnership"},{label:"The buried structure is the real story. Everything else was preamble.",next:"end-buried"},{label:"The rivalry pushed you both. The site benefited.",next:"end-rivalry"}]},"end-first":{id:"end-first",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Your name is on the site. Your name is first on the primary paper. The discovery is yours in the ways that matter academically and permanently. Renn is a significant contributor and will benefit from the association. The buried structure turns out to be a ceremonial space of considerable sophistication \u2014 its interpretation requires both your specializations, which is a fact that neither of you could have planned. The site required both of you. You arrived first. Both things are true.",choices:[]},"end-partnership":{id:"end-partnership",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The partnership held through two seasons of competitive pressure and significant findings. The papers are well-received. The credit arrangement, maintained under stress, produces something neither of you would have produced alone. At the second season closing dinner, Renn says: I'm glad you were three hours ahead of me. You say: I'm glad you had the drone. Both of you mean it, which is a better outcome than either of you expected from four days of uncomfortable negotiation in a canyon.",choices:[]},"end-buried":{id:"end-buried",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The buried structure is a sealed chamber containing three hundred and forty intact ceramic vessels, textile fragments, and a collection of pigment materials not found in any other site of the period. It is the largest intact cache of this cultural tradition ever discovered. The paper about it alone generates more citations than the rest of your career combined. Renn leads the analysis. Your name is first on the discovery credit. You both know what that arrangement means and neither of you would trade.",choices:[]},"end-rivalry":{id:"end-rivalry",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The rivalry didn't end \u2014 it adjusted. You and Renn work the same region, compete for the same grants, and occasionally collaborate when the site requires it. The canyon is the beginning of a long professional relationship that is too complicated to call either friendship or enmity. The site bears your name. The most cited paper has his name second. You have both built careers on the same three hours of head start. It was a good canyon to find first.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-second-map",packId:"explorer",title:"The Second Map",subtitle:"The famous map was wrong. This one isn't.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"The Varro map\u2014the most famous geographical document of its era\u2014has guided expeditions for decades. You've found a second map, older, that contradicts it. If the second map is right, everything built on the first is wrong. And someone doesn't want you to prove it.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"archive-room",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The second map is folded inside the lining of a book that wasn't supposed to be in this archive. You found the book because the catalog is wrong and you were following the error. The map is older than the Varro by at least a century, drawn on different materials, using a different cartographic convention \u2014 but depicting the same region. And it disagrees with the Varro on every point that has ever mattered.",choices:[{label:"Photograph and preserve the map before anything else.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Compare it immediately to your copy of the Varro.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Find who cataloged this archive \u2014 someone put this book here deliberately.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"archive-room",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You photograph the map systematically \u2014 full, quadrants, details \u2014 before unfolding it fully. The paper is different from the Varro's material: thicker, more fibrous, possibly from a different regional tradition. The ink is faded but legible. The cartouche in the lower corner is in a script you recognize as older than the Varro.",choices:[{label:"The material evidence alone is significant. Compare it to the Varro now.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"archive-room",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Side by side with your Varro copy: the second map places the coastal settlement fifty kilometers south. The river junction that the Varro shows at the valley's head appears here forty meters lower in elevation, which would completely change its hydrology. And a feature the Varro calls 'uninhabitable plateau' is, on the second map, labeled with what appears to be a settlement name.",choices:[{label:"Every major point of disagreement is significant. Find out who put this book here.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"archive-room",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The book's acquisition record is a single line in a ledger from 1887: 'donated, anonymous.' The catalog error that led you here is not an error \u2014 someone deliberately miscatalogued this book's position, which moved it from geography to philosophy, which is why no one has opened it in a century. The miscataloguing is too specific to be accidental.",choices:[{label:"Deliberately hidden. That changes what this is.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"archive-room",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You have the map photographed, compared to the Varro, and the archive record checked. Three conclusions: the second map predates the Varro by what appears to be a century or more; it disagrees with the Varro on every significant geographic feature; and someone in 1887 hid it in the archive deliberately. The question is what you do with it.",choices:[{label:"Get the map to a materials specialist for dating before making any claims.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Research the 1887 donation \u2014 anonymous doesn't mean untraceable.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Go to the field. The geography is the test \u2014 one of these maps is right.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"archive-room",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The archive director, when you explain what you've found, is professionally controlled and personally excited. She permits temporary removal for materials analysis under strict conditions. The lab finds: the paper is consistent with production methods from approximately one hundred and twenty years before the Varro. The ink composition is different from the Varro's. The map is older.",choices:[{label:"Older and different. The second map is not a copy or derivative of the Varro.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"archive-room",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Anonymous donations in 1887 archives aren't actually anonymous \u2014 the ledger notes the receiving librarian, and the librarian's correspondence is in a separate collection. His letters mention 'the map matter' to a colleague at another institution. His colleague's response describes 'significant pressure from the Geographical Society not to publicize the find.' Someone in 1887 tried to publish this and was stopped.",choices:[{label:"Stopped in 1887. The same forces may still exist. Research while you can.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"archive-room",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You plan a field verification. The coastal settlement displacement is the most testable claim \u2014 fifty kilometers is a clear enough difference that physical evidence should settle it. You begin the permit process, which is slow, and in the meantime begin the archive research and dating processes in parallel.",choices:[{label:"Field verification planned. Meanwhile, understand what you have.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"research-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The materials analysis confirms: the second map is approximately one hundred years older than the Varro. This has an immediate implication: the Varro was created after this map existed. The Varro's creator could have had access to the second map. Either the Varro represents independent parallel observation or \u2014 the harder possibility \u2014 it was created with knowledge of this map and deliberately differs from it.",choices:[{label:"Find out who created the Varro and what sources they used.",next:"b3a"},{label:"The deliberate difference possibility is alarming. Why would a cartographer create a false map?",next:"b3b"},{label:"Check whether the Varro's errors all point in the same direction \u2014 systematic error vs random.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"research-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Varro was created by a Genoese cartographer named Caprini, working for a consortium of trading interests in the 1430s. Caprini was paid well and died wealthy. The trading consortium had significant interests in the coastal settlements the Varro depicts. Settlements placed fifty kilometers north of their actual location would \u2014 you work this out slowly \u2014 affect which overland trade routes appeared most advantageous.",choices:[{label:"The Varro may have been commissioned to mislead competitors. That's a different kind of fraud.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"research-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Why falsify a map? Commercial advantage \u2014 directing rivals to wrong locations. Military strategy \u2014 misleading foreign powers about terrain. Political control \u2014 keeping populations ignorant of routes. The Varro has been used by four major expeditions as the primary reference. All four expeditions had difficulties at the points where the Varro disagrees with the second map.",choices:[{label:"Four expeditions led wrong by a false map. Research what they actually found.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"research-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Varro's errors, when you plot them: all coastal features are displaced northward. All elevation data at river junctions is elevated. The plateau is systematically described as uninhabitable where the second map shows habitation. This is not random error \u2014 it's directional. The Varro was systematically wrong in ways that would redirect overland trade north and make the direct southern routes appear impassable.",choices:[{label:"Systematic misdirection. The Varro was commercial fraud, not cartographic error.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"research-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The picture is forming: the Varro, one of the most celebrated geographical documents of its era, was almost certainly a deliberately falsified map created to benefit the trading consortium that commissioned it. The second map \u2014 hidden in an archive since 1887, when someone tried to publish it and was stopped \u2014 is the accurate one.",choices:[{label:"Find the 1887 suppression story in detail. Someone tried to do what you're doing.",next:"b4a"},{label:"The field test is the most decisive evidence. Make it the next priority.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Contact the Varro Foundation. They fund research based on its authenticity.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"archive-room",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The 1887 story, reconstructed from the librarian's letters: a scholar named Hendricks found the second map in a private collection, recognized its implications, and submitted a paper to the Geographical Society. The Society's response was to refuse publication and recommend the map be deposited in the archive 'pending further verification.' Verification never happened. Hendricks died two years later. His notes are in the same archive \u2014 also miscatalogued.",choices:[{label:"Hendricks did the work. Find his notes before anything else.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"research-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You plan the field expedition. The coastal settlement site is the priority \u2014 physical evidence of occupation at the second map's coordinates would be decisive. You file the permits, which takes three weeks, during which you continue the archive research. When the permits arrive, they've been reviewed by an institution you didn't apply to. Someone is watching the process.",choices:[{label:"Your permit was seen by someone who shouldn't know about it.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"research-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Varro Foundation is a well-funded institution that supports expeditions, publishes research, and maintains the Varro's reputation as the definitive document of its era. When you contact them to discuss your finding, the director is polite, interested, and within forty-eight hours has hired a lawyer who sends your university a letter expressing 'concerns about the premature disclosure of unverified findings.'",choices:[{label:"They hired a lawyer in forty-eight hours. That's not the response of a confident institution.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"archive-room",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find Hendricks's notes. They are comprehensive \u2014 he did everything right, identified every significant point of disagreement between the maps, conducted a partial field verification, and wrote a paper that would have been the definitive treatment. The paper was never published but the notes are here, complete. You are not the first person to make this discovery. You are the first person positioned to finish what Hendricks started.",choices:[{label:"Use Hendricks's field data as a foundation for your own verification.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Acknowledge Hendricks formally in any publication from the start.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Find out who stopped Hendricks and whether the same forces are still active.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"archive-room",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Hendricks visited the coastal site in 1885 and found habitation evidence at the second map's coordinates \u2014 pottery sherds, structural remains, the characteristic layout of a trading settlement. He photographed it. His photographs are in the notes. The physical evidence was documented a hundred and forty years ago and is presumably still there.",choices:[{label:"Hendricks found it. You just need to find it again, with better tools.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"research-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You draft an acknowledgment section for the eventual paper: 'The primary analysis was conducted by Dr. G. Hendricks in 1887, whose work was suppressed before publication. This paper completes his finding.' The acknowledgment is not legally binding at this stage, but it's on record. When you eventually publish, Hendricks will be credited.",choices:[{label:"His credit secured. Now find the field evidence.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"research-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Geographical Society that stopped Hendricks in 1887 has been through several reorganizations. Its successor institution is the body that reviewed your permits. The Varro Foundation was established in 1921 with significant funding from the descendants of Caprini's original trading consortium. The same network that commissioned the false map is still, in an attenuated form, protecting it.",choices:[{label:"A hundred-year-old network protecting a fraud. That's the context.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"coastal-site",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Field verification. You're at the coordinates given by the second map for the coastal settlement. Hendricks was right \u2014 the evidence is here: structural remains, a pottery scatter, a collapsed wall that a structural archaeologist would date to the correct period. The Varro's coordinates for the same settlement are fifty-two kilometers north. There is nothing there. You photograph everything for four hours.",choices:[{label:"The physical evidence is decisive. Document it with maximum rigor.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Get an archaeologist to co-sign the field observations \u2014 your expertise isn't archaeology.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Find the features Hendricks photographed in 1885 and match them.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"coastal-site",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maximum rigor: GPS logging, photogrammetric survey, pottery sherd sampling, structural measurement. Every piece of evidence documented to a standard that would hold up to the most skeptical expert review. The Varro Foundation will challenge everything \u2014 give them nothing challengeable.",choices:[{label:"Evidence documented beyond challenge. Continue the site survey.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"coastal-site",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You call a colleague in archaeology. She joins you within a day \u2014 she's been following your work since you mentioned the second map. She surveys the site independently and provides a signed field assessment: the remains are consistent with a trading settlement of the appropriate period, at the coordinates given by the second map. Her signature carries weight yours doesn't.",choices:[{label:"Independent expert, signed assessment. The evidence is now multi-authored.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"coastal-site",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Hendricks's 1885 photographs show three specific structural features: a collapsed arch, a curved wall section, a threshold stone with an unusual profile. You find all three within an hour. They are weathered by an additional hundred and forty years but recognizable. The continuity between Hendricks's documentation and yours is the strongest possible confirmation.",choices:[{label:"Hendricks's photographs and yours of the same features. The case is complete.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"coastal-site",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You leave the coastal site with a complete field record. The Varro is wrong about this settlement's location. The physical evidence is at the second map's coordinates. The Varro's error is systematic, not accidental, and its creator was paid by interests that benefited from the misdirection. The case is made. The question now is how to make it public.",choices:[{label:"Publish through a journal \u2014 the peer review process is the right one.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Present at a major conference first \u2014 establish the finding publicly before publication.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Contact academic journalists \u2014 the story is significant enough for broader coverage.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You submit to the leading journal in historical cartography. The editor responds in a week: this is a significant finding that requires careful review. She assigns three reviewers. Two respond within a month with positive assessments. The third \u2014 you learn later \u2014 was nominated by the Varro Foundation. His review is hostile and technically thin. The editor overrules him.",choices:[{label:"Overruled. The paper is accepted.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You present at the Historical Geography Conference. Three hundred specialists in the room, the full case laid out over forty minutes. The questions are rigorous and you answer every one. The Varro Foundation's representative in the audience \u2014 you recognize him \u2014 asks one question that attempts to undermine the materials dating. Your materials scientist, in the audience, answers it herself.",choices:[{label:"The conference establishes the finding publicly. Publication follows.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A science journalist at a major publication covers the story: suppressed finding from 1887, deliberate cartographic fraud, commercial interests protecting a false map for six centuries. The story goes wide. The Varro Foundation issues a statement calling it 'premature and sensational.' Three academics who know the field well publish responses supporting your evidence.",choices:[{label:"Public record established before the Foundation can act. The paper follows.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Varro Foundation's response to the publication is a combination of legal letters and academic challenges. The legal letters don't survive contact with your university's legal team \u2014 there's no defamation claim in demonstrating that a historical document is inaccurate. The academic challenges are addressed in a follow-up paper. Both papers are now in the permanent record.",choices:[{label:"Monitor the Foundation's response \u2014 they may escalate.",next:"b8a"},{label:"The academic community's response matters more than the Foundation's.",next:"b8b"},{label:"Make sure Hendricks's 1887 work is formally integrated into the record.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Foundation doesn't escalate beyond the initial academic challenges. Its board is reportedly divided \u2014 some members believe the Varro's reputation can survive as a historically significant document even if inaccurate. Others want to investigate the origins of the falsification. The organization is changing.",choices:[{label:"The Foundation is changing. Let the process run.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The academic community's response is, in summary: significant and plausible, requires further verification, but the evidence as presented is compelling. Two institutions announce they will revise the Varro-based research in their archives to note the dispute. Three expeditions that used the Varro as reference are re-examining their field notes in light of the second map's coordinates.",choices:[{label:"Field notes being re-examined. The implications are already spreading.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Hendricks's 1887 notes are submitted to the archive's permanent collection with proper cataloging \u2014 correctly this time. His paper, never published, is submitted to the same journal that published yours as a historical document of significance. The editor publishes it with a foreword explaining the suppression. Hendricks is formally in the record, a hundred and forty years late.",choices:[{label:"Hendricks is in the record. That was important.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A year after the initial finding. The papers are published, cited, and being debated. The Varro's status has shifted \u2014 not discarded, because it remains historically significant as a document, but the academic consensus now describes it as a deliberate falsification of commercial origin. The second map is being studied by cartographic historians across three institutions.",choices:[{label:"What happens to the expeditions that relied on the Varro?",next:"b9a"},{label:"The second map needs a proper name. Think about how it's designated.",next:"b9b"},{label:"The Foundation's evolution matters for the field's future.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two of the four expeditions that used the Varro as primary reference had documented anomalies at the points where it disagrees with the second map \u2014 anomalies they attributed to field error. Re-examined: the anomalies were the second map being correct. Their own field notes vindicate the second map from the outside.",choices:[{label:"Their anomalies were the truth. The evidence was always there.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The second map needs a proper designation. 'The Hendricks map' is proposed \u2014 it honors the first modern scholar who recognized its significance. The archive where it was found supports the designation. You propose it formally in a paper, noting that Hendricks's 1887 priority should be acknowledged. The designation is accepted by the field.",choices:[{label:"The Hendricks map. His name on the discovery that was his.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Varro Foundation reforms. Its new director \u2014 appointed after the board disputes \u2014 announces that the Foundation will support research into the Varro's historical context, including its commercial origins. She says: a document can be both historically significant and deliberately inaccurate, and the scholarship should address both. It's a reasonable position.",choices:[{label:"A reasonable position. The field moves forward.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"research-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The finding is complete. The Varro's false geography is established. The second map \u2014 the accurate one, older, deliberately hidden \u2014 is now in the record. Hendricks is acknowledged. The physical evidence at the coastal site is confirmed. What you found in a book that wasn't supposed to be in an archive has changed what's known about six centuries of cartographic history.",choices:[{label:"The catalog error was the beginning. Think about how you found it.",next:"end-accident"},{label:"The suppression story is as important as the finding.",next:"end-suppression"},{label:"The finding was made twice. Hendricks and you. That's the real story.",next:"end-hendricks"},{label:"The truth was in the archive the whole time. That changes how you think about archives.",next:"end-archive"}]},"end-accident":{id:"end-accident",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The catalog error. You were following an inconsistency that looked minor \u2014 a book in the wrong section, a ledger entry that didn't match. That error led you to the book, the book to the map, the map to the case. The person who miscatalogued the book in 1887 was hiding it. The person who followed the catalog error in the present was you. Two acts of careful attention, separated by a century and a half, make a discovery. You file your notes on the methodology under: 'the importance of following errors.'",choices:[]},"end-suppression":{id:"end-suppression",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The suppression is documented now as completely as the geographic finding: the 1887 attempt, the Geographical Society's involvement, the Varro Foundation's commercial origins, the hundred-year network of institutional protection for a fraudulent document. A historian of science writes the account as a case study in how false consensus persists. She cites your papers and Hendricks's notes. The suppression story becomes a reference case for people who study how knowledge gets buried.",choices:[]},"end-hendricks":{id:"end-hendricks",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The finding was made twice. Hendricks in 1887, you now. His version was suppressed; yours was not. The difference between those outcomes is partly luck and partly the structure of the institutions involved \u2014 the Varro Foundation in 2024 cannot do what the Geographical Society did in 1887. The academic record is harder to control now. Hendricks deserved the outcome you got. You got it partly because he preserved the evidence that made your case harder to challenge. He left the notes. You found them. Between you, the thing got done.",choices:[]},"end-archive":{id:"end-archive",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The truth was in the archive the whole time. In the miscatalogued book, in Hendricks's notes, in the librarian's correspondence, in the ledger that recorded the anonymous donation. Every piece of evidence needed to establish the case was in the archive \u2014 preserved, hidden, waiting for someone to follow the errors. You write a paper about archive methodology: how to find what's been deliberately lost. It is cited more than any paper you've written about cartography.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-second-opinion",packId:"detective",title:"The Second Opinion",subtitle:"The doctor said natural causes. He was wrong. Or he was lying.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"A wealthy man dies of heart failure during a medical procedure. His family hired you because the attending physician signed the death certificate within an hour and then left the country. The hospital says everything was routine. Nothing was routine.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Edmund Carver, sixty-seven, died on a Tuesday afternoon during a routine cardiac catheterization. The attending physician, Dr. Lomas, signed the death certificate at 4:18pm and boarded a flight to Lisbon at 7:30pm. The hospital has issued a statement calling the death an expected complication of a high-risk procedure. Carver's daughter hired you because her father's procedure had been risk-rated as low, and because Lomas hasn't answered his phone in eleven days.",choices:[{label:"Start with the hospital record. Get everything they'll give you.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Find Dr. Lomas. The quick exit is the first anomaly.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Talk to the family. Understand what they know before the hospital closes ranks.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"hospital",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The hospital's patient records manager provides the official discharge summary, the death certificate, and the procedure log. The procedure log shows the catheterization ran forty-two minutes \u2014 within normal range. The death certificate lists cause: ventricular fibrillation following catheterization. Standard enough language. But the pre-procedure risk assessment, which should be in the file, is missing.",choices:[{label:"Missing risk assessment is not routine. Push for the full file.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lomas has a Lisbon address connected to a private practice. He isn't responding to calls but his professional registration is still active. More useful: his colleague at the hospital, Dr. Ferris, covered Lomas's patients after the departure. Ferris requested Carver's full file the day after the death \u2014 and was told it was under administrative review. A colleague's routine file request blocked: that's not routine.",choices:[{label:"Ferris was blocked from the file. Find Ferris.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Carver's daughter says her father was healthy for his age \u2014 the catheterization was for investigation, not treatment. The pre-procedure assessment she received described the risk as 'low to moderate.' Low-to-moderate doesn't become ventricular fibrillation without something going wrong. She also says: Lomas was not her father's usual cardiologist. He was brought in six weeks ago at the hospital's suggestion.",choices:[{label:"Not the usual cardiologist, brought in six weeks before the death. Find his connection to the hospital.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three threads converge: a missing risk assessment document, a colleague blocked from the file, and a cardiologist brought in six weeks before the death who left the country the same day. These aren't individually conclusive. Together they're a pattern that requires explanation.",choices:[{label:"Find Dr. Ferris. He tried to access the file and was stopped.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Track down the missing risk assessment \u2014 where did it go?",next:"b2b"},{label:"Research Lomas's professional history before he arrived at this hospital.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Ferris meets you in a cafe near the hospital. He's careful. He says: Carver's procedure was not high-risk. He says he reviewed the pre-procedure data himself because Carver was referred to his department initially. He says: a ventricular fibrillation event during a low-risk catheterization is possible but highly unusual and requires investigation. He says: the file should not be under administrative review. He says: I don't know what happened but something did.",choices:[{label:"Ferris knows the medicine and he knows something is wrong. Find the missing document.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"hospital",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The risk assessment was filed digitally. You get the IT access log through a hospital administrator contact \u2014 the assessment was accessed and modified at 6:42pm the day of the death, two hours and twenty-four minutes after Lomas signed the death certificate. Modified, not deleted. Someone changed the risk rating after Carver was dead.",choices:[{label:"The risk assessment was modified after death. That's not administrative \u2014 it's evidence tampering.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lomas has a clean record professionally. But his career moved unusually \u2014 short stints at four hospitals in six years before joining this one. At each previous hospital, a patient death occurred within three months of his arrival. None investigated further. At two of those hospitals, Lomas departed quickly after the death. He's done this before.",choices:[{label:"A pattern of deaths and quick departures. Find whether Carver fits the pattern.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The modified risk assessment is the hardest evidence. The Lomas pattern is circumstantial but striking. Ferris's testimony establishes that the death was medically anomalous. You need to understand what motive could exist for killing a patient during a routine procedure.",choices:[{label:"Research Carver's financial situation. Who benefits from his death?",next:"b3a"},{label:"Find out who ordered the risk assessment modification and from which terminal.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Contact the families of patients who died at Lomas's previous hospitals.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Carver's estate: significant but not extraordinary. His daughter inherits most of it. His business partner, a man named Reeve, inherits a controlling stake in their joint property company. That stake was worth much more after a development approval that came through \u2014 the approval was signed the day before Carver's procedure. With Carver alive, the approval would have required his signature on the next step. Dead, Reeve controls it alone.",choices:[{label:"Reeve benefits significantly from the timing. Find his connection to Lomas.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"hospital",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The IT access log for the risk assessment modification: terminal 14, a workstation in the hospital's administrative wing. The login credentials belong to an account registered to a hospital administrator named Whitman. Whitman was the administrator who approved Lomas's appointment six weeks ago.",choices:[{label:"Whitman brought Lomas in and modified the file. Find Whitman.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two of the previous patient families are reachable. Both describe similar patterns: a sudden health deterioration during what should have been a safe procedure, a quick death, and then difficulty obtaining the full medical record. One family paid for an independent post-mortem that noted 'trace anomalies' in the blood work but lacked grounds for further investigation. The anomaly was an electrolyte imbalance consistent with potassium chloride administration.",choices:[{label:"Potassium chloride in the bloodwork. That's not a procedure complication \u2014 it's a method.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The picture is assembling. A physician with a pattern of patient deaths. A modified risk assessment. A hospital administrator who arranged the appointment. A business partner with a significant financial motive. And, from a previous case, a possible method: potassium chloride administered during a procedure, mimicking cardiac arrest.",choices:[{label:"Find the connection between Reeve and Lomas. They must have met somewhere.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Confront Whitman. He modified the file and arranged the appointment.",next:"b4b"},{label:"Get an exhumation order. Potassium chloride degrades but traces may remain.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Reeve and Lomas: you find the connection through a private members' club. Both listed as members for three years. A club that hosts around two hundred people whose paths cross regularly. A financial record in Reeve's accounts: a payment to a medical consulting firm \u2014 Lomas's registered company \u2014 for 'assessment services' six months before Carver's death. Seventy thousand pounds.",choices:[{label:"Payment six months before the death. The arrangement predates the procedure.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Whitman is visibly frightened when you arrive at his home. He says: I didn't know anyone would die. He says it before you've accused him of anything. He says: Reeve told me Lomas was a specialist who could be trusted to handle Carver's procedure efficiently. He says: I thought it was about expediting the medical review of the estate. He says: I thought Carver would be incapacitated, not dead.",choices:[{label:"He thought incapacitated, not dead. He's a witness, not the principal.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The exhumation order requires a magistrate's approval and a medical case supporting it. You build the case: modified risk assessment, Lomas's pattern, the previous blood work anomaly. The magistrate approves it. The exhumation and independent post-mortem take three weeks. The result: trace potassium elevation consistent with exogenous administration. Consistent is not conclusive, but it's more than circumstantial.",choices:[{label:"Trace evidence from the exhumation. Add it to the file and go further.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You have the core case: Reeve paid Lomas through a consulting arrangement six months before the death. Whitman arranged the hospital appointment. The risk assessment was modified after death. Lomas fled the country. Trace post-mortem evidence supports a method. The case is real. Getting it to the right authorities without it being suppressed is the next problem.",choices:[{label:"Go to the police. The evidence is sufficient for an investigation to open.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Find Lomas in Lisbon first. His testimony \u2014 willing or not \u2014 completes the case.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Go to the medical regulator first. They can act faster than criminal courts.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The detective inspector who takes the file is experienced and unhurried. He reads the summary carefully. He says: the post-mortem evidence is the weakest link. He says: Reeve's payment to Lomas is the strongest. He says: we'll open a formal investigation and request Lomas's return under the European arrest procedure. He says: this will take time. He says it like time is something he has.",choices:[{label:"Investigation opened. The time is the cost.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lomas is in Lisbon, at the address you found. He's not hiding \u2014 he's practicing, seeing private patients. When you knock on his door he answers it himself. He looks at you and says: I was expecting someone. He was expecting someone. He invites you in. He says: I want to tell you what happened. He says it with the relief of a man who has been waiting to say it.",choices:[{label:"He was expecting someone and he wants to talk. Listen carefully.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The General Medical Council's investigator takes the file seriously. The Lomas pattern \u2014 deaths at multiple hospitals, quick departures \u2014 is within their jurisdiction regardless of criminality. They open a fitness-to-practise investigation immediately and suspend Lomas's registration. The suspension triggers a flag that alerts Portuguese medical authorities. Lomas is now under two investigations.",choices:[{label:"Two investigations running. The GMC suspension changes Lomas's situation.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lomas tells you what happened \u2014 in Lisbon, or through an intermediary, or in a formal statement, depending on the path you took. His account: Reeve approached him through a private contact. The arrangement was presented as 'ensuring a smooth outcome' during the procedure. He was told the patient had significant cardiac risk factors that the official records understated. He administered a dose of potassium chloride during the catheterization, which induced the arrest. He was told this was a mercy \u2014 that Carver would have died within months anyway. He believed it. Or he chose to.",choices:[{label:"Lomas chose to believe a convenient story. Assess what he's willing to provide formally.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Ask who the private contact was that connected Reeve to him.",next:"b6b"},{label:"The mercy framing is a lie \u2014 Carver was healthy. Confront Lomas with the pre-procedure data.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Lomas will provide a full statement in exchange for cooperation credit. His lawyer negotiates this with the prosecution over two weeks. The statement names Reeve explicitly, describes the arrangement in detail, and provides the contact information for the intermediary. It is a complete account from the person who administered the fatal dose. It is also the account of someone who knew what he was doing.",choices:[{label:"Full statement, naming everyone. The case proceeds.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The private contact was a man named Harker \u2014 a fixer who connects people with particular needs to people with particular capabilities. Harker is a thread that, when pulled, unravels more than this single case. His client list, obtained through legal process, includes three other arrangements that follow the same pattern. Carver was not Lomas's first arranged death through this route.",choices:[{label:"Harker is a professional intermediary for medical murders. The investigation expands.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Confronted with the actual pre-procedure data, Lomas's face changes. He says: I was told the records were incomplete. He says: Reeve told me the real condition was more serious than what was documented. He says: I believed him. You say: why would you believe a businessman over a medical record? He has no answer. He knows why. Money.",choices:[{label:"He knew. He chose to believe what he was paid to believe.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The investigation expands as it deepens. Reeve is arrested. Lomas is arrested in Lisbon through the European warrant. Whitman cooperates fully, providing records of his communications with Reeve. Harker \u2014 if your investigation reached him \u2014 gives the police an entirely different category of case. The Carver family is informed of the full picture. The daughter calls you and doesn't say anything for a long time.",choices:[{label:"Ask the police what charges are being brought.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Make sure the hospital's institutional role is being investigated too.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Think about the Lomas pattern \u2014 the other hospital deaths.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Charges: Reeve \u2014 conspiracy to commit murder. Lomas \u2014 murder. Whitman \u2014 conspiracy, reduced to accessory after his cooperation. Harker, if implicated \u2014 conspiracy. The prosecution says the potassium chloride evidence, combined with the Lomas statement and the Reeve payment, is sufficient for trial. The modified risk assessment goes to obstruction charges.",choices:[{label:"Full charges. The trial is the next stage.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"hospital",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The hospital's board is being investigated for governance failures \u2014 the ease with which Whitman appointed Lomas without standard credential verification, the administrative review that blocked Ferris's file request. The CQC opens a separate investigation into patient safety processes. The hospital is not directly complicit but its systems were exploited. That's a different kind of failure.",choices:[{label:"Institutional failure as enabler. That finding matters for prevention.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The other hospital deaths. Two families now have investigations opened based on your pattern evidence. One exhumation confirms similar post-mortem findings to Carver's. The Lomas case is not the Carver case \u2014 it's a pattern of arranged deaths over five years. The full scope is something the police will spend years establishing.",choices:[{label:"Five years of arranged deaths. The case is much larger than one client.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The trial preparation takes eight months. You are called as a witness to present the investigation timeline and the documentary evidence you gathered. Your role was to find the first thread \u2014 the pattern, the payment, the modified file. The prosecution built the criminal case on your foundation. That's the right division of labor.",choices:[{label:"Prepare your witness statement carefully.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Check in with Carver's daughter before the trial begins.",next:"b8b"},{label:"Follow the Harker thread \u2014 is it running independently?",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your witness statement covers the investigation sequence, the evidence chain, and the sources. You write it so that a jury without technical knowledge can follow every step. The prosecution's barrister reviews it and says: this is a clear document. She says: the jury will be able to follow this. That's the standard for witness evidence in a murder trial.",choices:[{label:"Statement ready. The trial begins.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"detective-interview",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Carver's daughter says she's ready for the trial. She says: I hired you because something was wrong and I didn't know what. She says: now I know what. She says it with the quality of someone who has been living with an unbearable uncertainty and prefers a bearable grief. You say: the trial will be difficult. She says: I know. She says: I'm ready.",choices:[{label:"She's ready. The trial begins.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"detective-archive",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Harker investigation is running independently through a specialist organized crime unit. It's larger than your case and slower. Harker's client list represents years of arranged deaths for financial and legal advantage. Your case against Reeve and Lomas is the first to reach trial \u2014 the prosecution wants a conviction established before the larger Harker case goes forward.",choices:[{label:"First conviction, then the larger case. That's the sequence.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The trial runs fourteen days. Reeve's defence is vigorous \u2014 the payment was legitimate consulting, Lomas's statement is self-serving, the post-mortem evidence is inconclusive. Lomas's defence is that he believed the mercy framing. The prosecution's case is built on the totality: payment, pattern, modified file, Whitman's cooperation, post-mortem traces, and Lomas's own statement.",choices:[{label:"Reeve's defence is vigorous. What does the jury seem to make of it?",next:"b9a"},{label:"The Lomas defence \u2014 believed the mercy framing \u2014 is the weakest point. Why?",next:"b9b"},{label:"Wait for the verdict without trying to read it.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The jury is attentive through the financial evidence and particularly attentive during Whitman's testimony, which is delivered with the detail and specificity of someone who has been cooperating with investigators for eight months. Reeve's barrister challenges Whitman's credibility extensively, which is standard. The jury watches Reeve during Whitman's testimony. Reeve is still.",choices:[{label:"Still means controlled. The jury noticed.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The mercy framing defence fails for a specific reason: Carver's actual pre-procedure records, unmodified, show no serious cardiac risk. Lomas claims he was shown different records. Those different records don't exist. A doctor who accepts patient data from a businessman instead of a medical file and then administers a fatal dose is not deceived \u2014 he's complicit. The jury is told this clearly.",choices:[{label:"The prosecution made the distinction clearly. Wait for the verdict.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Fourteen days and then the jury retires. You wait. The waiting is its own kind of work \u2014 every instinct to predict is just anxiety wearing the clothes of analysis. You sit with the case file and think about what you couldn't prove, what the defence challenged effectively, what the jury will weigh. Then you stop and wait.",choices:[{label:"The verdict arrives.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"detective-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Fourteen days of trial. Three days of jury deliberation. The verdict on Reeve: guilty of conspiracy to commit murder. The verdict on Lomas: guilty of murder. Whitman receives a suspended sentence for accessory, reflecting his cooperation. The sentences are announced. Carver's daughter is in the public gallery. You are not.",choices:[{label:"Guilty on both counts. The case is complete.",next:"end-conviction"},{label:"Lomas convicted, Reeve acquitted on conspiracy \u2014 insufficient direct link.",next:"end-partial"},{label:"Both convicted. The Harker case will follow.",next:"end-harker"},{label:"The conviction matters but the prevention matters more. Think about what changes.",next:"end-reform"}]},"end-conviction":{id:"end-conviction",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Both convicted. Reeve: fourteen years. Lomas: life, minimum twenty. Whitman: suspended. The hospital's governance review results in a significant overhaul of credential verification and administrative oversight. Ferris, who was blocked from the file and tried to do the right thing, is appointed head of the department. Carver's daughter calls you the day after the sentencing. She says: thank you for finding the thread. You say: you found it. You came to me because you knew something was wrong. She says: yes. She says it like it cost something.",choices:[]},"end-partial":{id:"end-partial",scene:"ending-neutral",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"Lomas convicted of murder. Reeve acquitted \u2014 the jury found the payment evidence insufficient to prove conspiracy beyond reasonable doubt. Reeve's barrister built an effective case around the consulting arrangement's legitimate surface. The acquittal is the gap between what you know and what can be proven. Lomas is sentenced to life. Reeve leaves court without looking at the gallery. The Harker investigation continues. There may be another route to Reeve. You note this and keep the file.",choices:[]},"end-harker":{id:"end-harker",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Both convicted. Reeve and Lomas are sentenced. Nine months later, the Harker case comes to trial with Carver as the anchor precedent. Harker is convicted of conspiracy in four arrangements over seven years, two resulting in death. The other deaths are investigated. Two more families receive verdicts. The scale of what Lomas and Harker ran together is, when fully revealed, more than anyone expected when a missing risk assessment first flagged a problem.",choices:[]},"end-reform":{id:"end-reform",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The conviction establishes the case. What changes: hospital credential verification protocols are tightened nationally following the CQC review. The GMC introduces a new flag system for physicians with multiple patient deaths across different institutions. Ferris, who identified the anomaly and was blocked, writes the new protocol's medical section. Carver's death, and the investigation that followed, becomes the reason the system catches the next Lomas before he reaches a patient. That matters more than the sentence, though the sentence matters too.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-siege",packId:"medieval",title:"The Siege",subtitle:"They've been at the walls for thirty days. You have a way to end it. But the cost.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You are the castellan defending a city under siege. Your walls are holding. Your food will run out in three weeks. A messenger has come with a peace offer\u2014not from the besieging army, but from the city's own council. They want to surrender. You have to decide if a compromise is a betrayal or a survival.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"castle-walls",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Thirty days under siege. Lord Vayne's forces have established a full encirclement. Your walls have held, your garrison has performed, and you have lost forty-three people \u2014 some to arrows, some to dysentery, some to the grinding attrition of an enclosed city in late autumn. The city council has met without you and sent a messenger to Lord Vayne's camp. When you found out, you called them to account. They say they sent for peace terms. You say that was your decision to make. The council says: your decision nearly cost us everything.",choices:[{label:"Hear the peace terms before responding to the council's act.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Respond to the council's act first. Chain of authority matters.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Assess the military situation honestly before deciding anything.",next:"b1c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The terms Lord Vayne offered through the council: the city yields peacefully, the garrison lays down arms and departs safely, the lord's household is subject to terms of surrender, and the civilian population is unharmed. It's a generous offer for a besieging force. Generous enough to be suspicious.",choices:[{label:"Too generous. Find out what Vayne actually wants from this city.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You call the council and tell them clearly: negotiations with the besieging force are your responsibility as castellan. The council's senior member, Merchant Aldous, says: 'We have children dying of cold. We have food for three weeks. You did not act, so we did.' He says it without apology, which you respect even though you cannot allow it.",choices:[{label:"He's not wrong about the food. Understand the terms they negotiated.",next:"s2"}]},b1c:{id:"b1c",scene:"castle-walls",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your master of stores confirms: three weeks of adequate food, five of severe rationing. Your captain of the guard says: the walls are sound but the garrison is exhausted. Your physician says: disease is the greater threat now, not arrows. You walk the walls at dawn and count the fires in Vayne's camp. He has four times your number and no reason to assault when he can simply wait.",choices:[{label:"He's waiting because time is his ally. Understand what he actually wants.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You need to understand Lord Vayne's actual objective. He has besieged this city for thirty days at considerable cost. The peace terms he offered are generous. Something about this city or this moment is valuable enough for him to offer terms rather than wait for starvation.",choices:[{label:"Send a scout to gather intelligence on Vayne's situation and intentions.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Request a parley. Meet Vayne yourself and read him directly.",next:"b2b"},{label:"Ask the council what they learned when they sent their messenger.",next:"b2c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"castle-walls",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your scout \u2014 a woman who can move through Vayne's camp as a laundress \u2014 returns with useful intelligence. Vayne is expecting reinforcements for his own overlord's campaign by spring. He needs this siege resolved before he has to march. If he doesn't take this city by winter's end, he loses a significant opportunity elsewhere. His generosity is a deadline.",choices:[{label:"His generosity is a deadline. He needs this resolved.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"parley-ground",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Vayne meets you on horseback between the lines. He's practical and direct: 'I need this resolved before spring.' He says it without pretense. He says: 'Your people suffer for a cause your lord cannot win. I offer generous terms because I need to move, not because I am soft.' He says: 'I will not be so generous if I have to wait another thirty days.'",choices:[{label:"He's told you his leverage. Now he knows you know it. Think carefully.",next:"s3"}]},b2c:{id:"b2c",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Aldous says Vayne's representative was direct: the terms are time-limited. Vayne needs the city before he has to answer a summons from his own overlord in spring. He's offering generous terms now because he can't afford to be here in three months. The council understood this as opportunity. You understand it as leverage.",choices:[{label:"Vayne's deadline is your leverage. Use it.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Vayne needs this resolved by spring. That gives you negotiating power that a thirty-day siege might not suggest. The terms offered are the floor, not the ceiling. The question is what you're negotiating for \u2014 survival of the city, protection of specific people, or a continued defense that preserves honor at cost to everyone.",choices:[{label:"Negotiate better terms using Vayne's deadline as leverage.",next:"b3a"},{label:"The current terms are already good. Accepting them is not failure.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Find out if there is any possibility of relief from your lord's forces.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"parley-ground",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You send word to Vayne: the current terms are acceptable in principle but you require guarantees \u2014 in writing, witnessed by a churchman \u2014 that the city's merchant quarter will not be taxed above the standard levy for five years, and that three specific families, including the council members who opened negotiations, will be specifically protected. Vayne considers for two days. He agrees to the written guarantees.",choices:[{label:"Written guarantees, witnessed. Better terms secured.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The terms as offered: garrison departs safely, civilians unharmed, city yields peacefully. You have seen sieges that ended worse in every way. The council is right that the walls can't hold forever. Accepting these terms saves the people behind them. That is not nothing.",choices:[{label:"Not nothing. Consider how to accept with dignity.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"castle-walls",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your fastest rider returned yesterday: your lord's forces are engaged elsewhere and cannot relieve the city. Your lord sent no rescue because your lord cannot spare the forces. The defense that has cost forty-three lives has been sustained without any possibility of relief. Your garrison has been holding a city for a cause that cannot reach them.",choices:[{label:"No relief is coming. The decision to negotiate was already made by circumstances.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The negotiation is moving toward resolution. The terms are acceptable or improved. The military reality supports negotiation. The civilian population needs the siege to end. What remains is the question of how you personally navigate the surrender \u2014 your oath, your honor, your sense of duty to the lord who sent you here.",choices:[{label:"Your duty is to the people in this city, not only to your lord. Act accordingly.",next:"b4a"},{label:"Request a formal release from your oath before negotiating further.",next:"b4b"},{label:"The garrison has fought honorably. Their departure with arms and dignity matters.",next:"b4c"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your duty: you were sent to hold this city and protect its people. You have held it for thirty days, which has cost Vayne considerably and gained your lord whatever political capital the defense was worth. The people are your responsibility too. A castellan who holds the walls until the last child starves has not discharged his duty \u2014 he has discharged only its most visible part.",choices:[{label:"Duty to the people as well as the walls. Proceed with the negotiation.",next:"s5"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You send a rider to your lord with the full situation: no relief possible, three weeks of food, Vayne's terms. You request a formal instruction. The rider will take ten days round trip. In ten days your food situation will be critical. You make your plans on the assumption that the instruction, when it comes, will be: do what you must.",choices:[{label:"Make the plans on the assumption. Ten days is a deadline too.",next:"s5"}]},b4c:{id:"b4c",scene:"castle-walls",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The garrison's terms: your soldiers have fought well and deserve to leave with their arms, their dignity, and their pay to date. Vayne's offer already includes safe departure for the garrison. You push for one addition: your garrison captain and his family to be included in the specific protections, not just the general civilian terms. Vayne agrees.",choices:[{label:"The captain's family protected. The garrison's terms are right.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The negotiation is in its final stage. The terms are agreed or near-agreed. Your personal position is clarified. The garrison knows what's happening. The council, for once, is waiting for your lead. What remains: the formal act of surrender and the transition of authority.",choices:[{label:"The council should be part of the formal surrender. They started this.",next:"b5a"},{label:"You will handle the formal surrender yourself, as castellan. It's your responsibility.",next:"b5b"},{label:"Manage the transition carefully \u2014 Vayne's forces entering a city require careful handling.",next:"b5c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Aldous and the council are present at the formal surrender meeting. You include them deliberately: they opened negotiations, they speak for the civilian population, and their presence legitimizes what's happening as a civilian decision as well as a military one. Aldous says, afterward, that he hadn't expected to be there. You say: 'You made this possible. You should see it through.'",choices:[{label:"He made it possible. He sees it through.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"parley-ground",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You handle the surrender yourself. You meet Vayne at the city gate with the terms document, witnessed and sealed. You open the gate yourself. You watch your garrison march out with their arms, in order, heads up. Vayne watches them with the expression of a commander who respects a well-run defense even in defeat. He says: 'Your walls were held as long as they could be.' You say: 'Yes.'",choices:[{label:"As long as they could be. That's the honest assessment.",next:"s6"}]},b5c:{id:"b5c",scene:"city-streets",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The transition of authority is the dangerous moment. You work with Vayne's captain to manage the entry of forces into the city: a limited number, on a defined route, with your garrison's officers present during the initial occupation to ensure no opportunism. Vayne's forces are disciplined. Three incidents in the first day, all addressed immediately. The transition is controlled.",choices:[{label:"Controlled transition. The city passes peacefully.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The surrender is complete. The city is under Vayne's authority. Your garrison has departed with their arms and dignity. The civilians are unharmed. The terms are being honored. You sit in the castle hall that is no longer yours and think about the thirty-one days you defended it and the day you surrendered it.",choices:[{label:"Think about whether you made the right decision.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Think about what the council did and what it means.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Think about what comes next for you.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The right decision: you defended for thirty-one days, which was longer than anyone expected, which cost Vayne considerably, and which may have political value for your lord in ways you won't see for months. You then negotiated terms that protected the civilian population and preserved the garrison's honor. The city will survive. The people will survive. The walls will have a new flag. Those are the facts of it.",choices:[{label:"The facts of it. That's enough to work with.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The council acted outside the chain of command and they were right that something needed to happen. They were wrong in how they did it. Both things are true simultaneously. You will not punish them for it \u2014 Vayne's protections include them, and anyway, punishing people for saving their city from unnecessary suffering is not justice. You will not forget it either.",choices:[{label:"Both things true simultaneously. Remember but don't punish.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"castle-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"What comes next: you must report to your lord, who did not send relief and now has a city under enemy control. That conversation will be difficult. You will tell the truth: you held thirty-one days, you received no relief, and you negotiated terms that protected civilian lives. What your lord does with that truth is his decision.",choices:[{label:"Tell the truth. Your lord decides the rest.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You are on the road to your lord's seat, with your remaining officers and a report you have written carefully. Behind you, the city is adjusting to its new authority. You have a week of travel to organize your thoughts and decide what exactly you believe about the thirty-one days.",choices:[{label:"The defense was honorable. The terms were wise. That's the report.",next:"b7a"},{label:"The council's intervention was the decisive act. Acknowledge it honestly.",next:"b7b"},{label:"The absence of relief was the central fact. That is what your lord must answer for.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your report is clear and professional: the defense, the losses, the terms, the outcome. You include your assessment that the garrison performed with distinction and that the terms secured were better than the military situation warranted, due to Vayne's need to resolve the siege quickly. A competent assessment of a difficult situation.",choices:[{label:"Competent and true. Deliver it.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your report acknowledges the council's intervention as a factor: they opened negotiations independently, which created a channel that you were able to use to improve the terms. You write it in terms of outcome rather than procedure. The outcome is good. The procedure was irregular. You let your lord assess the weight of each.",choices:[{label:"Outcome and procedure, both on record. Let the lord weigh them.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"road",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The absence of relief: you include it in the report without accusation. Your scout's intelligence, the timeline of the request for relief and the response, the military situation as it was when you made the decision to negotiate. The report makes clear that the decision was made in the context of no rescue coming. That context belongs in the record.",choices:[{label:"It belongs in the record.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your lord receives your report in a hall that is warm and well-provisioned. He reads carefully. He is not angry \u2014 he's calculating the political implications of a thirty-one day defense and a negotiated surrender. He asks questions. You answer them honestly. He says: 'You held longer than I expected.' You say: 'The walls were good and the garrison was better.' He nods. He's working through something.",choices:[{label:"Ask what he intends to do with the city now.",next:"b8a"},{label:"Ask whether he considers the surrender a failure.",next:"b8b"},{label:"Wait. Give him time to reach his conclusions.",next:"b8c"}]},b8a:{id:"b8a",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"He says the city can be reclaimed in time \u2014 Vayne's position is temporary and his overlord's campaign will either succeed or fail and either outcome creates opportunity. He says: 'The thirty-one day defense is politically valuable. It cost Vayne in ways that the diplomats can use.' He says it with the satisfaction of a man who sees the whole board.",choices:[{label:"The defense has political value. He's already using it.",next:"s9"}]},b8b:{id:"b8b",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your lord says: 'A surrender that protects the civilian population and preserves the garrison is not a failure. A garrison destroyed and a city sacked would have been.' He says: 'I sent no relief because I had no relief to send. That is a failure of resources, not of judgment.' He says it with the care of a lord who understands that honest assessment is what you need from him right now.",choices:[{label:"Honest assessment received. Neither of you failed the other.",next:"s9"}]},b8c:{id:"b8c",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You wait. He reads the report again. Then he says: 'You made the decisions I would have made.' He says it like he's checked something against a standard. He says: 'The council's action was irregular. Their outcome was fortunate. I will not punish fortunate outcomes.' He says: 'What do you need?'",choices:[{label:"He asked what you need. Tell him.",next:"s9"}]},s9:{id:"s9",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your lord has assessed the situation. He considers the surrender manageable in the long political term. He has no quarrel with your decisions. The garrison is being re-formed for other duties. The city, under Vayne's control, is functioning under the terms you negotiated. The situation is stable.",choices:[{label:"Ask about the garrison captain whose family you secured protections for.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Think about your own position going forward. What command comes next?",next:"b9b"},{label:"The thirty-one day defense will be discussed and judged. Think about that.",next:"b9c"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your lord confirms the garrison captain will be reassigned with rank and pay maintained. His family, under Vayne's protections, is unharmed and will be able to join him if they choose. Your push to include the captain's family in the specific protections was worthwhile. Small decisions protect specific people.",choices:[{label:"Small decisions protect specific people. That's what command is, at the level where it matters.",next:"s10"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your next command: your lord has a frontier post that needs an experienced castellan. It's a smaller appointment than the city, but it's a real appointment from a lord who trusts your judgment. You take it. The frontier post has a wall that needs assessment and a garrison that needs discipline. You know how to do those things.",choices:[{label:"A frontier post and work you know how to do. Good enough.",next:"s10"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The defense will be discussed in terms of honor: some will say you held too long, others that you surrendered too soon, and neither group will have been there to count the forty-three dead or watch children survive on rationed grain. You decide not to argue with either group. The people who were there know what happened. That's sufficient.",choices:[{label:"The people who were there know. That's sufficient.",next:"s10"}]},s10:{id:"s10",scene:"lords-hall",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A month after the surrender. The city is stable under Vayne's authority, the terms holding. Your garrison is reassigned. Your lord is using the thirty-one day defense in his diplomatic correspondence. You have a new appointment. The forty-three people you lost during the siege have names that you have written in your own record, which you keep separate from the official one.",choices:[{label:"The names in your own record. That's the weight of command.",next:"end-names"},{label:"The terms held. That was what you could ensure.",next:"end-terms"},{label:"A month later and the decision was right. That's what matters.",next:"end-right"},{label:"The frontier post. New walls, new work, same duty.",next:"end-frontier"}]},"end-names":{id:"end-names",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"Forty-three names in your own record. You read them once a month, not as ritual but as reminder. The castle was held for thirty-one days and then yielded under terms that preserved the people behind the walls. The forty-three were lost in the holding, not in the yielding. That distinction matters to you. The official record says what the official record says. Your record says the names.",choices:[]},"end-terms":{id:"end-terms",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The terms held. Six months after the surrender, Vayne still occupies the city and the terms are still in force: the civilian population is unharmed, the merchant quarter is taxed at the agreed rate, the protected families are protected. The piece of paper you negotiated with a deadline and a deadline is the thing that makes the difference for two hundred families who went to sleep each night for thirty-one days not knowing if they would survive. Paper can do that, sometimes. You've seen worse outcomes from better walls.",choices:[]},"end-right":{id:"end-right",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"A month later the decision was right. Six months later it still is. A year later, when Vayne's overlord's campaign fails and the political situation shifts and the city changes hands again through treaty rather than siege, the terms from your surrender are part of the legal argument that establishes continuity of civilian protections. Something you negotiated under pressure with a man who needed to move by spring is cited in documents you will never read. That's how it works sometimes.",choices:[]},"end-frontier":{id:"end-frontier",scene:"ending-dawn",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The frontier post has walls that are worse than the city's were and a garrison that is better than any you've commanded before. Small fighters, experienced, who know their ground. You spend the first month assessing everything and the second month making it better. The wall gets repaired. The discipline gets tightened. The stores get counted properly for the first time in three years. You do what you know how to do, in a smaller place, with more attention. The siege taught you something about what defending a place actually means. The frontier post is where you find out if you learned it.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-audition",packId:"highschool",title:"The Audition",subtitle:"One spot. Thirty people who want it. One secret that could end it all.",length:"Medium",free:!0,brief:"The spring musical has one lead role left and callbacks are in an hour. You've wanted this since freshman year. But you just found out your best friend Maya is also auditioning \u2014 and she didn't tell you until this morning.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The callback list went up at 7:48 AM and by 7:51 the hallway outside the auditorium had reorganized itself entirely. You found your name. Then you found Maya's. Maya, who told you last week she wasn't trying out because of AP schedule. Maya, who has been your best friend since seventh grade and who has a voice that makes people stop walking. Callbacks are in one hour.",choices:[{label:"Text Maya congratulations and see what she says.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Find Maya before homeroom and talk to her face to face.",next:"s2"},{label:"Say nothing and focus entirely on your own preparation.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She texts back in forty seconds: a string of exclamation marks and 'I was going to tell you, I changed my mind last night, are you okay?' Which is very Maya \u2014 auditioning for the same role as you and immediately asking if YOU are okay. You have forty minutes before callbacks. You tell her you're fine and you'll see her there.",choices:[{label:"Head to the auditorium.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You spend twenty minutes before homeroom running lines in the bathroom mirror. Mr. Osei, the drama teacher, stops you in the hall and says: 'Don't overthink it in there. Just be present.' He says this to you specifically, which might mean something or might mean nothing at all.",choices:[{label:"Head to the auditorium when the bell rings.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The auditorium has that particular quality of a space that is usually full of noise and is now very quiet. Eleven of you. Mr. Osei and the accompanist Ms. Park are in the third row with clipboards. Maya is at the far end of the waiting chairs. She gives you a wave that is genuine and also terrible because now you feel guilty for the three minutes you spent this morning hoping she'd dropped out. Order is alphabetical. You go seventh. Maya goes eighth.",choices:[{label:"Go sit with Maya and run lines together.",next:"s3"},{label:"Sit separately and stay in your own head.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Talk to one of the other people auditioning to distract yourself.",next:"b2b"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find a seat near the back and go through your music quietly. From across the room Maya has headphones in, eyes closed, lips moving. You've both been preparing. The difference is you've been preparing for three weeks and she changed her mind last night, which might matter and might not, because Maya is the kind of person that last night is enough for.",choices:[{label:"When your name is called, go to the stage.",next:"s4"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You end up talking to Jordan, a junior who was in the ensemble last year. Jordan is calm in a way you find either admirable or suspicious. They tell you Mr. Osei already knows who he wants and callbacks are just paperwork. You don't know if this is true but it does something to your nerves either way.",choices:[{label:"When your name is called, go to the stage.",next:"s4"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You sit next to Maya and she immediately shows you a rhythm breakdown for the bridge that she worked out at midnight \u2014 it's genuinely clever. 'I know this is weird,' she says. 'I'm sorry I didn't tell you earlier.' You tell her it's not weird. You're both aware that it is a little weird.",choices:[{label:"Ask her why she changed her mind about auditioning.",next:"b3a"},{label:"Tell her you hope the best person gets it and mean it.",next:"b3b"},{label:"Be honest \u2014 tell her this is hard and you both know it.",next:"b3c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She's quiet for a second. Then: 'My mom asked if I was going to try out and I said no because of AP, and she got this look, and I just \u2014 I didn't want the look to be right.' You understand this completely and it makes you feel worse and better at the same time.",choices:[{label:"When your name is called, go up.",next:"s4"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"'Same,' she says, and she means it. You both mean it. You're also both aware that one of you is going to get the part and the other is not, and whichever way it goes there will be a conversation afterward that will need to be had carefully.",choices:[{label:"When your name is called, go up.",next:"s4"}]},b3c:{id:"b3c",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maya is quiet for a moment. Then: 'Yeah. It is.' She doesn't apologize again and you don't ask her to. You sit together until your name is called and the silence between you is the kind that belongs to people who know each other well enough to not need to fill it.",choices:[{label:"When your name is called, go up.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"hs-stage",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The stage is the stage. The lights are the lights. Ms. Park gives you a nod and starts the intro. The auditorium is a black quiet beyond the first few rows. You can see Mr. Osei's pen. You've sung this song in your room approximately four hundred times. You know every word and every breath. The question is what you do with the knowledge now that it matters.",choices:[{label:"Sing it safe \u2014 exactly the way you rehearsed.",next:"s5a"},{label:"Take the risk you've been considering \u2014 a key change in the bridge.",next:"s5b"},{label:"Slow the second verse and let the words breathe.",next:"s5c"}]},s5a:{id:"s5a",scene:"hs-stage",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You sing it exactly the way you rehearsed. It is good. It is technically correct. Mr. Osei writes something. Ms. Park smiles the small smile she gives when something is competent. When you finish there is a silence that is polite rather than stunned. You walk back to your seat. Maya goes up next.",choices:[{label:"Watch Maya's audition.",next:"s6"}]},s5b:{id:"s5b",scene:"hs-stage",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The key change works. You feel it the second you do it \u2014 the room shifts, Mr. Osei's pen stops, Ms. Park raises an eyebrow and adjusts to follow you. You hold the final note a beat longer than rehearsed. Your hands are shaking slightly when you sit down, which is not bad shaking. Maya goes up next.",choices:[{label:"Watch Maya's audition.",next:"s6"}]},s5c:{id:"s5c",scene:"hs-stage",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You slow the second verse and the song opens up in a way you didn't predict in your room. The words mean more when there's space around them. Mr. Osei puts his pen down and just listens for a moment. When you finish he says 'Thank you' in a way that has some weight. Maya goes up next.",choices:[{label:"Watch Maya's audition.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maya sings it like she wrote it herself. There is a moment in the second chorus where she does something with the dynamics that is not in the sheet music and that Ms. Park follows without hesitation. When she finishes the silence is the stunned kind. Mr. Osei writes something longer than what he wrote for you. Then callbacks are over and everyone is told the cast list posts tomorrow.",choices:[{label:"Walk out with Maya.",next:"s7"},{label:"Hang back and try to read Mr. Osei's expression.",next:"b6a"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mr. Osei is already talking to Ms. Park with his back to you. Ms. Park glances your way. Whatever she sees in your expression makes her say something to him and he turns, and he gives you a brief nod before going back to his clipboard. You don't know what the nod means. You tell yourself it means something.",choices:[{label:"Walk out and find Maya.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"In the hallway Maya says: 'That was terrifying.' You say yeah. She asks how you think it went. You say you don't know. She says: 'I went up there and thought, what would I do if nobody was watching, and then I just did that.' You think about what you did up there and whether it was that or whether it was something smaller.",choices:[{label:"Tell Maya what you actually did \u2014 the choice you made.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Say you did your best and leave it at that.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Ask if she'd do it differently if she could go again.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You tell her about the key change, or the slow verse, or singing it safe \u2014 whichever you chose. Maya listens and says: 'That's real. That was a real decision.' You don't know yet if it was the right one. Neither does she. But the act of saying it out loud makes it feel like it belongs to you.",choices:[{label:"Go home and wait.",next:"s8"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maya nods. You walk out together and talk about something else entirely for twenty minutes and it almost works as a distraction. Then you go your separate ways and spend the evening checking your phone for a cast list that won't post until morning.",choices:[{label:"Wait for the morning.",next:"s8"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She thinks about it. 'No,' she says finally. 'Because if I thought about it again I'd stop doing the thing that worked.' You understand this. The best thing you did on that stage was also the thing you did without fully deciding to do it. That's either a lesson or just how it went today.",choices:[{label:"Go home and wait.",next:"s8"}]},s8:{id:"s8",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The cast list posts at 7:15 AM. You find out before school from the school app. Whatever it says, you and Maya are going to have a conversation this morning in the hallway outside the auditorium, and one of you is going to be holding something good and one of you is going to be holding something that needs to be okay.",choices:[{label:"You got the part. Maya didn't.",next:"s9a"},{label:"Maya got the part. You didn't.",next:"s9b"},{label:"You both got parts \u2014 different ones.",next:"s9c"}]},s9a:{id:"s9a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your name is at the top. Maya finds you before you find the list. She says 'I saw' before you can say anything. Her face is doing the thing faces do when they're being okay about something that isn't okay yet. You say her name. She says: 'You were better. I knew in the wings.' You don't know if this is completely true. It's still the right thing to say.",choices:[{label:"Tell her what it means to you that she said that.",next:"b9a"},{label:"Ask what she's going to do now.",next:"b9b"}]},b9a:{id:"b9a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'I wouldn't have taken the risk I took without you in the room.' This is true. Somehow having Maya there made you want to do the real thing instead of the safe thing. She smiles, real this time. 'Good,' she says. 'Use me wisely.'",choices:[{label:"Go to rehearsal.",next:"e1"}]},b9b:{id:"b9b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She says she'll come to every rehearsal and run lines with you and she means it and she does. By opening night she knows your part better than you do. The show is good. Her presence in the wings on opening night is the thing you remember most about it.",choices:[{label:"Opening night.",next:"e1"}]},s9b:{id:"s9b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maya's name is at the top. You find the list before she finds you and you take thirty seconds by yourself to be whatever you are before you go find her. She's waiting by your locker. 'I'm sorry,' she says. You tell her not to be, that she was better, and you mostly mean it. The part you don't mean yet is the part you'll catch up to.",choices:[{label:"Tell her you'll be at every rehearsal.",next:"b9c"},{label:"Ask Mr. Osei about your role in the ensemble.",next:"b9d"}]},b9c:{id:"b9c",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You tell her you'll come to every rehearsal and run her lines with her. She looks at you for a moment and then says: 'You don't have to.' You say: 'I know.' You do it anyway. By opening night you know her part better than your own, and there is something in that which doesn't feel like losing.",choices:[{label:"Opening night.",next:"e2"}]},b9d:{id:"b9d",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mr. Osei gives you the second role, which has a good arc and one song and two scenes with the lead. It's not what you wanted. It is a real part. You play it well enough that someone in the audience asks afterward if you were the lead.",choices:[{label:"Opening night.",next:"e2"}]},s9c:{id:"s9c",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Both your names are on the list \u2014 different roles. You got the lead. Maya got the role just below, which has its own weight and its own song and they share three scenes. You find each other at the same moment, both of you reading the same list, both of you processing.",choices:[{label:"Say something.",next:"b9e"}]},b9e:{id:"b9e",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Maya says: 'Well.' You say: 'Yeah.' Then she says: 'Our scenes are going to be very strange.' You say: 'Or very good.' She thinks about this. 'Or both.' Rehearsals start next week. You've spent the last three years doing everything together and you're about to spend the next six weeks doing that on a stage in front of people, which is either the best possible outcome or something you haven't fully thought through.",choices:[{label:"Start rehearsals.",next:"e3"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The show runs four nights. You are good in the role \u2014 not perfect, but good in the way of someone who made a real decision during their audition and carried it through to performance. Mr. Osei tells you afterward: 'You showed me what you could do. Now I know what to cast you in next year.' Maya is in the front row on closing night. You see her before the lights go down.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The show runs four nights and Maya is excellent in the lead role. You play your part well and the second role turns out to have more to it than you expected. On closing night, watching from the wings, you feel something you didn't predict: pride in something you helped build, even in a smaller way than you planned. The part you didn't get taught you something the part you wanted wouldn't have.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The show runs four nights and you and Maya are in each other's scenes and it is strange and very good, both things, as predicted. Mr. Osei tells the cast on closing night: 'There were two performances in this show that I'll remember for a while.' He doesn't say whose. You and Maya look at each other from across the stage and both know he means you.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-project",packId:"highschool",title:"The Project",subtitle:"Three weeks. One grade. Four people who cannot agree on anything.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"Your AP History group project is worth 30% of the semester grade. You have three weeks, four people, and a topic no one wanted. Someone is doing no work. Someone is redoing everyone else's work. And there's a primary source in the archive that could change everything.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The groups were assigned, not chosen. You are doing a semester-defining project with Dani, who color-codes everything and has already made a shared doc that is frankly intimidating; Marcus, who is smart and present for approximately sixty percent of meetings; and Priya, who said 'sure, whatever' when the topic was assigned and has said very little since. The topic is the 1921 Tulsa Race Massacre. Your teacher Ms. Chen mentions there are primary sources in the county archive that haven't been digitized. Nobody else reacts. You write it down.",choices:[{label:"Organize the first group meeting and establish a schedule.",next:"s2"},{label:"Go to the county archive before the first meeting.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Talk to Priya one-on-one before anything else happens.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The archive is in the basement of the county courthouse. The archivist, Dr. Holloway, raises an eyebrow when you explain the project. She brings out a folder: three letters from 1921, from a survivor named Elias Carr, never cited, never scanned. 'Students don't usually come in person,' she says. You photograph every page.",choices:[{label:"Bring this to the first group meeting.",next:"s2"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You catch Priya at her locker. She's not disengaged \u2014 she's wary. Her grandmother was from Tulsa. This topic is not abstract for her. She's been quiet because she's not sure she wants to do a school project on something that happened to her family. You ask if she'd want to help shape how the project handles it. Something shifts in her expression.",choices:[{label:"Set up the first group meeting with this in mind.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"hs-library",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"First meeting. Dani has a timeline, task breakdown, and color-coded responsibility chart. It's genuinely excellent. Marcus says 'wow' and mostly means it. Priya is more present than she was in class. The problem: Dani's plan doesn't include any primary sources. When you mention the archive, Dani's expression suggests she does not love things that aren't already in the plan. Marcus looks at his phone.",choices:[{label:"Push for the archive \u2014 primary sources are the whole point.",next:"s3"},{label:"Ask Priya what she thinks before pushing either way.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Accept Dani's plan and add the archive as an optional extension.",next:"b2b"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"hs-library",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Priya says: 'My grandmother knew people who were there. Every account I've seen from newspapers was wrong about almost everything.' The room goes quiet. Dani puts her pen down. Marcus stops looking at his phone. The project has just become something different than a grade.",choices:[{label:"Go to the archive together.",next:"s3"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"hs-library",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Dani accepts this immediately and adds 'Archive (optional)' in a subdued color. Marcus seems relieved. Priya looks at the chart for a long moment and then says quietly: 'It shouldn't be optional.' Dani looks at the chart. Then she changes the color.",choices:[{label:"Move forward with archive as a core component.",next:"s3"}]},s3:{id:"s3",scene:"hs-archive",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The four of you at the archive is a different experience than going alone. Dr. Holloway brings not just the Carr letters but a second folder \u2014 photographs from 1921 that have never appeared in any published account. Priya goes very still when she sees them. Marcus starts taking notes with a focus you haven't seen from him. Dani has abandoned the chart and is just writing.",choices:[{label:"Focus on the Elias Carr letters as the center of the project.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Ask Dr. Holloway if Priya's family name appears in any records.",next:"s4b"},{label:"Photograph everything and decide the focus back at school.",next:"s4c"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"hs-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Carr letters are the project. Three letters written in the weeks after the massacre describing things that didn't make it into any official record. You divide them \u2014 one each. Priya takes the third letter because she asks to. When she reads it she says: 'He's describing my great-grandmother's street.' Quietly. Not performing. Just stating a fact that has just become real.",choices:[{label:"Continue building the project around the letters.",next:"s5"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"hs-archive",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Dr. Holloway checks the records. There is a name \u2014 not Priya's family name, but a neighbor's name, in a property destruction record. Priya photographs it. She doesn't say anything for a while. Then: 'Can I write this section.' Not a question. The project now has something no other group will have \u2014 a present-day connection to a primary source.",choices:[{label:"Continue building the project.",next:"s5"}]},s4c:{id:"s4c",scene:"hs-library",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You photograph everything and reconvene in the library. You spread printouts across a table and look at what you have. It's a different project than the one in Dani's original chart \u2014 better, but harder to organize. Marcus says: 'What if we structure it around the gap \u2014 what the official record says versus what actually happened?' It's the best idea anyone has had. Dani starts a new chart.",choices:[{label:"Build the project around that structure.",next:"s5"}]},s5:{id:"s5",scene:"hs-library",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Week two. The project is taking shape. But Marcus hasn't submitted his section and it's four days overdue. He has draft notes \u2014 you've seen them \u2014 and they're good. He's just not finishing. Dani has rewritten your introduction without asking. Priya is working every night and her section is extraordinary. The project could be exceptional or it could fall apart on Marcus and on Dani redoing things that were already done.",choices:[{label:"Talk to Marcus privately about his section.",next:"s6"},{label:"Confront the Dani rewrite situation at the next group meeting.",next:"b5a"},{label:"Divide the remaining work again and set hard deadlines.",next:"b5b"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"hs-library",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"At the meeting you say: 'Dani, I need to ask about the introduction.' Dani immediately says she improved it. You say it wasn't hers to improve. Marcus goes very still. Priya watches. Dani says: 'If it helps the grade\u2014' You say: 'It's Marcus's work and it changes his argument.' The room is uncomfortable. Dani eventually agrees to restore the original with her edits tracked separately.",choices:[{label:"Talk to Marcus.",next:"s6"}]},b5b:{id:"b5b",scene:"hs-library",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You set hard deadlines: Marcus's section due in two days, Dani's edits consolidated by Friday. Marcus says 'yeah, sure' which you don't fully trust. Dani writes the new deadlines in three colors. You watch Marcus not look at the document he's supposed to be finishing.",choices:[{label:"Talk to Marcus privately.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find Marcus at his locker after school. You ask him what's going on. He's quiet for a moment. Then: 'My section is about something my family talks about. We're from Tulsa. I knew that when the topic was assigned and I didn't say anything because I didn't know if I wanted to say anything.' He's been processing the same thing as Priya, separately, in silence, which is why nothing is getting written.",choices:[{label:"Tell him about Priya \u2014 that she's navigating the same thing.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Ask what he needs to be able to finish it.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Offer to write his section with him rather than alone.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Marcus looks at you for a moment. Then: 'She's from Tulsa?' You say her grandmother was. He's quiet. The next day he sends Priya a message. They end up talking for an hour. His section is submitted that night. It references one of the Carr letters directly and connects it to a family story he'd never written down before.",choices:[{label:"Bring the project together in the final week.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"He says he needs to talk to his mom first. He goes home that night and calls her. The next day he comes to school with three handwritten pages and a photograph his mother texted him \u2014 a relative, 1921, Tulsa, that no one in the project knew about. His section writes itself from there.",choices:[{label:"Bring the project together in the final week.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You sit with Marcus in the library that evening and write it together. He talks and you type and ask questions and he realizes as he's talking what his argument actually is. By 9 PM he has a complete draft. It's the most personal section in the project and also the most analytically rigorous, which is what happens when someone writes about something they actually know.",choices:[{label:"Bring the project together in the final week.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Final week. The project is good \u2014 genuinely good. Marcus's section. Dani's structure. Priya's section on her family's connection to the primary sources is the best writing in the document. The problem: it's due in two days and Priya's section references a specific claim in the Carr letters that you now can't verify because the archive is closed for a maintenance day. The claim is central. Removing it weakens the argument.",choices:[{label:"Email Dr. Holloway directly and ask for help.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Reframe the section so the claim is a question rather than a statement.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Ask Ms. Chen for a one-day extension and explain exactly why.",next:"b7c"},{label:"Submit it as written \u2014 you trust the source.",next:"b7d"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Dr. Holloway emails back in three hours with a photograph of the specific passage \u2014 she'd been intending to scan it. The claim stands. The project is submitted on time, complete, fully sourced.",choices:[{label:"Submit the project.",next:"s8a"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You reframe: 'the letters suggest' rather than 'the letters confirm.' The argument holds, slightly softened. Priya looks at the change and says: 'Actually that's more honest. The archive isn't closed \u2014 it's just that so much hasn't been digitized yet.' You submit on time.",choices:[{label:"Submit the project.",next:"s8b"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Ms. Chen grants the extension without hesitation: 'The archive being closed is a real obstacle. Document it.' You do. The extra day is enough \u2014 Dr. Holloway confirms the passage by email. You submit one day late with a note about the archival verification process.",choices:[{label:"Submit the project.",next:"s8c"}]},b7d:{id:"b7d",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You submit as written. Ms. Chen flags the specific claim and asks for the citation within a week. You provide it when the archive reopens. She accepts it. The grade stands. The lesson is that trusting a source and being able to cite it are related but not the same thing.",choices:[{label:"Get the grade.",next:"s8d"}]},s8a:{id:"s8a",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Submitted on time. You wait for Ms. Chen's comments. The four of you have been a group for three weeks and ended up being something more than what the assignment required.",choices:[{label:"Get the grade.",next:"e1"}]},s8b:{id:"s8b",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Submitted on time with the honest framing. You wait.",choices:[{label:"Get the grade.",next:"e1"}]},s8c:{id:"s8c",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Submitted one day late with documentation. You wait.",choices:[{label:"Get the grade.",next:"e2"}]},s8d:{id:"s8d",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Submitted on time, citation to follow. You wait.",choices:[{label:"Get the grade.",next:"e2"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Ms. Chen's comment on the final draft: 'This is what primary source research looks like when it's done right. The Carr letters reference alone would be notable. The family connection is exceptional.' Full marks. You four look at each other across the room. Something happened during this project that was not about the grade and also produced an excellent grade.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"A very good grade, with a note that the sourcing is careful and the archival work is outstanding. What Ms. Chen doesn't write in the comment \u2014 but what she says in passing to you afterward \u2014 is that she's recommending the Carr letters for the school's historical archive collection. Dr. Holloway gets a call the next week. Something you found is going somewhere permanent.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The grade comes back high. But the thing you actually carry from this project isn't the grade. It's the afternoon in the archive when four people who were assigned to each other became something more useful than that. Marcus emails you six months later: he's applying to study history. He attaches a draft of the personal essay. It's about Tulsa.",choices:[]}}},{id:"the-transfer",packId:"highschool",title:"The Transfer",subtitle:"New school. Nobody knows you. You could be anyone.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You transferred junior year \u2014 the worst possible time. You have six weeks before social positions calcify for the rest of high school. You can reinvent yourself. But there's someone here who knew you before, and they've already noticed you.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"First day. Westbrook High has 1,400 students and a social ecosystem that has been the same for thirty years. You've been assigned a locker next to Bex, who immediately tells you where everyone sits at lunch and why. This information is useful. The other piece: Sam Chen, who you went to middle school with and who knows exactly why you left your last school, is in your second-period class.",choices:[{label:"Talk to Sam immediately \u2014 control the narrative.",next:"s2"},{label:"Avoid Sam and hope they don't bring anything up.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Follow Bex's lunch map and establish yourself before dealing with Sam.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Sam makes eye contact in second period and gives you a small wave that is carefully neutral. After class they fall into step beside you: 'I heard you were coming here. I'm not going to say anything.' You don't know if this is a promise or a negotiation. You thank them. They nod and walk the other way.",choices:[{label:"Establish yourself at lunch first.",next:"b1b"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"hs-cafeteria",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Bex's map is accurate. The landing-zone table \u2014 cross-curricular, no fixed identity. A guy named Otto immediately explains the school's unofficial hierarchy in a tone that finds it both ridiculous and fascinating. You listen. By the end of lunch you have enough information to make choices, which is what you wanted.",choices:[{label:"Talk to Sam after school.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Sam is at their locker. 'I meant what I said,' they say. 'I'm not here to bring your old school with you. That's yours to do with what you want.' You ask what they remember. They tell you: what happened in ninth grade, the fact that it wasn't your fault, and that you spent most of sophomore year trying to disappear. You ask what they're going to do with that. Sam says: 'Nothing. Unless you want to talk about it.'",choices:[{label:"Tell Sam the version of yourself you want to be here.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Ask Sam to treat you like a stranger \u2014 they don't know you.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Actually talk about what happened.",next:"s3c"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You tell Sam you want to be the person you were before ninth grade \u2014 the one who joined things, had opinions, didn't check every room before entering. Sam listens and says: 'That person is still there. I noticed it in second period \u2014 you answered a question and then looked like you regretted it.' You had. You hadn't realized anyone could see the regret.",choices:[{label:"Let Sam know you. See how it goes.",next:"s4"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Sam nods. 'Sure. We're strangers.' They close their locker and walk away. They do a convincing impression of someone who's never met you for the next three weeks \u2014 right up until you need them not to be a stranger, and they're there anyway, because Sam has always been quietly decent.",choices:[{label:"Find your footing at Westbrook.",next:"s4"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You tell Sam. In the hallway, five minutes before the bell. The condensed version \u2014 what happened, what you did and didn't do, what the school did and didn't do, why it took two years and a transfer to feel like a person again. Sam listens without interrupting. When you finish: 'I thought it was something like that. I'm glad you got out.' It's the right thing to say. You feel lighter by about a third.",choices:[{label:"Find your footing at Westbrook.",next:"s4"}]},s4:{id:"s4",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three weeks in. You have a loose orbit around Bex and Otto's lunch table. Doing well in classes. Raised your hand in AP English twice without wanting to take it back. Problem: someone at the table, a junior named Waverly, has started to probe. Not maliciously \u2014 it's just how Waverly is. But the questions are getting closer to the thing you haven't said yet: why you transferred, what your old school was like.",choices:[{label:"Tell Waverly a partial truth \u2014 you needed a change.",next:"s5a"},{label:"Ask Waverly why they want to know.",next:"s5b"},{label:"Deflect and redirect \u2014 you're good at this.",next:"s5c"},{label:"Tell the whole table everything.",next:"s5d"}]},s5a:{id:"s5a",scene:"hs-cafeteria",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"'I needed a change' is enough for Waverly, who immediately relates it to their own falling-out with a friend group sophomore year, and the conversation becomes a group conversation about fresh starts. The question never returns in the same form. You haven't lied. You haven't said everything. You've found the version of truth that lets you keep existing in this space.",choices:[{label:"Continue building your life at Westbrook.",next:"s6"}]},s5b:{id:"s5b",scene:"hs-cafeteria",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Waverly pauses. 'I don't know. I ask everyone things. It's a bad habit.' Then: 'You don't have to tell me anything.' Something about this \u2014 the acknowledgment that the questions are a habit and not a right \u2014 makes you trust Waverly more. You end up telling them a version that is more than you planned and less than everything, and it lands right.",choices:[{label:"Continue at Westbrook.",next:"s6"}]},s5c:{id:"s5c",scene:"hs-cafeteria",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You redirect Waverly onto something Otto said three minutes ago and Waverly, being Waverly, follows enthusiastically. You are very good at this \u2014 two years of practice. The skill will serve you in the short term and is a thing you'll eventually have to put down, but not today.",choices:[{label:"Continue at Westbrook.",next:"s6"}]},s5d:{id:"s5d",scene:"hs-cafeteria",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You tell them. The table goes quiet. Otto says 'that's messed up' with real feeling. Bex asks what happened to the other people involved. Waverly doesn't ask probing questions for the rest of the year. Sam, across the cafeteria, catches your eye and nods. You feel, for the first time in two years, like a person in the room rather than watching from a slight distance.",choices:[{label:"Continue at Westbrook.",next:"s6"}]},s6:{id:"s6",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Six weeks in. Something unexpected is happening: you like it here. The partial-credit version of yourself you've been presenting has started to feel less partial. Mr. Osei, who teaches AP English, asks you to submit a personal essay for the regional writing competition. The essay would need to be real \u2014 the judges can tell when something isn't. You'd have to decide what real means for you right now.",choices:[{label:"Write the essay about what brought you here.",next:"b6a"},{label:"Write about something unrelated to the transfer.",next:"b6b"},{label:"Ask Mr. Osei if you can write about someone else's experience instead.",next:"b6c"}]},b6a:{id:"b6a",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You write it. Not all of it \u2014 the version a stranger can read without needing the context. But the real shape of it: a person who left somewhere, arrived somewhere else, and found that the arriving was harder and better than expected. Mr. Osei reads the draft and says: 'This is the one. Don't change the ending.'",choices:[{label:"Submit the essay.",next:"s7"}]},b6b:{id:"b6b",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You write about something else \u2014 something that is genuinely yours but doesn't require the transfer as context. It's good writing. Technically good. Mr. Osei says: 'There's something careful about this. Like you're writing around something.' You say: 'Maybe.' He says: 'The competition judges can tell.' You decide what to do with that.",choices:[{label:"Revise and decide.",next:"s7"}]},b6c:{id:"b6c",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mr. Osei looks at you for a moment. 'You can. But the strongest essays are first-person. What would you be protecting yourself from?' You think about this for a day. Then you go back and tell him you'll write it first-person.",choices:[{label:"Write and submit.",next:"s7"}]},s7:{id:"s7",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The essay is submitted. The competition results are three weeks away. In the meantime, something else has shifted: Waverly has invited you to join the school newspaper, Otto has asked if you want to come to a thing on Friday, and Bex has started saving your seat. You are, without having announced it, somewhere. The question now is who you are here, and whether that person is also the person you were before ninth grade or someone new entirely.",choices:[{label:"Accept all of it \u2014 join in fully.",next:"b7a"},{label:"Take it slowly \u2014 one thing at a time.",next:"b7b"},{label:"Talk to Sam about what's happening.",next:"b7c"}]},b7a:{id:"b7a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say yes to all of it. The newspaper, the Friday thing, the saved seat. It's more than you've agreed to in two years and you can feel the old instinct \u2014 check the room, watch for exits \u2014 running alongside the newer one, which is just showing up. Mostly the newer one wins.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},b7b:{id:"b7b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You take one thing \u2014 the newspaper. It's enough. A weekly reason to be somewhere doing something, with people who are there for the same reason. The rest follows at its own pace, which turns out to be faster than you expected.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},b7c:{id:"b7c",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Sam says: 'You're doing the thing.' You ask what thing. 'The thing where you were before ninth grade. The person who joined stuff.' You say you're not sure if that's true yet. Sam says: 'I know. But I'm watching it happen from the outside, and it's definitely happening.'",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Junior year ends. You placed second in the regional writing competition, which Mr. Osei announces to the class and which produces an emotion you weren't ready for. You've been at Westbrook for seven months and you have a table, a locker neighbor, an essay that is in print, and a version of yourself that feels less provisional than the one you brought through the door. The old school is far enough away now to be past tense.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Junior year ends and you are \u2014 here. Not the person you were trying to become when you arrived, not the person you were before ninth grade, but something that came out of both. Sam tells you on the last day: 'You showed up.' As a thing to be told about yourself, you find, it's enough.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"Junior year ends. You didn't win the competition, didn't join the newspaper, didn't fully explain yourself to Waverly. But you have a table and a locker neighbor and you raised your hand in class seventeen times and each time looked a little less like you wanted to take it back. That's the year. It's enough of a year.",choices:[]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The essay is submitted. Mr. Osei nods when he sees you and says nothing specific, which is either reassuring or carefully neutral. The competition results take three weeks. The three weeks contain: homework, lunch at the same table, two more newspaper meetings, a conversation with Waverly about something unrelated to any of this.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Week two of waiting. Sam asks how the essay is going. You say it's submitted and out of your hands. They say: 'That's the only thing you can do with it.' You say: 'I know, but it's harder than it sounds.' They say: 'Yeah. Good things usually are.' This is Sam being encouraging in the specific Sam way.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Week three. The results are due this week. Mr. Osei has not mentioned it, which is either professional or careful. The days have their own texture independent of the essay \u2014 school exists, continues, the newspaper has a deadline, Waverly has sent you three articles and asked what you think.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"e2"}]}}},{id:"the-election",packId:"highschool",title:"The Election",subtitle:"Student council president. One vote decides it. You know something you shouldn't.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You're running for student council president against your closest rival. Election day is tomorrow. Tonight you accidentally found out that your opponent has been coordinating a smear campaign using school accounts. You could use it. You probably shouldn't. You haven't decided.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"hs-library",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The election is tomorrow. You and Kieran have been running parallel campaigns for three weeks. The polls are too close to call. Then tonight, printing campaign materials in the library, you saw something on the shared printer computer: Kieran's campaign manager left a school account open. Emails coordinating fake social media accounts against you. It's an honor code violation. You took a photo without thinking. Now you're in your car deciding what to do.",choices:[{label:"Report it to the faculty advisor tonight.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Confront Kieran directly before reporting anything.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Tell your campaign manager Dev and decide together.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Delete the photo and compete on your own terms.",next:"s2c"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Dev says: 'Report it.' You say you're not sure. Dev says: 'You're not sure because it would feel like winning dirty, but it's not \u2014 they played dirty, you'd just be reporting it.' This is a clean argument and you disagree with it slightly without being able to explain why. Dev says: 'It's your call. I'll back whatever you do.'",choices:[{label:"Report it.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Confront Kieran first.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Don't use it.",next:"s2c"},{label:"Notice what the photo feels like now.",next:"b2a"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"hs-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The faculty advisor Mr. Pierce answers his phone at 9 PM. You tell him what you saw. He's quiet for a long time. 'Email me the photo.' You do. He says: 'This is serious. Don't talk to anyone about this tonight.' He'll speak to both campaigns in the morning before the election.",choices:[{label:"Go to school the next morning.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Think about what Pierce agreeing to look means.",next:"b3a"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You text Kieran: 'I need to talk to you. Tonight.' Kieran calls back in ten minutes. You tell them what you saw. Long silence. Then: 'That wasn't me. That was Jordana. I didn't know she was doing it.' You can't verify this. Kieran says: 'I'll take everything down right now.'",choices:[{label:"Trust Kieran and don't report it.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Report it anyway \u2014 you can't verify the story.",next:"s3a"}]},s2c:{id:"s2c",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You delete the photo. You go home and spend the night going over your platform and closing statement. The violation happened. You didn't use it. Whether this is integrity or missed opportunity depends on tomorrow.",choices:[{label:"Compete.",next:"s3c"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"hs-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mr. Pierce calls both campaigns in before first period. Jordana admits to the fake accounts. Kieran says they didn't know. Mr. Pierce suspends Jordana from extracurriculars for the semester. The election is postponed one week. Kieran pulls you aside: 'I really didn't know.' You believe them. One more week of campaigning with their team in disarray.",choices:[{label:"Run a clean campaign for the additional week.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Target Kieran's now-undecided voter base directly.",next:"s4b"},{label:"Use the extra week.",next:"b4a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You don't report it. The fake posts come down overnight. The election proceeds. In your closing statement you talk about the honor code and what it means to compete with integrity and several people in the auditorium look at you and then look at Kieran and you don't know what they know.",choices:[{label:"Wait for the vote.",next:"s4c"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"hs-auditorium",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Closing statements. Kieran goes first and is warm and funny and the room likes them. You go second with your policy platform, your specifics, your case for the mental health day. It is good. Whether it is enough is what the vote will answer.",choices:[{label:"Wait for the results.",next:"s4d"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You canvass classrooms, hold a lunch Q&A, get the paper to run your platform in full. Kieran campaigns alone without Jordana's infrastructure and it shows. The extra week is yours in a way you couldn't have planned.",choices:[{label:"Find out the result.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You reach Kieran's undecided voters directly. It works. But the week in which you benefited from your opponent's team's suspension is visible to anyone who looks carefully at the timeline.",choices:[{label:"Find out the result.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4c:{id:"s4c",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The vote happens as scheduled. You will always wonder if the fake accounts swung it and you will never know for certain.",choices:[{label:"Find out the result.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4d:{id:"s4d",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The vote was always going to be close. Now you find out how close.",choices:[{label:"Find out the result.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You win by fourteen votes. At the first council meeting Kieran shows up and asks if there's room for an opposing voice. You say yes. The first motion you pass is the mental health day. It passes twelve to one.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You win by six votes. The margin is close enough that people ask questions. You run a good council. You implement the mental health day. But there's a version of this you didn't take \u2014 the one where you won clean \u2014 and it sits with you longer than you expect.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You lose by nine votes. You'll always wonder. Kieran comes and finds you at the announcement: 'I owe you.' You say: 'Run a good council.' They do. The mental health day passes in October.",choices:[]},e4:{id:"e4",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You lose by eleven votes. Kieran is a generous winner. The mental health day gets proposed by a council member and passes anyway. You spend junior year on the environmental committee and discover you prefer the work to the title. The photo in your deleted folder clears itself after three months and you feel, when you notice it's gone, mostly relieved.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"hs-library",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The photo on your phone feels different sitting in the parking lot than it did in the moment you took it. In the moment it was automatic \u2014 your hand moved before your brain registered what it was seeing. Now it's a decision.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2a"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"hs-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mr. Pierce answering his phone at 9 PM means he was awake and available and takes the call. You don't know if he's the kind of faculty advisor who always answers late calls or if something about the caller ID made him pick up. Either way, he's in it now.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A week of extra campaigning changes the shape of the race in ways you didn't predict. The postponement, which felt like an obstacle, turns out to change which voters are reachable and which conversations are possible. The extra week belongs to whoever is willing to use it.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The votes are cast. You can't know the result until it's announced, but the day feels different from campaign days \u2014 quieter, more specific. Every person who passes you in the hall is someone who voted or didn't vote, and you don't know which.",choices:[{label:"Wait through the afternoon.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Talk to Dev.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Dev says: 'I feel good about it.' You ask why. He says: 'Because you ran the right campaign.' Not the winning campaign \u2014 the right one. You note the distinction. He notices you noting it. 'Both, ideally,' he says.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"3:30 PM. The result is coming. You're standing outside your last class. Your phone is in your pocket. Dev is three doors down. Kieran is somewhere in the building, waiting for the same thing.",choices:[{label:"Check your phone.",next:"xf3"}]},xf3:{id:"xf3",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The notification arrives. Whatever it says changes the next year of your life in small but specific ways \u2014 meetings or no meetings, policies or no policies, the experience of having run and won or having run and lost.",choices:[{label:"You won.",next:"e1"},{label:"You lost by a small margin.",next:"e3"},{label:"You lost by a large margin.",next:"e4"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The day before the election. You've done everything you can do. The campaign is either going to work or it isn't and the only thing left is the vote itself. Dev sends you a list of every promise you've made in the campaign. 'For reference,' he says. 'After tomorrow.'",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Kieran finds you in the hallway. 'Good campaign,' they say. Not sarcastic. You say the same back and mean it. Whatever happens tomorrow, you've both run something real. The honor code situation sits between you and neither of you references it. The silence is clean enough.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The morning of voting. Advisory period. The ballot is simple: one name or the other. You vote. The day continues around the vote in the way that days do \u2014 classes, lunch, the ordinary texture of school \u2014 while somewhere the count is accumulating.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-grade",packId:"highschool",title:"The Grade",subtitle:"The test was already taken. The answer key was already seen. Nobody asked you to look.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You didn't mean to see the answer key to Friday's chemistry exam. It was open on the teacher's computer. You closed it immediately \u2014 but you saw enough. The exam determines whether you pass chemistry. You haven't been doing well.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You went to ask Mr. Dominguez about your last test grade because you're borderline failing chemistry. His computer was on. The answer key was open. You read approximately four questions before your brain registered what you were seeing and you closed the window. He wasn't in the room. You left without the conversation you came for. Now you're in the hallway with four things you know, a test in two days, and a question about what to do next.",choices:[{label:"Tell Mr. Dominguez what happened immediately.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Tell no one and study hard without using what you saw.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Tell a trusted friend and figure it out together.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Study the four topics heavily and take the test.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your friend Cass says: 'You didn't do anything wrong. You saw something accidentally. Just study and forget it.' You ask if that's different from using it. Cass says: 'What are you going to do, un-see it?' The logic is clean. You also know that the four questions will be in your head on Friday and you won't be able to sort what you studied from what you accidentally memorized.",choices:[{label:"Tell Mr. Dominguez anyway.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Study hard and take the test.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Think about what you did and didn't see.",next:"b2a"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"hs-hallway",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Using the four questions as a guide to what to review is, you realize as you think it through, not different enough from using them. You'll study those topics more thoroughly. The accidental information will have done its work whether you call it studying or not. The question is whether that matters to you.",choices:[{label:"Tell Mr. Dominguez.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Study everything and take the test.",next:"s2b"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"hs-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mr. Dominguez listens to the whole thing without interrupting. When you finish he says: 'Show me.' You describe the four questions. He checks his computer. Closes the document. Looks at you. 'You came in here to talk about your last test,' he says. 'Let's do that first.' You spend twenty minutes going through your mistakes on the previous exam.",choices:[{label:"Accept whatever he decides.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Have the conversation you came for.",next:"b3a"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You study. Really study \u2014 everything, not just the four areas, because the only way to know you earned the grade is to earn it across the whole test. Eleven hours of chemistry over two days. By Friday morning you know the material better than you have all semester. The four questions are still in your head, tagged with the particular feeling of having seen them by accident.",choices:[{label:"Take the test.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Notice what eleven hours of studying means.",next:"b4a"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"hs-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mr. Dominguez finishes reviewing your previous test mistakes. Then he says: 'I'm writing you a new version of the four questions you saw. Different numbers, same concepts, slightly harder. You'll take the same test as everyone else. Nobody will know the difference. But you will.'",choices:[{label:"Accept this.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Tell him that isn't necessary \u2014 you'll take the standard test.",next:"b3a"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"hs-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mr. Dominguez going through your mistakes on the previous test is the conversation you came in to have before any of this happened. It's a useful conversation. The previous test mistakes are fixable in a way that the current situation is more complicated.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The test is in front of you. Question three is one of the four you saw. You know the answer. You also know the answer because you spent four hours on that exact concept. The two knowledges are the same knowledge now and you have no way to separate them. You answer it. You answer all twenty questions.",choices:[{label:"Hand in the test and wait.",next:"s4b"},{label:"Tell Mr. Dominguez after handing it in.",next:"b3b"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You hand in the test and then stay behind. You tell him. He looks at the test. Looks at you. Says: 'You got a C on the four questions you studied for specifically.' Then: 'How did you do on the rest?' You got a B or better on every other question. He says: 'I think you know what that means.' He records your overall grade. It is entirely yours.",choices:[{label:"Accept it.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You take the modified test. The four substitute questions are harder than you expected. When you get your grade back there's a handwritten note: 'Good recovery. Come back if the next unit gives you trouble.'",choices:[{label:"Find out your grade.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You wait. The test goes to Mr. Dominguez. He grades it over the weekend. When the grades post, you look at the number.",choices:[{label:"Find out your grade.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You pass with a B-minus \u2014 the highest chemistry grade you've gotten all semester. The four substitute questions were harder than the originals, which Mr. Dominguez tells you much later. You spend the rest of the semester coming in for help when you're stuck. You end with a C-plus. The specific self-knowledge of a person who did the harder thing when the easier thing was right there is not something that shows up on a transcript.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You pass. Your grade on those four questions is average \u2014 you know the material, but knowing it with the shadow of the key behind it muddled something in your confidence. The rest of the test you did cleanly. Mr. Dominguez says nothing about it again. The grade is yours with an asterisk that only you can see.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You get a B. It's the grade you needed. You pass chemistry. In the weeks after you find yourself occasionally unsure which things you know because you learned them and which you know because of four questions seen by accident. The uncertainty fades, mostly. What doesn't fade is a faint question mark about your own grades that wasn't there before.",choices:[]},e4:{id:"e4",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You get a C-plus. It is entirely yours \u2014 the studying, the test, the grade. Mr. Dominguez writes on the bottom of your returned exam: 'This is the right number for what you did.' You're not sure if that means the grade or the decision. You think it might mean both.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You closed the window before you finished reading. The four questions you saw are the four you saw \u2014 the ones after them are unknown. It's possible the test has questions you haven't studied at all that would have been worse. It's possible the four you saw were the easiest ones. You don't know.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Eleven hours of chemistry over two days is more than you've done for any test this semester. You know why. The question of whether what you know now is clean is separate from the question of whether you've learned it.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3b"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The test is submitted. Mr. Dominguez takes it to his desk without looking at you. The class continues around you. You have a period to wait before anything else matters.",choices:[{label:"Wait through the period.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Think about what you wrote.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You think about what you wrote. The four questions. The answers. Whether they felt like the answers you knew or the answers you saw. You can't tell. The test is done and you can't take it back and the grade will say what it says.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Monday. Test return day. Mr. Dominguez moves through the rows. The person ahead of you gets theirs. You look at the desk.",choices:[{label:"Receive your test.",next:"xf3"}]},xf3:{id:"xf3",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The test lands on your desk. You turn it over.",choices:[{label:"The grade is a B \u2014 you pass.",next:"e1"},{label:"The grade is a C-plus.",next:"e2"},{label:"The grade is exactly what you deserved.",next:"e3"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The weekend between the test and the results. You study the next unit in chemistry, which is either discipline or penance or both. The material makes more sense than it did at the start of the semester. Whether this is because of the eleven hours of studying or because of the thing that happened is a question you can't answer.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Monday morning. Before first period. Mr. Dominguez's class is third period. You have two periods of the regular school day before the grade exists in your life, which is a specific kind of countdown.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"hs-classroom",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Third period. The test is in the pile on Mr. Dominguez's desk. He goes through the material first \u2014 reviewing the concepts, not looking at the pile. You look at the pile. The pile is organized in some way that doesn't tell you where your test is.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-comeback",packId:"sports",title:"The Comeback",subtitle:"Down by six. Two minutes left. Your knee says stop. Your team doesn't know.",length:"Medium",free:!0,brief:"You're the starting point guard. Your team is down six with two minutes left in the state semifinal. You tweaked your knee in the third quarter and haven't told the coach. You can still move. The question is whether you should.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The timeout huddle is loud. Coach Reyes is diagramming the press break you've run a hundred times. Your knee has been a background noise for eleven minutes that you've been successfully ignoring. Jasmine, your co-captain, is watching you \u2014 she's been watching since the third quarter. Coach says: 'Tavares, you're the primary handler on the inbound. If they press, you make the decision.' Everyone looks at you.",choices:[{label:"Tell Coach about the knee right now.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Say nothing and run the play.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Tell Jasmine quietly before the huddle breaks.",next:"b1a"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You lean to Jasmine: 'My knee. Just so you know.' She processes it in one second. 'Can you push off it?' You test it. You can. 'Then run the play. Tell Coach at the next timeout if it gets worse.' Practical and probably right. You go.",choices:[{label:"Run the play.",next:"s2b"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'Coach. My knee.' The huddle goes quiet. She looks at you for exactly one second. 'Marcus, you're in. Tavares, stay visible \u2014 I need them thinking you might come back.' You sit. Marcus checks in. The play runs.",choices:[{label:"Watch the final two minutes from the bench.",next:"s3a"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You run the play. The press comes. You push off your left leg harder than you should and feel it sharply and get the ball up the court anyway. Jasmine hits a three \u2014 down three, one possession. Forty seconds left.",choices:[{label:"Drive to the basket and take the contact.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Find Jasmine on the wing \u2014 she's hot right now.",next:"s3c"},{label:"Run the set play Coach drew up.",next:"s3d"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Marcus is good. Not you, but good. He pushes the pace, gets fouled going to the basket \u2014 two free throws, converts both. Down two with a minute ten. The opposing team ices it at the free throw line in the final thirty seconds.",choices:[{label:"Face the result.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Watch from the bench.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Notice what the trainer sees.",next:"b2b"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You drive. You get the contact. You go down and the whistle blows and you know before you hit the floor that the knee has done something it cannot recover from tonight. You make both free throws sitting on the floor because the referee lets you. Down one. You cannot continue.",choices:[{label:"Watch the final seconds.",next:"s4b"},{label:"Feel the decision as you make it.",next:"b4a"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Jasmine catches the pass at the wing, pump fakes once, and rises. Clean shot. Tie game. Your knee held. The overtime coin goes your way.",choices:[{label:"Play overtime.",next:"s4c"},{label:"Make the pass.",next:"b4b"}]},s3d:{id:"s3d",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You run the set play. Jasmine cuts baseline, catches, one dribble, contested layup that rattles in. Down one. You foul immediately. The free throws: in, out. Rebound scramble and a half-court shot at the buzzer that hits the backboard.",choices:[{label:"Play overtime.",next:"s4c"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your team loses by one. In the locker room Coach Reyes says: 'That was the right call, Tavares.' She says it like she means it and like it cost her something. Both things are true.",choices:[{label:"Face the aftermath.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Watch how Marcus handles the moment.",next:"b3a"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Marcus hits one of two free throws. Your team loses by two. The knee, in the morning, requires an MRI.",choices:[{label:"Face the aftermath.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4c:{id:"s4c",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You win overtime by four. Your knee, in the morning, has swelling the trainer calls 'concerning.' You are out for two weeks and watch the state final from the bench in your warmup jacket.",choices:[{label:"Watch the final.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The knee, examined the next morning, has a partial ligament tear that would have been full if you'd kept playing. The trainer says: 'You probably knew something was wrong.' You say yes. She says: 'Good call.' You spend the bus ride home with your leg elevated and Jasmine's jacket over your shoulders and the specific knowledge of a person who chose the right thing and paid exactly the price they were willing to pay.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"A partial tear. You played on it seven minutes longer than you should have and made two free throws sitting on the floor, which the team will talk about for years regardless of the outcome. The outcome was still a loss. Those two things exist together and will for a while.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Your team wins the state final without you. You watch every second from the bench. It is theirs and also yours and the knee heals. You know for the rest of your athletic life what you can push through and what you can't, which is not knowledge everyone gets.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Rook catches your eye across the court as you settle onto the bench. He gives you a nod that says he understands, or at least that he's watching. You've played eleven games together this season. Some things don't need to be said in words.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b2b:{id:"b2b",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The trainer, from the sideline, sees you sit. She's been tracking that knee since you tweaked it \u2014 she always watches how players move after a contact. She doesn't come over. She's giving you the decision. But she's watching.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Marcus plays well in the first minute \u2014 a clean cut and catch that draws the defense. Down by one less now. The crowd shifts. Watching your own position played by someone who isn't you is an education in what you actually do that can't be learned any other way.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s4a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You push off and feel it and the feeling is specific \u2014 not sharp enough to stop you, sharp enough to register permanently. The decision to drive was made in the gap between knowing and stopping, which is where most decisions in a game actually live.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},b4b:{id:"b4b",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The pass to Jasmine is the kind of pass you've made a hundred times in practice and maybe three times in a game when it mattered this much. Your hands know the weight of the ball at this distance. The rest is trust.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Final buzzer. The game is over. One of the outcomes you were running toward or away from has happened.",choices:[{label:"Your team won.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Your team lost.",next:"xf3"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The locker room after a win. The knee is in there somewhere, waiting to be examined. The trainer will find it. Right now: the specific quality of a playoff win that you contributed to from wherever you were \u2014 floor or bench.",choices:[{label:"Face the knee.",next:"e1"},{label:"Celebrate first.",next:"xfb2"}]},xfb2:{id:"xfb2",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You celebrate for the three minutes before the trainer finds you. Three minutes is the right amount. Then she finds you.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"e1"}]},xf3:{id:"xf3",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The locker room after a loss. The knee is whatever it is. The season is over. Both things are what they are.",choices:[{label:"Deal with the knee and the loss.",next:"e2"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The final two minutes of regulation. Whatever happened with the knee \u2014 told or untold, benched or playing \u2014 the game is in its final form now. Both teams know the score. Every possession has the specific weight of a possession that might be the last.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"One minute left. The crowd has been on its feet for three minutes. The opposing team is in their defensive scheme. Your team is running the offense you've been running all season, or a version of it, or someone is improvising.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Twenty seconds. The math is clear. One or two possessions left. Whatever is going to happen is going to happen in the next twenty seconds, which is a very short time and also a very long time.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmx"}]},xfmx:{id:"xfmx",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The game has ended. The final buzzer is behind you. Whatever happened in those last minutes \u2014 the knee, the bench, the overtime \u2014 is now history and you are now in the locker room or beside the court or somewhere that is the immediate aftermath of something that mattered.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-scout",packId:"sports",title:"The Scout",subtitle:"She's watching from row seven. You've been waiting three years for this.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"A Division I scout is in the stands tonight. Your coach told you at warm-ups. You've had four turnovers by halftime \u2014 twice your season average \u2014 and you're down two. You've been playing for the scout instead of playing.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your coach Diaz told you at warm-ups: 'Pennfield's here. Row seven.' You spent the first half knowing exactly where row seven was at all times. Four turnovers, twice your average. Diaz is not yelling. He says: 'Tell me what's happening.' You know what's happening. You've been playing for the scout.",choices:[{label:"Be honest with Diaz about what's going on.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Tell Diaz you just need to get back to basics.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Ask Diaz to take you out for five minutes.",next:"b1a"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Diaz looks at you. 'You want to sit?' You say five minutes might reset you. 'Peterson goes in. I'll bring you back when I see you're ready.' Five minutes on the bench watching Peterson play your position clarifies something.",choices:[{label:"Tell Diaz you're ready.",next:"s3c"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'I've been playing to the scout.' Diaz nods like he already knew. 'Here's the thing about scouts: they're not watching what you do when everything is going well. They're watching what you do right now.' He doesn't say anything else. He doesn't need to.",choices:[{label:"Go out and play your game.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Ask Diaz to adjust your role in the second half.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Notice what playing for the scout feels like.",next:"b2a"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Diaz says: 'The scout is going to be at four more games this month. She sees a lot of players. The one thing that will stand out is if you play like yourself. You know what that looks like.' He moves on to the rest of the team. You sit with the word 'yourself' for three minutes.",choices:[{label:"Go back out.",next:"s3a"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You stop looking at row seven. This takes about three minutes of active effort. By the seventh minute of the third quarter you're not thinking about it anymore because you're in a game. Two assists that are the kind you make when you're not thinking about making assists. A mid-range shot you take because it's open, not because a scout is watching. The game is tied going into the fourth.",choices:[{label:"Keep doing what you're doing.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Try to take over in the fourth \u2014 make your case.",next:"s4b"},{label:"Find the thing that changed.",next:"b3a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Diaz adjusts you to the off-ball role for six minutes \u2014 cutting, screening, the work that doesn't show on the scoresheet but shows on film. By minute seven the defense has shifted to account for your movement and Martinez signals: 'I got it. Go.' You take the ball back.",choices:[{label:"Take over from here.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Keep the distribution game going.",next:"s4c"},{label:"Work the off-ball role.",next:"b3b"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Five minutes on the bench watching Peterson work hard and not quite do what you do is enough. You tell Diaz you're ready. Back in with six minutes left in the third, game tied, scout in row seven which you have stopped tracking.",choices:[{label:"Control the fourth quarter.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Distribute and let your team decide it.",next:"s4c"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You finish well. Whatever the final score, you find out afterward what the scout's report says.",choices:[{label:"Find out the result.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Find out what the scout reported.",next:"b4a"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You try to take over. It goes mostly right \u2014 big scoring numbers, but turnovers crept back in when you pushed too hard for the spectacular.",choices:[{label:"Find out the result.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4c:{id:"s4c",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You distribute. Your teammates win the game. Your assist numbers tell the story of what happened when you stopped playing for row seven.",choices:[{label:"Find out the result.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You win by six. Nineteen points, eight assists, one turnover in the second half. Diaz finds you after: 'She asked for your film.' Not a scholarship offer \u2014 film. Which is the first step. You go home and do not think about row seven because you stopped looking at row seven in the third quarter, and that is exactly when the game became yours.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You win by two. Twenty-three points, six turnovers for the game. Diaz says: 'You showed her two different players tonight. Make sure she sees the one you want her to see on film.' You don't know which one that is yet. That's the work.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Your teammates win the game. Seven points and eleven assists and a plus-minus that tells the whole story. A week later Diaz shows you the scout's postgame notes: 'She used the word decision-making twice.' Decision-making is everything at the next level.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"There's a specific quality to playing badly in front of someone whose opinion matters professionally. Different from playing badly in a game that's just a game. The turnovers feel annotated \u2014 you can see the notes being made in real time.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your first good decision of the second half is a no-look pass that you make without planning to make it. The planning is what's been wrong. The no-look pass is what happens when you stop planning.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s4a"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The off-ball role means you're a problem the defense has to track without the ball, which is different from being a problem they have to stop with the ball. Different kind of attention. Harder to guard in some ways.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s4a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find out after the game that scouts typically report on three things: athleticism, decision-making, and coachability. The third quarter, whatever it looked like from row seven, was evidence of at least two of the three.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"After. Whatever the score was, you're in the locker room. Diaz is going through the postgame. You're waiting for the specific thing he says to you, which will tell you what the scout saw.",choices:[{label:"Hear what Diaz says.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Talk to a teammate first.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your teammate says: 'You were different in the second half.' You ask how. 'Like you stopped auditing yourself.' This is accurate. The auditing \u2014 the constant self-evaluation in real time \u2014 is what was wrong. Stopping it was what changed.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Diaz finds you. He says something specific. What he says tells you what the scout reported.",choices:[{label:"She asked for your film \u2014 first step.",next:"e1"},{label:"She said she'd be back for more games.",next:"e2"},{label:"Good result \u2014 hard to say more yet.",next:"e3"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Fourth quarter. The adjustment from the third is holding. You're in the game rather than watching yourself play the game, which is the difference. Every possession has a quality that the first half lacked \u2014 presence, maybe, or just the absence of self-evaluation.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three minutes left. The lead is what it is. The scout is in row seven and you don't know where row seven is anymore, which is not because you forgot but because you stopped needing to know.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Buzzer. Locker room. The postgame is the postgame \u2014 Diaz going through the film in his head, teammates accounting for what happened, the specific quiet that follows a game that was genuinely contested. Then Diaz finds you specifically.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmx"}]},xfmx:{id:"xfmx",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Postgame. The locker room. Diaz has gone through the standard review. Now the room is settling into the specific quiet of players accounting for what happened \u2014 what they did, what went differently than planned, what they'd do again.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-rival",packId:"sports",title:"The Rival",subtitle:"You've beaten each other twice. The third time is the one that matters.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"State championships. You and Dominic Reyes have split your last two matches. You know his technique better than anyone. He knows yours the same way. Your coach has a new strategy. Dominic's coach is your former mentor.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The bracket worked out as everyone expected. Finals: you and Dominic Reyes. Your coach Val has a game plan \u2014 open conservative, force him to initiate, wait for the overextension she's seen on film. Your former coach and Dominic's current coach, Petrova, taught you both the same foundational system. You know exactly what Dominic is going to do. So does he.",choices:[{label:"Trust Val's conservative game plan.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Approach Petrova before the match and acknowledge the situation.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Discard the plan \u2014 go at Dominic immediately and see who blinks.",next:"s2b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Petrova sees you coming and waits. You say: 'I just wanted to say \u2014 I learned everything from you.' She says: 'So did he.' You say you know. She says: 'Then you know the match starts before the first point.' You walk back to your side and think about what that means.",choices:[{label:"Run Val's plan, but watch Dominic's setup before each exchange.",next:"s2c"},{label:"Think about what she meant on the walk back.",next:"b2a"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Conservative. You wait. Dominic comes at you early, you read it correctly, score first. Then second. Then Dominic adjusts \u2014 he always adjusts \u2014 and by the midpoint you're even, both of you running the same patient system Petrova taught, and whoever blinks first loses.",choices:[{label:"Stay patient \u2014 let Dominic make the error.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Introduce a technique Dominic hasn't seen from you.",next:"s3b"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You go immediately. Dominic is briefly surprised \u2014 you've never opened this way \u2014 and you score twice on that surprise alone. Then his system absorbs it. Both pressing, neither patient. Decided by execution and endurance.",choices:[{label:"Keep the pressure up \u2014 don't let him settle.",next:"s3c"},{label:"Pull back and let him think you've run out of gas.",next:"s3d"}]},s2c:{id:"s2c",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Val's plan, but you watch Dominic's setup before each exchange rather than your own preparation. Reading intention. You can see what he's planning a half-second before he executes. The problem is he can probably see the same thing from you. The question is who reads faster.",choices:[{label:"Trust your read and act on it every time.",next:"s3e"},{label:"Give Dominic a false read \u2014 show one intention, execute another.",next:"s3f"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You wait. Dominic waits. He blinks first, by a small margin, and his overextension is exactly the one Val identified on film. You take the point. Patience holds.",choices:[{label:"Hold to the end.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Practice the patience.",next:"b3a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The technique Val developed specifically for this match \u2014 Dominic doesn't read it the first time. You score. Doesn't read it the second time either. By the third he's adjusted but you're ahead.",choices:[{label:"Close out.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Use the technique Val held back.",next:"b3b"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You hold the pressure. Dominic buckles once \u2014 your point \u2014 then holds. Endurance contest. Both of you operating past preparation and into character.",choices:[{label:"Find the result.",next:"xfm1"}]},s3d:{id:"s3d",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You pull back. Dominic advances \u2014 then realizes mid-advance you're not tired, you're waiting. He's overextended. Your point. He resets faster than you expect. Chess from here.",choices:[{label:"Play it to the end.",next:"xfm1"}]},s3e:{id:"s3e",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Every point decided by who sees a fraction of a second further ahead. Dominic afterward: 'I couldn't tell what you were setting up.' You tell him you weren't sure either until you did it.",choices:[{label:"Find out who wins.",next:"xfm1"}]},s3f:{id:"s3f",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The false read works twice. Then Dominic adjusts and it stops working. You win anyway on the points you built. Two points is a margin. It holds.",choices:[{label:"Find out the result.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"See if the margin holds.",next:"b4a"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You win. Patience or reading or pressure \u2014 whichever you chose, it was enough. Dominic afterward says something that tells you both what the match was and what the next one will be. Petrova shakes your hand. Val is already taking notes for the next opponent. You have been the best version of what you've been building, and it was enough, and now you find out what comes next.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You win by a margin \u2014 enough. The specific technique or deception that built the lead holds under pressure. Dominic accepts it with the equanimity of someone who will be back. That uncertainty about whether it would have held without the edge you brought is the best thing about a rivalry at this level.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Close. One point, the margin of a single moment. Dominic accepts it as someone who will be here next year. Petrova tells you both: 'That is the match I hoped you'd have.' Val: 'That's the match I planned for.' Both of them right about different things, and somehow both things happened.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The walk back from Petrova to your side of the court takes about thirty seconds. In those thirty seconds you work out what she meant: Dominic is reading your preparation. The match starts when you decide what you're preparing for, not when the first point is called.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2c"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Patience is a skill that degrades under pressure, which is exactly when it matters most. You've practiced patience in training, which is different from exercising it when the score matters and someone is watching who can change the next three years of your life.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},b3b:{id:"b3b",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Val developed this technique for six months without telling you what it was for. She showed it to you two weeks before the championship. 'Save it,' she said. 'If you use it in a warm-up match, he'll have an answer.' You used it in no warm-up matches. Dominic has no answer.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two points is a margin that holds or doesn't. You've seen two-point margins evaporate in a final exchange. You've seen them hold. The margin doesn't tell you which it will be. Only the execution does.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The judge calls it. One of you is the state champion.",choices:[{label:"You won.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Dominic won.",next:"xf3"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Champion. Dominic extends his hand. You shake it. He says something. What he says will stay with you.",choices:[{label:"Hear what Dominic says.",next:"xfb1"},{label:"Find Val first.",next:"e1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Dominic says: 'I'll have an answer for that next year.' He means whatever technique or approach decided the match. He's already moved past the loss to the preparation. You say: 'I know.' Both of you do.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"e1"}]},xf3:{id:"xf3",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Dominic is champion. You extend your hand. He shakes it. Val finds you. Petrova finds Dominic.",choices:[{label:"Find out what Val says.",next:"e3"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Final third. You and Dominic are both in the space past fatigue where the body runs on pattern and the mind runs on something that isn't quite thought. Every exchange has been preparation for these. The preparation is done. What's left is execution.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Second-to-last exchange. One point separating you. Both of you have been here before \u2014 different matches, same margin. You know what Dominic does from one point behind. He knows what you do from one point ahead. Knowing doesn't make it easier to stop.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Final exchange. The judge is watching. Petrova is watching. Val is watching. The crowd is making the sound crowds make when something is about to be decided. You and Dominic are the only two people in the room who aren't watching \u2014 you're both inside it.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmx"}]},xfmx:{id:"xfmx",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The match is decided. One of you is champion and one of you is the person who will be back next year. The difference between those two positions is one point and one exchange. At this level, that's always how it is.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmy"}]},xfmy:{id:"xfmy",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The announcement is made. The judge is clear. One of you has won the state championship. Both of you already know before the announcement \u2014 you knew in the final exchange \u2014 but the announcement makes it official, makes it part of the record, makes it the thing that happened this year.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmz"}]},xfmz:{id:"xfmz",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You stand in the arena. The result is in the record now \u2014 the judge's call, the score, the champion's name. Dominic is across the space. Val is behind you. Petrova is behind Dominic. Four people who know exactly what that match was.",choices:[{label:"You won.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Dominic won.",next:"xf3"}]}}},{id:"the-team",packId:"sports",title:"The Team",subtitle:"The best player is also the reason you might lose.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"Your captain Reeves averages twenty-two points a game. He is also making every game harder to win. The coach won't act. Playoffs start in a week. You have to decide what to do.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Reeves averages twenty-two points and also, by your private count, six moments per game where a teammate is open and he shoots anyway. Coach Holt treats Reeves's stats as proof the system works. The system works for Reeves. You're the second-leading scorer and primary distributor, and in three playoff games last year Reeves averaged twenty-four points and one win.",choices:[{label:"Talk to Reeves directly.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Talk to Coach Holt about playoff adjustments.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Talk to the other starters first.",next:"b1a"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The other three starters agree immediately \u2014 this has been happening long enough for everyone to have already processed it. Torres: 'Reeves isn't going to hear it from us.' Kim: 'Coach won't change the system a week before playoffs.' Okafor says nothing, then: 'Someone has to say it out loud to someone who can do something.'",choices:[{label:"Talk to Reeves.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Talk to Coach Holt.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Notice what the team has been carrying.",next:"b2a"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You find Reeves after practice. He listens \u2014 he's not a bad listener, which is part of what makes this complicated. 'My numbers are good,' he says. You say playoff numbers aren't. He says: 'Playoff opponents are better.' You say: 'They're better at taking you away.' Silence. Then: 'What do you want me to do differently.' It's a real question.",choices:[{label:"Tell him specifically \u2014 three situations where you need him to pass.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Tell him you want him to trust you in the playoffs.",next:"s3b"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"sports-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Coach Holt listens. 'Reeves's production is why we're in the playoffs.' You say you know. 'What are you asking me to do?' You say: look at the playoff film from last year and tell you what he sees in the third quarter of each game. He says he'll look.",choices:[{label:"Wait to see if anything changes.",next:"s3c"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You tell Reeves specifically: the left corner when Torres is open, the second side pick-and-roll when Kim has a step, the fast break where you always have a trailer. He writes nothing down but you see him processing. 'I trust my shot,' he says. You say: 'In the playoffs, trust our shot.' He considers it long enough that you think it might have landed.",choices:[{label:"See what happens in the first playoff game.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Lay out the three specific situations.",next:"b3a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"'Trust you how?' You say: when you call the play, he runs it even if he sees the shot, because you see the whole floor and he sees his defender. Reeves: 'That's basically asking me to play second.' You say: 'It's asking you to play captain.' He says: 'Show me what you mean in practice tomorrow.'",choices:[{label:"See what happens in the first playoff game.",next:"s4b"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Coach Holt watches the film and runs a practice the next day where the second option on six consecutive plays is the pass. Reeves says nothing but adjusts visibly. After practice Coach pulls you aside: 'I saw it.' The system doesn't change on paper but changes where it matters \u2014 in the reads Reeves makes when the defense overloads.",choices:[{label:"See what happens in the first playoff game.",next:"s4c"},{label:"Watch how the coach delivers the message.",next:"b4a"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Reeves makes the pass in the left corner in the second quarter. Torres hits the three. Reeves comes back to you: 'That felt different.' You win the first round.",choices:[{label:"See how far you go.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Watch Reeves make the pass.",next:"b5a"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Reeves runs the plays. When you call it, he moves to create instead of shooting. Twice in the first round game this creates open looks for players who aren't him. You win by nine. In the locker room he says quietly: 'You were right about the floor.'",choices:[{label:"See how far you go.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4c:{id:"s4c",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The adjusted reads work. Reeves is making different decisions at the moment of choice. First-round win by seven. Coach runs the same read-focused practice before the second round.",choices:[{label:"See how far you go.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You advance three rounds. In the second, when the defense shuts Reeves down completely, he finds Kim for twelve points in the fourth quarter. You make the semifinal for the first time in six years. The specific pass in the left corner is the thing that changed everything, and both of you know it, and only you will ever say it out loud.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You advance. The team is a team for the first time in the two years you've been on it. How far you go is something the bracket decides. What Reeves said in that locker room \u2014 quietly, so only you could hear \u2014 is something else.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You advance three rounds. In the final you lose by two on a Reeves shot in the last four seconds that goes in and out. It was the right shot. He took it because it was right, not because he couldn't see you open on the other side. You don't win every one of the right ones. That's different from losing because of the wrong ones.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The other starters have been waiting for someone to say something for most of the season. The relief when you start talking is visible. It's also a reminder that you've all been carrying this, separately, instead of together \u2014 which is part of the same problem.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2a"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The three situations: left corner, second side pick-and-roll, fast break trailer. You've watched these moments on film. Reeves sees the same film. The difference is what each of you are optimizing for when the moment arrives.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s4a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The coach adjusts without saying he's adjusting. A practice where the right answer is always the pass rather than the shot, run six times without comment, is a message delivered at practice-level volume instead of conversation-level volume. Sometimes that's the right delivery method.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s4c"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The pass in the left corner, when Reeves makes it, surprises Torres enough that she almost doesn't catch it. She adjusts, catches, rises. Three points. Reeves doesn't look at you. He's already moving to the next setup. He's been listening since you talked.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Final buzzer. You've won or lost, but either way, the team played differently than it did before the conversation with Reeves.",choices:[{label:"Win and advance.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Lose but see what the season built.",next:"xf3"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Advance. Next round upcoming. The team that won this game is the team that can win the next one. Reeves finds you in the locker room.",choices:[{label:"Hear what Reeves says.",next:"e1"}]},xf3:{id:"xf3",scene:"sports-locker",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You lose but not in the same way as last year. Last year: Reeves shot twenty-four times. This year: the team decided the game together. The outcome is the same. The team is different.",choices:[{label:"Think about what the season built.",next:"e3"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Second quarter of the first round. The defense hasn't adjusted yet to the distribution game. Open looks are appearing where they didn't last year. Reeves has made two passes to open teammates. Torres hit the first. Kim hit the second. The scoreboard reflects it.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Halftime. You're ahead \u2014 not by much, but ahead. In the locker room Reeves says: 'The mismatches in the second half are going to be there.' He says it to the team, not to you specifically. He's seen what the defense is doing and where it opens. He's thinking about the team.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Fourth quarter. The defense has shifted. They're now covering your options, which means they're leaving Reeves. The original constraint has become the opportunity. Reeves reads it. He's been reading it for four minutes and waiting for the right moment.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmx"}]},xfmx:{id:"xfmx",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The playoff game is over. The outcome is what it is. In the locker room the team is quiet in the way that comes after games that demanded everything \u2014 not depleted exactly, but emptied of the accumulated tension that filled the pregame and the warm-up and the first quarter.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-injury",packId:"sports",title:"The Injury",subtitle:"Six months out. The sport you built your life around. The question nobody is asking.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You tore your ACL four months ago. Recovery is going well, physically. The question your surgeon, your physio, and your coach are not asking is whether you actually want to come back.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Clearance is six weeks away. The knee bends, extends, the numbers are where they need to be. Your physio Ortega says: 'You're ahead of schedule.' Your coach has already told the team you'll be back. Your parents have adjusted their schedules. Everyone is operating on the assumption that the injury is the problem and the end of the injury is the solution. Nobody has asked whether you want to come back.",choices:[{label:"Tell Ortega what you've been thinking.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Keep it to yourself until you're officially cleared.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Call a teammate who's been through something similar.",next:"b1a"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You call Dez, who came back from shoulder surgery two years ago. She listens to the whole thing. Then: 'The worst part of my recovery was the six weeks before I came back, because I didn't know which thing I was dreading \u2014 playing again or finding out I didn't want to.' You say that is exactly it. She says: 'You have to find out. One way or the other.'",choices:[{label:"Talk to Ortega.",next:"s2a"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Ortega has seen this before \u2014 you can tell by the way she doesn't look surprised, just careful. 'How long have you been feeling this?' Four months. Since about two weeks after surgery, when the initial focus of recovery gave way to the longer middle. She says: 'Is it that you don't want to play, or that you don't know who you are if you're not playing?'",choices:[{label:"Admit you don't know the answer yet.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Say it's the first one \u2014 you genuinely don't want to go back.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Say it's the second one \u2014 the identity question.",next:"s3c"},{label:"Notice how long you've been avoiding the question.",next:"b2a"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"sports-arena",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You go to a team practice as a spectator. Watching your team from the sideline for ninety minutes is data. During the good plays you want to be in there. The feeling is real. But at about sixty percent of the intensity you'd have expected, which is either the injury's effect on your psychology or something else entirely.",choices:[{label:"Talk to your coach.",next:"s3d"},{label:"Decide that sixty percent is enough and come back.",next:"b2a"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The injury happened in a specific way \u2014 a landing, a sound, a moment of knowing before you hit the floor. The recovery has been going well by every external measure. The question is whether 'going well' and 'what you want' are the same thing, and you've been avoiding the question for four months.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2a"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Ortega says: 'That's the right answer. You need more information.' She refers you to a sports psychologist, Dr. Wren \u2014 standard process, not because something's wrong. Dr. Wren's first-session question: 'When did sport stop being the thing you did and start being the thing you were?'",choices:[{label:"Work through it with Dr. Wren and come back.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Work through it and choose not to come back.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Sit with Dr. Wren's question.",next:"b3a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Ortega: 'Have you told anyone else?' No. 'Your coach needs to know. Your parents need to know. It's going to be a hard conversation and I'll help you prepare for it.' You spend the next two weeks telling people something they didn't see coming.",choices:[{label:"Talk to your coach first.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Talk to your parents first.",next:"s4b"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Ortega: 'That's useful, because those are solvable differently.' If it's identity, the question is what happens to who you are if sport isn't the center \u2014 a question every serious athlete meets eventually, at retirement or injury or the end of the career. You've met it early. Dr. Wren works through this with you over four sessions.",choices:[{label:"Come back to the sport with clearer eyes.",next:"xfm1"}]},s3d:{id:"s3d",scene:"sports-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Coach listens. Then: 'What does sixty percent feel like?' You try to describe it. He says: 'You've been at sixty percent on motivation before. Early season, after losses. You know what brings it back.' This is true. 'Come back and tell me if it changes.' Practical, and maybe not the whole answer, but a place to start.",choices:[{label:"Return to play.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"sports-office",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Coach sits with it for a moment. 'I appreciate you telling me before clearance.' He asks if it's permanent. You say you're not sure \u2014 maybe you need to step away and find out. He says the program will be here. He says: 'This is your call.' He means it.",choices:[{label:"Make the decision.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"See what the coach says.",next:"b4a"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your father goes through surprise and then quiet: 'You've been doing this for ten years.' Your mother: 'You don't have to decide today.' Which is right and also not quite what you need to hear, because you've already decided.",choices:[{label:"Tell them you've decided.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You work through it and come back. The return game is a B-minus \u2014 cautious, careful, correct \u2014 and afterward you feel something you hadn't felt in four months: glad to be here. Not the same as before. You know now that you're a person who plays this sport, not a person who is this sport. The difference makes you better at it.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You don't come back. The career ends at the point where it stopped making sense. What you keep: the capacity for sustained effort, the tolerance for failure, the knowledge of what a team actually is. You find, over time, that these things transfer. The identity question, which felt enormous at the time of the injury, resolves itself by being lived through.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You come back. Sixty percent becomes seventy by the second game, eighty by the fourth. Whether the doubt was the injury and the injury is healed, or whether you chose to be there and that choice is its own kind of wanting \u2014 you don't resolve the distinction. You play. The playing is enough.",choices:[]},e4:{id:"e4",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You return knowing the difference between what you do and who you are. Something is lighter in the first game back. The injury gave you the question and the recovery gave you the answer, and the answer is that you chose this, and choosing something is different from being it. You play three more years and they are the best three years of your career.",choices:[]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Dr. Wren's question \u2014 when did sport stop being the thing you did and start being the thing you were \u2014 doesn't have an easy answer. You think about when the answer stopped being 'when I'm twelve and I join the league' and became something else, something identity-shaped, and you're not sure you can name the moment.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"sports-office",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your coach has seen players come back from injuries who weren't ready and players who were. He's also seen players who decided not to come back and then regretted it, and players who decided not to come back and didn't. He doesn't tell you which category you're in. He says: 'This is your call.' He means it.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The decision sits. Whether to come back or not, the decision has been made. What happens next is living inside the decision.",choices:[{label:"Come back.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Don't come back.",next:"xf3"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"First practice back. The knee holds. The game is the game. You find out what sixty percent becomes when you're actually playing.",choices:[{label:"Find out.",next:"e3"},{label:"Find out something unexpected.",next:"e4"}]},xf3:{id:"xf3",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The career ends here. Not with injury \u2014 with choice. The difference matters in ways that take time to feel.",choices:[{label:"Find out what the choice produces.",next:"e2"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The conversation \u2014 wherever you had it and whoever you had it with \u2014 is behind you now. The decision exists in the world. What remains is the day-to-day of living inside the decision, which is always different from the moment of making it.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two weeks in. The decision is either becoming the new normal or revealing itself to be not quite right. Ortega sees you at the weekly check-in and asks how it's going. You tell her the true version.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A month later. The thing you decided is the thing you're living. The clarity you were looking for \u2014 about wanting, about identity, about what the sport was \u2014 is either there or still coming. Both are possible and you'll know by how the month feels.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmx"}]},xfmx:{id:"xfmx",scene:"sports-court",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The decision has been made and is being lived. Whatever you chose \u2014 to come back or to stop \u2014 the choice is now the new context for everything else. The medical staff, the coach, your teammates, your family: all of them are now relating to the person who made this decision.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmy"}]},xfmy:{id:"xfmy",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The first month inside the decision. Whether you came back or stopped, the first month is the data \u2014 how the days feel, what the doing or not-doing costs and gives, whether the decision was right or just necessary.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmz"}]},xfmz:{id:"xfmz",scene:"sports-clinic",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The first month is behind you. You're either in the sport again or you're not. The decision has become the new normal \u2014 not comfortable necessarily, but settled. The shape of who you are without or with the sport is clearer than it was thirty days ago.",choices:[{label:"Come back.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Don't come back.",next:"xf3"}]}}},{id:"the-mutiny",packId:"pirates",title:"The Mutiny",subtitle:"The captain is wrong. Everyone knows it. Nobody will say it.",length:"Medium",free:!0,brief:"Your ship has been becalmed for nine days. The water casks are down to three days' worth. Captain Mara has ordered you to sail east into known Admiralty patrol waters. Half the crew thinks she's lost her nerve. The other half thinks she's lost her mind. You are the quartermaster.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The order came at first light: east by northeast. You know the charts better than anyone except Mara herself, and the charts say east takes you across the Admiralty's most active shipping lane at the worst possible time. Rook, the bosun, is watching you from across the deck with an expression that says he's been waiting for you to say something. Two crew members have already approached you separately this morning.",choices:[{label:"Speak to Mara directly \u2014 challenge the order.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Ask Rook what he thinks before doing anything.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Follow the order. She's the captain.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Signal a crew assembly \u2014 surface the disagreement openly.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Rook says: 'I've sailed with her eleven years. She's not making a mistake.' You say the charts say otherwise. 'The charts don't know what she knows.' You ask what she knows. 'That's the question, isn't it.' He won't say more. But he's not dismissing you, which means he has doubts of his own.",choices:[{label:"Speak to Mara directly.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Follow the order.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Think about what nine days has done to the crew.",next:"b2a"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A crew assembly is the step before a mutiny. Everyone knows this. When you say the word 'assembly' within earshot of anyone, the deck goes quiet. Mara appears at the stern rail and looks down. She says: 'Quartermaster. My cabin.' You have not called the assembly yet but you've made it known you were considering it.",choices:[{label:"Go to Mara's cabin.",next:"s2a"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"ship-cabin",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mara's cabin has charts on every surface. She's been at this all night. When you come in she says, before you speak: 'The southern wind is seven days out. We have three days of water.' You say you know. 'East takes us through the patrol lane, but I know where the patrol ships are. Two are in port. One is north on a customs seizure. We cross in six hours.' You ask how she knows this. She holds up a letter. It's dated yesterday \u2014 from a fisherman at dawn.",choices:[{label:"Trust her. This changes things.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Ask to see the letter \u2014 verify the intelligence.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Push back \u2014 intelligence can be wrong.",next:"s3c"},{label:"Look at what Mara has been working with.",next:"b3a"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You follow the order. The crew watches you do it. Rook gives you a nod. By midday the eastern course has brought you within sight of a patrol vessel. Mara is already adjusting \u2014 she saw it before the lookout called it, which means she had a plan for this, which means she was ahead of the situation.",choices:[{label:"Watch how she handles it.",next:"s3d"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"ship-cabin",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You trust her. You go back to the deck and tell Rook: 'She has current intelligence. The lane is clear for the crossing window.' Rook says: 'I know. She told me at dawn.' You had not been told. You are the quartermaster. This gap is worth noting, after.",choices:[{label:"Make the crossing.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Think through the crossing window.",next:"b4a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"ship-cabin",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mara hands you the letter. A contact \u2014 a Harbour Master's clerk who has been feeding her shipping intelligence for three years. The patrol positions are listed by name and current assignment. Two in port. One north. The crossing window is accurate. You hand the letter back. She says: 'In the future, I'll brief you.' You say: 'In the future, I'll ask before I doubt.'",choices:[{label:"Make the crossing.",next:"s4b"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"ship-cabin",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'If that ship is not where your letter says it is, we are caught with no wind to run.' Mara says: 'Yes.' Then: 'If we wait seven days for the southern wind, we ration to half. Three people are already showing signs of the dry sickness. I chose the risk that doesn't kill my crew before we find out if the intelligence is right.' You have no response smaller than what she just said.",choices:[{label:"Make the crossing.",next:"s4c"}]},s3d:{id:"s3d",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Mara runs a flag code \u2014 merchant vessel on a chartered course. The patrol ship looks. Holds. Current-year coastal merchant codes, which means she has Admiralty codes, which means she has a source inside the Admiralty. The patrol ship turns north. You have just watched a captain solve a problem she anticipated using a resource she hadn't told you about.",choices:[{label:"Get to safe water.",next:"s4d"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The crossing takes six hours and is exactly what the letter said it would be. You reach fresh water by nightfall. You talk to Mara afterward about the briefing gap \u2014 not as a confrontation but as a process question.",choices:[{label:"Talk to Mara.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Find out who knew what and when.",next:"b5a"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The crossing is clean. Fresh water by nightfall. On the other side, Mara says: 'You were right to ask.' You say she was right not to explain in public.",choices:[{label:"Find the outcome.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4c:{id:"s4c",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The intelligence is accurate. The crossing is clean. Fresh water by nightfall. The three crew with dry sickness recover within a day. The math Mara did on risk and necessity was right.",choices:[{label:"Find the outcome.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4d:{id:"s4d",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Safe water, full casks, crew intact. You sit with Mara at the chart table that night and she explains the Admiralty source, the codes, how long she's been building the network.",choices:[{label:"Find the outcome.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"From that point forward, her intelligence briefings are shared with you before major decisions. The arrangement is better for both of you and better for the ship. She tells you later that she'd been testing how long you'd wait before asking. You tell her you'll ask sooner next time. She says: 'I know.'",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You have found the version of trust that includes verification, which is the only kind that works on a ship where everyone's life depends on it. Rook says nothing about the conversation you had with him. He doesn't need to.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You followed the order and you were right to follow it and you also know now that following orders and understanding them are different things. The next time Mara does something that doesn't make sense, you ask immediately. She answers immediately. Both of you are better for the brevity.",choices:[]},e4:{id:"e4",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"She tells you because you followed the order and because you didn't call the assembly. 'A mutiny I survived,' she says. 'A quartermaster I trust is harder to find.' You are now in the part of the ship's command that matters most.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Nine days becalmed. The crew has had time to think, which is not always a useful thing on a ship with three days of water. The opinions have hardened into something that will take either action or resolution to dissolve.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2a"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"ship-cabin",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The charts on Mara's cabin table are covered in marks you don't recognize \u2014 routes, timings, contact positions. She's been working this problem since the calm started. Whatever she decided, she decided it with more information than she gave you access to.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The crossing window is six hours. The calm water that's been your enemy for nine days is, in the crossing, your friend \u2014 no wind means consistent course, predictable drift, no unexpected sail behavior that might draw a patrol's eye.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s4a"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Rook knew at dawn. You're finding this out now. The information flow on this ship runs through channels you're not always in, which is either because Mara doesn't fully trust you or because she was protecting operational security. Both interpretations have implications.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The operation. Mara briefed you before the order. You understood the reasoning. You ran the quarter-deck with more confidence than any previous operation because you knew what you were doing and why.",choices:[{label:"Run the operation clean.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Notice what's different.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"What's different: you're not second-guessing the order because you already asked about the order and got an answer. The quarter-deck without the gap feels like a different ship. Same ship, different information flow. Rook notices. He says nothing. He moves his notes to the left side of the chart table.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2b"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The operation goes clean. You make the crossing, execute the cargo run, return to port with everything accounted for. Mara meets you at the chart table afterward with the next route already plotted.",choices:[{label:"Run the operation clean.",next:"xf2b"},{label:"Notice what's different.",next:"xfb1"}]},xf2b:{id:"xf2b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The moment of resolution. Everything has come together or not, and now you find out what it means.",choices:[{label:"Find out what comes next.",next:"e1"},{label:"Think about what changed.",next:"e2"},{label:"Think about the crew.",next:"e3"},{label:"Think about Mara.",next:"e4"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The briefing is now part of how the ship operates. Mara gives you the intelligence before the order. You ask the questions you need to ask. The order makes sense before it's given. This is what the crossing was about, it turns out \u2014 not the water or the patrol ships, but the process.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"ship-cabin",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The route intelligence for the next six weeks. Mara goes through it with you at the chart table \u2014 the contacts, the reliability ratings, the crossing windows and their risks. Rook is there too. Three people looking at the same charts for the first time in this configuration.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The crew, who knew something was different after the crossing, have adjusted to the new version of how the command deck operates. The adjustment is small from the outside \u2014 a briefing that happens before the order, questions asked and answered at the chart table. From the inside: everything.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-prize",packId:"pirates",title:"The Prize",subtitle:"The ship is yours. What's in the hold is not what you were told.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You've taken the merchant vessel clean \u2014 no blood, a perfect boarding. The manifest said luxury goods. The hold says something very different. The merchant captain is asking to speak to you privately.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"ship-hold",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The hold of the Constance Maerl has forty crates. The manifest said: crated glassware, Velasian wine, surveying instruments. What is actually here: one crate of glassware, seven crates of sealed Admiralty cipher correspondence, twenty crates of something dense and heavy wrapped in oilskin, and twelve crates that are breathing. Not loudly. But breathing. Your boarding party is looking at you. The merchant captain, a small woman named Aldric, is on the deck above, bound but calm.",choices:[{label:"Open one of the breathing crates first.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Open one of the oilskin crates.",next:"b1b"},{label:"Go speak to Aldric before opening anything.",next:"s2"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"ship-hold",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"People. Fourteen in the crate you open \u2014 cramped, frightened, alive. By your estimate: twelve crates, forty to sixty people total. Aldric's ship is a transport. The question is which kind and for whom.",choices:[{label:"Go speak to Aldric immediately.",next:"s2"},{label:"Think through the logistics of what you're seeing.",next:"b3a"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"ship-hold",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Weapons \u2014 current-model military muskets, still in transit wrapping, marked with a colonial settlement code you recognize as a contested territory. Someone is arming a colony. The Admiralty cipher correspondence above suggests they know about it.",choices:[{label:"Speak to Aldric.",next:"s2"},{label:"Consider what the weapons tell you.",next:"b4a"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Aldric says: 'I was wondering when you'd come up.' She is calm in a way that means she was expecting to be boarded. You ask which. She says: 'The people in the hold are under my protection. Colonial labor contracts being contested \u2014 they're being transported to a tribunal. If you take this ship, those cases don't get heard.' Flatly, without drama. 'The weapons are a separate issue. I don't know who put them there.'",choices:[{label:"Believe her about the people. Doubt her about the weapons.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Take her at her word on both counts.",next:"s3b"},{label:"The prize is the prize \u2014 take everything.",next:"s3c"},{label:"Ask who she actually is.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Watch Rook while you think.",next:"b5a"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"She's a colonial advocate, appointed by a reform group in the capital to transport contested cases to the tribunal. The ship's manifest was the merchant house's document, not hers \u2014 she didn't know about the weapons in the bottom layer. Believable. Unverifiable.",choices:[{label:"Let the ship go.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Take the weapons and correspondence, leave the rest.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Take everything and figure it out in port.",next:"s3c"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You take the weapons and the Admiralty correspondence. Leave the people, the glassware, the wine. Aldric watches you make this decision. She says: 'The correspondence contains the names of three colonial advocates including mine.' This is new information.",choices:[{label:"Take it anyway.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Leave the correspondence.",next:"s4b"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You believe her. You board back with empty hands and tell Rook the hold isn't what the intelligence said. He says: 'We boarded a clean ship for nothing?' You say: not for nothing. You know your Savia contact's intelligence is compromised. Aldric, as she goes, throws you a small sealed tube. 'Read it in a week.'",choices:[{label:"Sail away.",next:"xfm1"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"ship-hold",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The prize is the prize. You take the ship into port. The people in the crates come out into the light and the harbor master's men arrive immediately. The situation becomes complicated in the way that situations with forty frightened people and Admiralty cipher correspondence always do.",choices:[{label:"Deal with the harbor master.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You take the correspondence. The weapons go to a fence. The correspondence goes to a broker who deals in information, who sells it to someone you'll never identify, who uses the names in it in a way you'll never learn.",choices:[{label:"Find the outcome.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You take the weapons and leave the correspondence. Aldric sails. The weapons sell in the free ports for enough to keep your ship operational for four months. Your Savia contact gets a visit from Rook that results in better intelligence going forward.",choices:[{label:"Find the outcome.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The weapons sell well. The correspondence reached someone who used the advocates' names in a way you'll never learn. You made a clean boarding and came away with a good haul. You don't know what you set in motion. This is the thing you will occasionally think about on long watches.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Six weeks later you hear through the trade network that the colonial labor tribunal ruled in favor of the contested workers. You had a small part in that by not having a larger part in stopping it. The weapons paid for four months of operations. Aldric's letter, when you read it, is an introduction to a network worth more than the weapons.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The letter, opened a week later, is an introduction to an advocate network that operates in the colonial ports \u2014 people who will pay for specific information and occasionally need things transported that the official networks cannot carry. Aldric assessed you in the boarding and decided you were worth the risk. It is the beginning of a different kind of work.",choices:[]},e4:{id:"e4",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The harbor master takes the weapons as port fees and confiscates the correspondence as Admiralty property. The people are released by order of a colonial advocate who appears within the hour \u2014 Aldric's network was watching. You come out of it with the glassware, the wine, and the surveying instruments. Rook says: 'That's not nothing.' You say: 'It's not what we came for.' He says: 'It rarely is.'",choices:[]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"ship-hold",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Forty to sixty people in twelve crates. The logistics of this \u2014 the air, the time, the temperature \u2014 are not casual. Someone planned this transport with care. Whether that means Aldric is who she says she is, or that someone else is managing the cargo, is not a question the hold can answer.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The weapons are current-model and in transit wrapping. They haven't been opened. They were loaded, sealed, and shipped \u2014 a supply operation, not a personal cache. Someone with access to a military quartermaster put these here. The Admiralty correspondence above them suggests someone official knows.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Rook's hand on his belt knife is not a decision \u2014 it's a habit. You've seen him do it when the situation is unclear and when the situation is clear. The difference is whether he lets go of the handle or tightens his grip. Right now his grip is loose. He's waiting for you.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Port. The operation is concluded. Whatever you took and whatever you left, the Constance Maerl is somewhere in the southern waters and you are in a free port with a haul and a story.",choices:[{label:"Count what you have.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Think about Aldric.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"port-town",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Aldric assessed you in thirty seconds in a hold. She made a decision based on that assessment. The decision was to give you a letter rather than a lie. She was right about what kind of decision you'd make, which means she read you accurately in a boarding situation, which is its own kind of competence.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2b"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"What the operation produced: the cargo, the intelligence failure identified, the letter if you kept it, the knowledge of the Constance Maerl's network. Not what the manifest said. Something more complicated and more interesting.",choices:[{label:"Count what you have.",next:"xf2b"},{label:"Think about Aldric.",next:"xfb1"}]},xf2b:{id:"xf2b",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The moment of resolution. Everything has come together or not, and now you find out what it means.",choices:[{label:"Find out what comes next.",next:"e1"},{label:"Think about the intelligence failure.",next:"e2"},{label:"Think about Aldric's network.",next:"e3"},{label:"Think about the cost.",next:"e4"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Port. The cargo is sold. The intelligence failure has been addressed \u2014 Rook's visit to the Savia contact produced a replacement, someone with a cleaner record. Aldric's letter sits in the ship's strong-box or doesn't, depending on what you kept.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The broker who handles the weapons or the information \u2014 whichever you took \u2014 asks no questions about provenance. This is the free port guarantee: the product is sold on its merits and the history is not the history. You appreciate this and also know it applies to other people's products too.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Back on the water. The Constance Maerl boarding is three days behind you and already taking on the quality of a significant thing that is being integrated into the understanding of how things work. Rook has mentioned it once. Mara has not mentioned it. You've mentioned it to yourself several times.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmx"}]},xfmx:{id:"xfmx",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Port. The operation is concluded. The Constance Maerl is somewhere in the southern waters. You have what you have from the boarding and you are in a free port where what you have can become money or information or the beginning of a network, depending on what you do next.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-map",packId:"pirates",title:"The Map",subtitle:"It's real. You're sure of it. Your partner thinks you're wrong.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You pulled a map from a wreck three months ago. Your navigator says it's a forgery. Your gut says it's not. The location it marks is four weeks' sail. The treasure it promises would set the crew for life.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"ship-cabin",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The map has been on your chart table for three months. Fenwick, your navigator, has examined it seventeen times. Each time: 'The cartography is wrong.' The projection postdates when the map claims to have been made \u2014 either it's a forgery or the mapmaker knew something about coordinate systems before anyone else did. Fenwick says the first is more likely. The vellum is old. She says vellum can be old and the map can still be wrong. You sail in three days. The location is four weeks past your last planned stop.",choices:[{label:"Extend the voyage \u2014 follow the map.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Take Fenwick's assessment seriously. Don't go.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Get a second opinion from a cartographer in port.",next:"b1a"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"ship-cabin",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The cartographer looks at the map for twenty minutes. 'The projection is Aldrine's method. Aldrine published in 1692. This map's claimed date is 1658.' You say: 'Forgery?' He says: 'Or Aldrine wasn't first.' He charges eight silver and gives you nothing conclusive. Fenwick says: 'That's not reassurance.' You say: 'It's not dismissal either.'",choices:[{label:"Follow the map.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Don't go.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Think about who would make a forgery this detailed.",next:"b2a"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You tell the crew. The vote \u2014 you run a voting crew \u2014 is eleven to four in favor. Fenwick votes no and accepts the result. She says: 'If I'm right, I want it noted.' You note it in the log. Two weeks in, the map's coastal landmarks start appearing exactly where the map says they should be \u2014 not where any published chart puts them.",choices:[{label:"Push on \u2014 the landmarks are real.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Stop and resurvey before going further.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Think about what the crew voted for.",next:"b3a"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You run the southern routes as planned. At the last stop, a trade captain tells you a ship called the Andover found something in the southern chains three weeks ago and is keeping quiet about it in the way that means it's significant. The location he describes is four weeks from where you were.",choices:[{label:"Decide what to do.",next:"xfm1"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Week four. The location on the map is an island on no published chart but undeniably there in front of you \u2014 a low green island with a specific rock formation on the southern tip that the map drew with detail no forger would bother with. Fenwick is at the rail. She says nothing for a long time. Then: 'I want to be in the first boat.'",choices:[{label:"Land and explore.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Circle the island and assess first.",next:"s4b"},{label:"Think about what the keel can take.",next:"b4a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You stop and resurvey. Two days. The coastal features are real and not on any published chart. Fenwick spends the second day with the map and finds, in the corner, a cartographer's mark she missed \u2014 a personal cipher matching one from a private collection she studied in a library in Vellaren. The mark is from 1661. Before Aldrine published. She says: 'I owe you eight silver.'",choices:[{label:"Land on the island.",next:"s4a"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"ship-island",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The map marks the interior \u2014 a valley behind the central ridge, three stones, a cache below. You find the valley. The stones. Below them, a sealed iron chest that has been there long enough that the lock is fused with rust. What's inside is not quite what the map promised but it is substantial and real.",choices:[{label:"Take it and go.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Document the site fully before disturbing anything.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"ship-island",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You circle the island. The south shore has a cove not on the map, and in the cove there is a ship \u2014 not wrecked, anchored. Someone got here before you by a short margin or is here for a different reason.",choices:[{label:"Approach and find out who it is.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Land on the north shore and get to the cache first.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"ship-island",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The chest holds coin, documents, and an inventory of three additional caches the map did not mark \u2014 a dead man's distributed hiding system. Fenwick spends the return voyage deciphering the inventory documents. You arrive in port with two of three additional locations identified. Fenwick has had the map on her chart table every night of the return voyage and hasn't said she was wrong. She doesn't need to.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"ship-island",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You document the site. The documentation \u2014 which you later sell to a geographic society along with a copy of the original map \u2014 earns nearly as much as the chest. The society funds a return expedition that discovers two more caches. You are credited as original finders and receive a percentage. The map Fenwick said was a forgery becomes a reference document.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"ship-island",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The other ship is a colonial geographic survey vessel. They arrived three days ago and haven't found the cache, which isn't on any of their charts. You are now in a negotiation about respective claims, conducted in a cove off an island that doesn't officially exist, between a pirate vessel and a government survey ship.",choices:[]},e4:{id:"e4",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You sail for the location. The Andover is there \u2014 anchored in the cove of the island not on any chart. Their captain waves: 'We wondered if someone else had the map. We thought we had the only copy.' There were two. The island has enough for two ships to share without conflict, if both captains are reasonable. Fenwick, looking at the island she said you'd never find, says nothing for a very long time.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"ship-cabin",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A forgery built on old vellum would be expensive and specific \u2014 someone went to significant effort to make it convincing. Forgeries that detailed are usually made to sell to someone who will act on them. Which means either the map is real, or someone wanted you to believe it was real and sail to a specific location.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2a"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The crew who voted against the extension voted against it for practical reasons \u2014 water, time, money. The crew who voted for it voted for it for a variety of reasons, some practical and some that are harder to name but that have to do with what it means to chase something you're not sure is there.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two feet of water below the keel on a moving sea in a narrowing channel. The Cerro has survived things that should have killed her. She is, as Rook says, sounder than she looks. You've also learned that what a ship can survive and what you should ask her to survive are not always the same question.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The meeting with the geographic society. Vasques has read everything \u2014 the map, the inventory, the navigation key, Fenwick's field notes. She is systematic and specific in a way that suggests she has been waiting for something like this.",choices:[{label:"Hear the offer.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Ask about the island's status.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"port-town",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The island is on no chart, which means it has no colonial status, which means whoever discovers it in an official capacity can register it. Vasques is thinking about this. You are thinking about this. Fenwick, when you tell her later, says: 'That's the second find.' She means: the island itself is worth more than the cache.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2b"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The society's offer: full funding for the return expedition, geographic credit for the discovery, publication rights to Fenwick's notes and navigation key, and a percentage of any historical value the additional caches produce.",choices:[{label:"Negotiate the terms.",next:"e1"},{label:"Accept without conditions.",next:"e2"},{label:"Ask what they get from it.",next:"e3"},{label:"Decline and fund it yourself.",next:"e4"}]},xf2b:{id:"xf2b",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The moment of resolution. Everything has come together or not, and now you find out what it means.",choices:[{label:"Negotiate the terms.",next:"e1"},{label:"Accept.",next:"e2"},{label:"Ask what they get.",next:"e3"},{label:"Go yourself.",next:"e4"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The geographic society meeting is scheduled. In the meantime, Fenwick is working through the navigation key with a focus that has displaced her usual fastidiousness about the ship's charts. Rook says: 'She's more interested in dead cartographers than living routes.' You say: 'Temporarily.'",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The coin from the cache is sold through a historical broker who specializes in provenance-neutral transactions. The price is good. The provenance is 'recovered from a maritime salvage site,' which is technically accurate. Fenwick negotiates the price. She is better at this than you expected.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Vasques, the geographic society's secretary, sends a preliminary note before the meeting: she's verified the vellum, the ink, the projection system, and the cartographer's mark in the corner. Her note says: 'We believe you have found something significant.' Fenwick reads the note twice. She hands it back without comment.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmx"}]},xfmx:{id:"xfmx",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The meeting with Vasques and the geographic society is tomorrow. Tonight, in the ship's cabin, you and Fenwick go through the navigation key one more time. The island, the cache, the inventory, the two additional locations \u2014 it's all real and it's all documented and tomorrow it will enter the official record.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmy"}]},xfmy:{id:"xfmy",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The night before the geographic society meeting. Fenwick has the navigation key and the field notes in order. You have the cache inventory. The coin is sold. The island is real, documented, and about to become officially known. Tomorrow the expedition enters the record.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmz"}]},xfmz:{id:"xfmz",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You're outside the geographic society office. The meeting is in ten minutes. Fenwick is reviewing her notes. Rook is not here \u2014 he said this was your thing, which is both true and a gift, since his opinion of academic institutions in general is known.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmw"}]},xfmw:{id:"xfmw",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Vasques leads you into the society's library. Three other members are present. On the table: a copy of the map, Fenwick's field notes, and the navigation key, all spread out and already annotated in the margins. They've been working with the materials since you sent them. This is going to be a substantive meeting.",choices:[{label:"Go to the meeting.",next:"xf2"}]}}},{id:"the-storm",packId:"pirates",title:"The Storm",subtitle:"Three days out. No shelter. The ship can survive it. Maybe.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"The storm came from the south with twelve hours' notice and no good options. Your ship can weather it \u2014 probably. The island to the northeast is Admiralty territory and would mean arrest. The reef to the south might be passable at high tide.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The barometer has been falling since dawn. By noon the southern horizon was black in the particular way that means ten hours at most. Your ship \u2014 the Cerro \u2014 is sound but not a storm ship. Rook has the options: run northeast to the Admiralty island of Kael's Point and take arrest, run southeast to the Santine Reef where there might be a passage at high tide, or heave to and take the storm in open water and trust the Cerro and your crew.",choices:[{label:"Run for the reef \u2014 attempt the passage.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Heave to. Take the storm in open water.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Run for Kael's Point. Take the arrest.",next:"s2c"},{label:"Let the crew vote.",next:"b1a"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The vote: eight for the reef, five for heaving to, four for Kael's Point. The four who voted Kael's Point are the four who've served the least time and have no warrants. The reef it is, with full awareness that eight people made the decision and all seventeen will live or die by it.",choices:[{label:"Run for the reef.",next:"s2a"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Santine Reef at high tide: a channel your charts say is forty feet at its shallowest, two hundred feet wide. Your charts are six years old. Fenwick is at the bow with a lead line. The storm is six hours behind you and gaining. You reach the reef entrance at what your calculations say is ninety minutes past high tide \u2014 the channel is shallowing.",choices:[{label:"Attempt the passage \u2014 close enough to the tide window.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Turn back. The timing is too tight.",next:"s3b"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You heave to. Sea anchors out, minimum sail, all hands on deck in safety lines. The storm hits at midnight. Twelve hours of sustained force that tests every joint of the Cerro and the endurance of every person on her.",choices:[{label:"Ride it out.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Think about what a storm teaches.",next:"b2a"}]},s2c:{id:"s2c",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You run for Kael's Point. The garrison there is small \u2014 four ships, none of them fast. You make it seven hours before the storm fully hits. The garrison commander boards you at dawn, politely, with four armed men.",choices:[{label:"Cooperate and negotiate.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Find a way out before the processing is complete.",next:"s4b"},{label:"Consider the garrison commander.",next:"b4a"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Fenwick calls the depth: thirty-eight feet. Thirty-five. The channel is narrowing on the starboard side where the chart showed it shouldn't. The storm is visible on the southern horizon. Thirty-three feet. The Cerro's draft is thirty-one. Two feet of water below the keel on a moving sea in a narrowing channel.",choices:[{label:"Hold nerve and push through.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Turn around now while you still can.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Think through the math of the reef.",next:"b3a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You turn back from the reef. The option that remains is open water. You heave to fifteen miles from the reef and take the storm as it comes.",choices:[{label:"Take the storm.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The garrison commander isn't unreasonable \u2014 he has a storm outside and limited holding space. You reach an arrangement: your charts of the Santine Reef and two months of route intelligence in exchange for release without record. You sail out after the storm with your crew, your ship, and a garrison commander who knows your routes and owes you nothing.",choices:[{label:"Find the outcome.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The processing takes two days. On the second night, when the garrison is occupied with storm damage on their own vessels, Rook has arranged a distraction and a boat. Twelve of seventeen crew make it to the Cerro. Five are left in the garrison holding.",choices:[{label:"Get clear.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You hold nerve. The Cerro clears the reef with eighteen inches to spare on the shallowest point. The shelter of the reef wall is immediate and complete \u2014 the storm passes over the open water side and you sit in the lee watching it without being in it. Fenwick is already fixing the chart. She notes the passage time, depth, channel width. It will be correct now.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"The Cerro survives \u2014 she is, as Rook has always said, sounder than she looks. You lose the foremast yard and two weeks of usable sail. The crew is intact. All seventeen. When the sea flattens you are fifty miles from where you started with a damaged rig and a crew that just spent twelve hours finding out what they're made of.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You sail out of Kael's Point with your crew, your ship, and charts now known to the garrison. The garrison commander knows your routes. Whether this is debt or vulnerability depends on what he does with it. You won't know for a while. Rook says: 'Better than a prison cell.' You say: 'Yes.' The calculation isn't finished yet.",choices:[]},e4:{id:"e4",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You get clear. Five crew are still in the garrison. You spend the next three weeks arranging their release through a broker in the free ports \u2014 the better part of what you earned on the last three runs. They get out. The garrison commander knows you went back for your people, which earns you a particular kind of reputation in a particular kind of network, the kind where being known for something specific has value you can't always spend directly.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Twelve hours of storm is twelve hours of sustained decision-making at the edge of capacity. It's also the most information you'll ever get about your crew in the shortest amount of time. You learn things about people in a storm that you wouldn't learn in three months of regular sailing.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The reef at high tide is one calculation. The reef at ninety minutes past high tide with a storm closing from the south is a different calculation, and the variables are not friendly. Fenwick is at the bow calling depth because she knows what's at stake and she's the best person for the job.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The garrison at Kael's Point has processed boats before \u2014 not many, but some. The commander knows that a crew willing to turn themselves in to survive a storm is a crew that has something worth surviving for. This creates a negotiating position, of sorts, if you're willing to use it.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s4a"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The storm is over or you're in safe water or you've negotiated your way through the garrison. The Cerro is intact. The crew is intact. The cost is whatever it was.",choices:[{label:"Account for the cost.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Think about what the crew went through.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"What the crew went through: twelve hours or a garrison or a reef passage, depending on what you chose. What you know now about each person on this ship that you didn't know before the storm: more. Storms are expensive. They're also efficient at producing information.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2b"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Cerro runs south. The storm is behind you in weather terms and will be in front of you in planning terms for the next season \u2014 you'll make different decisions with this storm in your memory. Rook is at the bow. He's been quiet since the weather broke.",choices:[{label:"Account for the cost.",next:"xf2b"},{label:"Think about the crew.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb2:{id:"xfb2",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Rook says: 'Good call.' Whatever you chose \u2014 reef or open water or garrison \u2014 he says it was the right call. You ask how he knows. 'Because the ship is here and the crew is here. Those are the only two things that measure a call in a storm.' You say: 'We lost the foremast yard.' He says: 'The foremast yard is fixable.'",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"e1"}]},xf2b:{id:"xf2b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The moment of resolution. Everything has come together or not, and now you find out what it means.",choices:[{label:"Run clean.",next:"e1"},{label:"Think about the crew.",next:"e2"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The storm is behind you. The Cerro has whatever damage she has \u2014 a foremast yard, a day of repairs in port, or nothing depending on what you chose. The crew is intact. The decisions you made in the twelve hours before the storm are now in the past and will go into the log and become part of the ship's record.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Port. The repairs \u2014 if any \u2014 are being assessed. The garrison negotiation \u2014 if any \u2014 is resolved. The Santine passage \u2014 if completed \u2014 has been added to Fenwick's charts with every sounding she called. Whatever the storm cost, the accounting is happening.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Back on the water. The routes continue. Rook says something about the storm that he's been waiting to say for a week. It's short. It contains everything he thought about what you did and who you are as the ship's captain. You listen. You take it in.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmx"}]},xfmx:{id:"xfmx",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"After the storm. The Cerro is running south in clear weather. The crew is intact. The ship is damaged or undamaged depending on what you chose. The routes continue. The season continues. The storm becomes part of the story you tell about this ship and this crew.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmy"}]},xfmy:{id:"xfmy",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The storm is three days behind you now. The Cerro is running clean in clear weather. The crew has slept. The damage \u2014 if any \u2014 is assessed and understood. Rook has been at the bow for an hour, which is what Rook does after something significant when he's processing.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmz"}]},xfmz:{id:"xfmz",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Open water. The storm is more than a week behind you now. The repairs are done \u2014 the foremast yard, if it broke, has been replaced in port. The routes continue. The Cerro is running well. The storm has become part of the story.",choices:[{label:"Talk to Rook.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Leave him to it.",next:"xf2"}]}}},{id:"the-port",packId:"pirates",title:"The Port",subtitle:"Three weeks ashore. The life you could have had is right in front of you.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"The Cerro is in dry dock for three weeks. For the first time in four years you have time and money and no plan. Then you run into someone from before the ship. Then you get an offer. Then you have to decide who you are when the ship isn't under you.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Vellaren is a free port and a complicated one. Three weeks while the Cerro's keel is repaired. Money for the first time in six months. Nothing that needs doing right now. On the second day you run into Seren, who you knew before the ship, before the southern routes, before you became the person you are now. Seren is a chandler's factor. They look exactly the same. You look, you suspect, nothing like you did.",choices:[{label:"Have a drink with Seren and see what happens.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Keep it brief. That life is behind you.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Ask what Seren has been doing.",next:"b1a"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"port-town",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Seren has been in Vellaren four years running the chandlery supply chain for three free port operators. Steady, good work. The same harbor, the same faces, a room that is the same room every night. You had that once. You chose the ship. Hearing Seren describe the harbor at dawn from the same window for four years produces something unexpected.",choices:[{label:"Have a drink with Seren.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Say goodbye and go do what you came to do.",next:"s2b"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"port-tavern",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three drinks. Seren is still the same person \u2014 same directness, same quality of listening. At the end of the evening Seren says: 'Stay.' Just that. 'You have money. The Cerro needs work. You could stay.' You say it's not that simple. Seren says: 'I know. I just wanted to say it was an option.'",choices:[{label:"Stay in Vellaren for the three weeks.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Keep to the plan. The ship is the plan.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Ask what staying would actually look like.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Think about what three drinks with Seren means.",next:"b4a"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say goodbye and mean it politely. You spend the three weeks doing what you came to do: refit, resupply, gather intelligence. Efficient. On the last day, a message arrives from a southern trade captain with an offer that requires a decision before you sail.",choices:[{label:"Read the offer.",next:"s3c"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"port-town",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Seren describes it: the chandlery has a position that opened last month. Shore-side logistics, good pay, same harbor. 'You know ships, cargo, routes better than anyone in this port. That knowledge doesn't have to be used the way you've been using it.' You think about this longer than you expected to.",choices:[{label:"Take the three weeks to decide.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Tell Seren you'll think about it but you're not staying.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Think about what the chandlery work actually involves.",next:"b3a"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You stay in Vellaren. Not in the chandlery position \u2014 not yet \u2014 but in the city. You eat meals in the same place. Sleep in the same room. You and Seren have the longer conversation the drinks started. You work alongside the dry dock crew on the Cerro's refit, which you've never done before \u2014 you've always managed the crew who do it. By the third week you know two things: the city is not the ship, and you're not sure which is what you need.",choices:[{label:"Sail with the Cerro as planned.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Stay in Vellaren and let the crew take the Cerro out.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Ask Rook what he thinks.",next:"b3a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Cerro is refitted. On the morning of departure Seren is on the dock. They don't say anything \u2014 they wave, which is the right thing, because there's nothing to say that hasn't been said. You sail out of Vellaren harbor in good wind and by afternoon the port is behind you.",choices:[{label:"Sail the southern routes.",next:"xfm1"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The offer is from a captain named Voss who runs a legitimate southern trading company and wants a route pilot who knows the reef systems. The pay is substantial. The work is legal. Your knowledge \u2014 built over four years of illegal navigation \u2014 is exactly what they need.",choices:[{label:"Take the offer.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Decline. The Cerro is your ship.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Consider what Voss's knowledge implies.",next:"b5a"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"port-town",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The chandlery work is logistics \u2014 where things are, where they need to be, how to move them. It is, you realize, not that different from what you do on the ship. The difference is the risk profile and the legal status and the fact that you go home to the same room every night.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You sail. The three weeks gave you the confirmation that the ship is the choice. Seren is in Vellaren. You are on the water. Both things are right. You sail the southern routes for two more years and then retire to a different harbor \u2014 not Vellaren, but one of the same kind \u2014 and write up your charts for the geographic society, which pays well and asks no questions about how they were made.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You stay. Rook takes the Cerro out as captain, with your commission and your blessing. You work the chandlery logistics for six months before the routes pull you back \u2014 not to piracy but to navigation work for a survey company. You and Seren share the harbor for three years. The Cerro comes back to Vellaren twice. Rook brings her in clean both times.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The southern routes. The Cerro under good wind. Whatever Vellaren was, it is behind you. You are the captain of the Cerro on the free routes and that is a specific thing that a specific person does, and you are still that person. The harbor is always there. You are choosing this while you can still choose it.",choices:[]},e4:{id:"e4",scene:"port-town",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You take the offer. The Cerro goes to Rook with full ownership transferred. He sails out on the morning tide and you watch from the dock next to Seren. The legitimate route work is steady and your knowledge is genuinely valuable. You miss the ship for about a year in a way that is sometimes sharp. Then the sharpness goes and what remains is the shape of who you were, which is not nothing.",choices:[]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"port-tavern",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three drinks is not enough to make the decision for you. Three drinks with Seren is enough to remind you that the decision exists and has been deferred in a particular way for four years \u2014 not avoided exactly, but not examined either.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b5a:{id:"b5a",scene:"port-town",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Voss's letter is written by someone who knows your routes well enough to describe them specifically \u2014 not the public routes but the actual routes, the ones that aren't in any chart you'd sell. This means either your intelligence network has been compromised or Voss has access to sources you don't know about.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Cerro is in the southern routes. Vellaren is behind you. The three weeks are behind you. Seren is in a harbor you've chosen not to put in at. All of this is behind you and also not behind you because the thing about decisions is that you carry them forward.",choices:[{label:"Sail and find out.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Think about Vellaren.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Vellaren. The three weeks. Seren at the dock as you left. The chandlery position. The dry dock and the caulking and the meal in the shade of the Cerro's hull. All of it is specific in the way of things that mattered. You carry it. Rook is watching you think. He says nothing. He's seen captains come back from port before.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2b"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"First run complete. The routes are the same routes. The Cerro is the same ship. You are \u2014 slightly different from who you were before Vellaren, in ways that will take time to name.",choices:[{label:"Sail and find out.",next:"xf2b"},{label:"Think about Vellaren.",next:"xfb1"}]},xf2b:{id:"xf2b",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The moment of resolution. Everything has come together or not, and now you find out what it means.",choices:[{label:"Think about who you are now.",next:"e1"},{label:"Think about what changed.",next:"e2"},{label:"Think about the ship.",next:"e3"},{label:"Think about coming back.",next:"e4"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The first run after Vellaren. The routes are the same routes. The Cerro is the same ship. You are what you are, which is a person who spent three weeks in a harbor and made a decision and is now back on the water. The water is good. The decision was the decision.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Rook, a week out: 'You're different.' You ask how. He says: 'You know exactly where Vellaren is.' You say you've always known where Vellaren is. He says: 'You've always known it as a port. Now you know it as something else.' He's right. You don't argue.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The second run. The routes settle into what they are \u2014 the specific texture of work you know how to do, in weather you know how to read, with a crew you know. By the end of the second run, Vellaren is somewhere behind you in the way that things you've decided about are behind you.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmx"}]},xfmx:{id:"xfmx",scene:"ship-deck",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Out of Vellaren. The harbor receding. The southern routes ahead. The three weeks are behind you: the dry dock work, the meals with Seren, the decision you made about what kind of life you're living. All of it is behind you now in the way that things you've resolved are behind you.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-first-date",packId:"dating",title:"The First Date",subtitle:"Everything is going well. Which is exactly when you say the wrong thing.",length:"Medium",free:!0,brief:"The date is going better than any date you've had in two years. Good conversation, the restaurant was the right call, they laughed at something you said. Then you said the thing. You're not sure how bad it was. You need to figure out whether to address it or let it pass.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The thing you said: you mentioned your ex. Not on purpose \u2014 it came out naturally in a story, and without thinking you said the name in the middle of a sentence and then completed the sentence and then the sentence was done. Alex was looking at you with an expression that was carefully neutral. Which is not how Alex has been looking at you for the previous forty minutes. The previous forty minutes had been good. You have approximately three seconds to decide what to do.",choices:[{label:"Address it directly \u2014 acknowledge you mentioned your ex.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Let it pass and keep the conversation moving.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Make a small self-deprecating comment and redirect.",next:"b1a"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'Sorry \u2014 mentioned the ex. Classic first date move.' Alex's expression shifts from carefully neutral to something closer to actual amusement. They say: 'It happens.' Then: 'How long were you together?' Which is either a genuine question or a test. The fact that they asked suggests the carefully neutral expression wasn't as bad a sign as you thought.",choices:[{label:"Answer honestly.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Give the short version and redirect.",next:"s3b"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'I just mentioned my ex. Sorry \u2014 that slipped out.' Alex says: 'I noticed.' Not unkind. Then: 'Are you over it?' Which is a fair question on a first date and also slightly terrifying. You take a moment before answering, which is the right choice.",choices:[{label:"Say yes, you are.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Say mostly \u2014 and be honest about what mostly means.",next:"s3c"},{label:"Say you're not sure it's first-date material.",next:"s3d"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You let it pass. The conversation continues. Alex continues to engage \u2014 laughs at the next thing, follows up on something you said earlier, shares something personal in return. The carefully neutral expression is gone. Either it wasn't as bad as you thought, or Alex decided not to make it a thing, which is its own kind of information about who Alex is.",choices:[{label:"End the evening on the momentum you've rebuilt.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Circle back at the end \u2014 address it before you say goodnight.",next:"s4b"},{label:"Notice what the careful neutral expression is doing.",next:"b2a"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say yes. Alex looks at you for a second. 'How long has it been?' Two years. They nod. 'That's enough time.' Then, after a pause: 'Mine was eighteen months ago. I mentioned her to someone on a date six months after and then didn't go on another date for four months because of how that went.' You say you're glad you mentioned it, then. Apparently you're both people who do that.",choices:[{label:"See where the evening goes.",next:"s4c"},{label:"Think about which version of the ex you mentioned.",next:"b3a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say two years together, two years ago, all fine. Alex nods. Then: 'Two years is a long time.' You say it was. They let it rest there. The conversation moves on naturally and you spend the rest of dinner not thinking about the thing you said, which is the outcome you wanted.",choices:[{label:"Finish the evening.",next:"s4a"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'Mostly. There are things I've worked out and things I'm still working out.' Alex says: 'That's honest.' Then: 'Same, honestly. Mine was different \u2014 we wanted different things, it was clean, but clean doesn't mean simple.' You are having a conversation more real than most first dates reach.",choices:[{label:"See where the evening goes.",next:"s4c"}]},s3d:{id:"s3d",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'Probably not first-date material, honestly.' Alex says: 'Fair.' Then, after a moment: 'For the record, I'm not keeping score. You mentioned an ex. It happens.' The even way they say it makes you like them more.",choices:[{label:"Finish the evening.",next:"s4a"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The evening ends naturally, well. Outside, a moment that resolves cleanly. Whether there's a second date is the question.",choices:[{label:"Find out.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Notice what the moment outside means.",next:"b4a"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"At the end of the night you say: 'I want to circle back to the ex thing earlier. I didn't intend to bring that into the evening.' Alex looks at you with something definitely warmer than carefully neutral. 'I know. It's fine.' Then: 'I appreciated that you acknowledged it.'",choices:[{label:"See if there's a second date.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4c:{id:"s4c",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The dinner went to places neither of you planned. Time disappeared. Both of you had to check your phones to notice. Outside, Alex says: 'I'd like to do this again.'",choices:[{label:"Find out.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Two days later Alex texts to suggest the second date. The ex mention sits in your memory as a small thing you handled by not handling \u2014 by reading that Alex was not going to make it a big thing and letting them lead on that. Which is its own skill, and one you're glad you had tonight.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Alex says: 'Second date?' You say yes. They say: 'Good. No agenda for that one \u2014 just see what happens.' Walking home you think about the fact that circling back to the thing was the right call. Not because it changed anything about the evening but because it told Alex something about who you are when you've said something you didn't mean to say.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The date ends with plans for a second one. The ex mention turned into the most honest moment of the evening, and Alex was looking for honest. You find this out gradually over the next several weeks, which is the right speed to find it out.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The carefully neutral expression is doing work. You've seen Alex's real expressions for forty minutes \u2014 the genuine amusement, the interested attention, the thing they do when they're about to say something they've been thinking about for a moment. The careful neutral is different. It's holding something still.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2a"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two years is long enough that the relationship is past tense in most practical ways. It's also long enough that certain things about it are still present tense \u2014 habits of thinking, reflexive comparisons, the specific texture of knowing someone that doesn't fully go away. You're not sure which version just came out in the sentence.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"date-street",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The moment outside \u2014 the brief negotiation of what the end of the evening is \u2014 is its own kind of information. The way it resolves says something that the conversation inside didn't get to say directly.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The walk home. The evening behind you. You're holding whatever the evening gave you \u2014 the ex mention, the recovery, Alex's expression shifting from carefully neutral to something warmer. The date ended the way it ended.",choices:[{label:"Think about the evening.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Think about Alex.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"date-street",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Alex. The forty minutes before the ex mention. The carefully neutral expression. The shift afterward, however it happened. The way they said 'it happens' or 'I know' or 'same' \u2014 whichever version occurred. You don't know what Alex is walking home thinking. You know what you're walking home thinking.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2b"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Home. The date is over. It was two hours. You know more about Alex than you did before and less than you'd know after another two hours, which is what second dates are for. Whether there will be a second date is a question that will answer itself.",choices:[{label:"Think about the evening.",next:"xf2b"},{label:"Think about Alex.",next:"xfb1"}]},xf2b:{id:"xf2b",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The moment of resolution. Everything has come together or not, and now you find out what it means.",choices:[{label:"A second date happens.",next:"e1"},{label:"Alex texts first.",next:"e2"},{label:"You text first.",next:"e3"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"After the date. The evening is in the past now. You go home, or you walk for a bit, or you sit somewhere \u2014 wherever the end of a good first date finds you. The date was what it was: two hours with someone you didn't know before and know a little now.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The day after. Normal day. You go to work or school or wherever you go. The date is a thing that happened yesterday. You think about it at unexpected moments \u2014 something Alex said, the way the carefully neutral expression shifted, the specific quality of the evening after it found its level.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two days after. The question of whether there will be a second date has either answered itself or is still open. You have your phone. Alex has their phone. The move could come from either direction. You decide what you're willing to do.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmx"}]},xfmx:{id:"xfmx",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The evening is over. Whatever it was \u2014 good enough for a second date, good enough for a text, good enough to remember as a good evening \u2014 it happened. You walked home or drove home or sat somewhere, and the date settled into the past tense.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-long-distance",packId:"dating",title:"The Long Distance",subtitle:"Eight months in. The conversation neither of you have had yet.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"Eight months together, six of them long distance. The arrangement was supposed to be temporary. Neither of you have said it isn't working. Both of you know someone has to say something. The call is tonight.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You've been together eight months, long distance for six of them \u2014 the first two were in the same city, then Jordan's position moved and the plan was 'let's see how it goes.' Six months of seeing how it goes. You've been to Jordan's city twice. Jordan has been here four times. The visits are good. The five weeks between visits are long. You haven't said this. Jordan hasn't said it. The call is in forty minutes.",choices:[{label:"Have the conversation tonight \u2014 bring it up directly.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Have a normal call. Wait for the right moment.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Text Jordan before the call to flag that you want to talk about something.",next:"b1a"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You text: 'Can we talk about something tonight? The distance thing.' Jordan's reply comes in four minutes: 'Yes. I've been wanting to also.' Which is either the best possible response or a response that means something different than what you hope.",choices:[{label:"Have the call.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Think about what six months of Thursday calls means.",next:"b2a"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You bring it up twenty minutes in. You say: 'I want to talk about the distance.' Jordan says: 'Okay.' Then, before you continue: 'I've been wanting to say something for a while and I keep not saying it.' You say: 'Same.' Then both of you wait, which is exactly what happens when two people have the same conversation to have.",choices:[{label:"Say what you've been not saying.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Ask Jordan to go first.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Think about what Jordan carrying the same thing means.",next:"b3a"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A good call \u2014 Jordan had a work thing that went well, you finished something you'd been stuck on, you talk about the show you're both watching. Forty minutes in, Jordan says: 'Can I ask you something?' You say yes. 'Are we going to talk about the thing we're not talking about?' So they've been waiting too.",choices:[{label:"Talk about it.",next:"s2a"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'I don't think the distance is working. I want to be with you in a way the calls and visits aren't giving me. I don't know what the solution is \u2014 I don't know who can move \u2014 but the current arrangement isn't what I want long-term.' Silence. Then Jordan: 'I needed to hear you say that. I've been saying it to my friends for three months.'",choices:[{label:"Ask what Jordan wants.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Lay out what you're able to consider.",next:"s4b"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Jordan says: 'I think I need us to make a decision. Either someone moves or we admit nobody is going to move and figure out what that means.' You ask what Jordan is able to do. 'My position isn't moveable right now. Another year at least.' You let that sit.",choices:[{label:"Tell Jordan what you can consider.",next:"s4b"},{label:"Ask if a year is the actual timeline.",next:"s4c"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Jordan says: 'I want to be in the same city. My position isn't moveable for at least a year \u2014 but after that, I don't have a reason I'd have to stay.' A year is a timeline. A year is not forever.",choices:[{label:"Commit to a plan for the year.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Say you need to think about what a year means.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Think about what a year actually contains.",next:"b4a"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: you can visit more, look at whether any of your work is flexible, have a conversation about timeline \u2014 but you can't promise a move without knowing it's going somewhere real, and you need to define what 'somewhere real' means. Jordan: 'That's fair. That's what I want to know too.'",choices:[{label:"Talk about what going somewhere real would look like.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Set a six-month checkpoint.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4c:{id:"s4c",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You ask if a year is firm or approximate. 'The position runs two years total. I'm a year in.' You ask what's holding them there beyond the position. Jordan is quiet. Then: 'My family is there. That's the part I didn't say.' This is new information. A position ends. A family situation is something else.",choices:[{label:"Ask about the family situation.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Tell Jordan this changes how you're thinking about it.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You commit to a year with a real plan: more visits, a clear end date, a shared understanding that you're both working toward the same city. The call ends at midnight. It is the best call you've had in eight months because it was the first where you both said the actual thing. A year of Thursday calls is different when they have a direction.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You say you need to think about what a year means for you specifically. Jordan: 'I know. I'm not asking for an answer tonight.' The call ends with both of you having said the real things and neither of you having resolved them. You spend the next week thinking. The conclusion you reach is going to bring you closer or clarify that the distance isn't the only problem.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You talk about what going somewhere real would look like \u2014 timelines, what each of you needs to feel certain. By the end of the call you don't have a plan but you have a shared definition of what you're working toward. The Thursday call ends at 1 AM. You don't notice until you hang up.",choices:[]},e4:{id:"e4",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You set a six-month checkpoint: another version of this conversation with more information about what's possible. Jordan agrees. You put it in both your calendars. It's not a decision but it's a structure, and the structure is better than the previous arrangement, which was no structure and a building weight of things unsaid.",choices:[]},e5:{id:"e5",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Jordan tells you about their mother's health \u2014 the situation that started two years ago, why Jordan took the position in that city in the first place. The position was never just a career decision. The call goes past midnight. You know Jordan better at the end of it. The distance has a shape and a reason now. The shape changes what's possible and what isn't.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Six months of Thursday calls. The calls are good \u2014 they're better than most of the daily interactions you have with people you see in person. But a good Thursday call is a different thing from a life that contains someone, and you've been trying not to notice the difference for long enough that it's become difficult to not notice.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2a"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Jordan saying they've been wanting to say something for a while is information. They've been carrying the same weight in the same direction, which could mean the weight points at the same destination or could mean it points at different ones. You won't know until you say the things.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A year is a specific amount of time that is also an abstract amount of time when you're trying to decide something now. Twelve months of Thursday calls. Eight more visits, maybe. The question is whether the end of a year produces the thing you're working toward or just produces the end of a year.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"After the call. The conversation is in the past now \u2014 the real things said, the decision or non-decision made, the shape of what comes next roughly visible. The distance is still the distance. The relationship is still the relationship.",choices:[{label:"Think about the year ahead.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Think about Jordan.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Jordan. The six months of calls. The four visits. The conversation tonight. The family situation you didn't know until tonight. The position that ends in a year. All of it is data about a person you've been with for eight months and who you know well and don't know completely, which is true of everyone but feels more specific at a distance.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2b"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The year ahead. Twelve months of Thursday calls, or more. Visits that take planning and cost money and feel too short. The accumulation of distance in both the literal and the other sense. And at the end: either the same city or the same knowledge that it's not going to be the same city.",choices:[{label:"Think about the year ahead.",next:"xf2b"},{label:"Think about Jordan.",next:"xfb1"}]},xf2b:{id:"xf2b",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The moment of resolution. Everything has come together or not, and now you find out what it means.",choices:[{label:"Figure out the year.",next:"e1"},{label:"Decide to wait.",next:"e2"},{label:"Plan something specific.",next:"e3"},{label:"Think about going somewhere real.",next:"e5"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"After the call. The apartment is quiet. The distance is the same distance it was before the call. The relationship is the same relationship. What's different is the conversation you now have underneath both.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The week after. Thursday call scheduled. The Thursday calls have a different quality after the conversation \u2014 not lighter exactly, but more honest. Less being fine, more being specific about how fine or not fine things are.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A month later. Whatever you decided \u2014 a plan, a checkpoint, a difficult conversation that's still unresolved \u2014 is now a month old and has been lived inside for thirty days. The distance is still the distance. What's changed is how you're carrying it.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-breakup",packId:"dating",title:"The Breakup",subtitle:"You know what's coming. So do they. Someone has to say it first.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"The relationship has been over for a while. Not officially. But the last three months have been a different relationship than the one you started. Both of you know it. Tonight you're having dinner and one of you is going to say it.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The dinner was Riley's suggestion. You both knew what the dinner was when it was suggested \u2014 there's a specific quality of 'we need to talk' and this was that quality. You're at a restaurant you both like. Riley is looking at the menu with the focus of someone not actually looking at the menu. The last three months: two weeks fine, six weeks of something wrong neither of you named, four more weeks of being careful with each other. You are going to end this tonight. You have known this for four weeks. The question is how.",choices:[{label:"Say it before the food comes.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Wait until after the food \u2014 let the meal happen.",next:"s2b"},{label:"Ask Riley how they're doing and see if they go first.",next:"b1a"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Riley says: 'Honestly? Not great.' Then: 'I think we both know what tonight is.' So they knew too. You say you think so. Riley says: 'Do you want to do the food first or.' You say whatever Riley wants. Riley says: 'After the food. I'm actually hungry.' There is something almost funny about this that you both catch at the same moment.",choices:[{label:"Have dinner, then have the conversation.",next:"s2b"}]},s2a:{id:"s2a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'I think we should talk about where we are.' Riley puts the menu down. 'I've been trying to figure out how to start this for two weeks.' You say: 'I don't think the last three months have been us.' Riley says: 'No. They haven't.' The waiter comes. You both order automatically from memory.",choices:[{label:"Say what you think happened.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Ask Riley what they think happened.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Skip the analysis \u2014 just name where you are now.",next:"s3c"}]},s2b:{id:"s2b",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You have dinner. It is a good dinner in the particular way of a meal between two people who have been good together and are about to stop. Careful and warm and sad without anyone saying the sad part yet. Riley talks about work. You talk about your sister. By the end of the meal you are both quiet.",choices:[{label:"Say it now.",next:"s2a"},{label:"Notice what being careful with each other means.",next:"b2a"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'I think we stopped being the same people who started this. Not in a bad way \u2014 just in a way that moved us apart.' Riley: 'I've been thinking that too. I didn't want to say it because I didn't want to be the one who said it.' You say: 'Same.' The symmetry is almost a relief.",choices:[{label:"Talk about what that means for ending it.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Ask if Riley thinks there's a version where it works.",next:"s4b"},{label:"Think about what changed first.",next:"b3a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Riley says: 'I think I changed what I need and I didn't tell you. And you changed too. I knew it was happening and I didn't know what to say about it.' You ask what they need now. 'I need someone in the same stage of life.' Hard to hear, accurate, respectful.",choices:[{label:"Agree \u2014 this is accurate.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Ask if there's a version where this changes.",next:"s4b"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'I'm not happy in this. I think you're not happy either. I don't want to analyze why \u2014 I just want to say that clearly.' Riley: 'I haven't been happy for a while. I've been hoping it would shift.' You say: 'Me too.' Two people hoping, being careful, neither of them shifting \u2014 that's the three months.",choices:[{label:"Talk about how to end it well.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Ask what Riley needs from this conversation.",next:"s4c"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You talk about how to end it well \u2014 things you share, mutual friends, the practical layer. Riley is good at this, better than you expected, or maybe you expected it because you know Riley well enough to know they'd be good at even the hard things.",choices:[{label:"End the evening.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Ask if you'll be okay as people \u2014 not together, just as people.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Work through the practical layer.",next:"b4a"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Riley considers it. 'I've asked myself that a lot. I keep coming back to: maybe. But maybe isn't a relationship.' You say: 'No. It isn't.' The maybe sits between you and you both let it go.",choices:[{label:"End it with honesty.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Take a week before making it official.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4c:{id:"s4c",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Riley says: 'I need this to be clean. I've had messy endings and I don't want this to be one, because you don't deserve that and I don't either.' Clean is the right word. You talk about what clean looks like: when, how to tell people, what happens to the things you share.",choices:[{label:"End the evening.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"Outside, Riley says: 'I'm glad we did it this way.' You say you are too. You hug in the way of two people who know they are not going to do this again for a while and are saying goodbye to the version of themselves that has been doing this together. It is sad and necessary and complete.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Riley says: 'I think so. Eventually.' Then: 'I liked who I was with you. Even at the end. I just need to like who I am by myself first.' You understand this. You walk home knowing the relationship is finished and that it finished the right way, which is the thing that will allow you to be people to each other again in a year when enough time has passed.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You take the week. Nothing changes except that you both know it's the last week, which changes everything about the week in small ways. At the end of it you have the shorter, easier version of the same conversation. The extra week was for you, not for the relationship. This is okay. People need different things when they're ending something.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You've been careful with each other for four weeks. Careful is not the same as kind \u2014 it's what you do when kindness is no longer sufficient, when you're protecting something that's already gone and both of you know it and neither of you wants to be the one to say it's gone.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2a"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The thing that changed first, you think, was something small \u2014 a conversation that went differently than expected, a preference that turned out to be incompatible, a future that neither of you described to the other and that turned out not to match. You don't know which one it was. The not-knowing is part of it.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"date-street",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The practical layer of ending something \u2014 the things you share, the mutual friends, the decisions about what to say to people \u2014 is real and necessary and also a way of existing in the ending without fully being in it yet. Riley is good at this layer. You're getting better.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Home. The relationship is over. It was real, it ended, it was done the right way. You're sitting with all of that in the quiet of the apartment.",choices:[{label:"Think about what you carry.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Think about Riley.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Riley. The good months and the careful months and tonight. The way they handled the practical layer without making it clinical. The thing they said about liking who they were with you, if they said it. You'll think about them. That's normal and appropriate and will reduce with time.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2b"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The apartment. Tomorrow will be the first day of something that isn't the relationship. That's a real transition and takes a real amount of time. You're at the beginning of it.",choices:[{label:"Think about what you carry.",next:"xf2b"},{label:"Think about Riley.",next:"xfb1"}]},xf2b:{id:"xf2b",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The moment of resolution. Everything has come together or not, and now you find out what it means.",choices:[{label:"Find out what the beginning produces.",next:"e1"},{label:"Think about what the relationship was.",next:"e2"},{label:"Think about what comes next.",next:"e3"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"After the dinner. The relationship is over \u2014 officially, specifically, done the right way over food you both knew well and in a restaurant you both liked. The practical layer is sorted. What remains is the texture of the days that follow.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The week after. You tell the three people who are yours to tell. The conversations are short. People are kind. The specific sadness of ending something real settles into the days like weather \u2014 present, changing, not permanent.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A month later. The relationship is further past tense now. Not gone \u2014 it's yours, it stays yours \u2014 but past tense. You run into Riley once, at a place you both knew. The moment is brief and not painful, which tells you something about where you both are.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-friend",packId:"dating",title:"The Friend",subtitle:"Three years of friendship. One confession. The next twenty-four hours.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"You told your best friend how you feel. Last night. After too many drinks and a moment that felt right. They didn't say anything back. This morning they texted asking if you want to get coffee.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The text was: 'Hey. Coffee today? Same place?' Which could mean they feel the same way and want to tell you in person. Or they don't and want to be kind about it in person. Or they don't know and need to see you to figure it out. Or they want to pretend last night didn't happen and see if you'll let them. Jamie has been your best friend for three years. Last night you said: 'I'm in love with you. I've been in love with you for about a year and I needed to say it.' Jamie looked at you for a long time and said: 'Can I have some time?' You said yes. This morning is this morning.",choices:[{label:"Text Jamie what you're walking in feeling.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Go to the coffee shop and let Jamie lead.",next:"s2"},{label:"Call Jamie before you go.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You text: 'Going to be honest \u2014 I'm nervous. I don't know what version of this I'm walking into.' Jamie texts back immediately: 'Same. I'm already here.' Then: 'I have things to say. I just needed to say them in person.'",choices:[{label:"Go to the coffee shop.",next:"s2"},{label:"Think about why you said it.",next:"b2a"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Jamie answers. You say: 'Is the coffee a good thing or a kind thing?' Jamie: 'What do you mean?' You say: 'Are you bringing me there to say yes or to say let's figure out how to be okay.' Jamie: 'I'm bringing you there because you're my best friend and I need to see your face when I say what I'm going to say.' You put on your jacket.",choices:[{label:"Go to the coffee shop.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Jamie is at the corner table. The one you always sit at. Two coffees \u2014 yours made the way you always have it, which Jamie ordered without thinking. Jamie says: 'Hi.' You say: 'Hi.' Jamie: 'I spent all night thinking about what to say.' You say: 'Same.' Jamie: 'You first or me?'",choices:[{label:"Say: You first.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Say: Me \u2014 and repeat what you said last night, sober.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Say: Can we just sit here for a minute.",next:"b2a"},{label:"Notice what the coffee is.",next:"b3a"}]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The hundred times at the corner table. The way Jamie orders your coffee without thinking about it. The specific quality of being known by someone who's been paying attention for three years without being asked to. You said it because the alternative \u2014 carrying it past the point where you could carry it quietly \u2014 was worse than saying it.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Jamie says: 'I've been going back and forth since last night on whether what I feel is what you said last night, and I don't know the answer yet. What I do know is that I didn't want it to stop \u2014 when you said it. I kept waiting to feel scared and I mostly felt \u2014 not scared.' You ask what not scared feels like. 'Like maybe I've known something for a while and didn't have a word for it.'",choices:[{label:"Tell Jamie you can figure it out together.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Ask what Jamie needs from you right now.",next:"s4b"},{label:"Sit with what not scared means.",next:"b4a"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'I meant what I said last night. Sober I mean it exactly the same way. You're my best friend and at some point in the last year that became something else and I told you because not telling you felt worse than the risk.' Jamie looks at you for a long moment. Then: 'The risk part. What were you risking?' You say: this. Having to sit here not knowing. Jamie: 'We're sitting here. We're okay.' Then: 'I need to tell you what I've been thinking.'",choices:[{label:"Listen.",next:"s4c"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You try. The first week is awkward in the specific way of two people who know each other completely and are re-learning how to stand next to each other.",choices:[{label:"See how it goes.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Jamie says: 'I need to not rush this. I need to know what it is before I put a name on it.' You say you can do that. 'What would that look like?' Jamie: 'Like this. More of this. And we talk about it as we go.'",choices:[{label:"Agree to that.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4c:{id:"s4c",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Jamie says: 'I've been trying to figure out if what I feel for you is friendship so close it looks like something else, or if it's something else I've been calling friendship because it was safer. I think it might be the second one.' Not a yes. Not a no. The most honest version of I don't know you've ever heard.",choices:[{label:"Suggest trying \u2014 seeing what the something else is.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Say you can wait \u2014 the friendship isn't going anywhere.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Ask what scared looks like, if not scared is a good sign.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You try. It is awkward in the first week in the specific way of two people who know each other completely and are re-learning how to stand next to each other. By the third week the awkward has mostly resolved into something that is the friendship plus something else, and the something else has a name now that you both agree on. The corner table remains the corner table.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"More of this. You talk about it as you go. Three months of more-of-this and talking-about-it-as-you-go. The name for it arrives one day without either of you announcing it, which is probably the right way for it to arrive.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"neutral",text:"You say the friendship isn't going anywhere. Jamie: 'Is that enough for you right now?' You say: right now, yes. The something else is something you can both keep having access to without forcing it into a shape it might not be ready for yet. You finish your coffees. You hug outside in the way you always hug outside. Nothing has been decided. Nothing has been ruined.",choices:[]},e4:{id:"e4",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You ask about scared. Jamie says: 'Scared would be: I can't lose you, so I'm going to say no. Not scared is: I can't lose you, so I'm going to be honest.' You say those lead to different places. Jamie: 'Yeah. I know which one this is.' The coffee takes another hour. When you leave the corner table it is different from every other time you have left it, and both of you know that, and both of you are walking toward the difference.",choices:[]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Three years of friendship contains a lot of information about what someone does when things are hard. Jamie, when things are hard, shows up specifically and quietly. That's what the coffee is. That's what ordering yours without thinking is. You're watching it happen and you see it now for what it is.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Not scared. You asked what not scared feels like and Jamie said 'like maybe I've known something for a while and didn't have a word for it.' The not-having-a-word-for-it part is the part that's been sitting between you in all the conversations where you've talked about everything else.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Six weeks after. The something-else has a shape now. You and Jamie are navigating it.",choices:[{label:"Think about what the something-else is.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Think about the three years.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The three years are still there. The corner table, the regular coffee, the hundred times of ordinary friendship. None of that is gone. What's been added to it is something that came out of it rather than something that replaced it. The friendship didn't become the relationship. The relationship grew from the friendship.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2b"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The corner table on a Wednesday that is now a regular Wednesday. Both of you ordering what the other will have before they say it. The friendship, but different. The something-else, but built on the something-first.",choices:[{label:"Think about the something-else.",next:"xf2b"},{label:"Think about the three years.",next:"xfb1"}]},xf2b:{id:"xf2b",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The moment of resolution. Everything has come together or not, and now you find out what it means.",choices:[{label:"Find out what this becomes.",next:"e1"},{label:"Think about the coffee.",next:"e2"},{label:"Think about telling people.",next:"e3"},{label:"Think about what you said.",next:"e4"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The weeks after the corner table. The something-else is present and being navigated. Some days it's easy \u2014 you've been friends for three years and the ease of that remains. Some days the new configuration requires more attention.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"A month in. The people who knew you both before are starting to notice something different. They don't say anything yet. The thing that's different is subtle enough that it can still be not-said. But Priya, who set up the date you almost didn't go on, says: 'You seem like you're somewhere else lately.' You say: 'Somewhere good.'",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"date-cafe",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Six weeks. The corner table on a Wednesday that started as unusual is now a regular Wednesday. The something-else has been named \u2014 to each other, not yet publicly \u2014 and the naming changed something and also didn't change anything, the way naming usually works.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}},{id:"the-setup",packId:"dating",title:"The Setup",subtitle:"Your friend swears you'll love them. Your friend has been wrong before.",length:"Medium",free:!1,brief:"Your friend set you up. They were very confident. The date is in an hour and you've just seen something in their social media that you're not sure what to do with.",startNodeId:"s1",nodes:{s1:{id:"s1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Your friend Priya set this up and Priya's track record is genuinely good \u2014 two long-term couples. The person, Sam, is a landscape architect who volunteers at a literacy program and makes very good pasta, apparently. An hour before the date you found their Instagram. Mostly unremarkable. Then a comment from eight months ago \u2014 the kind of opinion about a public issue that you disagree with strongly, stated as if it's obviously correct. It might be a dealbreaker. It might be a conversation.",choices:[{label:"Text Priya and ask about it.",next:"b1a"},{label:"Go on the date. One comment isn't a person.",next:"s2"},{label:"Cancel. You're not interested in an evening of disagreement.",next:"b1b"}]},b1a:{id:"b1a",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Priya: 'Oh, the waterfront development thing? Yeah, we've argued about it. Sam is wrong but they argue well.' Then: 'It's not a core values thing. It's a zoning policy opinion. Go on the date.' This is useful information. The category of the disagreement has shifted from dealbreaker-adjacent to genuinely-discussable.",choices:[{label:"Go on the date.",next:"s2"},{label:"Still not sure \u2014 what if there are more opinions like this?",next:"s2"},{label:"Think about what one comment predicts.",next:"b2a"}]},b1b:{id:"b1b",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You draft a cancel text. Then you sit with it. One comment, eight months old, on a post about local zoning. You're about to cancel a date with someone Priya says is great because of one comment. You delete the draft. You might disagree with them about everything. You can find that out in person.",choices:[{label:"Go on the date.",next:"s2"}]},s2:{id:"s2",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Sam is already there. They stand up when you arrive \u2014 charming or old-fashioned or both \u2014 and says: 'Priya said I'd recognize you immediately. She was right.' Smooth without being polished. You sit. Sam asks questions and listens to the answers. The Instagram comment is in the back of your mind.",choices:[{label:"Bring up the topic organically and see where Sam lands.",next:"s3a"},{label:"Don't bring it up \u2014 let the date be the date.",next:"s3b"},{label:"Ask Sam directly: tell me something you believe that people disagree with.",next:"s3c"},{label:"Notice how Sam listens.",next:"b3a"}]},s3a:{id:"s3a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The waterfront development comes up naturally. You mention the neighborhood, Sam mentions the changes, you mention what you think. Sam says: 'I've gone back and forth on that. Eight months ago I thought one thing. Now I think \u2014 the housing shortage argument is right, the heritage argument is also right, and the decision is about which right thing you're willing to lose.' You say: 'You posted about it differently.' Sam blinks. Then laughs. 'You went through my Instagram.'",choices:[{label:"Own it \u2014 yes, you did.",next:"s4a"},{label:"Say you stumbled across it.",next:"s4b"}]},s3b:{id:"s3b",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The date is good. Sam is observant and direct without being blunt. You disagree about one thing over dinner \u2014 unrelated to the Instagram comment \u2014 and the disagreement is interesting rather than uncomfortable. By the end you've almost forgotten about the comment. Then Sam: 'Can I ask you something? Priya said you might have some reservations going in. Anything you want to clear?'",choices:[{label:"Tell Sam about the Instagram comment.",next:"s4c"},{label:"Say no reservations \u2014 you were wrong to have them.",next:"xfm1"}]},s3c:{id:"s3c",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Sam says: 'Okay \u2014 I believe that urban density is more important than architectural preservation in a housing crisis. Most people I know disagree.' Then: 'What do you believe that people disagree with?' You say yours. Sam: 'Huh. I think you're right actually. I haven't thought about it that way.' Genuine or the perfect thing to say \u2014 you can't tell yet.",choices:[{label:"Pursue the conversation \u2014 test whether it's genuine.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Let it be what it is and enjoy the rest of the date.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4a:{id:"s4a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You say: 'Yes. The comment about the waterfront. I wasn't sure what to make of it.' Sam: 'I changed my mind since then. The density argument won.' Then: 'For the record \u2014 I went through yours too. You posted a very strong opinion about pineapple on pizza in 2019.' You had entirely forgotten this. The evening becomes easier after this.",choices:[{label:"See where the rest of the date goes.",next:"xfm1"},{label:"Notice the equalizing effect.",next:"b4a"}]},s4b:{id:"s4b",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Sam says: 'The waterfront post? I've changed my mind on that.' Then, with a look: 'It's okay that you looked. I looked too. Your 2021 bird photo series is genuinely good.' You had forgotten you posted those.",choices:[{label:"Finish the date.",next:"xfm1"}]},s4c:{id:"s4c",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"You tell Sam about the comment. 'Oh, the waterfront thing. I've changed my mind on that.' Then: 'What made you decide to come anyway?' You say Priya said you argue well. Sam laughs. 'That is a very Priya thing to say.'",choices:[{label:"End the evening.",next:"xfm1"}]},e1:{id:"e1",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"Genuine \u2014 Sam follows the thought through dinner and three days later sends you an article with a note: 'you were right, changed my mind more.' The date ends with plans for a second one. Priya is insufferably pleased about this and you let her be. The comment was eight months old and people update their positions. You almost didn't find out.",choices:[]},e2:{id:"e2",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"The date ends well. Outside, Sam says: 'I'm glad we did this.' You say you are too. The Instagram comment is something you'll tell Sam about eventually, maybe on the third date, when it will be funny. For now it's just the thing that almost made you cancel a good evening before you knew it was going to be one.",choices:[]},e3:{id:"e3",scene:"date-street",canBack:!1,isEnd:!0,endType:"win",text:"You say no reservations and mean it by the time you say it. The date ends with Sam asking if you want to do this again and you saying yes without thinking about it. Priya texts the next morning: 'So?' You send back a thumbs up and she responds with seventeen exclamation marks. The comment told you very little about Sam. The date told you much more.",choices:[]},b2a:{id:"b2a",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"One comment, eight months old, on a post about local zoning policy. You've formed opinions about people based on less. You've also been wrong about people based on exactly this kind of evidence. The question is whether the comment is the kind of information that predicts other information, or whether it's the kind that doesn't.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s2"}]},b3a:{id:"b3a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Sam listens in the way that means they're actually processing what you're saying rather than waiting for you to finish so they can say the thing they were already going to say. This is rarer than it should be on a first date and you find yourself noticing it.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"s3a"}]},b4a:{id:"b4a",scene:"date-restaurant",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The Instagram confession moment \u2014 you went through their profile, they went through yours \u2014 has the quality of a mutually revealed embarrassment that functions as an equalizer. Both of you were doing the same thing. Neither of you is embarrassed by the same thing being done to you.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm1"}]},xf1:{id:"xf1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Home after the date. The Instagram comment that almost stopped the evening from happening. The evening that happened anyway. The mutual social media reconnaissance that turned into an equalizer. Priya is going to ask.",choices:[{label:"Think about what to tell Priya.",next:"xf2"},{label:"Think about Sam.",next:"xfb1"}]},xfb1:{id:"xfb1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!0,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Sam. The standing up when you arrived. The questions and the listening. The waterfront comment recanted, the bird photos remembered, the mutual Instagram reconnaissance acknowledged. You looked them up and they looked you up and you both showed up anyway and the date was good. That's something.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf2b"}]},xf2:{id:"xf2",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Priya texts: 'So???' The three question marks indicate the level of investment. You look at your phone. The date was good. You're going to tell Priya it was good. The question is in how much detail.",choices:[{label:"Think about what to tell Priya.",next:"xf2b"},{label:"Think about Sam.",next:"xfb1"}]},xf2b:{id:"xf2b",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The moment of resolution. Everything has come together or not, and now you find out what it means.",choices:[{label:"A second date happens.",next:"e1"},{label:"Think about what the date actually was.",next:"e2"},{label:"Tell Priya.",next:"e3"}]},xfm1:{id:"xfm1",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"After the date. Home. The evening is behind you. The Instagram comment that almost stopped it sits in your memory as an almost \u2014 a decision you made, to go anyway, that produced a good evening. Priya was right. Your almost-cancellation was wrong. These two things can both be true.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm2"}]},xfm2:{id:"xfm2",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The day after. Normal day. The date was good. You're either going to text Sam or wait to be texted. The question of who moves first on a first date that went well is its own small negotiation.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfm3"}]},xfm3:{id:"xfm3",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"Two days after. Priya has texted three times, each text with increasing punctuation. You have not yet responded to Priya. You have been thinking about how to respond \u2014 not to Priya, but to the question of what the date was and what you want the next thing to be.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xfmx"}]},xfmx:{id:"xfmx",scene:"date-apartment",canBack:!1,isEnd:!1,endType:null,text:"The date with Sam is behind you. Priya is waiting for a report. You have the report: it was good, the Instagram comment was irrelevant, the mutual reconnaissance was equalizing, the evening was worth the decision to go. You decide how to tell Priya, which is its own small question.",choices:[{label:"Continue.",next:"xf1"}]}}}]},240,[]);
